    <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"SoftwareApplication","name":"LCNDA — The Digital Ancestors' Notebook","alternateName":"Le Carnet numérique des ancêtres","applicationCategory":"Genealogy","operatingSystem":"macOS, Windows, Linux","softwareVersion":"0.11.0","datePublished":"2026-11-28","license":"https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.html","isAccessibleForFree":true,"offers":{"@type":"Offer","price":"0","priceCurrency":"EUR"},"inLanguage":["fr","en","es","de","it","pt","pl","nl","cs","sv","da","sk","nb","ro","fi","hu","hr","sl","lt","lv","et","is","tr","lb","sq","mt","vi","id","ru","uk","el","bg"],"downloadUrl":["download/LCNDA-0.11.0-mac-arm64.dmg","download/LCNDA-0.11.0-windows-x64.exe","download/LCNDA-0.11.0-linux-x86_64.AppImage"],"releaseNotes":"carnet-numerique-des-ancetres-en.php#v0-11-0","description":"A local app (nothing on the Internet) to organise, document and pass on a family history: records, sources, trees, maps, transcription of old records, estates and heirs."}</script>
    <!-- FAQ-JSONLD:DEBUT -->
    <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","inLanguage":"en","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What does a record’s “🛡 Reliability” score mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A score out of 100: the reliability of each fact (a date “about…” counts as estimated), the share of facts with a source, and the 🛡 strength of the link with each parent. The record’s “🛡 Reliability” block gives the reasons and how to gain points. Below 50 (Settings → ⚙️ Check thresholds), the record is “to review before publishing”: filter and column in Lists → People, widget “🛡 Least reliable records”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note the illnesses and known causes of death in the family (🩺 Family health)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is hidden at first, because it is private: Settings → Data → “🩺 Family health” → “Show”. On the record, the “🩺 Health” block (arranged like the other blocks): “＋ Add” an illness, a disability, a cause of death or an epidemic lived through, with its date (as in a record), its source, a note and “To check”. The cause written on the death record appears there by itself, and the epidemics during the person’s lifetime (“🌍 The world around”) are noted in one click. The whole family: Lists → “🩺 Family health”. This is family memory: the app gives no advice and calculates no risk."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see an illness across the branches of the family?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “🩺 Family health” → “🌳 By branches” tab (or the “🌳 By branches” button of the 🩺 block on the record): choose the person at the centre and the illness with the picker. The fan of ancestors colours those in whom it is noted; “The four sides” sorts these people by grandparent. Only numbers of people, never a percentage."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does family health go into my exports?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. It is never in the website, family sharing, packages, books, simple mode, games, or when printing the record; when hidden, it does not appear in search either, and nothing shows in view-only mode 🔒. Only GEDCOM can carry it, if you tick “🩺 Include family health” when exporting. The causes written on death records remain events, exported as before."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the guided tour (first steps) again?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “FAQ / guide” → “▶ Restart the guided tour” (or type “tour” in the quick search). In 6 steps, the app circles the real buttons: create a record, add a parent, add a photo, add an event and its place, make a backup, find Settings and the FAQ. Click the circled button to do it for real (the tour waits until you have finished), or “Next” to just look; “Previous”, “Skip the tour”, and on the keyboard → ← Esc. A step whose button is hidden (locked record, hidden block) is skipped. The tour is offered once at first launch; it never opens in simple mode or view-only mode."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the first-launch screen for (“I want to do something else”, sample database)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The very first time an empty database is opened, under “Create and open the database”, the link “I want to do something else ▾” offers 9 uses (🌳 genealogy, 🐾 animal lineage, 🔭 counting animals, 🏠 house or village, 🎨 artist, 📚 archives, 🔤 surname, 🏭 company, ✨ memories): the app then only shows the useful tabs, without deleting anything. You can also import (Hérédis, MacFamilyTree, GEDCOM), “Start with an empty database” or “🧪 Try with a sample database”: an imaginary family (5 generations, nobody born after 1950, nobody living) kept separately in “My genealogies”, with a “🧪 Sample database” banner to go back to your genealogy. To change later: Settings → Appearance → Menus → “🧭 How you use the app” (several uses at once, “First screen on opening”, “🧭 See the first launch again”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What was the weather on the day of a birth, a marriage or a death (weather of the past)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, under the place of each event, a small “🌦” badge gives the icon and the temperature (at the hour of the record if known, otherwise in the afternoon). On hover: a sentence (“It was very cold: −6 °C in the morning…”) and the reliability; on click: the day in 4 moments (since 1940), the comparison with the 30-year normal, the station, the days around, the source. The date must be known to the day and the place must be placed on the map. Everything works without the Internet: the data is in the app (the base set) or in a pack imported once. Old weather remains an estimate: it helps you imagine the day, it does not replace the record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What do “Measured”, “Station 31 km away”, “Estimated” and “Rough reconstruction” mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“Measured”: a station less than 15 km away (and less than 150 m difference in altitude) took readings that day. “Station … km away”: the nearest is between 15 and 40 km away, so it is indicative; rain becomes “it rained in the area”, because it changes quickly from one village to the next. “Estimated (grid cell ≈ 30 km)”: no station close enough, the value comes from the ERA5 reanalysis (1940 → today). “Rough reconstruction”: from 1836 to 1939, the 20CR reanalysis (≈ 100 km), hidden until you ask for it in the settings. Before that, or with nothing: “No known reading near here before 1863 (first station: Toulouse)”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare two old maps of the same area (two periods)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → “⇆ Compare” button (top bar): on the left the background you usually choose, on the right a second background (“Right”: Cassini, État-Major, IGN 1950…). “▏ Line”: drag the white line (or the ← → arrows on the keyboard); “◫ Side by side”: the two backgrounds next to each other, centred on the same spot (the points are hidden); “◐ Transparency”: the second background on top, with adjustable transparency. × closes the comparison; the choice is remembered."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the provinces before 1789, the departments of 1790 or vanished communes on the map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → “🏛 Borders ▾”: tick “Provinces of the Ancien Régime”, “Departments of 1790”, “Vanished or merged communes” (and, if you like, renamed communes). A click on an area or a circle gives its name, its date and, for a vanished commune, its present-day commune. The provinces and departments of 1790 are APPROXIMATE outlines drawn from today’s departments (there is no free official source of their real outlines). Vanished communes come from the official INSEE history (since 1943), to download once."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where are the old borders (vanished communes)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"They come with the app: two official INSEE files (Code officiel géographique 2026, Licence Ouverte 2.0, 5.5 MB), installed by themselves and checked by their fingerprint, without the Internet, for all your genealogies. Settings → “🏛 Old borders” shows their size, their fingerprint and their licence; “📦 Install the old borders” puts them back if you removed them. The old names of communes also help the place picker (“formerly “…””). “🗑 Remove” takes them away, with “↶ Undo”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see where a surname is concentrated, century by century?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → New → Maps → “🔤 Surname map” → “From the app” tab: choose the name in the list (the most frequent first) or a person (their name is used), then “Use this data”. Coloured departments or circles per place; the ▶ slider under the drawing goes through the centuries one by one. Never a living person or a confidential record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see on a map the places of the ancestors I share with my DNA cousins?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → New → Maps → “🧬 DNA cousins map” → “From the app”: choose the DNA test. Each match linked to a record in the tree (DNA tab) gives the calculated common ancestor and their places; colour by group or by shared cM. The names of the matches (often living) are only written if you tick the box."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see on a map what I have already searched and what is missing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → New → Maps → “🔎 Map of your research” → “From the app”: your record indexes (registers consulted), the communes of your search sweeps and the records your ancestors are missing (“What’s missing?”). Green = all seen, orange = partly, red = gaps to cover; choose the kind of record and the period in the settings. A click on a commune: what has been seen and what is missing, with “↗ Open”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see a heat map decade by decade?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → New → Maps → “🔥 Heat map over time” → “From the app”. The ▶ slider under the drawing (the same as for all diagrams) goes through the decades; in the settings: the decade alone or everything up to it, the same scale for all or one for each."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see on a map the years when deaths rise (crises, epidemics)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → New → Maps → “⚰️ Crises and epidemics” → “From the app” → “Take this data” (or The world around → “Family deaths by year” → “🗺 On the map”): for each municipality, the app compares a year’s deaths with the usual level (the 5 years before and after, without the year itself); a dot marks the year that exceeds 2.5 times the usual level with at least 3 deaths (adjustable thresholds). Small villages are grouped with their neighbours, children can be counted separately. A click gives the people and what was happening at the same time (the crises of 🌍 The world around and your events): a reference point, not an explanation, to check in the records. Never a living person or a confidential sheet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see on a map where the godparents and witnesses came from?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → New → Maps → “🕸 Godparents and witnesses on the map” → “From the app” → “Take this data” (or 🕸 Relationship network → Link tab → “🗺 See on the map”): each link becomes a line between the village of the record and the one the godparent or witness came from (written in the record, otherwise their residence at the date, otherwise their birth); the more links, the thicker the line, and links within the same village make a ring. A click on a line gives the records. Priest and registrar are unticked at first; living witnesses are never counted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see on a map how far my ancestors lived, generation by generation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → New → Maps → “⭕ The life area” → “From the app” → “Take this data” (or 🧭 Far from the cradle → Link tab → “⭕ See the life area on the map”): for each generation, a circle holding 80 % of its births, marriages and deaths around their middle; a table gives the radius, the surface and the places outside the area. Settings: 50, 80, 90 % or by hand, circle or hull, by generation, by century or for one life, one area per box or one at a time with ▶. Fewer than 5 events: no area."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see my father’s line and my mother’s line on a map, and where they meet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → New → Maps → “👫 The two lines side by side” → “From the app” → “Take this data” (or, on a person’s sheet: ⋯ → “👫 Their two lines on the map”): father’s side in blue, mother’s side in orange; the municipalities where both lines passed are circled, and the ▶ slider shows them getting closer decade after decade. Also: the four grandparents, two people, two names, two lists. Settings: overlaid, side by side or ⇆ swipe; same municipality, same time or within a few km. A living starting person is never a dot."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the family’s occupations on a map, century by century?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → New → Maps → “🔨 Occupations on the map” → “From the app” tab → “Take this data”: a pie chart per municipality, one colour per main sector (the classification of the Occupation tree); the ▶ slider goes through the centuries one by one. A click on a municipality gives its occupations. Settings: by occupation family, a single occupation and its variants, a small map per century, where to place the occupation. From an old occupation’s sheet: “🗺 Where it was practised”. Never a living person or a confidential sheet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide the weather (everywhere, for one person or for one event)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everywhere: Settings → Data → “🌦 Weather of the past” → “Weather on records”: Always, On hover or Never; below, the kinds of events (birth, baptism, marriage, death, burial, census…). For one person or a single event: right-click on the badge (or ⋯ in the details, or ⋯ of the “🌦 Weather of their life” block) → “🙈 Hide the weather for this event” or “… for this person”, with “↶ Undo”. Everything hidden is found in the settings, under “Hidden weather”, and shows again with a click on ×. The “Weather of their life” block can also be moved or hidden like the other blocks of the record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the altitude of a place matter for the weather?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the mountains, a nearby station in the plain does not tell the weather higher up: the app adds 1 km of distance per 10 m of difference in altitude and never says “Measured” when the difference exceeds 150 m. Each place automatically gets the altitude from the relief map (a grid of about 1 km taken from the Copernicus DEM, supplied with the weather, for France, Belgium, Switzerland, Luxembourg, Italy, the Netherlands, Spain, Germany, Portugal, the United Kingdom, Poland, Denmark, Czechia and Slovakia, islands included; outside France, Belgium, Switzerland and Luxembourg, only the altitude, no weather without the Europe pack), shown as “≈ 312 m (relief map)” in grey. To correct it: Charts → Places → the commune, “Altitude” box (in metres), or “⛰ Altitude of the place” in the details of a day; “↺ Put back the map altitude” returns to the automatic value. The day’s details give the altitude of the station and yours (“Station: Chamonix, 1,042 m — your place ≈ 1,035 m”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I give the time of the record for the weather?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the event window, “Time of the event” box: write it as in the record (“at 3 o’clock in the morning”, “14:30”). Below, the app says how it understood it and converts it to world time: local solar time before 1891, Paris time until 1911, German time in the occupied zone (1940–1944), then summer time (and the rules of Belgium, Switzerland and Luxembourg). It takes the nearest value (since 1940); before 1940, only the day. The time as written does not change."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I install the full pack or the Europe pack of the weather?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “🌦 Weather of the past”: an “📦 Install” button for each pack (base, Europe, full). The app downloads the pack from the project’s data page, in pieces: a bar shows the progress, “⏸ Pause” stops it and it picks up where it left off, even after you close the app. Then it checks it (its fingerprint) and installs it, with “↶ Undo”. No weather comes with the app: only what is needed to download it. Without internet, “Import a file…” takes a .lcmeteo file you have already downloaded or made (how-to: docs/METEO.md). “🗑 Delete the pack” removes it (with “↶ Undo”). “↗ About the data” gives each source and its licence."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see the weather other than on the record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The “🌦 Weather of their life” block on the record (to switch on in the weather settings: each big day of the life, and curious sentences such as “Born during the coldest week of the winter of 1900–1901 in Agen”); the “🌦 Weather of the past” widget on the dashboard (“100 years ago, on the day Jean and Marie married, it was raining in Auch”) and the ancestors’ badges in “🎂 Dates to celebrate”; Timeline → “🌦 Weather” in the column; the page layout → Object menu → “🌦 Weather box for an event…” (a dynamic object, with its source). Never a living person or a confidential record in the widget."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I track animals (rings, microchips, GPS collars, sightings)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Switch on the “🐾 Animal tracking” tab: Settings → Appearance → Top tabs (it is hidden at first), or “↗ Show the tab” in the list of animals. Its page is made of blocks like the dashboard: map of sightings, latest sightings, tracked animals, today’s GPS track, figures, counts at a site, animals to find, search by ring or chip, GPS import, weather, veterinary follow-up, groups. You can also put the usual dashboard widgets there (left column, “Other widgets of the app” section)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I have a tracking page per species?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In Tracking, “＋ New page”: its name, its icon, the species and the site. The page starts from blocks suggested for this species and only shows its animals (a block can keep its own choice with ✎). The page’s ⋯ (or a right-click on its button): rename, change the icon, duplicate, page shown on opening, delete (↶ Undo)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put a tracking block on the dashboard?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The block’s ⋯ → “📌 Also put it on the dashboard”: it is copied with its settings to the “Overview” page. The tracking blocks are also in the dashboard column, in the “🐾 Animal tracking” section, and work even if the tab is hidden."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note a bird’s colour rings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The animal’s record → “🔖 Rings and marks” block (section of the same name) → “🎨 Colour rings” → ✎: click one of the 4 positions (left or right leg, top or bottom), then one of the basic ring colours (white, black, red, orange, yellow, light green, dark green, light blue, dark blue, purple, pink, grey, brown, metal) or “Other colour” (the shared colour window: eyedropper, # code, recent colours), and the engraved code. A change keeps the old combination in the history."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which marks are offered depending on the species?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Bird: metal ring, colour rings, wing tag, GPS tag. Cat, dog: microchip, tattoo, collar, tag, GPS collar. Horse: microchip, UELN, description. Cow, sheep: ear tag, tattoo. Fish, turtle, bat: PIT, scute mark, forearm ring, transmitter. Marine animal: photo-identification (fin, fluke, scars), Argos tag, fin tag, PIT. Wild mammal: GPS or VHF collar, ear tag, ear notch, tattoo, microchip, photo-identification. Primate: photo-identification, microchip, tattoo. Everywhere: “Other mark”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I misread a ring code: how do I say so?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “New sighting”, write “?” for a character you could not read (“7?”) and [K|X] when you are unsure (“7[K|X]”, “3[3|8]”). Or click a character: sure → not read → “I’m unsure between…”. The app looks for the matching animals, with a lower score for doubtful characters, and the sighting keeps the reading: “reading: 7[K|X] (doubt)” with the “Partial reading” badge."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find an animal seen outdoors?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“＋ New sighting”: for a bird, click the colours seen on the legs (“?” = colour not sure, “∅” = no ring) and the code read; for a cat, a dog, a cow, a marine animal… the number read, even partly (“250 26 9…”). The app suggests the most likely animals (colours, code, seen near here) with a percentage. Otherwise: “＋ Create the record of a new animal” or “✎ Keep without a record”. A sighting can have several photos, sounds or videos (“Photos, sounds or videos” box): you see them as small thumbnails (3, then “+N”) in lists, blocks and the map; a click shows them large, one after the other."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import the track of a GPS tag or collar?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⤒ Import a track…” (Tracking, the animal’s record, or the “Quick GPS import” block): GPX, KML, GeoJSON, CSV (Movebank export, Tractive, Weenect, Invoxia, Kippy collars…). Step 2: the number of points, “one every N min”, “split into nights”, the animal (suggested automatically if the tag is on its record). Afterwards “↶ Undo” removes the points."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see the health of an animal in care?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The “🩺 Health” tab of its record: the timeline of states (healthy, injured, ill, in care, released, dead), the weight curve and each event (what it has, why, where, who treated it, care, medicines, weight, photos, outcome). The “🩺 Veterinary follow-up and state” block shows all the animals at a glance, with the most frequent causes of injury."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note that an animal belongs to a pack, a troop or a pod?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The “👥 Group” tab of its record: “＋ Joined a group” (the group, its role: alpha, matriarch, young, satellite… or a free word, the date). Then “Change role” or “Left the group…” (gone, excluded, dead, moved to another group). The timeline shows its whole social history; the group page shows its current and past members."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see all the animals and all the sightings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “🐾 Census” (also in the Tracking ⋯): Sightings, Tracked animals, Sites, Groups tabs; the shared bar (search, filter by species, site, year, ring reading…), List or Cards, sort. “⤓ Export ▾”: CSV, Movebank, GPX, KML or “🧾 Open in the spreadsheet”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I share my animal tracking with someone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In Tracking (⋯), the Census or an animal’s record: “📤 Share”: one record, chosen animals, a species, a site or all the tracking, with or without the GPS points and the photos. The observers’ names only go if you tick the box. The other person opens it with “⤒ Receive shared tracking…”: they see what will come in, then compare and choose for each difference (mine, theirs, both) before merging; “↶ Undo” puts everything back as before."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I check that there are no mistakes in my database?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Data quality → “✅ Database check”: a single list of what seems impossible or very rare (born after the father’s death, married after their own death, a 57-year-old mother, 31 February, duplicates, misplaced places, impossible journeys, baptism far from the place of birth, burial before death, sex that does not match the Sosa number, “between 1900 and 1800” written backwards, a date that is too long (a note pasted into it), “-5” read as the year 5, a person married to their grandson or to their ancestor, an animal recorded as a person’s parent or spouse…). The “Merge duplicates” list shows the same duplicates, with the same number. Badges 📅 Dates, 🎂 Ages, 👪 Parents, 👥 Duplicates, 📍 Places and 🔴 🟠 🟡 with the number found; “Filter ▾” for a branch, your ancestors (Sosa) or someone’s descendants; “⤓ CSV” to work at the archives. On each line: “↗ Record” (the child and the parent), “✎ Fix” (opens the event concerned) and “This is not a mistake”. Nothing is changed without you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"A check alert is wrong: how do I stop seeing it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“This is not a mistake”: choose a reason (“I checked in the record”, “These are two different people”, “The place is right”…), add a note if you like, then “Stop reporting” (“↶ Undo” right after). It goes into the “🙈 Exceptions” badge, from where “↺ Report again” brings it back. If one of the dates concerned changes later, the alert comes back by itself, marked “Dates changed” (box ticked automatically). Two different people are no longer suggested in “Merge duplicates” either."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I am merging two records that each have their own parents: what happens?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The record you keep receives both families of parents (nothing is deleted). Before merging, “⚖️ Compare” (Settings → Merge duplicates) says so in a yellow ⚠️ box, and so does the question before the merge. Afterwards, the “✅ Database check” points out the record with two families of parents: open it and remove (“Remove”) the wrong family."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I go through the check alerts one by one?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “✅ Database check”, “▶ One by one”: the alert in large, a small timeline of the dates, the family alongside. Dates are corrected on the spot (write them as in the record, Enter to save) and the alert is recalculated straight away. On the keyboard: C fix, N not a mistake, L later, ← → previous / next, Esc to go back to the list."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I change the age from which a mother is flagged as “too old”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: Settings → “⚙️ Check thresholds”: age of the mother and of the father (to check 🟠 / impossible 🔴), young marriage, longevity, delay after the father’s death, gap between two births, distant baptism, likeness of duplicates… Each slider is saved straight away (“↶ Undo” in the message), double-click = starting value, “↺ Reset to default” at the top. You also choose the record banner there: always, only 🔴 or never."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does “⚠️ 2 things to check” at the top of a record mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is the database check banner: what seems impossible for this person (“Married on 17 April 1863, after his death”, “His daughter Louise is born 4 years after his death”). It is folded: “See ▾” unfolds it, with “✎ Fix” and “Not a mistake”. An alert set aside does not come back there. The “✅ Database check” widget on the dashboard gives the number by kind and the % of records without an alert."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why are shortcuts not written the same on a Mac and on a PC?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Shortcuts are written for your computer: the Command key on a Mac, Ctrl on Windows and Linux, as small grey keys, the same everywhere (tooltips, menus, texts and Settings → Keyboard shortcuts). Leave the mouse on a button for a moment: a black tooltip says what it is for, with its key."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is in the “⋯” menu and under the right-click?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The same small action menu everywhere: Open, Edit, Rename, Duplicate, Export… and Delete in red at the very bottom. A right-click on a list row, a thumbnail or an object in an editor (Cut, Copy, Paste, Bring to front…, with their keys) opens this same menu, where the mouse is. Shift + right-click: the browser’s usual menu."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I print a record, a booklet or a list?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🖨 Print” (or ⌘P) always opens the same window: the preview of the page, the size (A4, A5), Portrait or Landscape, what is printed (photos…), then “🖨 Print”. “⤓ PDF” makes a PDF file: in the print window, choose “Save as PDF”. Only the content of the screen is printed, without the menus."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I print a presentation, a folded booklet, a slideshow or a single page?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everywhere, “🖨 Print” (or ⌘P in an editor, or “⤓ Export ▾ → 🖨 Print”) opens the same window, without a new tab. At the top: A4, A5 or ✎ Other, Portrait or Landscape. In the blue “What gets printed” area, the tool’s choices: slides with or without notes and how many per page, entries or table of the catalogue, one photo per page or a contact sheet, image and text of a transcription, bleed and crop marks of a page layout… Then “Pages”: All, The one shown, or Choose (“1-3, 7, 10-12”) and the number of copies. The preview on the right changes with each choice; ‹ › to see the other pages. The size and your choices are remembered for next time, tool by tool."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import my tree from Gramps?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In Gramps: Family Trees → Export, choose “Gramps XML Package (family tree and media)”: you get a .gpkg file that contains the tree and its photos (a plain .gramps file works too, but without the photos). Then Settings → Import → “⤒ Import a tree…”: the window shows what will come in (people, families, places, sources, media, witnesses), then “⤒ Import”. Everything is taken over: multiple names (married name, nicknames), events with their witnesses, godfathers and godmothers, places with their hierarchy and coordinates, sources, citations and repositories, notes, labels (with their colour), attributes. Only the photos stored in the package are copied: a path that would point outside the package is refused. Right after, “↶ Undo” removes everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import a tree from Ancestry or MyHeritage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Export the tree as GEDCOM on the site (Ancestry: Trees → Tree Settings → Export Tree; MyHeritage: Family Tree → Export to GEDCOM), then Settings → Import → “⤒ Import a tree…”. The app recognises the site: married names, main photo, labels, links to the site’s records and documents (kept in the citations), dates written in English (“Abt. 1850”, “Bet. 1850 and 1855”). Photos that stayed on the site are never downloaded: their addresses are kept in an “🔗 Online media” note on the record. To add this tree to a database that is already filled without duplicates, use Settings → “Merge a tree” instead."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import my tree from RootsMagic, Family Tree Maker or Généatique?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"RootsMagic: Settings → Import → “⤒ Import a tree…” and choose your .rmtree (or .rmgc) file directly: it is read without being changed (people, names, families, adoptions, events, places and localities with coordinates, sources, citations, media, notes, witnesses). Family Tree Maker encrypts its .ftm files: no other software can open them; in FTM do “File → Export → GEDCOM”, then import the .ged (military service, adoptions _FREL / _MREL, main photo are understood). Généatique: its own file format is not documented; export a GEDCOM (File → Export): ANSI, ANSEL or UTF-8, places with locality and INSEE code, godfathers, godmothers and witnesses are taken over. For a cousin’s tree, “Merge a tree” also accepts the .rmtree."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import a GeneWeb file (.gw)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In GeneWeb, export the database as a .gw text file (the “gwu” command, or Geneanet’s .gw export). Then Settings → Import → “⤒ Import a tree…” and choose the .gw file: the window shows what will come in (people, families, events). Taken over: families and children, births, baptisms, deaths (also “killed”, “died young”), burials, events from the pevt and fevt blocks, witnesses, godparents, adoptions, notes, sources, titles, GeneWeb dates (about, before, after, between, Julian and Republican calendars). An unreadable block is skipped and reported. Right after, “↶ Undo” removes everything. A .gwb database (GeneWeb’s folder) cannot be read: you need the .gw."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import a GEDCOM-X file (FamilySearch)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Import → “⤒ Import a tree…” and choose the GEDCOM-X file, in JSON (.json) or XML (.xml). People, other names, couples, parent-child links (with adoption), dated and placed facts, places with coordinates, sources and pages, notes, FamilySearch and Wikidata identifiers are taken over. The GEDCOM-X exported by the app reads back without any notable loss."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import from Legacy, Family Historian, Ancestral Quest, Reunion, Ancestris, webtrees, Brother’s Keeper, PAF, WikiTree, Geni or GenoPro?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Export a GEDCOM from that software or site, then Settings → Import → “⤒ Import a tree…”. The app recognises the software and keeps its special features: private records (_PRIV) → confidential, Legacy tags (_TAG) and Family Historian flags (_FLGS) → labels, webtrees “to do” → note, Wikidata identifier → record, adoptions (_FREL, _MREL), accents of old Mac or DOS files; other data specific to the software goes into a “Data specific to …” note. GenoPro’s .gno file is not read (no reliable public description): go through its GEDCOM export."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export my tree WITH the photos (GedZip, Gramps package)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Export → “⤓ To other software and sites” → “📦 GedZip with the photos” (GEDCOM 7 + media folder in a .gdz) or “🌳 Gramps package with the photos” (.gpkg). The window first gives the size and the number of files. With “For sharing”, living people and confidential records are hidden, and a photo showing one of these people is not put in the package. Both packages can be reopened here with “⤒ Import a tree”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a record’s Wikidata identifier for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For a well-known person (artist, mayor, scientist…), the Wikidata “Q…” number is noted in the “External references” block (✎). It gives a ↗ link to their page, and the “🌐 Complete from Wikidata” button: only when you click, the app reads the Wikidata entry on the Internet and shows you what is missing in your database (birth, death with the place, sex, occupation, a note with the description and the Wikipedia links). Nothing is replaced; you tick what you keep, and “↶ Undo” removes everything right after. The identifier also goes into GEDCOM and GEDCOM-X exports."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put my tree on Geneanet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Export → “⤓ To other software and sites” → “🌐 For Geneanet”: a GEDCOM ready for Geneanet (UTF-8, places in the format “[Locality] - Commune, INSEE code, department, region, country”). Living people and confidential records are always hidden: anonymous (“Living person”, to keep the links between branches) or removed, as you choose. Geneanet does not receive the photos: only those with an online address go, as a link. Then on Geneanet: My tree → Import a GEDCOM file."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I move my tree into Gramps, or send it as GEDCOM-X (FamilySearch)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Export → “⤓ To other software and sites”: “🌳 To Gramps” makes a .gramps file (in Gramps: Family Trees → Import) with places arranged by country, region, department, commune; “🔗 GEDCOM-X” makes FamilySearch’s JSON. Tick “For sharing” to hide living people and confidential records. The photos are not in these files, only their names."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put the family’s birthdays and name days in my calendar?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Export → “📅 Birthday calendar” (or Family calendar → ⤓ Export the birthdays): choose your ancestors (Sosa), the whole database or a branch, only the living or everyone, and births, marriages, deaths and 🎉 name days. The .ics file opens in Apple, Google or Outlook; each date comes back every year (a 29 February: the last day of February). Approximate dates (about, before…) are left aside and counted. Confidential records are never in it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put my cousins and the archive services in my phone’s contacts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Export → “📇 Contacts” (or Lists → Directory of the living → ⤓ Export ▾): a vCard file (.vcf) with the addresses, e-mails and phone numbers you noted: directory of the living, archive services, DNA cousins, correspondents in the letter register. Open it on your phone or your computer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see my family’s places in Google Earth?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Export → “🗺 Places (KML)” (or Lists → Places → ⤓ Export ▾): a .kml file with one folder per kind of event (Birth, Marriage, Death…) and one marker per place. “🗺 Places (GeoJSON)” does the same for QGIS, uMap… Places without coordinates are left aside; the events of hidden living people are not included."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import my genealogy society’s transcriptions (Nimègue)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Import → “📜 A society’s transcriptions (Nimègue…)”, or Lists → Record indexes → “⤒ Import transcriptions”. A Nimègue file (v2 or v3, with or without a header line, even in an old Windows encoding) or a society’s CSV, Excel or LibreOffice table: the columns are recognised automatically (date, surname, first name, father, mother, godfather, witnesses, shelfmark, image…) and you can correct them in the preview. Each record becomes a line in the record index of its commune, with its people and their role (child, spouse, deceased, father, godmother…). Tick “Create or link the records”: for each person who looks like a record in your database, the app asks “is this the same person?” — nothing is linked or created without your choice, a person mentioned in several records gives only one record, and an existing record is never changed. A record already imported is pointed out. Right after, “↶ Undo” removes everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import my family from an Excel or LibreOffice spreadsheet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Import → “👥 Table of people”. One row per person: first name, surname, sex, dates (written as in a record: “7 May 1925”, “about 1784”, “08/12/1960”), places, occupation, note, and the father, mother and spouse links by row number, by name (“Pierre MARTIN”) or by Sosa number (2 and 3 are the parents of 1…). The preview shows the records, couples and children that will come in, and the links that could not be made (a name used by two rows, unknown number). If a person may already be in your database, you choose: the same record, or create one. “↶ Undo” removes everything right after. Cells that contain a spreadsheet formula are never taken over."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I exchange my sources with Zotero, EndNote or JabRef (RIS, BibTeX)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"To bring references in: Settings → Import → “📚 References (RIS, BibTeX)” — each one becomes a source in your database, ready to cite (title, authors, publisher or archive service, date, address, shelfmark, notes; accents understood, even written the LaTeX way). A source already there is not taken again. To send them out: Settings → Export → “⤓ Sources (RIS, BibTeX)”. The exported file reads back without loss. The Research workshop has its own .bib and .ris buttons, which also keep the archive collection, the shelfmark and the notes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I fill in the coordinates of my places from Google Earth or a GeoJSON file?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Places → “⤒ Import coordinates (KML, GeoJSON)”, or Settings → Import → “📍 Place coordinates”. Each point in the file (KML, KMZ or GeoJSON) is matched with a place in your database by its name, and the department settles namesakes. Line by line, you tick: add the coordinates to a place that has none, replace those of a place (unticked at first), choose between several places with the same name, or create the place. The KML and GeoJSON files exported by the app read back without loss. “↶ Undo” puts everything back as before."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I open my genealogy in Obsidian (linked Markdown notes)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Export → “⤓ To other software and sites” → “🗒 For Obsidian”: a .zip file to unzip, then “Open folder as vault” in Obsidian. One note per person (identity, events, sources, notes) and one per place, linked by [[…]] links to the parents, children, spouses and places; the “Genealogy” note lists them all. You choose the whole database, your Sosa ancestors or a branch. Living people and confidential records stay hidden (“Living person”) or removed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see my family network in Gephi, yEd or Graphviz?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Export → “🕸 Network (GraphML)” for Gephi and yEd, or “🕸 Network (Graphviz)” for a .dot file (dot -Tsvg reseau.dot -o reseau.svg turns it into a drawing). Each person is a point with their name, sex, years, generation and Sosa number; each parent-child link and each couple is a line. Tick “one point per family” to put the couple between the parents and the children. Same privacy rules as the other exports."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I send my DNA segments to DNA Painter, or import DNA Painter, Genetic Affairs and LivingDNA?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🧬 DNA” tab → 🧩 Chromosomes → “⤓ Export for DNA Painter”: a CSV file (the format of FamilyTreeDNA’s chromosome browser, which DNA Painter reads in “Paint a new match”). To import: “⤒ Import (CSV)” also recognises the export of a DNA Painter profile (segments, side, group, colour), Genetic Affairs’ AutoCluster and AutoTree files (each cluster becomes a group, the grid gives the shared matches, AutoTree gives the common ancestor) and LivingDNA’s list of matches. A raw DNA file is never imported."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import my photos from Google Photos or Apple Photos with their date and place?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Media and documents → “⤒ Import photos”. Google Photos: the .zip archive from Google Takeout (1 GB at most; beyond that, unzip it and choose the photos with their .json files). Apple Photos: File → Export, with “Title, Keywords, and Caption” and “Location Information” (or with the .xmp files). The app takes over the date the photo was taken, the description, keywords and albums (as labels) and the GPS position. Places and named people are only suggested: tick “Link” for each one after checking. Everything is read on your computer, nothing is sent to the Internet. HEIC photos are left aside (export them as JPEG). “↶ Undo” removes everything that has just come in."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which DNA files can I import?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🧬 DNA” tab → Matches (or Chromosomes) → “⤒ Import (CSV)”: the lists of matches and segments exported by Ancestry (with DNAGedcom), MyHeritage, 23andMe, FamilyTreeDNA and GEDmatch. The company and the columns (cM, segments, longest segment, relationship, side, common ancestors, link to the profile or tree…) are recognised automatically; you can correct them in “Recognised columns”. “↶ Undo” right after puts everything back as before. A raw DNA file (your SNPs, the “raw data” file) is always refused: out of caution for your privacy and your family’s, and because in France a genetic test outside a medical or research setting is forbidden."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import a file (GEDCOM, CSV table, setting, list…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"All the “⤒ Import…” buttons open the same 3-step window: 1. drag the file (or paste it, or choose it); 2. you see what will come in (“12 people”, “34 rows”, the name of the setting…); 3. “⤒ Import”. When possible, the message that follows offers “↶ Undo”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the tab menu go at the bottom, or the column on the right?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: Settings → Appearance → “Tab menu” (top or bottom) and “Side column” (left or right), then Save."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What do the colours of the badges (In progress, Done, Sosa…) mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everywhere in the app, the same colour means the same thing: blue = in progress, green = done or certain, yellow = to check, red = problem, grey = closed or archived, black = death. The Sosa number is always solid blue, on the record as in the picker. The record’s colours can still be set in Settings → Appearance → “Badge colours”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the date show “today at 10:22” or “Tuesday”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The dates when something was created or edited are all written the same way: “today at…”, “yesterday at…”, the day of the week if it was less than 7 days ago, then “17 Oct 2026”. Hover over it to see the full date. The dates of your genealogy (births, records…) do not change."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Something is missing in a window: where is the error?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Just under the field to correct: it has a red frame, with the sentence that says what to do (“Give the widget a title.”), and the cursor is already there. If there are several things to correct, the window says so at the top (“2 things to fix before saving”). The window stays open: nothing you wrote is lost, and the red goes away as soon as you correct it. The message at the bottom left is now only used when saving itself fails."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why do all the windows have the same size and the same bottom?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There are only 4 sizes: small for a question or a choice, medium for a form, large when there are two columns or a preview, very large for galleries of images and templates; editors (drawing, page layout, editing…) take the whole screen. The bottom of the window always stays visible when you scroll: “Delete” on the left, “Cancel” then the main button on the right; a window that only shows something has “Close”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why do the buttons say “Create the book”, “Link” or “↺ Reset to default”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everywhere, the same action has the same word and the same symbol. “Create the …” makes something new, “Save” keeps something that exists, “Add” puts one thing into another, “🔗 Link” attaches two records, “↺ Reset to default” returns to the starting settings, and a window that only shows something has “Close” alone. The symbols: ⤒ import, ⤓ export or download, 💾 save, 🖨 print, ✎ edit, 🗑 delete, ↗ open, ⚙️ settings, 🔍 search, ＋ add or new, 📋 copy, ⧉ duplicate, ⭐ favourite, 📁 file, × close."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"A block or a list is empty: how do I start?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"When there is nothing, the app always says so the same way: a large box for an empty page or list, a small line in a block (“📚 No sources”). Next to it, the button to start (“＋ Add…”) does the same thing as the usual button."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a child, a parent or a spouse?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the record, then in the Parents, Spouses or Children blocks click “＋”. You can choose a person who already exists or create a new one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I show a block (Departures, WWI soldier, Resistance member…) on a record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Record → Identity (pencil) → “Blocks to show on the record”: tick the block. To have it on all records: Settings → Appearance → “Blocks shown on request”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own dashboard widget?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Widget editor (or “＋ Create a widget” at the bottom of the dashboard column) → “＋ New widget”. Start from scratch (figures, list, chart, statistic, app screen, text, shortcuts), from any element of the app (a list, a diagram, a statistic, a record, the map, a tool), from an app widget (an editable copy, or the same widget dressed with your title, icon and colour), from one of your widgets or from an example. The preview updates while you adjust it. It then appears in the left column of the dashboard; “Share” turns it into a file for someone close to you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange the dashboard into pages and groups?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Dashboard → “Reorder the widgets”. “＋ New page” adds a page (List, Cards, Search…): its buttons appear at the top, under the title; tick the one that opens by default, and “Arrange its widgets” to organise it (widgets added from the left column go onto the page shown). “＋ Group” creates a frame with a title that gathers the widgets that follow it; ▶ makes it a sub-group, with as many levels as you like. The ▾ button to the left of the frame folds or opens the group."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I move a row with the mouse?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Wherever there are ↑ ↓ arrows, the ⠿ (or ↕) handle in front of the row can be dragged to the place you want."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own block?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Block editor → “＋ New block”. Start from a template, an app block (Departures, WWI soldier, Resistance member, Grave…) or one of your blocks, add or remove fields by clicking the buttons, then save. It then appears in Identity → “Blocks to show on the record”. The “📊 Chart” field places a chart fully set up in the Chart editor (“New chart…”, then back to the block); with the criterion “Relative to the person on the record”, it counts their ancestors, descendants, family… The “🧱 Widget” field places one of your widgets (figures, list, chart…) or an app screen (the person’s record, a list, a diagram…)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a label?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In almost every form, the “🏷️ Labels” line: click “＋”, type the beginning of the name or create a new label. The list of all labels is in Lists → Labels."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I spot a generation mistake (a father confused with his son of the same name)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → ✅ Database check (🎂 Ages badge): besides ages at marriage and parents who are too young or too old, a set of siblings spread over two generations, a Sosa ancestor too young or too old for their place in the line, a death before birth or a marriage after death."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I protect a record against changes?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The padlock at the top of the record locks it: nothing can be changed there any more, not even by working together."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who is the root person (de cujus), and why do they have Sosa number 1?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The root person (de cujus) is the starting person of the tree: they are Sosa number 1, their father 2, their mother 3, and so on. Go menu → “Root person” (⌘⇧H or Ctrl+Shift+H) opens their record. To change: Settings → Appearance → Sosa numbering, look for the person then “Recalculate”; the choice is saved in the database, so it follows the backup and another computer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a widget of shortcuts to anything?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Widget editor → “＋ New widget” → 🔗 Shortcuts, then “＋ Add shortcuts…”. Three tabs: 🔎 In your database (a person, a place, a Note, an investigation, a search sweep, a Workshop project, a table, a book, a media item, a diagram, a chart, a house, a map, a transcription, a work, a magazine, an employer, a source, a label… with the filters “All (n)”, “👤 People (n)”), 🧭 Screens and tools (screens, your own tabs, lists, tools such as photo retouching or video editing, actions such as “＋ New person” or the backup, tool projects) and 🌐 Website. You can put as many as you like in the same widget, rename them, give them an icon, a colour, show the person’s photo or the media image, add subheadings, arrange them (⠿ or ↑ ↓) and choose tiles, list or small buttons, the size and the number of columns. The widget goes on the dashboard, in your tabs and in a Note; make several if you like."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change my shortcuts directly on the dashboard?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click the ✎ pencil of the shortcuts widget (on the dashboard, in one of your tabs or in a Note), then “✎ Edit the shortcuts”: “＋ Add shortcuts…” to put in what you want, ⠿ or ↑ ↓ to put them in the order you want, rename them, change their icon, remove them (↶ Undo), choose the layout, then “Save”: the widget redraws straight away. No need to go through Settings."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I quickly add a record or a list to my shortcuts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The ⭐ button (Add to shortcuts), at the far top right of a person’s record, a list, a Note, an investigation or Charts: choose which shortcuts widget to put it in (or create a new one, placed on the dashboard). For a list, the current search, filters and sorting are kept: the shortcut reopens the list just as it was. If the target has been deleted, its tile turns grey “not found” with a “Remove” button (↶ Undo possible)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a shortcut that creates a new house, a new video edit, a new book…?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click the ✎ pencil of the shortcuts widget (or Settings → Widget editor) → “＋ Add shortcuts” → “＋ Create…” tab (the same section is also at the top of “🧭 Screens and tools”): one shortcut per editing tool (🏠 New house, 🎬 New video edit, 📐 New book, 🎚️ New photo retouch, ✒️ New drawing, 📰 New announcement, 🎞️ New presentation or slideshow, 📜 New transcription, 🎧 New sound project…). A click on the shortcut opens the tool directly on a new item (an empty document, or the tool’s assistant)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the order of the search types, or hide some?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Research → “Reorder” (at the bottom of the left column): drag a row or use ↑ ↓, and click the eye to hide or show a type. It is saved straight away (“↶ Undo” in the message at the bottom left); “↺ Reset to default” puts back the starting order. A hidden type that another tool opens (a record index, the log…) stays in the menu, marked “(hidden)”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is the order of the map backgrounds the same everywhere?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The order chosen in Settings → Maps and map backgrounds → “Map backgrounds” (categories and backgrounds) is followed by all the background menus: the Map, photo maps, the slideshow, drawn maps, a village’s page (“Map backgrounds as buttons”) and the address plan. An unticked background no longer appears there (unless it is the current one)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own tab in the top bar?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Tab editor → “＋ New tab”. Start from an empty composed page, an app screen, a copy of the dashboard, one of your tabs or a template (My research desk, My village, Tree and map…). In the page, place whatever you want: any app screen (a list, a diagram, a statistic, a person’s record, the map, one of your lists, a Settings tool), with its height and its menus if you like, widgets, your charts, free texts. “↗ Full size” opens the screen. A tab can also be a simple shortcut to a screen. Choose its name and icon; arrange or hide it in Settings → Appearance → “Order of the main menu”, with the other tabs; “Share” turns it into a file."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a tab to the menu?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The small grey “＋” at the end of the menu (or Settings → Menus, column and lists → “Menu” tab → “＋ Add a tab…”): tick a card and the tab arrives at the end of the menu, before Settings (“↶ Undo” in the message). The cards “recommended for you” follow how you use the app. Unticking hides the tab; a shortcut is “Removed” in “Order and names of the main menu”. A tab added by hand is never hidden by a change of use. The box “Show the ＋ at the end of the menu” removes it; no ＋ in viewing mode or simple mode."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the order of the menu tabs, or add any list or tool to it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The “＋” at the end of the menu → “⠿ Menu order”: drag ⠿ (or reach ⠿ with Tab then press Alt + ↑ / ↓), the menu changes right away (“↶ Undo” in the message); it is the same order as in Settings → Menus. In “2 · A shortcut to a list” and “3 · A shortcut to a tool”, “＋ Another list…” and “＋ Another tool…” open the choice menu: search, tick one or more lines, “Add to the menu”. Your tabs go to the end of the menu (“Put my tabs at the end of the menu” box, ticked at first); an order you have already set does not move, and “⤓ Group my tabs at the end” brings them together whenever you want."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get the 📄 Documents tab?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“＋” at the end of the menu → tick “📄 Documents”: it arrives at the end of the menu. Your photos, records and letters are filed there from A to Z; nothing has to be typed again."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the “🌾 Their terroir” block show?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The PDO and PGI products of their municipality (when their trade relates to them: winegrower, cheesemaker…), the animal breeds of their region, the headdress and costume of their region (also useful for dating a family photo), the schools of the past in the municipality, and the meaning of their first name and surname (Wiktionary). The data comes with the app: only a first name or surname goes on the Internet, never for a confidential record or a living person. Each part is turned on or off in Settings → “🌾 Their terroir”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the “🪨 Land and words of their country” block show?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The soil of their village (the rock, the altitude), the village’s name in the regional language and the name of its inhabitants, vanished villages, old fruit varieties of their region, sundials, a few words of the French of their area, old measures, legends and great fears of their lifetime, the great criminal cases of the region (never linked to your family), festivals, their name given to an asteroid or a rose, and their famous namesakes (online, never merged). Each part is set in Settings → “🪨 Land and words of the village”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the “🎡 The world of their village” block show?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Shows premiered in the year of their marriage (otherwise at age 20), the sports clubs of their town, the Tour de France at their home, the World’s Fair of their time, the coincidences of their life (born, married or died on the day of a great event), the lord of their village before 1789, vanished churches and the fairs of their region. You also find it on a village’s record and in the monograph; the lists are in Lists. Choose the parts in Settings → “🎡 The village world”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the “🌟 Around their life” block (Wikidata) show?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Famous people born in the same place (within 50 years) or on the same day, their famous namesakes (same name, not necessarily family), the great events nearby during their life and the hit song in the year of their birth, each line with “↗ Wikidata”. The Wikidata data (CC0) comes with the app: everything works without the Internet, only the ↗ links open the web. When you type a place, a small line also recognises a former name (“Lemberg” → Lviv). Settings → “🌟 Wikidata”: the switch, what is shown, the distance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the “🔎 What concerns them” block for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It gathers what the app’s data can say about a person: the best suggestions first (with “↗ source”), the rest under “▸ see the N”. “✓ Keep” puts a suggestion on their record in one click (as a note with its source, as a source alone or as a “context” event); “＋ Also add to…” copies it to their spouse, children, parents…; “✗ Not useful” hides it. “⤢ See all” opens the full report (The person, Their places, Their time, To look for) and offers to install what is missing. On a village’s record: “What concerns this place”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide the relationship shown at the top of the record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The record’s ⋯ menu → “🧭 Hide the relationship” (then “Show the relationship” to bring it back). The choice applies to all records and is remembered; “↶ Undo” is offered right away."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the “🔔 Objects and traces” block show?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The bells of their municipality and their inscriptions (godfather, godmother, priest… when a name from your database can be read there), the village’s objects in museums, plants picked in their municipality in their lifetime (herbaria), plaques and steles, tales and songs collected near their home, the pilgrimages of their region, the hospices near the place of their death and, for a woman, the convents nearby at age 20. Only the INSEE code, the position or the name of the municipality goes on the Internet, nothing for a living or confidential record. Settings → “🔔 Objects and traces”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does “⚖️ The rules of their life” show, and where are the aids for reading records?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record: the custom of their land before 1789 (and what it said about dividing the inheritance), the salt tax in their lifetime, their marriage and the closed times (Advent, Lent), the sayings of their birthday, the wind and the traditional “pays” of their municipality. In 📖 Reading help: Latin abbreviations (Cappelli), handwriting models of the period, the meaning of old words (online) and “📅 Feast → date” (“le jour de la Saint-Martin 1702” → the exact date). Settings → “⚖️ Rules and records”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How can I see my ancestor’s village as they saw it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The “🖼️ Their village as they saw it” block: museum paintings depicting their municipality (those painted in their lifetime), old photos (1850–1950) with the one closest to age 20, the village seen from the air around 1950 next to today, the woods of 1850 (did they live on the edge of a vanished wood?) and, on request, what could be seen from their home, the hidden traces of the laser relief and the houses of their time still standing. Each place is requested online once, then kept; nothing for a confidential record or a living person. Settings → “🖼️ See their village”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does “⛰️ Their land and its people” show?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The dangers of their land (avalanches, landslides, quarries for a quarryman, volcanoes that ruined the harvests, wrecks offshore for a sailor) and the people and places around them: the map of their name (INSEE deaths already imported), the post relays on their route, the walking time to their spouse (relief included), the parcel of a locality on today’s cadastre, their village in Algeria, Quebec heritage, the Historical Dictionary of Switzerland and the 1950 US census. Settings → “⛰️ Their land and its people”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make the record of a castle or a church?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a village’s record or in the monograph: “🏰⛪ The buildings of the municipality” → “＋ Make a record” (also from the small card of a Wikidata castle, or from a record: “🔗 Link to a record card?” when the address mentions a castle or a church). The record gathers its history, its lords or priests, your family’s records that took place there, its registers, its protected objects and its sources; what comes from Wikidata or Mérimée is only copied when you click. All your records: Settings → “🏰⛪ Building records”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I send a map or a retouched image to Video editing or the Animation studio?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the vector drawing tool or in photo retouching: File → “📤 Send to Video editing or the Animation studio…”. Choose a new edit, an existing edit, a new Studio film or an existing film: the image is stored in the media and placed on the timeline (or as a new scene of the film)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I animate a map or a chart through time (Animation studio)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Editing tools → “🎞 Animation studio”, or “🎞 Animate” under a dated diagram. A film is made of scenes (title, diagram, family migrations, name race, living pyramid, self-drawing curve, growing tree, changing borders) laid out on a timeline: the camera, captions, music and your voice are set scene by scene. “⤓ Export” makes a video (WebM or MP4), a GIF, images or subtitles; “🎬 Continue in Video editing” sends the film to editing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I show France’s borders changing over time?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Animation studio → “🏛 Changing borders” template (or “＋ Scene” → “The changing borders”): France from 1760 to today, year by year (Lorraine, Corsica, Revolution and Empire, 1815, Savoy and Nice in 1860, Alsace-Moselle from 1871 to 1918), with the dates that matter. Settings: framing, colours, years. These are approximate borders, drawn from today’s boundaries."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the app suggest the right diagram for me (“I want to show…”)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: in “New diagram”, write what you want to show (“the most common first names”, “where people came from”…) then “Suggest”. The app suggests three diagrams made from your database, the best suited first; “Make this diagram” opens it in the editor."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can my statistics palette have more than 40 colours?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: Charts → 🎨 Stats and tree colours. The palette has 100 colours, all different; “＋ 10 colours” adds some (up to 400), “− 10 colours” removes some (“↶ Undo” puts them back). The cycle starts again after the last colour."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which box designs are there for trees?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → 🎨 Stats and tree colours → “🌳 Tree boxes”: 14 boxes to choose from (white, man / woman colours, with photo, mini, parchment, medallion, shield, postcard, postage stamp, polaroid, museum label, pastel by generation, outline only, golden night), then the width, lines, dates and Sosa number."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see who is hidden behind a bar in a statistic?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click the bar, the column, the slice or the legend line: a window shows the people counted, with a search, sorting by name or by birth and a CSV export. “others” also shows the grouped values."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does “others” have stripes?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“others” gathers all the small values: it no longer sets the chart’s scale (otherwise the real values would have almost no colour left). Its stripes say that it goes beyond the scale."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I save a chart (or a statistic) as an image?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → My charts → the chart: “PNG image” (sharp, for a forum) or “JPG image” (lighter, for an e-mail), “CSV” for a spreadsheet, “Print / PDF”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I keep my DNA results?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The “🧬 DNA” tab, hidden at first: Settings → Appearance → menu order, tick “DNA”. Be careful: in France, taking a genealogical DNA test is forbidden outside a medical or research setting (Civil Code art. 16-10, Penal Code art. 226-28-1: a €3,750 fine); the warning and the links to the official texts are in “⚖️ Law and warnings”. The tab stores tests, haplogroups, matches (CSV import), groups (Leeds method), segments (chromosome browser), offers a cM calculator and shows the Y, mt and X lines of the tree. Everything can be set in “⚙️ Settings”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I draw a coat of arms, a logo, or redo an image as vectors?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → “✒️ Vector drawing” (or “🛡️ New coat of arms”), the ✒️ button of a record’s coat of arms, or Settings → Boards → Shapes (✒️ on a shape). Illustrator-style tools: pen, pencil, shapes, text, direct selection of points, layers, groups, masks, Pathfinder, gradients. “Coat of arms” tab: shield, partition, charge, tinctures, charges and automatic blazon. Coat of arms → “Vectorise an image” turns a scanned coat of arms into paths. SVG, PNG, JPG, WEBP, GIF, BMP import; SVG, PNG, JPG, WEBP, PDF export; save to “My shapes”, to a record’s media or as a coat-of-arms image."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I draw the plan of a family house, in 2D and 3D?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “🏠 Family houses” (or Editing tools → House plan). ⬚ Drag a room: its walls are made automatically and its area is shown; ▬ draws walls by clicking (snapped to the grid, the corners, right angles); 🚪 and 🪟 one click on a wall. Furniture: more than 450 objects (box bed, kneading trough, spinning wheel, stove, longcase clock…), or your own: ⤒ Import an image seen from above, or a 3D file (OBJ with MTL, STL, glTF, GLB). Materials for walls (stone, brick, half-timbering, panelling, wallpaper…) and floors (terracotta tiles, parquet, flagstones…), floors, roof, a scanned plan to trace (scaled in two clicks), and the “3D view” where you turn around and visit. The house is linked to a place, its inhabitants and its censuses, can have several periods (1850, 1900…); each room keeps its memories."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I place a chart in a Note, an investigation, the Workshop or a person’s record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Wherever you place a diagram (📊 block of a Note, “＋ Diagram” of an investigation, of a Workshop project, Diagrams block of a person, dashboard), the window offers: ＋ New diagram, 📊 From a chart (one of yours, an example or an app statistic, drawn with the type of your choice and kept up to date), 📈 One of my charts (a chart from the Chart editor, placed as it is and kept up to date; you choose it in the usual search menu, with its thumbnail, its drawing, where its figures come from and the number of places where it is already placed) and ⤒ Import (a diagram or chart shared as a file, a CSV, a pasted table, a Wikipedia page, an image of a chart). “＋ Create a new chart” leads to the Chart editor."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I create my own chart or diagram?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everything is done in Settings → Chart editor (formerly called “Statistics editor”): “＋ New chart” (with your own figures or the app’s) and “＋ New diagram” (hemicycle, timeline of a group, organisation chart…). All your charts and diagrams are gathered in Lists → “Charts and diagrams”, as a list or as cards with their thumbnail, where you can search, filter, sort and create others. Charts → “My diagrams” and “My charts” also show them in large. Your charts are stored in your genealogy: they follow the backup and another computer, and can be found in the quick search (⌘K)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I sort My diagrams and My charts into folders?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In Charts, left column: “＋ 📁 Folder” to the right of “My diagrams” or “My charts”. A click on a folder opens or closes it (▸ / ▾), and the app remembers; the number on the right says how many items are inside, sub-folders included. On hover: ✎ to rename it, 🗑 to delete it (nothing is lost: what it contains moves up one level). To file a diagram, drag it onto a folder (it lights up in blue) or out of the folders to take it out; drag a folder onto another to make it a sub-folder, or between two folders to change the order (three levels at most). You would rather not drag, or it is one of My charts? Open it and click “📁 File”. After each filing, “↶ Undo” undoes it. The numbers D1, D2… and M1, M2… do not change."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where are my charts stored, and how do I find one again?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your charts (made in Settings → Chart editor, formerly “Statistics editor”) are stored in your genealogy, like your records: they follow the full backup, restoration and another computer. Those that were kept in your browser moved there by themselves, once only, without losing anything. To find one: type a few letters of its name in the search menu (“📈 One of my charts”), in the quick search (⌘K or Ctrl+K) or in Lists → “Charts and diagrams”. Each row shows its thumbnail, its drawing (bars, pie…), where its figures come from and in how many places it is placed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a chart with my own figures (not those of the tree)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Chart editor → “＋ New chart” → “✍️ My own figures” (or, in “2. Chart”, Data → “✍️ My own figures”). A table appears: one row per value (the name, then the figures), “＋ Row”, “＋ Series” for several columns of figures (e.g. 1900 and 1950). You can paste cells from a spreadsheet into a box, “📋 Paste a table” (the first row of headings gives the names of the series), “⤒ Import a CSV”, or “📊 Start from a chart” to copy figures (from one of your charts or an app statistic) and adjust them. With several series, choose a drawing from “My diagrams” (columns, line, stacked, radar…) to see them all."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I place one of my charts everywhere (record, investigation, page layout…) and keep it up to date?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A chart saved in the Chart editor has a name and can be placed everywhere: as a widget on its own or with other parts on the dashboard, and wherever there is “＋ Diagram” (block of a Note, investigation, Workshop project, page layout, table) → “📈 One of my charts” (the usual search menu: type a few letters of the name). For a person’s page or an investigation, you can also do it from the chart editor: “📌 Also place it on…”. When you change the chart (figures, drawing, colours, name), it changes everywhere it is placed; “✎ Edit” on the placed chart reopens the Chart editor."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can my charts use the timelines, slices and links of the diagrams?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. In the Chart editor, “Drawing” offers the simple drawings, then all the types of My diagrams: slices (pie, ring, mosaic, gauges, pictograms…), figures (columns, line, radar, pyramid, heat map…), assemblies (hemicycle…), links (word cloud) and, with a list of people, the lives timeline, the chronological timeline and the migration flow (place of birth → place of death). The types are the same on both sides: ⚙️ in the diagram gallery (Charts → My diagrams → ＋ New) (or ⚙️ Arrange the types in the gallery) for their order, to hide those you do not use and to make your own groups."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which diagrams for my genealogy (fan, sunburst, network, arcs, Venn)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, the “Genealogy” group offers: 🪭 the ancestry fan (half circle, three quarters or full circle, from 3 to 10 generations) where each box is coloured according to a figure or a category — research rate, region, department, occupation, century, name…; 🌞 the lineage sunburst (rings from the centre outwards, each slice sized by its figure, for example the number of descendants); 🕸 the network of relationships (witnesses, godfathers, godmothers, neighbours…: the points spread out by themselves, you can drag them, zoom with ＋ − or Ctrl + wheel, ↺ to reset to default, double-click to free a point); 🌈 the link arcs (people on a line, joined by arcs: pedigree collapse, marriages between families); ⭕ the Venn diagram of 2 to 4 sets. Each one is filled in by hand, by pasting a table, with a CSV, or through the “From the app” tab (your Sosa ancestors, a line of descendants, the witnesses of records, your labels, profiles and name groups), then adjusted, exported and placed everywhere like the others."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the seasons and the long timespan of my family (calendar, month rose, spiral)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, “Timelines” group: 🗓 the coloured calendar (each day is a box that is darker or lighter depending on the number of events; all years together to see the season, or one row per year), 🌸 the month rose (12 slices, one per month, with several series: births, marriages, deaths…) and 🌀 the time spiral (one turn per century or per decade; a date = a point, a start and an end = a line, like a life). Dates are written as in a record: “7 May 1925”, “08/12/1960”, “12 Thermidor Year II”. The “From the app” tab takes the births, baptisms, marriages, deaths and burials of your whole database or of a person’s ancestors."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which diagrams can tell my family’s story (who knew whom, survival, occupations, marriages, pedigree collapse, births, places lived)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”: 🤝 Who knew whom (Timelines) — the lives of several generations one under another; the app tells who knew whom, for example “your great-grandmother knew her great-grandson for 6 years”, and colours in yellow the years when the most generations were alive together; 📉 Survival curve (Figures) — the share still alive at each age, one curve per century, per sex or per branch, with the age at which half are still alive (living people count up to their age today); 🔀 Occupations from father to son (Genealogy) — bands that follow occupations over 3 to 5 generations, in green if the occupation is kept; 💍 Marriage radius (Genealogy) — one point per couple, at the distance between the two spouses’ places of birth, with one circle per century and a small table; 🧮 Pedigree collapse (Genealogy) — per generation, the possible ancestors (2, 4, 8…), the places found and the different people; 📅 Months of marriages and conceptions (Timelines) — Lent and Advent marked, conceptions = birth minus 9 months; 👶 Birth spacing (Timelines) — one row per mother, her children at the age she was, the gap in months and the children who died young; 🏘️ Timeline of places lived (Timelines) — one band per person, coloured by commune, department or country. The “From the app” tab fills them with your database (the starting person is chosen in the usual search menu); a click on a bar, a point or a month opens the record or the list of the people concerned. The occupation comes from the record or the first “Occupation” event; the marriage radius needs the coordinates of the places."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I colour a map according to my figures (departments, regions, countries)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, “Maps” group: 🗺️ the map coloured by area. In Settings, choose the map (France, Belgium, Switzerland, Luxembourg, Europe, World) and its level of detail (departments, regions or communes; provinces; cantons; countries or NUTS 1 and 2 regions; countries or continents). In Data, one row per area: its name or its code (35, 2A, FRH03, VD, BE…) and its figure; you can paste a table or import a CSV, and rows that are not found are flagged under the map. “From the app” counts your births, marriages or deaths (or only those of your ancestors, over a period): each place in your database is attached to its area according to its coordinates. Gradient or class colours, labels, legend; the value shows on hover. The overseas territories are in insets."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose the part of the world a map shows?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the preview of a map: the wheel zooms, dragging moves, Shift + drag (or “▭ Draw a frame”) frames an area, “↺ Reset to default” goes back to the starting frame. This framing is saved with the diagram. In Settings, “Part of the map” also offers ready-made frames (Western Europe, Mediterranean, North America, North Africa, Indian Ocean, Caribbean, Quebec and Acadia… or, for France, Brittany-Normandy, South-West, Corsica…) and “Projection” the drawing of the globe (Equal Earth or Robinson for the world, Lambert for Europe)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I draw my family’s migrations on a map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, “Maps” group: 🧭 the migration map. Each row is a flow “departure → arrival → how many”; the arrow is thicker when there are more people. “From the app” takes from your database the place of birth → place of death, birth → marriage, or from one generation to the next (parent’s birth → child’s birth), possibly for your ancestors only. “Add a flow” lets you choose the two places with the place search menu; a place without a position is chosen in “The places in the table”, found automatically (🔎) or typed with its coordinates (“Brest 48.39, -4.49”). Same backgrounds and same framing as the coloured map."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I colour the communes (towns) on a map, or see Paris in large?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the map coloured by area, Settings → “Level of detail” → “Communes (towns)”, for France (overseas included), Belgium, Switzerland and Luxembourg. One row per commune: its INSEE (35238) or official code, or its name; if there are several communes with the same name (Saint-Martin…), add the department: “Saint-Martin (Gers)”, “Saint-Martin 32”; otherwise the row is flagged “namesakes” under the map. The arrondissements of Paris, Lyon and Marseille and the former merged communes are recognised. “From the app” attaches each place by its INSEE code, then its name and department. To see the communes of a single department, choose “Part of the map” → “Zoom on a department…” and type its name or number. On maps of France, “Zoomed inset on Île-de-France” shows Paris and the inner suburbs (or the whole region) in large in a free corner (under the map on a phone), with the same colours, labels and tooltips."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see on a map where my ancestors were born (or married, died), one point per event?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, “Maps” group: 📍 the point map (one point per event, coloured by kind or by century; the points of the same place spread out in a spiral) or ⬢ the hexagon map (the map cut into equal cells, darker where there are more events; cell size in Settings). “From the app” tab: births, deaths, marriages, residences or one kind of event, only your ancestors, between two years; places without coordinates are found among the communes of France, and “🔍 Find places without a position” looks for the others. A click on a point or a cell opens the record, or the list of people."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a distorted map, where departments grow according to the number of ancestors?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, “Maps” group: 🫧 the distorted map (cartogram). Each department, region or country becomes a circle (or a square) whose size follows its figure, placed near its real position, with the real map faintly underneath. “From the app” → “Different people” + “Only my ancestors” gives the number of ancestors per area. Settings: shape, size, colours, labels."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I tell an ancestor’s life on a map, step by step?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, “Maps” group: 📖 the storytelling map. “From the app”: choose the person, their events become the steps. Under the drawing: ◀ ▶, ▶︎ play and the slider move the life forward; the map follows the step (or shows the whole life, Settings → “The map”), the path already travelled is in red, the timeline at the bottom shows the years; a click on a step of the timeline goes there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What other drawings are there for descendants, godparents and causes of death?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, “Genealogy” group: 🧱 descendants as rectangles (each child under their parent, width = number of descendants; click = the record); 🎀 names passed on (godfather or godmother → godchild who bears their first name, one row per name, colour according to the relationship: grandfather, uncle, sister…; “From the app” reads the “godfather” and “godmother” witnesses of births and baptisms); 🎗 causes of death as rings (the large groups in the centre, guessed from the words of the record, the details around; click = the list of people). The cause is noted in the “Cause” field of a death."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which figure diagrams for my family (ranking, streams, histogram, box, dumbbells, figure cards…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, “Figures” group: 🏁 the changing ranking (the most given first names, decade by decade: the app calculates the ranks from the numbers); 🌊 stacked streams (the family’s occupations over 200 years); 📶 the histogram (type or paste raw values, for example ages at death: the app sorts them into bins whose width you choose); 📦 the box plot (median, quartiles and outliers calculated for each group, for example age at marriage by century); 🏋️ dumbbells (two values per row: the husband’s and the wife’s age); ↗️ the before / after slope; 🪜 the waterfall (a total that goes up and down, with subtotals); 🍭 🍡 horizontal or vertical lollipops; 🐝 the beeswarm (one person = one point, their name on hover); 🔲 small line charts in a grid (same scale or not). “Key figures” group: 🔢 figure cards (a big number, the arrow and the change from the previous value, a mini line, a colour according to your thresholds) and 🎯 the target bar (value, target and zones, for example “64% of ancestors found in the 8th generation”). The “From the app” tab → “🌳 Data from my tree” fills them with your database: ages at death and at marriage, first names, occupations, births by name or by commune, database counters, ancestors found per generation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see my DNA as a diagram (painted chromosomes, cM ruler)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, “DNA” group: 🧬 chromosome painting (the 22 pairs and the X, one bar for the copy received from the father and one for the mother’s; each segment shared with a cousin is painted, coloured by branch, by cousin or by side; at the top, the share of the genome already painted) and 📏 the cM ruler (each match is a point on a cM scale, under which the ranges of relationships from the Shared cM Project are lined up; write a name in “Show the possible relationships of…” to see the most likely ones in %). “From the app” tab: choose the tested person then their test, the app takes the segments or matches from the DNA tab. A click on a segment or a point opens the cousin’s record if they are in your tree. Without a test in the app, fill in or paste the table (chromosome, start, end, cM, cousin, side, branch)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What other figure diagrams are there (ridgelines, mosaic, dot plot, Nightingale rose, occupational mobility)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”: 🏔️ ridgelines (Figures group: one curve per century or per sex, overlapping like mountains, for example age at death; click on a ridge: the list of people); ⚫ the dot plot (one point per series on the same line, for example the average age at death of women and men by commune); 🔁 the mobility table (the father’s occupation in rows, the son’s or daughter’s in columns, each cell darker when it is frequent, framed when it is the same occupation; occupations can be grouped into families; click on a cell: the people); Slices group: 🧱 the variable-width mosaic (each column is wider the bigger its total, for example occupations century by century) and 🌹 the Nightingale rose (one slice per month, its area follows the figure, series stacked or overlaid: births, marriages, deaths or conceptions). “From the app” → “🌳 Data from my tree” fills them with your database."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Diagrams: what do the options in “From the app → Chart” mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a diagram’s editor, “From the app” tab: choose “🧮 Count in my database” in “Chart” (a ready-made starting point, or “Custom”), then set, always in the same order: what is counted (people, births, marriages, deaths, events, families, places, media; “each person only once” for events), the group of people (your whole database, your ancestors, your descendants, the paternal or maternal branch, a label, one of your lists, a search in the names or the people you choose), the period (from … to …, written as in a record), the grouping (surname, first name, sex, occupation, generation, commune, department, region, country, century, decade, year, month, age, number of children…), the series (by sex or by century, for stacked columns, the pyramid, the heat map), the sorting, the top N and “Others”, “Unknown” shown or hidden, the unit (number, %, average or median of age at death, age at marriage or number of children) and “🔄 Keep up to date” (the diagram is recalculated when your database changes). The sentence above the preview sums up the setting (“People born between 1800 and 1900, my ancestors, by department, top 10”). Each option has its “?” tooltip. The other types (maps, timelines, calendar, survival, occupations, marriages, tree figures…) keep their panel and gain the same group of people and the same period. An example, an app statistic or one of your charts can also be sorted, cut to the top N and switched to %. Your diagrams already made do not change."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which diagrams about life and death, couples, places and my research (child mortality, widowhood, signatures, proof…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”. “Life and death” group: 👶 Child mortality (by decade, the share of children who died before 1 year and before 5 years), 👨‍👩‍👧 Surviving siblings (for each couple: children born, reaching adulthood, dying young), 🗓 Seasons of births and deaths (month × period). “Couples and families” group: 💔 Widowhood and remarriage (the time before remarrying, men and women), ⏳ Length of marriages, 👴 Age of the parents at each birth (scatter plot), 🤰 Conceptions before marriage (first child born before the marriage or less than 8 months after), 💑 Age gap between spouses. “Places” group: 🧭 Far from the cradle (how far away the ancestors were born, generation by generation). “My research” group: ✍️ Able to sign, ✅ What you have proved (ancestors’ births, marriages and deaths with a source, without a source, not found), 📚 Registers consulted (the years seen and the gaps, according to the record index tracking and your sourced records). The “From the app” tab fills them with your database (without living people or confidential records), with the group of people and the period; a click on a bar or a point opens the list of people. The dark years, the incoming names, marriages between villages and the witnesses and godparents already existed: 📉 Births, deaths and crises, 🌳 Surname tree, 🎼 Chord diagram (marriages between villages) and 🕸️ Network of relationships (witnesses, godparents…)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which diagrams for a relationship, completeness, the ahnentafel, DNA (Leeds), the wheel of the year and two-axis figures?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”. “Genealogy” group: 🔗 the relationship path (choose two people: the shortest path step by step and the sentence “… is the second cousin of …”; in-laws and pedigree collapse are taken into account), 🧩 tree completeness (share of ancestors known in each generation, as bars or as a fan), 📜 the ancestry table (one row per Sosa number; “🖨 Print the table…” in “From the app”). “Maps” group: 🎯 the family’s centre of gravity (the average point of the places of birth of each generation and its path) and 🌈 the generations map. “DNA” group: 🧬 the Leeds matrix (your matches from 90 to 400 cM sorted by grandparent colour, from the DNA tab). “Timelines” group: 🎡 the wheel of the year (birthdays, marriages, deaths, name days). “Figures” group: 📊 bars + line (second axis on the right), ↔️ diverging bars, 🏔 stacked hills, 🧵 parallel coordinates, 📶 step line (how many in the family were alive, year by year). “🔒 For sharing” removes the names of living people; a confidential record is never named. A click on a box opens the record (or the list of people)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I annotate a diagram (arrow, text, area, “WWI”)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the diagram editor, “⚙️ Customise” tab → “✏️ Annotations”: ＋ Arrow, ＋ Text, ＋ Area, ＋ Vertical or horizontal band, ＋ Date marker (a date, or “from… to…” for a period). Drag them on the preview (and their small circles to enlarge them); double-click to change the text; Delete or × to remove them (“↶ Undo” right after). They are kept with the diagram, wherever it is placed, on export (PNG, SVG, PDF) and when printing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I give the same style to all my diagrams?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⚙️ Customise” tab → “🎨 Style”: a ready-made style (Plain, Archive, Bright colours, Printable black and white, Colour-blind) or your own (palette, font, background, line thickness, corners, text size, grid). “💾 My saved styles” keeps it under a name (and exports or imports it). “Apply to all diagrams” puts it on all of them (“↶ Undo” puts each one back as before); “⭐ Default style” gives it to new diagrams. “As before” removes the style."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare side by side (small multiples), by sex, century, branch, place or generation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⚙️ Customise” tab → “▦ Small multiples” → “Repeat the diagram by”: a small drawing per group, side by side, with the same scale. “The series in the table” works as soon as there are several columns of figures; for sex, century, branch, department, country or generation, first get your figures with “🧮 Count in my database” (“From the app” tab), ticking “Keep up to date”. For columns, bars, lines, areas, stacked and radar."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a diagram scroll through time (▶ the years)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As soon as the data has dates (years as categories, timelines, members of a group, schedule…), a ▶ slider appears under the preview: play, speed (slow, normal, fast), loop, “⤢ All” to go back to the full drawing, and “🎬 Video” (WebM or MP4, depending on the browser). The scale does not move during playback. With “Reduce animations” (Settings → Accessibility), playback moves one step per second, without sliding. To show it also where the diagram is placed: “⚙️ Customise” → “▶ Playback over time”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add an average, a median or a target to a chart?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⚙️ Customise” tab → “📏 Reference lines and axes” (columns, bars, stacked, lines, areas): ＋ Average, ＋ Median (calculated automatically, over all the series or just one), ＋ Target, ＋ Value. In the same place: “Logarithmic scale” for very large differences, and “Second axis (on the right)” to draw a series with its own scale."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does every diagram have a “Chart” menu in “From the app”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. In a diagram’s editor, “From the app” tab, each type starts with “Chart”: ready-made starting points. For columns, bars, pies…: “🧮 Count in my database” and the app’s statistics. For the types that have their own panel (maps, timelines, fan, calendar, survival, occupations, marriages, DNA, child mortality, widowhood…): for example “Birth regions of my ancestors (6 generations)”, “Departures (born here, died elsewhere)”, “Who was alive in 1789”, “My ancestors, by century”; the choice sets the fields of the panel (and the group of people or the period), then click the panel’s “Use…” button. “✏️ Custom” leaves everything to you. The scatter plot, the flow (Sankey), the organisation chart, the two timelines, the figure cards and the target bar also take their data from your database (age at marriage and at death, father’s → son’s occupation, department of birth → of death, your ancestors as an organisation chart, the life of the starting person, the quality of your database…)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note that a person signed a record (or “cannot sign”)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “Edit an event”: under the witnesses, “✍️ The person (or the husband) signed” and, for a marriage, “✍️ The wife signed”: Not known, Yes, or No (cannot sign). Each witness also has their own “✍️ Signed the record” box. The “✍️ Able to sign” diagram shows, period by period, the share of husbands, wives and witnesses who could sign."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Diagrams: how do I make a Lexis diagram, a ternary diagram, candlesticks, a curve with a margin or a horizon chart?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"New diagram: “📐 Lexis diagram” (Life and death: years at the bottom, ages on the left, each life as a diagonal, with marriages, children, departures and historical bands), “🔺 Ternary diagram” (Parts: three parts adding up to 100%, for example occupational sectors by municipality, decade or family) and “🕯 Candlesticks” (Figures: wheat price, open, close, low, high, moving average). Others are settings: Curve → “Range” (low and high columns, or “🌳 Data from my tree” → Average and margin); Stacked hills → Shape “Horizon”; Leeds matrix → “Clustering tree on the left”; Parents’ ages → “Only the paternal (or maternal) line”; What you have proved → “One row per ancestor”; Places of residence timeline → “What is followed: marital status” and Axis “Age”; Occupations from father to son → “The same people at several ages”. In “From the app”, choose the group, “＋ Criterion” and the period; everything taken stays editable in the Data tab (rows, values, colours), by hand or by pasting a table."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Diagrams: how do I make a quarters table, a tree of a name’s variants, pedigree collapse loops or a double fan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → My diagrams → ＋ New. “🛡️ Quarters table”: the 8, 16 or 32 ancestors of one generation with the coats of arms from their records (Lists → Coats of arms). “🌿 Tree of a name’s variants”: the forms of a name on a year axis, linked to the one they come from. In the existing types: Kinship path → “Pedigree collapse loops” (all paths between the two parents and the inbreeding coefficient); Hourglass → “Two fans” shape; Network → “The whole family, one dot per couple”; Venn → 2 to 8 sets, as crossed bars from 5; Accordion tree → “In circles”; Ancestor grid → “Periodic-table cells”; Pyramid → “A census”; timelines → “Show date uncertainty”; Decorative tree → “Bow tie”. “From the app” fills the table (person, generations, group of people, period, ＋ Criterion); everything can still be edited by hand afterwards."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a diagram of the woodcutters who had 3 children born in Pernay (combined criteria)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a diagram’s editor, “From the app” tab, “Chart” → “✏️ Custom”. Under “Only the people…”, “＋ Criterion” adds as many criteria as you want, the same as in My lists: occupation, number of children (“3 and 3” = exactly 3, “3” then an empty box = at least 3), place of birth, place of death, period of birth or death, sex, living or not, age at death, Sosa generation, label, without a source… “not” reverses a criterion. With two or more criteria, choose “all these criteria (AND)” or “at least one (OR)”. Then “What is counted” and “Group by” (decade of birth, commune, occupation, age at marriage, age at first child, number of unions, branch, with or without a source…). The sentence above the preview reads the setting back and says how many people are found (“People, occupation “woodcutter”, having 3 children, born in “Pernay”, by decade — 2 people found”). A click on a bar or a slice opens the list of these people. The ready-made starting points are arranged by section (People, Couples, Children, Places, Occupations, Dates, My research, Media); those that make no sense for the chosen drawing are greyed out “not for this diagram” (for example decades in a pie chart)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see my censuses as a grid, draw a proof file, a word tree of records, parish areas or a star map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “＋ New diagram”, group “My research”: 🗓️ Census grid (one row per person, one cell per census from 1836 to 1936: found, not found, to search, not yet born, already dead, outside France; “Worklist to print” form; a click on a cell opens the person page), ⚖️ Proof diagram (the question, the clues for and against with their weight, the searches and conflicts, the conclusion and its certainty, top to bottom or left to right; a hypothesis to compare gives one column per answer) and 🌿 Word tree of records (what follows or precedes “fils de”, “veuve de”… in your transcriptions). Group “Maps”: Choropleth map → Level of detail “Parishes” (an area around each church, from the pre-1790 parishes of your places) and Migration map → “From the app” → “✳️ All the links of a place” (incoming spouses, children who left, emigrants) or the “Star map” setting. Map → 🧭 Tools → Distances and travel times → “⭕ In one day”: the circles reached on foot, on horseback, by stagecoach or by train depending on the year, and your family’s places inside them. For each one, “From the app” takes the group of people (ancestors, branch, label, list…), the period and the “＋ Criterion” rules; the table in the Data tab stays yours: add, remove or correct a row, change a colour, paste a table or a CSV; nothing is overwritten until you ask “From the app” again."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own chart from external data (Wikipedia, spreadsheet, image)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Wherever you place a diagram, “⤒ Import”: 📄 File (CSV, or an LCNDA diagram or chart), 📋 Paste a table (first column = the names, following columns = the figures, a row of headings becomes the names of the series), 🌐 Wikipedia page (paste the address: the tables and chart images of the page appear, choose one; you need the Internet), 🖼️ Image of a chart (pie, bars, columns, line or hemicycle: the app spots the colours and suggests the slices, lengths or seats; you name them, correct them, give the real top value). Then you choose the drawing and set everything in the editor. The step-by-step guide is also in the ⚙️ of the diagram gallery."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I say that a person was an athlete, an elected official, a soldier, a farmer… (profiles)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, the “🧩 This person was…” block → “🧩 Choose their profiles…” (or Edit the record → “Blocks to show” → “🧩 Profiles…”). Tick one or more profiles (Artist, Athlete, Politician, Local elected official, Soldier, Farmer, Teacher…), then “Apply”. Each profile adds its sections to the person’s page. A person can have several; unticking a profile hides its sections without erasing what you wrote in them. The “Artist” box on the record and the “Artist” profile are the same thing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own profile (brass band musician, beekeeper…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the profiles window: “＋ Create my own profile…”. In 3 steps: 1 · the name, the icon and the colour; 2 · the sections, taken from other profiles (the artist’s concerts, the athlete’s statistics…) or your own (a table, linked records, images, a text); 3 · the order of the page. Your profile goes into “✎ My profiles” and is ticked like the others."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the page of a person who has several profiles (artist, athlete, mayor…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🧩 This person was…” block → “Open their page”. At the top: “All together” (a single page with the sections of all their profiles, and the life timeline coloured by profile) or one profile at a time. “⚙️ Organise the page”: arrange the profiles and sections (⠿ or ↑ ↓), hide some (👁), choose the width (full line, ½, ⅓), “Setting specific to this person”, “Hide empty sections”. The coloured edge says which profile a section comes from."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record a politician’s career (terms of office, elections, votes)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tick the “🗳 Politician” profile (or “🏛 Local elected official”), then open their page: 🏛 Terms and positions (level, place, dates, predecessor, successor), 🗳 Elections (ballot, round, registered voters, votes, %, result, opponents, poster, election manifesto, results by commune), 👥 Parties, groups and committees, 📜 Texts, votes and proposals, 🏢 Government, 🗣 Speeches, ⚠️ Controversies, 🗺 Map of terms, and the career timeline. “⚙️ Organise” offers templates: Local elected official, Member of parliament, Minister, Activist, Diplomat, Senior civil servant."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record an athlete’s career (clubs, medals, records, statistics)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tick the “🏅 Athlete · sportsperson” profile, choose their sport or sports (more than 120, each with its own fields: position and shirt for rugby or football, time and wind for athletics, horse for riding, grades for judo, Elo for chess…). Their page: 🏟 Clubs and teams, 🥇 Medals and titles, ⏱ Records, 📊 Statistics by season (columns depending on the sport, “＋ Add a column”, “🧮 Calculated column”), 🏁 Results (wins, losses, by competition), coaches, injuries and career change, and the timeline of clubs and selections."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep an artist’s bibliography, works, discography or filmography?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, tick “Artist” (Edit → Blocks to show) and choose their discipline. The blocks 📚 Bibliography, 🖼️ Works and inventory, 💿 Discography (musician, singer, composer, conductor, band) and 🎬 Filmography (filmmaker, actor, videographer) then appear; a block already filled in always stays visible. “See all, edit” opens the catalogue full screen: 🎨 the artist’s page, then one tab per kind (Books, Works, Records, Tracks, Films, Exhibitions; “＋” for the others). Each list has the same bar (🔍 Search, Filter ▾, List | Cards, Sort: by year, by title, catalogue number, value or “My order” with ⠿ ↑ ↓); ＋ New, ⤒ Import, ⤓ Export (PDF, printing, CSV spreadsheet, 💾 re-importable file). If the record is not ticked “Artist” yet, the app offers to tick it when you first save."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make an inventory of an artist’s works?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Artist’s catalogue → 🖼️ Works and inventory: inventory number (suggested after the last one), catalogue raisonné number, technique, support, dimensions, signature, where the work is (in the family, museum, private collection, sold, lent, lost…), exact place, owner, condition, estimated value, provenance, exhibitions, photos. The “Inventory” view gives the table with the total of the values; “🖨 Print the illustrated inventory” puts it on paper with the photos. ⤒ Import: a table from your spreadsheet pasted as it is (columns No., Title, Date, Technique, Dimensions, Place, Owner, Condition, Value… recognised automatically), or the known works from Wikidata (those in museums, with their image)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a complete discography, track by track, as on Discogs?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Artist’s catalogue → 💿 Discography: each record has its type (album, EP, single, compilation, live, soundtrack…), label, catalogue number, format, country, date, genre and styles, barcode, production, studio, its photos (front, back, disc, booklet, inside), its credits, its tracks (no., title, duration — the total duration is calculated; 📝 for the lyrics, notes and annotations, the track’s credits and the version) and its editions and pressings (format, label, catalogue number, country, year, barcode). 📋 “Paste a track list” fills everything in at once. Click on a record: its page as on Discogs, with lyrics that unfold."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import a discography from Discogs (or MusicBrainz, Apple Music)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Catalogue → 💿 Discography → ⤒ Import → Discogs: paste the address of the artist’s page (…discogs.com/artist/…), of an album (…/master/…) or of a record (…/release/…). For an artist, their releases appear to be ticked (appearances unticked); each album comes with its tracks, durations, credits, label, format, covers and the list of its editions. Discogs limits the pace: a little more than one second per record. To search by name, you need a free Discogs token (discogs.com → Settings → Developers) to put in 🔑 Keys. Without a key: MusicBrainz (albums, tracks, editions, covers from the Cover Art Archive), Apple Music (tracks and durations) or Wikidata. The covers are brought into the record’s media (tick box). You need the Internet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import a filmography (IMDb, TMDb)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Catalogue → 🎬 Filmography → ⤒ Import: “Wikidata” with the person’s name, or “An IMDb address” (…imdb.com/name/nm…): IMDb has no free access, but Wikidata links each IMDb entry and gives films, jobs (actor, directing, screenplay, music, cinematography, editing, production), roles, director, length and the film’s IMDb identifier. With a free TMDb key (🔑 Keys), “TMDb” adds posters, summaries, cast, countries and companies. Everything can then be corrected by hand (role, awards, festivals, notes)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a sculpture, a poster, a play, a concerto… (all the types of works)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the person’s catalogue: “＋ New work” (on their page) or “＋” at the end of the tabs. The “What kind of work?” window arranges 131 types into 12 families (visual arts, music, stage, writing, moving image and sound, design and crafts, space and landscape, digital and games, model-making and hobbies, tastes and scents, linking records, my types). Type in the box (“bronze”, “opera”, “podcast”, “coppersmith”…), filter by family, then click a type: its record opens with its own fields. “☰ See the list” opens the list of that type instead of a new record. ⚙️ Manage the types: hide those you never use."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record the casts of a bronze sculpture?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The sculpture’s record → “🔥 Casts and editions” → “＋ Add a cast”. Each cast is a small record: engraved number (1/8, EA I…), founder, date, process (lost wax, sand), patina, patinator, foundry mark, weight, posthumous cast, “ours”, where it is and who owns it, provenance and sales, photos. The table of casts can be filtered (all, during the artist’s lifetime, posthumous); the planned edition (“8 + 4 EA”) recalls the legal limit. Enlargements, reductions and versions are linked at the bottom of the record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I follow a play, its productions and its tours?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Create the play (author, genre, acts, characters, publication), then “＋ Add a production”: company, director, team, music, cast (character, performer, understudy). In each production: “＋ Add a performance” (date, town, venue, audience, première…) and “＋ Add a tour”. The play’s record shows everything as a Tree, a Table, a Calendar or on the Map."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own type of work (carnival float, carved clog…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“＋ New work” → “＋ Create my own type…”. In 5 steps: 1 · name, plural, icon, family, start from scratch or from an existing type, “part” records (a float has editions); 2 · the fields, arranged in groups: 24 kinds (text, date, duration, dimensions, list of choices, person + role, place, linked record, image, table, calculation…), or taken from other types in the library; each field has its help, its starting value, “required”, “shown by default” and “in the search”; 3 · the possible links to other types, named in both directions; 4 · the columns of the list and the lines of the thumbnails; 5 · the preview. Your type has the same tools as the others; ⚙️ Manage the types → ⤓ to export it and give it away."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where is the list of craft trades, and how do I record a piece of ironwork?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“＋ New work” → “Design and crafts” family → “⚒️ The 16 fields of the craft trades…”: the official French list (order of 24 December 2015), with search by trade or speciality. Choosing a trade opens the right type of work with the trade noted. A piece of ironwork: kind (gate, banister…), patron and decision, where it is installed, designer, metal, techniques (forged, riveted, repoussé…), dimensions, decoration, hallmark, workshop and journeymen, preparatory drawings, commission (quote, paid, deadlines), protection (Historic Monuments, Mérimée database), states and restorations."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note that a person was a craftsperson (apprenticeship, compagnonnage, MOF)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tick the “🔨 Craftsperson” profile (or, in the craft trades window, “🔨 “Craftsperson” profile”). Their page gains its sections: 🧑‍🏭 Training and apprenticeship (master, contract, CAP diploma, schools), 🛠 Compagnonnage (guild, journeyman name, Tour de France, masterpiece), 🎖 Titles (Meilleur Ouvrier de France, Maître d’art, Entreprise du patrimoine vivant), 🏭 Workshop · business, 🔏 Hallmarks and marks, ⚒️ their pieces. “…” on a section to write it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import a bibliography?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Catalogue → 📚 Bibliography → ⤒ Import: Open Library by ISBN (on the back of the book), all the books by an author, or the books on a subject (they arrive under “About him / about her”), or Wikidata. Cover, publisher, year, pages, ISBN and language are filled in. Each book gives its link with the artist (by them, about them, illustrations, translation, preface, exhibition catalogue) and where to find it (library, shelfmark, at home)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I show, hide or arrange the fields of an artist’s record (record, track, painting…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, “⚙️ Shown fields”: by groups (Release, Tracks and credits, My copy, Identifiers…), 👁 shows or hides a field, ⠿ (drag) or ↑ ↓ arranges it, and the form follows the same order. A hidden field keeps what is written in it; when reading, an empty field is never shown (“Hide empty fields” switch). Tabs: The record, The list (the columns), The thumbnails, The export (what goes into an entry and into the book). The setting applies to all artists, or only to this one (“Setting specific to…”). “💾 Saved settings”: save under a name, ⤓ export, ⤒ import; ↺ Reset to default."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add my own fields to a record, a work, a book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⚙️ Shown fields” → “＋ Add a field” (or at the bottom of the form): a name (“Bought by”, “Listened to on”…) and its kind: text, long text, number, date, period, duration, dimensions, measure + unit, amount, list of choices, labels, yes/no, rating out of 5, web link, code, person, place, linked record, image · sound · video, document, colour, note. Tick “For all records…” to have it everywhere. Your fields go into “✎ My fields” (or the chosen group), can be renamed (✎) and removed (🗑, with “↶ Undo”; “Put back a removed field” brings them back)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own kind of record (sculpture, poster, carnival float…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the catalogue, “＋” at the end of the tabs → “＋ Create a kind of record…”: a name, the plural, an icon, a family, and “Start from…” nothing or an existing kind (its fields are taken over). Then “⚙️ Shown fields” to add, arrange and hide its fields. It has its list, its filters, its export, and its records link to others like the app’s kinds."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change or remove the emoji in front of a menu tab (🧬 DNA, 📒 Notes…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → “Order and names of the main menu”: to the right of each tab, the small box contains its emoji. Type or paste another one (⊞ + .), or empty the box to have none, then “Save”. “↺ Reset the names to default” also puts back the original emojis."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I quickly choose an emoji for each menu tab?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → “Order and names of the main menu”: the “▾” button to the right of the emoji box offers the suggested emoji, “No emoji” or others. “😀 A suggested emoji for every tab” fills all the empty boxes at once. Then click “Save”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I prefer checkboxes to switches: is that possible?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: Settings → Notifications and buttons → “Switches” → “Checkboxes”. Settings that apply right away are then drawn as checkboxes everywhere; they work the same way. “Switches (recommended)” puts it back as before."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I transcribe a document with the full tool, and read the handwriting with the AI on my computer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a document page (Documents tab), “📜 Open in the Workshop” opens the transcription workshop on its image: the transcription already started, otherwise a new one. For the AI that reads the whole page on your computer (Qwen3-VL with Ollama): Settings → Handwriting recognition (HTR) → “🔍 Install or check in-depth reading…”. Nothing goes to the Internet except the model download."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I have the same record or the same book twice, three times: how do I note it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On its record, in “📦 My copy (collection)”: the first copy is described as usual (condition, storage, price, dedication); below, “Other copies (duplicate, triplicate…)” → “＋ One more copy” adds a line per copy, each with its condition, its edition or pressing, its place, its price, its value and its dedication. ⚙️ Shown fields lets you show or hide this line."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I link a track to its takes, its mixes and the records it appears on?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The track (🎵 the written work: lyrics, music, publisher, SACEM…) has its recordings: “＋ Add a recording” (take, mix, remaster, version, live), each with its date, studio, duration, sound file (▶), credits, “made from” (the mix of a take…) and “chosen take”. On a record, each track can point to a recording (purple badge): the track’s record then shows the tree of takes and mixes and, for each, the releases (record, track, format, year, duration). “Tree | Table” changes the view."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record the provenance, exhibitions and bibliography of a painting?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the work’s record: “Provenance” (one line per step: date, how — the artist, given, sold, bought back, inheritance… —, who, detail, price) reads like a timeline; “Exhibitions”: each exhibition is a record (🏛), with the catalogue number and the lender; “Bibliography of the work”: the book or article (📚 record) and the page. Also: catalogue raisonné and inventory numbers, where it is and who owns it, states and restorations, value and insurance, related works (study, sketch, replica, engraving after, pendant), editions and casts."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make the book of the catalogue raisonné or the discography?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the list of works (or records), “📖 Create the book…” — or Page layout → “🖼 Catalogue raisonné” / “💿 Discography”. Choose the artist, the design, the format, the works (those of the filtered list, or all), the order, the parts of the book (to tick and arrange: cover, introduction, chronology, entries, exhibitions, bibliography, index, concordances…), the fields of each entry and the layout (a double page per work, one, two or four per page). The book opens in the page layout; each entry follows the work’s record: File → “↻ Update the dynamic objects” picks up a corrected provenance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I organise an artist’s page?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Catalogue → 🎨 Page → “⚙️ Organise the page”: each section (biography, artistic identity, awards, discography, works, exhibitions, bibliography, filmography, concerts…) can be arranged (⠿ or ↑ ↓), hidden (👁) and take the whole line, half or a third. “Template” starts from the discipline (Musician, Painter, Writer, Filmmaker, Photographer, Sculptor, Architect); “＋ Add a section” for your own (“Their instruments”…). A section’s “…” button edits the artist’s profile (stage names, bands and their members, labels, galleries, training, awards, official links, press)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why “Mentioned in artists’ catalogues” on a person’s record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A person chosen in a catalogue (credit on a record, owner of a painting, dedication, band member…) sees this block on their record: “Paris canaille (1958) — accordion”. The links read both ways: the record’s page also shows the person in “🔗 Linked to”. A name without a record (grey “?” badge) can become a record later."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record a concert or a show with its setlist (setlist.fm-style)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Artist’s catalogue → 🎤 Stage tab (or “＋” → 🎤 Stage and shows → Concerts and performances) → “＋ New concert or performance”. Choose the kind of evening (concert, comedy, theatre, dance, circus, orchestra, storytelling): the setlist adapts (songs, sketches, scenes, acts, works). Each line points to the record of the track, sketch or scene (“＋ Create” if it does not exist yet); 📝 on a line for the cover, the 👤 guests with their role, the dedication, the 1st or last time, a version, “unfinished”, the duration. “＋ An encore”, “📋 Paste a list” (one title per line), “📋 Copy another evening’s setlist”. The title fills itself in (“Joseph MARTIN in Tulle, 14 July 1958”). On the record, a title without a record has “＋ record” to create it in one click."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the most played tracks, the first and last time, the map of concerts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🎤 Stage tab: the top bar offers List, Cards, Map, Timeline and 📊 Statistics. The statistics (most played titles with the first and last time, evenings per year, country, town and venue, average length, titles per evening, typical setlist of a tour, never played live, compare two evenings, roles and understudies in the theatre, conductors and soloists, who goes on stage most often) are calculated on the filtered list: filter a tour, a town, years or a title played to see them change. On the map, the size of the marker = the number of evenings; a click shows the list for that place."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I group the dates of a tour?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On an evening’s record, “Tour” field → “＋ Create the record “…””. The tour’s record shows its dates (each evening shows “date 12/31”), the route on the map and the typical setlist (the title most often played in each position, encores included). “＋ Add a date” creates the next evening, already linked to the tour."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record the performances of a play, the evening’s roles and the understudies?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An evening of the “🎭 Theatre” kind: the “Production” field makes it one of its performances (the production’s record lists them all, with “＋ Add a performance”); “Evening’s roles”: the role, played by 👤, regular, standing in, understudy, created the role; also incidents, curtain calls and box office takings. The actor’s filmography counts their performances and stand-ins."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I turn a concert recording into “live” recordings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the evening’s record, 🎙 Recordings box: choose the sound or video, then “→ Turn into “live” recordings”. Each track in the setlist that has a record gets a “Live” recording (date, place, recording, timing noted on the line); “↶ Undo” right after if needed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make the filmography and stage credits of an actor or actress?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Catalogue → 🎭 Roles tab. Each detailed role is a record: the work (film, series, episode, production…), the character, the kind of role (lead, supporting, extra, voice, dubbing…), the director 👤, the credits, the shoot (period, locations), the co-stars, the fee, the contract, performances and stand-ins, the original voice, the anecdote. The list also gathers the films in the catalogue, the series (episodes ticked), the evening roles of performances and records from other catalogues that mention the person. Tabs Cinema, Television, Theatre, Voice, Advertising…, search, filters (kind of role, years, director, co-star), sorting, List | Cards | Timeline; a click on a line shows its card; “＋ Make it a detailed role record” for a line coming from elsewhere."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see all the people who have profiles (artist, athlete, elected official…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “This person was… (profiles)”: one row (or one card, “Cards”) per person with the coloured badges of their profiles. Filter ▾ by profile, group, number of profiles, occupation, customised page or locked record; Sort ▾ (A → Z, most profiles, most records…). A click opens their page. The “Occupations” list stays separate: it sorts the occupations (a text), this one the people."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a section of a person’s page ¾ or ⅔ wide, or change the colour of its edge?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their page: “⚙️ Organise the page”. Each section has its width (full line, ½, ⅓, ¼, ⅔, ¾) and a colour badge for the top edge (app colours, recent colours, eyedropper, # code, “None” = no edge; ↺ = the profile’s colour). In “All together”, a profile’s badge changes its colour (badges, edges, timeline). The same goes for the artist’s page."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I set the pages of all the people who have profiles in one go?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → Person and animal records → “This person was… (profiles)”. On the left the standard setting (order, visibility, width and colour of the sections; order, colour and visibility of the profiles; templates), on the right an exception for one person (chosen in the picker). At the bottom, the customised pages: “↗ Open” or “↺ Reset to default” (the page goes back to the standard setting, with ↶ Undo). “💾 Saved settings” keeps everything under a name."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why can I no longer edit a person’s catalogue or page?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Their record is locked (or the app is in view-only mode): the catalogue, its work records and their profile page open read-only (“🔒 Locked record” at the top); the buttons to edit, add a profile or a record are hidden. Unlock the person’s record to edit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why “Tick Craftsperson?” when I add a piece of ironwork or ceramics?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A craft piece (ironwork, ceramics, glass, goldsmithing, cabinet-making, instrument-making…) goes into the “Craftsperson” profile (or “Artist” if it is already ticked). A work of art (sculpture, painting…) asks for “Artist”; a tour or a series, “Artist” or “Actor”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I show the “Actor” profile on a person’s page?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tick the “Actor, actress, performer” profile (🎭 Roles tab → “🎬 Tick the profile”, or the record’s profiles). Their page gains its sections: roles (with Cinema, Television, Theatre…), filmography, television, stage credits, voice, advertising, music videos, statistics (roles per decade, most frequent directors and co-stars, lead / supporting), training, agents and companies, castings, awards, skills and voice, stage evenings. “⚙️ Organise the page” arranges them, hides them, chooses their width; “…” on a section writes the profile."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record a series, its seasons and its episodes (IMDb-style)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Catalogue → “＋” → 🎬 Films and shows → Series and programmes → “＋ New series, programme”. On its record: “＋ Add a season”, then “＋ Several…” creates all the numbered episodes at once (you then write their title, date, director, screenplay, guests, filming locations, music, video). Click a season to see its episodes, an episode for its card. Same model for a television or radio programme, a podcast, a radio serial, a manga in volumes or a book saga (cycle → volumes)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record the episodes in which a person acted or worked?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the series’ record, the “👤 … in this series” panel: 🎭 Role (character, kind of role) or 🛠 Job (screenwriter, director, composer…). Tick the episodes in the list (Shift+click for a whole range), or write “episodes 2 to 7” → Tick, or “the whole season”, “the whole series”; add the credited name and the fee, then 💾 Save. Their filmography then shows “recurring · S3 ep. 2-7 (6 episodes)”. “＋ A person…” for another crew member; “Remove” offers “↶ Undo”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I deleted a record or a work by mistake: how do I get it back?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Right after, “↶ Undo” in the message at the bottom left: the record comes back, with the same number and all its links. To keep a catalogue elsewhere: ⤓ Export → 💾 File, then ⤒ Import → “A file exported from here” (the old “JSON backup” can be re-imported too)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record an invention and its patents?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the inventor’s record: open their page (catalogue) → 💡 Inventions → “＋ New invention”. Field, problem, principle, first idea, workshop; “📜 Patents”: one line per country (country, no., filing, grant, applicant, status) and, with 📝, the title, the claims, the class, the patent attorney; then prototypes, licences, manufactured objects, prior art and lawsuits, awards (Lépine competition…)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record a discovery (species, comet, gene, site…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The person’s page → 🔬 Discoveries → “＋ New discovery”: choose the “Kind” (species, comet, gene, fossil, archaeological site, wreck…) and the matching fields appear. Then the place (on the map), the discoverers 👤, the naming (name, in honour of, status), the publications and “recognised or disputed”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a scientist’s profile (thesis, positions, students, publications)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tick “🔬 Scientist” on their record, then open their page: each section (thesis, laboratories and positions, publications, academic genealogy, missions, academies, things named after them, notebooks…) has its “…” button to write in. Institutions are Employer records. ⚙️ Organise the page → “Template: Scientist” for the starting order."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import a researcher’s publications (DOI, ORCID, HAL)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The scientist’s page → 📚 Publications → “⤒ Import (DOI, ORCID, HAL)”: paste DOIs, their ORCID identifier or their name in HAL, tick what to take. Those already there are recognised (same DOI or same title); their name among the authors is linked to their record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the academic genealogy (supervisor, students)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The “🌳 Academic genealogy” section of the scientist’s page: their thesis supervisor and their mentors above, their students below. If a student has their own record with this thesis supervisor, they are added automatically."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record an expedition and draw its route on the map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tick “🧭 Explorer” on their record, then page → 🧭 Expeditions → “＋ New expedition”: “🗺 Route”, one line per stage (place, dates, means, ▲ summit): the map draws itself. Then members 👤 and their roles, firsts, notebooks and films, collections and where they are today, accounts, place names."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note that a person appeared in the newspaper, on the radio or on TV?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, the “📰 In the media” block → “＋ Add”: the kind of media (press, radio, TV, podcast, web, newsreels, book, poster), the type of appearance (interview, portrait, report, obituary, birth or marriage notice, review, published photo, small ad…), the date, the outlet (chosen in “Lists → Magazines and periodicals”, or written as it is), the title or programme, the page or duration, the journalist, the other people with their role (★ main, ☆ secondary), the subject, the tone, an extract, and the scan, sound or video (the images, sounds and videos window). The block shows a timeline by year and “📰 Press · 📻 Radio · 📺 TV” badges to sort what you see."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see all the family’s articles and programmes?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “Press review”: all the appearances of all the people, with the usual bar (🔍 Search, Filter ▾ by person, kind of media, type, tone, List | Cards, Sort). From a record, “See all” opens the same list already filtered on the person (“Joseph MARTIN ×” badge: one click removes it to see everyone). ⚙️ chooses the columns, “⤓ Export” gives a spreadsheet, a PDF or a file."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record a prize, a medal, a decoration or a competition (even an unusual one)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, the “🏆 Competitions and awards” block → “＋ Add”: the category (sport, game, food, unusual, agricultural, artistic, school, professional, decoration… or your own word), the award, the competition (a 🏅 record that gathers its whole list of winners: “＋ Create” directly from the field), the edition, the level (local → world), the event, the date, the place, the performance, the rank, the medal, the title won, the prize, the organiser, the jury or teammates, the trophy or the cutting. For a decoration (Légion d’honneur, Mérite agricole, medals…) the grade, the promotion, the decree, the file (Léonore database) and the sponsor also appear. Lists → “Honours list” gathers the whole family, with its timeline."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record school, diplomas and classmates?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, the “🎓 Diplomas and education” block: “＋ A stage” for each school or training (primary school, secondary school, high school, university, apprenticeship, compagnonnage, military training…) with the establishment (a record that gathers everyone who went there), the years, the class, boarder or day pupil, the teachers and classmates (picker with a role), the subjects, the results, the end-of-year prizes, the class photos and the school reports; “＋ Add” in “Diplomas” for a diploma (ready-made list: certificat d’études, brevet, CAP, baccalauréat, degree, driving licence, military certificates… or free text). The block shows the timeline of the path, the table of diplomas, the latest class photo with the classmates, and the school record books and reports."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I find all the family’s tracks, albums, plays, films, inventions, terms of office…?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “🎨 Creations and careers” group: “Everything that was created or lived”, then one list per family (🎵 Music, 🎭 Stage, 🎬 Films and series, 📚 Writings, 🖼 Visual arts, ⚒️ Crafts, 💡 Inventions and discoveries, 🧭 Expeditions, 🏛 Political careers, 🏅 Sport, 🎓 Education, ✎ My types…), for all the people. The same bar as the other lists: 🔍 Search (title, person, place, year), Filter ▾ (kind with the number, person, period, place, with image, with sound or video), List | Cards, Sort. “Group by kind” puts a subheading per kind (“🎵 Tracks (34)”); untick it to mix everything. A click opens the record in the person’s catalogue, “↗” opens the person’s record, “＋ New…” asks for the person then the kind. Press review and Honours list are in the same group."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"A person is mentioned as a classmate, jury member or guest in someone else’s record: where can I see it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their own record, in the same block: the appearances, awards and stages stored with another person where they appear are shown too, with “in the record of …” and their role. A click opens the record concerned."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which shapes are there for the decorative tree to print?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → 🌳 Decorative tree → ＋: fan (half circle, 240°, three quarters 270°), wheel, drawn tree (trunk, branches, foliage), tree of boxes from bottom to top, or ancestry table from left to right. Up to 12 generations for the fans and the wheel, 8 for the drawn tree, 7 for the boxes (the number offered follows the shape). The tree then opens in the vector drawing so you can retouch everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own style or my own size for the decorative tree?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the tree window: “＋” next to Style (or 🎨 My styles): a name, the starting style, the font, and each colour (paper, ink, title, the person’s box, paternal and maternal sides, empty boxes, trunk, leaves) with a preview. ⚙️ next to Size (or ⚙️ My sizes): your sizes in cm (from 5 to 500), and for all of them, sizes as well as styles, tick the ones you see and arrange them with ↑ ↓. The same settings are in Settings → “Editing, retouching, page layout and sound” → 🌳 Decorative tree, with the shape, size and style offered at the start."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange the shapes of the decorative tree or give them a shortcut?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⚙️ Customise” in the tree window, or Settings → “Editing, retouching, page layout and sound” → Buttons and options → 🌳 Decorative tree: hide or arrange the shapes and buttons. Just below (and in Settings → Keyboard shortcuts), give a key to a shape, to “My sizes”, “My styles” or to “Create the tree”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose a custom size for a map (width, height, unit, resolution)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → 🗺️ Map → “＋ New map”: in “Size”, take a size from the list (A0 to A6, US Letter, postcard, square, 10 × 15, 13 × 18 photos…, 16:9 screens) or “✎ Custom size…”: width × height, the unit (mm, cm, px, inches), ▯ Portrait / ▭ Landscape and the resolution (dpi) for export as an image. The size is remembered for the next map and stored in “⭐ My sizes” (at the top of the list, in all tools); “× Remove from My sizes” takes it away. To print the screen (a map included) in a custom size: 🖨 Print → “✎ Other…”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the size of a drawing or a map after creating it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the vector drawing: File → “📐 Document size / artboards…” (also in the 📄 Document tab): the size from the shared list or a custom size, the unit, the orientation, the resolution suggested for export, the background (colour or transparent) and the bleed (3 mm at first, added around each page when exporting “with bleed and crop marks”). The objects stay in place, are rescaled with the artboard, or keep their size and are recentred. ↶ (⌘Z) goes back to the previous size; 💾 Save keeps it with the drawing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I put the title, author or rights of a drawing, a book or a retouched photo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"File → “ℹ️ Information…” in the vector drawing (title, author, subject, description, keywords, rights, language, date: in the exported SVG and PDF), in the page layout (title, author, subject, description, keywords, language and optional ISBN, checked: in the PDF, HTML and ePub) and in photo retouching (title, author, rights, description, keywords, date, linked people: written into the PNG or JPG image, readable by Photoshop, Lightroom or the file explorer). This information is kept with the document; ↶ undoes it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the page size of a book I have already started?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the page layout: File → “📐 Document size…” (or ⚙️ Document tab): page size (shared list or custom size), orientation, facing pages, margins, columns and bleed. “Apply the size to”: all pages, the page shown, or some pages (“2-5, 8”) — a page with its own size prints on a sheet of its size. The objects stay in place or are rescaled. ↶ (⌘Z) goes back; 💾 Save keeps everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I enlarge or shrink a photo, or add a margin around it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In photo retouching: Image → “📏 Image size…”: width and height (pixels, %, cm, mm, inches), 🔗 keep proportions, resolution (dpi), resample or not and the method (bicubic smoother for enlarging, sharper for reducing, nearest neighbour to keep sharp pixels); “Fit to a size” takes a size from the shared list. Image → “⬚ Canvas size…” enlarges or crops the canvas around the photo: anchor on 9 points, coloured or transparent background, “Relative” to write what is added. The area appears around the photo and the final size is shown below; everything is applied when you save or export the image, and ↶ undoes it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I rotate a photo (on its side, upside down, mirrored)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In photo retouching: Image → “🔄 Image rotation”: ↻ 90° clockwise, ↺ 90° anticlockwise, 180°, “Angle…” (a free angle: the canvas grows, transparent or coloured corners, and “✂️ Crop to remove empty corners”), “📐 Straighten the horizon” (draw a line along the horizon or a wall: the angle is suggested automatically), horizontal or vertical flip. The whole image turns as one: layers, masks, selection, texts, shapes, adjustment layers; width and height swap, the resolution stays. A single step: ↶ undoes everything. A phone photo still on its side offers “↻ Put it upright”. The commands can have their own shortcut (⚙️ Customise → retouching shortcuts). In quick retouching: ↺ ↻ in the left column and the Image menu. The original is kept: saving makes a new version."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I draw a house to the nearest centimetre?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the plan, when drawing a wall (▬), a dimension (📏) or a free room: click the starting point then type the length on the keyboard (350, 3.5 m, 35 mm) and Enter; add the angle with @ (350 @ 90: upwards; 0 to the right, 180 to the left). With the room tool (⬚), type “420 x 350”. At the top left: the mouse position, the length and the angle of the current line. 🧲 Snap: grid (1 to 100 cm), corners, middles of walls, along walls, alignments (pink guide lines), angle step (90° to 1°). Alt: without snapping. The arrows move what is selected by 1 cm (⇧: one grid step). In Properties: length, angle and coordinates of walls, distances from a door or window to the ends of the wall, X and Y of furniture."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare two periods of the house?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the plan, the 👻 layer menu (next to the floors) offers “🕰 Previous period”, “🕰 Next period” or a period of your choice: the same floor, as it was, appears in sepia dotted lines under the one you are drawing (same transparency as the floors layer). Switch on “Legend: ― added · ┄ gone” in the Floor tab: walls added since are underlined in green, those that have gone are dotted, with their number at the top of the plan. In 3D, the “🕰 Other period” switch shows its walls transparently. With the 🕰 Year slider (🏠 House tab), “👻 Show the previous period as a ghost” always keeps the previous period underneath."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a mezzanine, a double-height living room or a stairwell?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The simplest: click the room (the living room) then, in Properties → “▨ Floor”, “🏗 Mezzanine open onto this room”: the floor above is added if missing, the mezzanine covers part of the room, the rest becomes a void with its railing, and a staircase arrives at the edge of the mezzanine. By hand: on the floor above, the ▨ “Void” tool (O key) and drag diagonally; or tick “No floor: void onto the floor below” on a room. The plan shows a hatched “VOID”, the floor below “double height” in dotted lines; in 3D the floor is cut open; during the visit you cannot walk into the void. The railing (wood, metal, glass, low wall or none, height of your choice) is placed automatically along the edges without a wall."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does a staircase cut through the floor above?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A staircase placed (straight, turning, spiral, miller’s ladder) automatically opens the stairwell in the floor of the storey above, with a railing around it except on the arrival side; on the floor above, its outline is drawn in dotted lines with “↓”. Its height follows the floor height plus the thickness of the floor (untick “Height: that of the floor…” to fix it). The panel gives the number of steps, their height and the tread, and warns if the staircase is too steep (Blondel’s rule, 2 h + g between 60 and 66 cm). “⇅ Reverse the direction of climb”, quarter turn to the left or right. For a house drawn before: “Open the stairwells of all staircases”. During the visit, going up you arrive just after the last step."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add dormers, an eyebrow dormer, roof windows (Velux) or gutters?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Floor tab → “🏠 Roof (3D view)”: “＋ Roof window”, “＋ Dormer” (two small slopes) or “＋ Shed dormer” (one slope rising towards the roof). For each: the slope (front or back), its place along the roof, its width and height; × removes it (↶ puts it back). The “Gutters and downpipes” box places them along the eaves. Everything is visible in the 3D view."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"My house is L-shaped (or T, U): how do I get the right roof?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Floor tab → “🏠 Roof” → “One roof per wing”. The house is cut into rectangular wings according to its rooms; each one gets its own roof, at the same pitch: where two roofs meet, the valley is made automatically. Dormers then have a “Wing” choice."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make an attic with a sloping ceiling (and the Carrez law area)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the top floor, with a sloping roof: Floor tab → “🏠 Attic” → “Attic: the ceiling follows the slope of the roof”, then the height of the low walls (knee wall). The plan hatches the places under 1.80 m, the bottom of the screen gives the ceiling height under the mouse, each room shows its “Carrez law” area, and the 3D view, the section and the visit follow the slope."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a curved wall (round tower, apse, arched wall)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click the wall, then in Properties → “⌒ Curvature (sagitta)”: how far its middle bulges out from the straight line (+ on one side, − on the other; 0 = straight). The plan, 3D, the visit and the section follow the curve: the room’s floor too, the dimension gives the length of the arc and the sagitta, and doors and windows are placed on the curve."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the house in cross-section?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🏠 House tab → “🧰 House tools” → “✂ Draw the section line”, then two clicks on the plan (the red “A — A” line stays drawn). The section shows the floors, the hatched floor slabs, the cut walls with their doors and windows, the staircase, the roof and the heights; “🖨 Print…”, “⤓ Export as SVG” or “as PNG”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know the living area of the house, room by room?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🏠 House tab → “🧰 House tools” → “📐 Areas per room”: each room, each floor and the whole house, on the ground and according to the Carrez law (only where you can stand up, 1.80 m and more). “🧾 In the spreadsheet” opens the same table in the Spreadsheet, with the totals as formulas."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I check that the house plan holds up?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🏠 House tab → “⚠️ Check the plan”: a list in the style of the Database check (🔴 impossible, 🟠 to check, 🟡 curious) — a door wider than its wall or placed on a wall that has gone, a window cut by the floor or under the slope of the roof, a room without a door, a staircase that is too steep (Blondel’s rule) or that does not cut through the floor, a void that opens onto nothing… A click on a line takes you to the object."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the house change over the years?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Create dated periods (🕰 The periods, 🏠 House tab); “🧰 House tools” then has a “🕰 The year” slider: as you drag it, the plan and the 3D view take the most recent period before that year. “▶ Watch it change” plays through them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see who lived in a room during the visit?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click the room → “🕯 Memories of the room” → “The people of the room” (the usual picker, main and secondary, a role on each badge: their bedroom, born here, servant…), then their years. During the visit (🚶 Visit), when you enter the room, a small card shows these people; a click opens their record, “✦” the memories of the room."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I prepare a guided tour of the house?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"3D view → “🚶 Visit”: position yourself, look at what should be shown, then “📍 Stop here” and write what is said at that spot. Then, 🏠 House tab → “▶ Guided tour”: you move from one stop to the next by sliding, and the text is read aloud. “✎ The stops” to rewrite or reorder them; “⤓ Tour images” gives one image per stop and the texts, to drop into 🎬 Video editing to make a film."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare an old photo with the house today?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"During the visit, stand where the photo was taken, turn in the same direction, then “📷 Period photo here” and choose the photo (images window). It shows over the 3D view with a “Then ⟷ Now” slider. A 📷 marks it on the plan and in 3D; a click on it takes you back there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I show the hamlet, the farm and its outbuildings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Draw each building as a house in the list (the barn, the bread oven, the house next door…), then in the main house: 🏠 House → “🏘 Buildings on the land…” → ＋ each building (picker), and set its offset and rotation. They appear in dotted lines on the plan and in 3D in their place. The census household number is noted in the same place."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I send the house tour to someone who does not have the app?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🏠 House tab → “📤 Share the tour”: an .html file to send (e-mail, USB stick). It opens with a double-click in any browser, without the Internet: the house in 3D, its plans and the guided tour, read-only. It contains neither the inhabitants, nor the memories, nor the notes, nor the photos."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the house be shown in simple mode?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: Settings → Accessibility → 🧓 Simple mode → “The big buttons” → “＋ Add a tile” → “🏠 The house”, then choose the house. In simple mode, this big button shows the house in 3D turning slowly, with big buttons to turn, move closer, change floor and listen to the guided tour — without being able to change anything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make the video of the house’s guided tour?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🏠 House tab → “🧰 House tools” → “🎬 Tour video…” (you need stops: 3D view → 🚶 Visit → 📍 Stop here). Choose the format (landscape, square, portrait) and the opening title: the camera glides from one stop to the next and the text of each stop is shown as a subtitle. “⤓ Export the video” gives an MP4 or WebM file (depending on the computer); “Also keep a copy in the media” stores it with your videos. “⤓ Tour images” is still there for Video editing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I put a glass roof or an exit on a flat roof?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: with a flat roof (Floor tab → 🏠 Roof → Shape “Flat”), the buttons become “＋ Glass roof”, “＋ Roof lantern” (a glazed peak) and “＋ Roof structure” (the small building of the exit onto the roof). Each one can be set (place, crosswise, width, depth) and is visible in 3D and in the section, like the dormers of a sloping roof."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I walk around the house in simple mode?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In simple mode’s “🏠 The house”, the “🚶 Walk around inside” button: big buttons ⬆ Forward, ⬇ Back, ⬅ ➡ Turn, ⏫ ⏬ change floor, and “🏠 See the whole house” to go out. No keyboard or mouse needed; nothing can be changed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I show who lived in a room, with their role?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click the room → “🕯 Memories of the room” → “The people of the room”: the usual picker with the two areas “★ Main” and “☆ Secondary” and a role on each badge (their bedroom, born here, servant… or a free word; a person without a record is possible). The years are noted just below. Roles written before are kept as they are."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I measure in the house’s 3D view?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"3D view → “📏 Measure”, then two clicks on the house: the distance, the ground distance and the difference in height. “Dimensions” shows the length of each wall and the ceiling height; “Top”, “Front”, “Side” position the camera. The thickness of the floors between two storeys is set in the Floor tab."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I line up a floor with the one below or above?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top of the plan, next to the floors: “👻” chooses the floor shown transparently (below, above, both, or none). Its walls and the outline of its rooms are shown in dotted lines, brown for the one below, blue for the one above. The transparency is set in the Floor tab."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I move a room with its walls?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Drag the room: its walls (doors and windows included), its furniture and its texts follow; neighbouring walls attached at a corner stretch to stay joined. A wall shared with another room does not move. To move only the floor: ⇧ while dragging, or untick “🔗 Walls and furniture follow the room” in its properties."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get more furniture and objects in the house plan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“Furniture” tab: more than 4,000 objects old and new, sorted by room (Bedroom, Living room, Dining room, Kitchen, Bathroom, Laundry room, Hall, Study, Children, Garage, Garden…) and by kind (Lighting, Decoration, Appliances, Music, Storage, Farm and animals, Structure). Type a word in “Search”: box bed, kneading trough, lamp, guitar, living room, bathroom, toy… (the number of objects found is shown; Esc empties the box). Click on the object: it is placed in the middle of the view. Each object has its real dimensions, its drawing seen from above and its 3D shape; its colour, second colour and material can be changed in “Properties”. Style furniture (wardrobe, chest of drawers, bed, sideboard, armchair, chandelier…) exists in every style that suits it: periods (Louis XIII to Art Deco), regions (Provençal, Norman, Breton, Basque…), from elsewhere (Biedermeier, Victorian, Gustavian…) and modern (1950s, Scandinavian, industrial…); the “🎨 All styles” menu shows only one of them, or hides them all (“Without style furniture”). There are also trees and the vegetable garden, farm animals, toys, instruments, cars…"}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the house plan get slow with a lot of furniture?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No: in 3D, each piece of furniture is grouped into a few volumes (one per material), even with 200 objects placed. If the 3D view stays slow on an old computer, untick “All floors” or use “Top”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own object for the house plan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Furniture → “＋ Create an object”: “Start from an existing object” takes the size, shape and material of a piece from the catalogue (a linen cupboard, a dough bin…), then you change what you want: its name, its category (or a new one), its size in cm, its shape seen from above and in 3D, its material (oak, walnut, cast iron, copper, wicker, earthenware…), its colour and a second colour (legs, handles), its legs, its height from the floor, its direction, its rounded corners, its doors and drawers, its period, and if you like a photo seen from above (from the computer or “🖼️ A photo from the media”). The preview shows the object in 2D and 3D. It is kept for all your houses, in its category and in “My objects”; ✎ to edit or delete it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put an image in a frame or on a painting in the house (recreate a painter’s studio)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the frame, the painting, the canvas, the easel, the poster, the mirror or the gallery wall: in its settings, “🖼 Image” section, “🖼 Change” opens the window of images from your media (filtered on the house’s first inhabitant). You can also drag an image from the computer straight onto the object, in the plan or in the 3D view: it goes into the media and is placed on it. A memory attached to the object can serve as the image. Settings: fill, fit or centre, drag and zoom in the preview to crop, rotate by 90°, mat and frame (colour, width). Gallery wall: one image per frame (badges 1 to 6). The house only keeps the media number and its settings; if the image no longer exists, a plain canvas replaces it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What trade and workshop objects are there in the house plan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Furniture → “Trades, workshop”: more than 100 objects sorted by trade — painter (studio, field and table easels, stretched canvases from size 6 F to 100 F, stacked canvases, canvas racks, palette, paint box, side table, model’s platform), sculptor, blacksmith and farrier, joiner and cabinet-maker, cooper, wheelwright, shoemaker, tailor and seamstress, weaver, potter, baker, butcher, grocer, apothecary, clockmaker, printer, bookbinder, photographer, schoolteacher, doctor and midwife, barber, miller, winegrower, beekeeper, washerwoman, lacemaker, instrument maker, notary and town hall, post office, miner, fisherman, sailor, rural policeman. Look for the trade (“baker”, “clockmaker”…) in the Furniture search box."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find antique furniture and objects from the past?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Furniture → tick “🕰 Antique only”: only furniture and things from the past remain (linen cupboard, dough bin, longcase clock, Breton dresser, settle chest, box bed, wooden cradle, spinning wheel, butter churn, oil lamp, Godin stove, andirons, mantelpiece, treadle sewing machine, school bench…), in each category and in the search. Untick to see everything again."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the material, the legs or the second colour of a piece of furniture already placed?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click on the furniture → Properties → “🎨 Material, legs, details”: the material (oak, walnut, cherry, cast iron, copper, stone, marble, wicker, straw, earthenware, fabric…), a second colour (legs, handles, trims), legs (straight, turned, ball, glides, castors), rounded corners, the number of doors and drawers (wardrobes, sideboards, chests of drawers) and its period. The 3D view follows straight away."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I link a house to people or censuses?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the house, “House” tab: “＋ An inhabitant…” for a person, “＋ A census…” for one or more censuses (tick “Also add the people of the household” to make them inhabitants in one go). At the top of the tab, “🔗 Linked to” shows everything that is linked; a click opens the record. In Lists → “🏠 Family houses”, “Linked to” shows it too (▦ Cards or ☰ Table)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a house to a census?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the census (the person’s record → Censuses → ✎). At the bottom, “🏠 House”: choose the house and its period, then “🔗 Link” (it is saved straight away), or “＋ New house” to draw it, already linked. “↗ Open the house” opens it directly at the period of this census."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see a person’s houses?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, the “🏠 Houses” block: the houses where they are an inhabitant, linked to a room, or in a linked census, with their image, the period and “↗ Open”. It can be arranged like the other blocks (Settings → Appearance → record blocks)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the house change over the years?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the house, “🕰 Periods” (at the top): the house you drew becomes the first period (a name, a year or a date), and “＋ New period (copy of this one)” makes a copy at another date (1900, 1936…) that you change: a room added, a wall knocked down, other furniture, another roof. ‹ › or the menu at the top move from one period to another, in 2D as in 3D; “▶” plays them in date order. House tab → “🕰 The periods”: ✎ to rename, change the date or link the period to a census, × to delete it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I link a period of the house to a census?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"House tab → “📋 The censuses”: the menu next to each census chooses its period (or ✎ on the period → “Linked to the census”). From the census window, “↗ Open the house” then opens it at that period."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I download free, royalty-free 3D furniture?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Furniture → “📦 Online library”: models from Poly Haven, royalty-free (CC0), can be searched and imported in one click. “ZIP pack” tab: download a CC0 pack (Kenney, Quaternius…) from the author’s site, choose the .zip file and tick the models to keep. They arrive in “My objects”, with their licence, author and source (✎ to see them)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide or arrange the furniture categories and objects?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Furniture → “⚙️ Arrange”: tick what you want to see and arrange the categories with ↑ ↓, then the objects in each category. “↺ Reset to default” goes back to the original order."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a map to the right scale, without distorting it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → 🗺️ Map → “＋ New map”: choose the size, then locate the map (the place and the width covered): the scale is then correct. The “Scale grid” (100 m, 1 km… squares, never printed) helps place each thing in its place, and a selected object shows its real length or area."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are “Coat of arms mode” and “Map mode” at the top of the drawing for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tick them to show on the right the coat of arms or map tab and menu; unticked, they stay hidden to keep the editor simple."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I grey out (or not) the rows I have unticked in lists to arrange?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Menus, column and lists” → Column tab → “👁 Hidden rows in lists to arrange”: “Leave them as they are”, or “Grey them out” with the level of grey you want. It applies to all the lists where you tick what is shown (menus, lists, Reorder, Customise…)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange the lists (or the Settings pages) into groups?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the bottom of the left column, “Reorder”: tick or untick “Arrange in groups”. In groups: arrange, rename, create or delete groups, move a list to another group, choose whether groups are open or closed. As a single list: arrange with ↑ ↓. Each way keeps its settings, you find them again when switching. “My lists” always stay at the bottom, apart: they can only be arranged among themselves. The same setting is in Settings → “Menus, column and lists” → “Lists” tab (for Lists, “Reorder” takes you straight there)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put a drawing into a group or a layer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Layers tab: drag the row onto the group (it turns blue); or choose the object then “📁 Put into…”. Double-click on a name to rename it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange or hide the buttons of the House tool, or give it my shortcuts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the tool, “⚙️ Customise” (at the top), or Settings → “Editing, retouching, page layout and sound” → “🏠 House (plan and 3D)” editor: top bar, tools in the left column, 3D view bar and panel tabs, to tick and arrange, to keep as named sets; and below, your keyboard shortcuts for each command."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What can I ask “Ask a question”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Search → Ask a question in French, or straight in the quick search (⌘K): a question is recognised on its own, “🔎 Ask the question” shows at the top, then the short answer (“Petrus MAZAUD : 2 enfants”). Questions are written in French. You can ask: a person’s relatives (children, grandchildren, brothers and sisters, uncles and aunts, nephews and nieces, cousins, parents-in-law, sons-in-law, godparents…); “mes grands-parents”, “ma grand-mère paternelle”, “mes cousins germains”, “mon lien avec Paul Mazaud” (“me” = the starting person); “qui est Jean Dupont ?” (summary and link to the record); lists with a place, an occupation, dates (“vers 1850”, “années 1850”, “au XIXe siècle”), an OR (“nés à Lille ou à Quimper”, “laboureurs ou tisserands”) or something missing (“sans date de décès”, “qui ne sont pas nées à Lille”, “sans parents connus”, “sans source”); “mort à la guerre”, “les vivants”; calculations (records, averages, most frequent first names). Common mistakes are understood (“enfans”, “Petrus Mazeau”, “à lille”, “St Pierre”) and shown in “I understood”. A word that was not read is flagged: “I did not take into account: …”. For missing records, “where to look”, duplicates or ⚖️ estate files (heirs, overdue deadlines, letters without a reply), the answer sends you to the tool with a button. Settings → 🔎 Search: “Answer questions in the quick search” (yes or no)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can “Ask a question” calculate, not just list?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes (Research → Ask a question in French). Besides lists (“Which ancestors were bakers in Lille before 1850?”), it answers questions that need a calculation: the relationship between two people (“What is the relationship between Jean Dupont and Marie Durand?” → second cousins, cousin once removed, common ancestors, civil degree); records (who lived longest, died youngest, largest family, married youngest or latest, biggest age gap between spouses, oldest ancestor); averages (age at marriage, at death, at first child, children per couple); the most frequent (first names, surnames, occupations, communes, months and seasons); one person (“When was… born”, “Where did… die”, “How old was… at their marriage”, “How old would… be today”); periods (“Who lived through the Great War?”, “Who was 20 in 1914?”, “Who was alive in 1900?”); counts (“How many ancestors do I have?”, by generation). You can add “men”, “women”, “ancestors”, “in the 19th century”… The list of examples is under the box: “And also questions that need a calculation”. The questions are typed in French."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the app recognise faces in photos?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, on your computer only: Lists → Media → “👤 Faces” (this tool brings together the old “Face detection” and “Who is who?”). Step ① Find the faces: choose the photos, then “🔍 Analyse”; the list “Photos with faces to name” opens each photo in the viewer with the faces framed (click on a frame to say who it is). Step ② Who is it?: first identify a few faces by hand, at least once per person; “💡 Suggestions” then says “this is probably Marie Martin” (very likely or likely, with the percentage of likeness): ✓ Yes, Someone else… or Ignore, or accept them all at once for the very likely ones; “👥 Grouped unknowns”: faces that look alike from one photo to another, to name in one go. Each confirmed face becomes an ordinary identification on the photo; “↶ Undo” removes it. The model (≈ 13 MB) is downloaded once, checked by its fingerprint; the photos are not sent anywhere."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I read a ten-year index with the app?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Record indexes (or Research → Archive record index) → “📑 Read a ten-year index”. Five steps: ① the photo of the index (media window), the commune, the period (“1853-1862”) and the kind of records; ② drag the blue lines between the columns (Surname, First names, Date, Kind of record…) or take a known template, then “↧ Find the rows”; ③ the app reads each row on this computer (printed index), or with the handwriting reader if it is installed; otherwise you type it in by hand, with the row’s strip in front of you; ④ you check the grid, with the image alongside (uncertain cells are in yellow, dates without a year take the one from the row before, a date outside the period is flagged); ⑤ the “Ten-year index” record index is created (with “↶ Undo”), then the app suggests the records: 🔗 Link or ＋ Create the record (with the birth or death)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"The reading engine does not load: can I still index my table?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. In “📑 Read a ten-year index”, at step ③, “✍ Type the rows by hand”: a quick grid (Enter or ↓ for the next row), with the strip of each row of the image above. The engine (printed text) is downloaded once with the Internet; the handwriting reader is installed in Settings → Handwriting recognition."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find a photo in the database from another one (reverse search)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Search for a similar image”: in the viewer (button under the photo), in the ⋯ or right-click of a thumbnail in Lists → Media, or at the top of Media. Start from a photo in the database, or drop / paste (⌘V) an image from your computer: it is only used for searching, it does not go into the media. The results are sorted: ＝ identical (the same file), ≈ very close (resized, re-saved, retouched, another format), ⬚ cropped / zoomed (a piece of the photo, framed in blue in the preview), ～ similar (same scene, another shot), with the percentage. “See” puts the two images side by side; “🔍 Compare two photos” opens the comparison tool. Everything is calculated on this computer: the app keeps a “fingerprint” of each photo (calculated in the background, in small batches; if you close, it resumes where it left off; a retouched or rotated photo is recalculated by itself)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find duplicate photos, even retouched or cropped?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Media → “👯 Duplicate images” (it replaces the old “Duplicate search” and also finds duplicate sounds and PDFs). The groups are sorted into Perfect (the same file), Retouched, Cropped / zoomed, Resized and Other format; “Sensitivity” suggests more or less different photos. In each group, the largest is suggested as “★ Kept” (“★ Keep” to choose another), then “👯 Keep ★ and merge”: its links gather those of the others (records, events, labels, albums, collections, investigations, portrait, face areas put back in the right place). The others go “🗑 To the bin” (the file stays on the disk) or “🗂 As versions” of the kept photo. “↶ Undo” puts everything back as before, and the “🗑 Duplicates bin” lets you “↶ Restore” a merge later. “🙈 Not a duplicate” is remembered (“🙈 Not duplicates” to go back on it)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare two photos to see if it is the same person?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Compare two photos”: from the viewer (“🔍 Compare with…”), Lists → Media, a record’s Media block (🔍), or Comparison → Images. The two photos side by side, as a fade (slider) or as a curtain, with the same zoom; “✨ Find the eyes automatically” (face model, on this computer) or “👁 Place the eyes” (two clicks per photo) brings the two faces to the same size; black and white, contrast, notes. The calculated likeness stays cautious: “this is not proof” (age, light, family resemblance…). “💾 Save” keeps the comparison, linked to the two photos and the people: Lists → Editing tools → 🔍 Compare two photos."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does the 👤 Faces tool work?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Media → “👤 Faces”. Two tabs: ① Find the faces (the app spots the faces in the chosen photos and calculates their “fingerprint”) and ② Who is it? (it suggests a name based on faces already identified). Start small: the app picks the lightest batch automatically, often “Photos marked with faces” (the person’s photo, a record’s portrait, faces already identified, “portrait” or “face” label). You can also take the photos shown in the Media list (with your search), those of a person, a label, an album or a folder, a period (“between 1900 and 1950”), the N most recent, or all. “Skip photos already analysed” avoids redoing the work."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Face analysis takes a long time: can I pause or stop it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The analysis runs separately, without freezing the app: you can keep clicking meanwhile. The bar shows how many photos are done, the time per photo and the estimated time remaining. “⏸ Pause” stops after the current photo, “▶ Resume” starts again; “■ Stop” cuts straight away. Progress is kept: even after closing the window, “▶ Resume” starts again where you stopped. An unreadable photo, or one that takes much longer than the others (at least a minute and a half), is skipped and noted, without blocking the others; “↻ Retry the skipped photos” does them again."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"DNA: how do I group my matches and guess where to place a cousin?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"DNA tab → “🔗 Automatic groups”: matches that match each other (shared matches noted or imported, overlapping segments) form clusters, shown in a coloured matrix; each cluster gets its frequent names and places, its side, and the common ancestral couple (certain if a match is already linked to a record, probable from the names in the tree). One click sorts them into groups. “📍 Place a cousin”: for a match (or cM typed by hand), the possible relationships (Shared cM Project) then the couples in your tree they probably descend from, with a percentage and the clues (side, names, places, cluster, age); “Keep” notes it in the match. The test must be linked to the record of the tested person. It is a lead, never a proof."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"On which sites should I look for an ancestor? (🌍 Where to look?)","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, the “🌍 Where to look?” block (or ⋯ → “🌍 Where to look?”, or Research → Type of research → “🌍 Where to look?”) suggests the sites to consult, the most useful first, according to their places (country, department), their period, their life (soldier → Mémoire des hommes and military registers, sailor → maritime register, emigrant → Ellis Island, Castle Garden, notary or inheritance → Minutier central…) and what is missing (unknown birth → the registers of their commune…). Each line says why; “↗ Search” opens the site in your browser with their name already filled in. “✓ Already searched” notes the result (Found, Nothing found, To review) in the Research log; ⋯ → “＋ Make it a lead” adds it to their investigation. “Free only” hides paid sites (the same setting as in Online archives). Any new site added to “Search the Internet” is taken into account automatically."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does “🌍 Where to look?” know sites outside France?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: it now offers about fifty more free sites (Geneabank, FreeREG, Irish censuses, WieWasWie, Geneteka and the archives of Poland and the Baltic states, old newspapers from Belgium, Switzerland, Germany…, war dead of the Commonwealth, Germany, Italy, Canada…), chosen from the person’s countries and years; a register of war dead is never suggested for someone who died outside a war."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a “brick wall” and how do I work on it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An ancestor where you are stuck: one or both of their parents are unknown. Research → Type of research → “🧩 Brick walls” lists them automatically, from the nearest generation to the most distant (Sosa), along with those you marked by hand (record → ⋯ → “🧱 I’m stuck here”, or “＋ Add a wall…”). “Open” shows what has already been searched (Log, letters, leads), the sites to try (🌍 Where to look?), the clues in your database (same name, born 20 to 50 years earlier; godparents and witnesses; household in a census; DNA) and a note. The status (To search, In progress, Solved, Abandoned) changes in one click, with “↶ Undo”; when the parents arrive in the database, the wall turns “Solved” by itself, with a well done. The “🧩 Brick walls” widget shows them on the dashboard, and “🗓 My research week” suggests one per week."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see only newspapers, records or military sites in “🌍 Where to search?”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Above the list, a row of badges per kind of source (📜 Records, 📰 Newspapers, 🎖 Military, 🚢 Emigration…) shows the number of sites: one click keeps only that kind, several = one or the other. The same boxes are in “Filter ▾”, with the country, “Free only” and “In French only”. The choice is remembered for everyone; “Clear all” removes it. On a record, “🎖 All military” opens the filtered page without changing that choice. When nothing is left, the page says why (sites set aside and their reasons)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is this record online at the archives? (🟢 🟡 📮 🔒 ❔ badges)","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a record, next to each birth, baptism, marriage, death and burial: 🟢 online (verified limit for this département), 🟡 maybe, 📮 to request (and from whom: town hall, departmental archives, Central civil registry, ANOM, diocesan archives…), 🔒 restricted until a given year (75 years), ❔ to check. A click explains why, with the official page and its check date. ✓ “I found it online” or ✗ “Not online” corrects the limit for the whole département or just the commune. The app never visits the archives’ website: it opens the page. Timeline and Events list: Settings → 🏛 Online records at the archives."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide the archive badges (🟢 🟡 📮 🔒 ❔)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “🏛 Online records at the archives” → turn off “Badges on records”: they disappear from every record. In the same place, the timeline and the lists have their own setting. Turn it back on to see them again."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I prove that an ancestor really is the son (or daughter) of someone? (⚖️ Proof file)","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open a proof file: on the record, ⋯ → “⚖️ Proof file…” (choose the question: a parent, a couple, a date, a place, “same person as…”), or click the 🛡 badge next to a parent, or right-click on a box or a line of a tree in Charts. In the file: the question, the clues 👍 for and 👎 against (a text, the source or citation chosen in the picker, the kind of clue: direct, indirect or negative, the quality of the source: original, derivative or authored), the searches done (linked to the Research log), the conflicts between sources and how you resolve them, your conclusion and your level of certainty (proved, probable, possible, disproved, to check). Everything is saved by itself; a removed line comes back with “↶ Undo”. 🖨 Print and “📝 In the word processor” turn it into a document. All the files: Lists → Research and organisation → “⚖️ Proof files”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the “proof standard” the proof file talks about?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The method of professional genealogists, in 5 steps: search widely (and note what you did not find), cite the source of each clue, weigh each clue (a record written on the day itself is worth more than a memory), explain the conflicts, write your conclusion. The proof file follows these 5 steps; “💡 How to reason?” recalls them in the window."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the small 🛡 badge (Proved, Probable, Assumed, No source) next to the parents mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is the strength of the parent-child link, calculated by the app without changing anything in your database. In order: the proof file for this link if it has a conclusion; otherwise your choice by hand; otherwise the sources. Proved: a birth, baptism or marriage record of the child is cited and it names the parent (same source on the parent’s record, or their name written in the citation). Probable: a source names the parent elsewhere, or the birth record is cited without showing that it names this parent, or the citation is marked “secondary source”. Assumed: sources about the child, but none about this link (or marked unreliable, or a “doubtful” date). No source: nothing cited. Click the badge to see why, open the file or choose the level by hand (↶ Undo)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the weak links in my tree? (🛡 Strength view, Weak branches)","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In Charts, on the ancestor tree, the descendant tree or the hourglass, switch on “🛡 Strength view”: each line takes the colour and thickness of its level (legend at the top; thick green = proved, blue = probable, yellow dashes = assumed, grey dots = no source, red = disproved). Lists → Research and organisation → “🛡 Weak branches” lists your Sosa ancestors whose link is assumed, without a source or disproved, from the nearest to the most distant, with “⚖️ File” and “🌍 Where to look?”. Also: the “🛡 Weak branches” widget and, each week, a “🛡 Link to strengthen” in “My research week”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see everything I have already searched, so as not to search the same place twice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Research → Type of research → “🗓 Research log” (the same log is in Lists → Archives and sources). Everything that has been searched, from the most recent to the oldest: record indexes, shelfmarks from archive visits done, investigation leads, communes of search sweeps, requests (records to order). Each line says where (service, shelfmark, commune, years), when, and the result (Found, Nothing found, Partly). The top bar searches for a shelfmark, a commune or a name, and filters by kind, result, person, archive service or commune; “List | Cards”, sort, “⤓ Export as CSV”. A click opens the item in its tool."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note the result of a record index and who I did it for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Research → Archive record index → ✎ Edit (or ＋ Add a record index). “Result”: Found (green badge), Nothing found (grey) or Partly (yellow), in addition to the state and the free text. “People searched for”: choose the records of the people for whom you opened this register (several possible) — they stay separate from the people noted in it. The record index then appears in the “🔍 Already searched” block of their record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does “⚠️ Already in your tracking” mean when I type a shelfmark?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"While you fill in a record index, a request (records to order) or an archive visit shelfmark, the app checks whether you have already searched in the same place: the same shelfmark (in the same service), or the same archive service + the same commune + the same type of record over overlapping years. If so, the message says what, with the result and the date (“Nothing found, on 12 March 2025”) and “↗ Open”. You can still save."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"After a visit to the archives, how do I note what I found?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Archive visit planner → on the mission sheet, “✓ Done on…”: give the day of the visit. The sheet moves to the “✓ Visits done” tab (the past stays readable). For each shelfmark, choose the result (Found, Nothing found, Partly, Not consulted); “Edit” adds a note (“Record no. 12, image 34”). “＋ Create the record index” creates the shelfmark’s record index and links it (or links an existing record index with the picker in “Edit”). Each shelfmark goes into the Research log."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I sort my documents by archive service (departmental archives, municipal archives, notaries…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Archives and sources → “🏛 Archive services” → “＋ New service” (you can start from a department’s Departmental Archives: name, acronym and website fill in). On the service’s record: address, opening hours, website, series (“3 E — Notaries”), and “✓ Already consulted there”: all your record indexes, visits done and requests made at this service, sorted by series. In a source’s window and in a media item’s window, “🏛 Archive service” is chosen with the picker, with the series and the shelfmark. If you wrote a service by hand in your research (“AD 19”), the list offers to create its record; its other names (“AD Corrèze”…) are noted on the record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get a ready-made citation of a source, in Evidence Explained or Chicago style or for a forum?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, next to each source (and each source of an event), “❝”: the same source written in four styles — classic French, Evidence Explained, Chicago, Geneanet / forum —, as a note and as a bibliography entry, each with “📋 Copy”. “⭐ My style” puts it first everywhere. “❝” at the top of the “Sources & citations” block gives all the record’s citations in one style: “📋 Copy all”, “⤓ Export as text”, “🖨 Print”. In a page layout, Text → “📚 Cite a source” has a “Style” menu (the note and the bibliography are written in that style); the research report (Settings → Exports) ends with the bibliography in your style."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare two hypotheses (“born in Naves or in Tulle?”) and update the record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, the “⚖️ Hypotheses” block → “＋ New hypothesis”: the question, the event concerned and the kind (Place, Date…). Then “⚖️ Compare”: one column per answer (the record’s value is already there; “＋ Another answer” adds a place, a date or a text). In each column, “👍 For” and “👎 Against”: add a source, a media item or a record index with the picker (or write the evidence), and give it a weight (weak, medium, strong). The column with the highest total (for − against) is “Leading”. “✓ Keep …” changes the place or the date of the event on the record and confirms the hypothesis; “↶ Undo” puts everything back as before. All hypotheses: Settings → Hypotheses."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"On a person’s record, where can I see what has already been searched for them?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The “🔍 Already searched” block: the record indexes in which they are searched for or noted, the shelfmarks of visits done that concern them, the leads of their investigations, their search sweeps and the related requests — where, when, the shelfmark and the result, with “↗ Open”. “🗓 Log” opens the whole log filtered on them. The block can be arranged, set and hidden like the others (Settings → Appearance → record blocks)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is missing from my genealogy, and where should I look for it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Research → Type of research → “🔍 What’s missing?”. For each ancestor in the line (up to 12 generations): the missing birth, marriage, death or parents, but also censuses (1836-1936), the military register (men born from 1847 to 1921), the estate and the marriage contract; with the commune, the window of years, the archive service (Departmental Archives of such-and-such department, according to the place) and the series to consult according to the period: parish registers before 1792, civil registration and ten-year indexes afterwards, notaries (3 E), registration (3 Q), recruitment (1 R), censuses (6 M). Sort by archive service, commune, ancestor or kind of record; “📌 To tracking” adds the search “to do” in the Archive record index, and what is already there is shown. “🖨 Print” makes the list for a visit to the archives."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I extract an ancestor’s signature or create a monogram, a wax seal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → “🔤 Signatures and seals”. ✍️ Choose the image of a record (media or computer), frame the signature by dragging: yellowed paper, stains and the lines of the register disappear (threshold set automatically, to fine-tune), the ink becomes a clean vector line, in its original colour or a chosen ink; the cut-out image (transparent background) can also go into the person’s media. 🔏 For a monogram: interlaced initials (copperplate, round hand, Gothic, Roman capitals, Didot), in a wax seal, an ink stamp, a medallion, a shield, a lozenge or a bookplate, with a legend going around it (“MARTIN FAMILY · TULLE 1832”). Everything opens in the vector drawing to be retouched, printed or exported."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Page layout: how do I see whether text overflows and make it continue in another box?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the bottom of the page layout, an indicator: 🟢 “No errors”, or 🔴 with the number of errors (text overflowing its box, fact contradicted by the records). It updates by itself while you write; a click opens the detailed check with “Go” for each problem. A box whose text overflows has a small red + at the bottom right: click it, then click the text box that should receive the rest (on any page) — the text continues there, as in InDesign. Also: Object → “➜ Thread the text box to another…”, “⤵ Flow the text onto new pages” and “Break the thread”. Esc cancels."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Page layout: is the text of a book checked against the records?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. When a text names a person in the database (“Jean MARTIN, born on 28 August 1923 in Tulle”), the date and place of birth, baptism, death, burial or marriage are compared with their record. A different date counts as an error (🔴), a different place (even without a date: “born in Tulle” while the record says Naves) or a name carried by several records as “to check” (🟠), and a fact missing from the record is pointed out so you can add it. “He” or “she” refer to the person named just before; the “date Birth — place” lines of a record page refer to the name in the title. Nothing is changed automatically: you correct the text or the record. The details are in File → “✅ Check before printing” (or a click on the indicator)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I write text that follows a circle or a curve?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Vector drawing → Object → “➰ Text on a curve…”: circle, arc, wave, the chosen path, or the templates “motto around a seal” and “name on a ribbon”. In Properties → Text: position on the path, offset, side (underneath to write the right way round at the bottom of a circle), spread the letters. The shape is changed with Direct selection (A)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I print a coat of arms in black and white (heraldic hatching)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Coat of arms mode → “🖨 Heraldic hatching”: or = dots, argent = plain, gules = vertical lines, azure = horizontal, vert = diagonals from dexter, sable = cross-hatching, purpure = diagonals from sinister. “Back to colour” cancels; “＋ Legend” adds the table of hatchings."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a gradient with several colours or hatching in a drawing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Properties → Colour → Kind of fill: linear or radial gradient (“＋ Add a colour”, position of each), or Pattern (hatching, dots, grid, cross-hatching, chequerboard)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I find fleurons, frames, corners, drop caps for a drawing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The “Ornaments” tab of the vector drawing: 44 old ornaments with search and categories; click to place in the centre, or drag onto the drawing. The Colour palette recolours them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I align or distribute objects in a drawing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Properties → Position and size: six alignment buttons and two to distribute, relative to the selection or to the artboard (the page, the panel). When moving, enlarging or drawing, pink lines snap to edges and centres; hold ⌘ (or Ctrl) to do without."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a double-sided or folded announcement card, with its envelope?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Announcement window → Folding (double-sided, folded in 2, 3 or 4 panels) and “✉️ Matching envelope” (size chosen automatically or as you like, recipient taken from the people, sender). In the drawing, the pink lines are the folds. ⤓ Export → PDF (one page per side) or “🗞 Double-sided on A4” (sides placed, crop and fold marks)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need to click “Save” in Settings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No: each change is saved straight away. “✓ Saved” appears to the right of the page title, and a message at the bottom left offers “↶ Undo” to go back on the last change. “↺ Reset to default”, always to the right of the title, puts back the original settings (and “↶ Undo” brings yours back)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where have the other buttons at the top of a page gone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top of each page: the main button in blue, two others at most, then “⋯” which opens the rest (Assemble photos, Retouch, Export…), and ⭐ to add it to your shortcuts. The small blue text above the title says where you are (e.g. “Lists · Media and documents”); the number of items is written under the search bar."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see a list as thumbnails?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top of the list, “List | Cards”: in “Cards”, each item has the same thumbnail everywhere (image, name, two useful lines, badges, date), with “Open” and “⋯” for Edit, Rename, Duplicate or Delete. The choice is remembered for each list."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep, export or import a saved setting?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a “💾 Saved settings” frame (click its title to open it): “＋ Save the current settings”, a name, then Enter. Each setting has “Apply” and a ⋯ menu: replace with the current settings, rename, duplicate, ⤓ export (lcnda-reglage-….json file), delete (with “↶ Undo”). On the other computer, “⤒ Import” at the bottom of the frame. A file from another tool is refused with a message; if the name already exists, the app asks for another one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export a drawing, a page layout or a plan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⤓ Export ▾” at the top of the tool always opens the same window: PDF, PNG, JPG, SVG, Print, the tool’s file (to reopen later), with the quality, bleed and crop marks, the file name and a copy in the media."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put a family tree in my book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the page layout: Object → “🌳 Insert a family tree”. Choose the person, the shape (ancestors, descendants, hourglass or fan), the number of generations and the style of the boxes. The tree follows your database: it remakes itself on opening, or with File → “↻ Update the dynamic objects”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a footnote or cite a source in a book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Double-click in a text, place the cursor, then Text → “¹ Note” or “📚 Cite a source” (“AD Corrèze, 3 E 123, f° 12”). The numbers put themselves back in order. Document → Footnotes: at the bottom of the box or at the end of the chapter. Object → “Insert the bibliography” lists the sources cited."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make an index of places or occupations?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the page layout: Object → “📍 Insert an index of places” or “🔨 … of occupations”. The index looks for the places and occupations of your database in the text and notes the pages. You can also mark a word with the “Place to index” or “Occupation to index” style."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make an e-book (ePub)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Page layout → ⤓ Export → “📱 ePub”. The file opens in Books, Kobo or Calibre, with the cover, the table of contents, the images and the notes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get a cousin to proofread my book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose some words or an object, then Review → “💬 Comment” or “✎ Suggest”. To send it: ⤓ Export → “💬 Review”. Your cousin imports the file into 📐 Page layout, comments and sends you theirs back; you do Review → “⤒ Import their comments”. Suggestions are accepted or refused in one click. The “📝 Annotated PDF” can be read on paper."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the red indicator of the page layout check?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Overflowing texts, dates and places that contradict the records, impossible dates (30 February), impossible or wrong ages (“died at 140”), misspelt names (“Jean MARTAIN” → MARTIN?), places unknown to your database and wrong Sosa numbers. Click the indicator to see the details."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does the app write a person’s biography?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, the “📖 Biography” block writes their life in French from what the record contains (“Jean naît le 3 mai 1832 à Tulle, fils de…”), without inventing anything, in the feminine or masculine, with “vers 1784” for an approximate date. Choose the length (Short, Normal, Detailed), the tone (Neutral, Warm) and, in “⚙️ What is included”, the occupations, the witnesses, the censuses and the sources (in brackets or as notes). “✎ Edit” then “💾 Keep my version”: your version is used everywhere (family book, page layout with the {biographie} field). “↻ Regenerate” shows what has changed (green: added, red struck through: removed). “Place in ▾” puts it into a Note, a book or a presentation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I tell “a day in the life” of an ancestor (⏳)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, ⋯ → “⏳ A day in their life…” (or, in the “📖 Biography” block, the “⏳ A day…” button). Choose a day of their life (an event, or the shortcuts Birth, Marriage, Death…) or “✎ Another date” (“7 May 1925”, “12 Thermidor Year II”). The app writes a short story in French using only its own rules, without the Internet: their age, their family that day, their village (village page, recorded population, church, your notes), their occupation (dictionary of old occupations), the day of the week, the saint of the day, the Republican date, the weather of the past if the data is installed, the big events of the year and, only when a reliable figure exists, a price or a wage from the table supplied with the app. Each sentence has its mark: ✓ from the record (with “↗ See the event”), 📚 reference (source cited in the Landmarks), 💭 imagined (in italics); the “No imagined sentences” switch removes them. Then: 📋 Copy, 📝 In the word processor, 🔊 Read aloud, “Place in ▾” (page layout, media, end of their biography, with ↶ Undo). A confidential record does not leave the app; for a living person, the window says so."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What was happening in the world during my ancestor’s life?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, the “🌍 The world around” block puts their life next to the major events in France and the world (wars, epidemics, laws, inventions, regimes, mostly 1500–1960) with their age: “Jean is 38 when the war of 1870 begins”. Local events (department, commune) only appear if they lived there. “＋ Add a local event” for a flood, a fire, a village fête. The whole list and the source of each event: Lists → History → “🌍 The world around”. The Timeline shows them too (“Historical timeline” box)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What did a clog-maker, a day labourer, a weaver do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, click the occupation (Identity → “Occupation / activity”, it has a 🛠️): the occupation’s page opens — what they did, their tools, the old words, the ancestors who practised it (list, map, timeline) and the images in your database about it. “✎ Complete the page”, “＋ Add images” or “📒 Make it a Note” to enrich it. The complete dictionary (more than 500 occupations with their spelling variants): Lists → People and families → “🛠️ Old occupations”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see my ancestors’ occupations in a tree (🧬 Occupation tree)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → “Occupation tree” (or, on a record, ⋯ → “🧬 Occupation tree”; or ⌘K “occupations”). Choose the person in the “Person” box, then “⬆ Ancestors” or “⬇ Descendants” and the number of generations. Each box shows their occupations (record, “Occupation” events, censuses), with a colour per broad sector: agriculture, crafts, industry, trade, transport, services, army, clergy, liberal professions… (the classification comes from the dictionary of old occupations). A thick coloured line and “＝ same occupation”: the child took over their parent’s occupation; “⇄ other occupation”: they changed; dotted lines: one of the two has no occupation recorded. At the top: filter by sector (badges with the number) or search for an occupation; “🔒 Without living people or confidential records” to show or print. At the bottom: the legend and “Passing on the occupation” — the share of sons who took over their father’s occupation (and of daughters their mother’s), by century, in the tree and in your whole database. A click on a box opens the record. “⤓ Export”: PDF, PNG, JPG, SVG or 🖨 Print."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see all the places of a person’s life on an animated map (🗺 Life map)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record: ⋯ → “🗺 Map of their life”; or Map → left column → “🗺 Life map” (in Route mode: “▶ Animated life map”); or ⌘K “life map”. Their events (birth, baptism, marriages, residences, occupations, death…), their censuses, their journeys, their placements, their military service and their schools (🎓 block) become numbered stages, in date order, on a map background supplied with the app (no Internet needed). ▶ starts playback (⏸ to stop, speed 🐢 / Normal / 🐇), ◀ ▶ move from one stage to the next, ⤢ shows the whole life; the timeline at the bottom and the list on the right are clickable. Places without a position are listed separately (open the place and give its position so that it comes onto the map). “👪 With the family” adds the spouse and the children (in pink and green). “🎬 Export as video” makes an MP4 or WebM video of the stages, with a copy in the media if you like; living people and confidential records are never in it. “📊 Keep as a diagram” stores it in My diagrams (the “Storytelling map”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I tell a life story as a slideshow?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, click ✨ (or Presentation and slideshow → “✨ Life story”). Choose the person, the length, the style, the photos and music if you like. The slideshow prepares itself from the record: birth on a map, baptism, marriage, children, occupations, censuses, moves, events of the time, death, grave, descendants. Missing information does not produce a slide. Everything can be changed afterwards."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are a slide’s notes?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Reminders for you, for example the sources of the story. They are not visible during playback and become the speaker’s notes in a presentation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export a slideshow, a sound or a video edit?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⤓ Export ▾” at the top: a single window for all formats (video, images, PDF, MP3, WAV…). Tick “Also keep a copy in the media” to keep it in the app."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I drag a file into a tool?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Drag (or paste with ⌘V) a photo or a sound onto the slideshow, the presentation, the video edit, the sound waveform, the image of a record, the drawing or the page layout: it goes into the media and is placed in the tool."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export a table, a diagram or a chart?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click “⤓ Export”: a single window offers PDF, PNG or JPG image, SVG (diagrams), Print, or the tool’s file. Choose the quality and the file name, and tick “Also keep a copy in the media”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why can’t I make a PNG image of a chart?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The browser (Chrome, Edge, the app) refuses to turn an app chart into an image. Choose “PDF” in the export window: the file is sharp, to print or to send."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I drag files instead of choosing them?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: in “Add media”, on a table, an investigation, the photos of a collection, the favourite image on the dashboard, the file areas of records and in the tools (drawing, page layout, retouching, slideshow, video editing). The area turns blue on hover; you can also paste (⌘V). A .csv file dragged onto a collection’s spreadsheet fills the cells."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose a font?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The “Aa All fonts…” button opens a single list: search, recent fonts at the top, fonts from the app and from the computer, each written in its own font."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I sort a list?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top of the list: “N items · Sort:” and the menu (newest, oldest, A → Z…). At the bottom, “See the next N ↓” loads the rest."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own theme (colour design)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → “✎ My themes” → “＋ New theme…” (or “Duplicate” on an existing theme). Choose while looking at the preview: the main colour (buttons, links), the background of the page, of panels and of cards, the text, the colour of the lines around frames and their thickness (from 0 to 8 px; 0 = no lines). Tick “Dark theme” for a dark background: the app switches to dark mode. If the text is hard to read on a background, a yellow message says so. Save: it is added to the “Preset” list, in “✎ My themes”; choose it then “Apply”. “⚙️ Manage the presets” arranges, shows or hides themes, and for yours: edit, rename, duplicate, export, delete (with “↶ Undo”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why “⚠️ On this background, this colour is hard to read” under “Main colour”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The main colour is also used to write links, small badges and texts. With some backgrounds (especially a dark theme and a dark colour, such as blue #0066cc on “Dark”), it is hard to read: the app measures the difference in brightness (the contrast, as in “✎ My themes”) and warns when it is below 4.5. It is only a warning: your colour stays chosen. “Use the theme’s colour” puts back the one suggested by the Preset (saved straight away, “↶ Undo” to go back). The warning goes away by itself as soon as the colour or the Preset reads well. In Charts → Statistics, in dark mode, figures written in a statistic’s colour are lightened to stay readable; the bars keep your colours."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the thickness or colour of the lines around frames?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"With a theme of your own: Settings → Appearance → “✎ My themes” → “＋ New theme…” (or “Edit”). “Lines around (borders)” gives their colour, separate from the text colour, and the “Thickness of the lines around” slider their thickness, from 0 to 12 px (2 px at first, double-click to go back to it). The app’s themes keep their 2 px black lines."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add my own avatar shape?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Portraits and avatars” → “1 · The shape” → “＋ Create a shape”. Three ways: “📚 Library” (the shapes of the tables: hearts, coats of arms, stars, trees… with search and families), “✏️ Draw a polygon” (click to place the points, drag them, double-click to remove one; triangle, star, shield templates…), or “⤒ Import an SVG” (drag or paste the file: only its filled paths are kept, never a script). Give it a name and “＋ Create the shape”: it is chosen straight away, and stays in the row of shapes (with ✎)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I have the same portrait (circle or square of initials) everywhere?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Portraits and avatars”. “1 · The shape”: one shape for everything (circle, rounded square, square, tilted square, diamond… and yours), which applies to portraits and to the small numbered squares of the blocks; it is saved as soon as you click. “2 · The portrait”: “Start from” As before (at first, each screen keeps its own drawing), Single circle (the same circle everywhere, in the colour of the gender) or Record square (black border, photo in black and white). Below: rounding, size of initials, border, colours for man / woman / unknown / animal, the record’s photo, black and white, one or two letters, Sosa badge; the preview shows the record, a list, the picker, the tree and the badges. “↺ Reset to default” goes back to “As before”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the left column (style of entries, numbers, tabs, width, on the right)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Menus, column and lists” → “Column” tab. “Where?”: left or right. “The column’s design”: “Start from” As before (at first) or New (consecutive numbers, the same tabs everywhere, “List | Cards” above the list), then each choice as a picture: style of entries (framed buttons by default, continuous list, airy list, buttons without shadow, side bar, rounded badges, binder tabs, cards with a marker, soft background), top tabs, numbers (consecutive, original letters or none), size, “List | Cards” and width (240 to 420 px). The preview follows each click. It applies to all the columns (People, Charts, Map, Investigations, Research, Comparison…)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the shape of the app’s elements (rounded corners, thin lines, plain, compact…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There is no longer a “Designs to choose from” page: each shape is stored where it is used. The style of records (lines, shadows, corners: As before, Soft, Old paper, Plain) in Settings → “Person and animal records”, with the shape of the header: it only changes the records of people and animals; the lines, corners and shadows of the left column in “Menus, column and lists” → Column; the page background in “Theme and colours” (🧩 Page background); Movements in Settings → “♿ Accessibility” → “🎞 Movement” tab; Density, Icons and Lists in Settings → “Menus, column and lists” (one tab each); Buttons at the end of “Notifications and buttons”; the header of records in “Person and animal records” → “Shape of the header”; the boxes of trees in Charts → “🎨 Colours of statistics and trees”. For each: click the large card you prefer (👍 / 👎 to help you), then fine-tune in “More settings”; the preview shows real screens of the app with your data. It is saved straight away (“✓ Saved”), with “↶ Undo”. The font, the text size and the colours are set in “Theme and colours”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where have some settings gone (order of lists, of charts, widget library, language, DNA)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"They are now where you use them. The order of the lists: Lists → “Reorder” (a window). The order of diagrams and statistics: Charts → “⠿ Reorder the charts” (under the colours). The widget library: Dashboard → “🧩 Widget library”; the widgets in place: “Reorder the widgets” and the ✎ of each widget. The language, the default calendar and the player: Settings → “Theme and colours”, after “Displayed name”. The DNA display: DNA → ⚙️ Settings. The order of the Settings pages: “Reorder” at the bottom of the Settings column."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I set the tree boxes (white, male / female colours, with photo, mini)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In Charts → “🎨 Colours of stats and trees” (at the bottom of the left column), “🌳 Tree boxes” section: choose a card (the preview shows a real tree with your data), then the width of the boxes, the lines, the dates and the Sosa badge. Just below, “Colours by sex” brings everything together: the colour of each sex (bands and pale background of the “Male / female colours” boxes, and round portraits) and the background and text of the “White” boxes. Everything is kept with “Save”. These boxes used to be in Designs to choose from: your choices are carried over as they were."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where have the “Ready-made sets”, the font and the dashboard of the designs to choose from gone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"They have been removed: they duplicated Appearance and sometimes stopped it working (for example, with “Old archives”, changing the font or the Preset no longer did anything). The font and text size are chosen in Appearance (“Font”, “Text size”); for an archive atmosphere, take the “Paper archive” Preset in Appearance and, if you like, “Old paper” in Designs to choose from → A1. The dashboard stays as before (widths 1, ½, ⅓…). Your old choices are cleaned up by themselves: the rest of your designs is kept (Movements becomes A7, Lists A8)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why are some settings switches and others checkboxes?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A switch (the little button that slides) changes what you see straight away: it is a setting, kept by itself. A checkbox is for choosing items in a list, or an option that only counts when you click the button (“Save”, “Create”, “⤓ Export”…)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I write a time or a duration?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As you would say it: “14 h 30”, “14:30”, “9h05” or “noon” for a time; “2 h 15”, “1 min 30”, “90 s” or “1:30” for a duration. Just below, the app writes what it understood (“✓ Understood: 1 min 30 s”) or warns you (“⚠️ Impossible time”). For numbers, the unit (px, mm, cm, %, s…) is written inside the box, on the right."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do the designs to choose from change my Accessibility or Appearance settings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The first frame of Appearance (Preset, Main colour, Font, Text size, Tab menu…) always comes first: a design only changes the shape (lines, shadows, corners, density, icons, record header, tree boxes, buttons, lists, movements), never the font, the text size or the colours. The colour of the record lines follows the colours of your Preset; a theme of your own that has its own lines (✎ My themes) keeps them. Accessibility also keeps priority: if “Reduce animations” is ticked, nothing moves, whatever the movements. The male / female colours of the tree boxes are the same as those of the portraits (Appearance → 👤 Portraits): changing them in one place changes them in the other."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange or hide the shapes offered?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Portraits and avatars” → “1 · The shape” → “⚙️ Manage the shapes”: untick a shape to hide it from the row, drag ⠿ (or ↑ ↓) to arrange it, then “Save the order and the shapes shown”. Your own shapes can be edited, duplicated, exported and deleted (with “↶ Undo”). “↺ Reset to default” puts back the original order and shows all the shapes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide the big frame at the top of a record (for a slideshow)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Person and animal records” → “Record header” → Frame: “Hide the whole frame” (kept straight away). Records then start with the parents, spouses and children, then the blocks. To put it back: same place, “Show the frame”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide or arrange the buttons at the top right (split screen, new window, 🔒, 🧓, 🧮, My genealogies)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → “Buttons at the top right”: untick a button to hide it, drag its handle ⠿ (or Alt + ↑ / ↓) to change the order; it is kept straight away (“↶ Undo” at the bottom left), and the preview shows the top bar. “↺ Reset to default” puts back the app’s order, with all the buttons except 🧓 (hidden at first). The 🧮 and 🧓 buttons also follow their own switches (calculator settings; Settings → Accessibility → 🧓 Simple mode): it is the same setting. Kept with the 💾 Saved settings of Appearance and the site defaults."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange the square buttons at the top of a record, or put them in 2 columns?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Person and animal records” → “Record header”: drag the buttons (✨ life story, 👁＋ new observation (animal records), 🔖 bookmark, 🗑 delete, 🔓 lock, 🕘 history, 🧩 This person was…, hidden at first: tick it to show it) or use ↑ ↓, untick those to hide, choose “In 2 columns” if you like: it is kept straight away (“✓ Saved”, “↶ Undo”). The preview shows the result. It is kept in the “💾 Saved settings”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I put the “🧩 This person was…” link at the top of the record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Person and animal records” → “Record header” → “🧩 This person was…” link: a square 🧩 button in the column of buttons, or at the bottom of the column of badges (under To check, Outside the genealogy, Artist). It is hidden at first: tick 🧩 in the list of buttons to show it (untick it to hide it again). A click opens the person’s profiles page (or the choice of their profiles if they have none yet). Not on animal records."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a birthday to my calendar (phone, Google, Apple, Outlook)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On an event of the record, click 📅: every year or just once, with a reminder the day before. For the whole family: Lists → Family calendar → “⤓ Export the birthdays (.ics)” (direct ancestors, the whole database, a branch, living only). Open the file to add it; importing it again creates no duplicate. Approximate dates or dates reduced to the year cannot go into a calendar."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I restore an old photo in one go?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Retouch → 🧭 Restore tab (or Image) → “✨ Restore in one go”. Tick the steps (denoising, dust, creases, contrast, sharpness, colourisation) while looking at their previews, set the strength, then “Apply”. Each step becomes a “✨” layer that you can hide or adjust; ↶ undoes everything at once."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I repair a crease or a tear on a photo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🪡 Repair tool (R key): paint over the crease and it is rebuilt from what surrounds it. “🔍 Find the creases” finds the fine lines and shows them to you in red before filling them in (to check: it can also mark real lines)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare before and after a retouch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Under the photo, “◧ Curtain”: drag the line, ⇅ changes the direction. ⤓ Export → Before / after saves a side-by-side or curtain image, with a caption."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make portraits in the same format?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Retouch → Image → “🪪 Crop as an ID photo”: the eyes are made horizontal, at the same height, in 3:4; “Use as portrait” sends it to the record. For several records: Editing tools → Retouch → “🪪 ID portraits”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see my projects as large thumbnails?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools: next to “Sort”, choose “Cards” (one card per project) or “List” (the table). The choice is kept for each tool."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see a list as cards rather than rows?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Above each list, just before “Sort”, click “List” (one row per item: small image, name, details, date) or “Cards” (one card per item with a large image). Each list remembers your choice and your sorting: Boards can stay as cards and People as a list."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I sort the cards like the table?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: in “Cards” mode, the “Sort” menu arranges the cards like the columns of the table (by name, by date…)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the big buttons of simple mode?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Accessibility → “🧓 Simple mode” tab → 06 “What is shown” → “The big buttons”. For each tile: ⠿ or ↑ ↓ to arrange it, the switch to show or hide it, ✎ to change its name, its short sentence, its icon and its colour (↺ resets the tile to default). “＋ Add a tile”: ⭐ A person (opens their record in sentences), 📸 An album (its photos as a slideshow) or 💌 A note from the family (a text in large print, read aloud with 🔊); ⧉ to make another one the same, × to remove it. Just below, “Layout”: how many per row (1 to 4), height, spacing, title size and font of the tiles. Everything is kept in the “💾 Saved settings” of simple mode."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I show the tree to an elderly person (simple mode)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🧓 Simple mode”: very big buttons (🌳 The tree, 📷 The photos, 👥 The people, 🎂 On this day), sentences read aloud and nothing that can be changed or deleted. Open it with the “🧓 Simple mode” button at the top of the app (hidden at first: tick “Show the button…” at the very top of the “🧓 Simple mode” tab), the dashboard widget or Settings → Accessibility → “🧓 Simple mode” tab → “▶ Open simple mode”. The first time, the app first shows you its settings. So that a tablet always opens on it: “Open the app directly in simple mode” (on this device), or the address …/#mode-simple."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I leave simple mode?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the home screen only: keep your finger pressed on “🔒 Leave simple mode” (3 seconds at first, adjustable), or hold the Enter key down. If a 4-digit code has been chosen, the app then asks for it. The code is kept hidden (hashed), never in plain text; it is not real protection, it only avoids leaving by mistake. Esc only does “Back”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I set up simple mode (who is watching, colours, size, voice)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Accessibility → “🧓 Simple mode” tab. Eight sections with their “Why?”: who is watching (to write “your mother”, “your great-grandson”), colours (the contrast is checked live, “Fix” raises it to 7:1), sizes, font (Atkinson Hyperlegible, Luciole, OpenDyslexic…), voice, what is shown, slideshow and photo frame, exit. The preview on the right shows the tablet. Each change is kept straight away (“↶ Undo” at the bottom left); “Ready-made sets” applies everything at once, “💾 Saved settings” keeps, exports and imports your settings. Your “♿ Whole app” settings also apply here, unless you choose “Specific to simple mode”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What can you see in simple mode?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The tree with the person in the middle, their spouse, their parents and their children, the relationship written in plain words (“your grandmother”, “the husband of your granddaughter Émilie”); the photos in large size with “🔊 Listen” and “👥 Who is there?”; a simple record whose story is written in sentences (birth, baptism, marriage, occupation, death); the people by family, by first name or by searching (big keyboard, 🎤 dictation); “On this day”: birthdays, marriages, photos of the month and what is coming soon. Living people can be shown, reduced to their first name or hidden; confidential records are never shown; removed photos and records are not shown (without being deleted)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I simplify the screen (“one thing at a time” mode)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Accessibility → “🧩 One thing at a time”. The app hides the rarely used tools (DNA, comparison, split screen, dashboard settings, deleting or locking a record…) and enlarges the main buttons. Nothing is deleted: the “Simple mode — Show everything” banner, at the bottom right, brings everything back in one click."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I have a screen reader read the description of images?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Accessibility → “Describe the images”. Each photo in your media is read with its description, otherwise its title, its date, its place and the people linked to it. To write it yourself, open a media item: “Image description (read aloud)” field."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I always show the subtitles of videos and montages?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Accessibility → “Subtitles always shown”. Videos that have subtitles show them automatically; in the montage, the “Subtitles” track is visible during playback even if it is hidden. The exported video does not change."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I fill in a field by speaking (voice dictation)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Accessibility → “Voice dictation”. A 🎤 appears in the field you are writing in: click it (or Ctrl + Shift + Space), speak, and the text is written at the cursor. Say “comma”, “full stop”, “new line”… for punctuation, Esc to stop. If your computer does not offer dictation here: on a Mac, the system dictation (🎤 key or Fn twice); on Windows, Windows + H."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which font should I choose to read more easily (dyslexia)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Accessibility → Text → Font. “Very legible” (Atkinson Hyperlegible) and “OpenDyslexic” are stored in the app: nothing to install, and they work without the Internet. “Comic Sans” is your computer’s font: if it is missing, the app uses OpenDyslexic instead."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I ask a question with a precise date?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: “Who was born on 7 May 1950?”, “born in May 1950”, “born on 15 Brumaire Year II”, “born in Year II”, “died aged 70”, “died before age 5”. If the date does not exist, the app tells you instead of listing everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does the question in plain language know who I am talking about?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“How many ancestors does Jean Martin have?” counts those of Jean Martin; without a name, it is the starting person, and the app says so. A first name alone is enough if it is unique; otherwise the app shows you the records to choose from."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I ask for cousins, half-brothers or inconsistent dates?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“The first cousins of Louis Martin”, “the half-sisters of…”; “brothers and sisters” separates those who have the same parents from the half-siblings. “Who died before being born?” or “inconsistent dates” lists the dates to check. Animals are not counted among the people; ask “who are the animals”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does “pedigree collapse” mean in the Sosa list?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An ancestor who appears at several Sosa numbers (for example cousins who married each other): this is normal, it is not a duplicate to merge."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the Date box accept 29 February 1500?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Before 1583, the Julian calendar was used, in which every year divisible by 4 has a 29 February. Years before 1000 are understood, but the app does not sort them yet (yellow warning)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens when I remove a parent?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The other parent remains the child’s parent. If you put the parent back afterwards, the child returns to the couple."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How are half-brothers and half-sisters arranged?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If someone had several spouses, a child added is no longer automatically put with one of them: add the 2nd parent and the child joins the right couple. A child never has three parents."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why can’t I remove a spouse?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A couple who have a marriage, sources, notes, media or children together cannot be separated with one click: the message says what to remove first."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the lock on a record block?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A locked record no longer changes at all: events, sources, photos, labels, name variants, hypotheses, family links, notes. Unlock it to edit it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why is the merge refused?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You cannot merge two spouses, nor an ancestor with their descendant: they are not the same person."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need to type accents to search?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No: “helene” finds Hélène, “jean-baptiste” finds Jean Baptiste. The quick search also looks in the other names of a record (variants)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does Lists → Places show fewer places?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The list only shows the places of your genealogy (and those you created), not the 80,000 reference communes. Lists → People does not show animals, and a marriage shows both spouses."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does a GEDCOM export keep?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Labels, animals, note titles and dates in the Republican or Julian calendar are kept, and the import reads them back. An impossible link (someone who would be their own ancestor) is ignored on import and reported."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How are Republican and very old dates sorted?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A Republican date (“15 Brumaire Year II”) gets its Gregorian equivalent and its year, for sorting and ages. Years before 1000 are understood (“about 845”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the button show “Saving…”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"While saving, the button waits: a double-click or a repeated Enter no longer creates a duplicate. It goes back to normal as soon as it is finished, even if there is an error."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the Enter key do in a window?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a one-line box, Enter saves the window (like the blue button); it no longer closes it and loses what was typed. In a large text, Enter goes to a new line."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does “changed in another window” mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If the same record, the same board or the same layout is open in two tabs, LCNDA no longer silently replaces what the other one saved: reload to see the other version, or choose “Save anyway” / “Keep my version”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What if LCNDA stops responding?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The message “No connection with LCNDA” says so, what you typed stays in the window and “Try again” restarts the saving. If you close or reload the tab with unsaved input, the browser asks first."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does Esc work?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Esc closes windows and tools (drawing, house…) when nothing is in progress. If there are changes, it asks first, and pressing several times no longer makes you lose anything. In a search box, Esc empties it first."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does ⌘Z (Ctrl+Z) do outside the editors?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It immediately undoes the last record created (if nothing has been added to it), a lock or a family link, with “↷ Redo”. Otherwise it explains how to undo by hand."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens to the witnesses and godparents of a deleted record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Deleting a record or an event also removes its witnesses and godparents; restoring puts them back."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I reopen an exported slideshow?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A file exported with “⤓ Export → 💾 File” is reopened with “⤒ Import a slideshow” in the list of slideshows."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I zoom on a phone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the house, the drawing and the presentation, two fingers zoom the plan or the slide, not the page."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I get back a widget removed from the dashboard?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: “↶ Undo” in the message at the bottom left. An empty dashboard offers “＋ Add a widget”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the bar above the lists for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"All lists now have the same bar, always in this order: 🔍 Search (“/” key to go there, Esc or × to empty it), “Filter ▾”, “List | Cards” and “Sort”. In “Filter ▾”, each section shows its choices with their number (Kind, State, Place 📍, Person 👤, Label 🏷, Period…); the chosen filters appear under the bar as blue badges (“Women ×”): a click on a badge removes it, “Clear all” removes them all. “Sort” always starts with “Newest, Oldest, A → Z, Z → A”, then the list’s own sorts; clicking a column heading also sorts. Each list remembers your settings, even after closing the app. A list that is still empty shows no bar, only the button to get started. The Treasures (Letters, Candles, Shares, Places passport, Missing records, Gazettes) also have the bar: “Lit” or “In progress” is the starting choice, as a badge (× to see everything); a sealed letter is never searched in its text. The two calendars (My diary, Family calendar) only have 🔍 and “Filter ▾” (Kind, Person, Place): the search works in all views. In “My lists”: “List | Cards” and “More ▾” for the other layouts (by group, draggable columns, timeline, gallery), “Filter ▾” has a section per column (the ▾ of a column heading opens it) and these filters are stored in the list; “⤓ Export ▾” and “🖨 Print” are at the top, Edit, Duplicate and Delete in “⋯”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why doesn’t the app ask me “Are you sure?” before deleting any more?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For a note, an event, a row, an item in a tool…, the deletion happens straight away and a message at the bottom left offers “↶ Undo” for 6 seconds (hovering over it pauses the time). After Undo, “↷ Redo” does the deletion again. The question remains only for what cannot be recovered: a whole record, a database, emptying the bin."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Are the CSV files I download safe?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: a text beginning with =, +, - or @ is preceded by an apostrophe, so that Excel, Numbers or LibreOffice do not take it for a hidden formula."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I visit my house as if I were there?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"House → 3D view → “🚶 Visit”. Walk with W A S D or the arrows, Shift to run. Click the view to look around with the mouse, Esc to release it, Esc again to leave. You go through doors, not through walls. On a staircase: E to go up, C to go down (or the ⬆ ⬇ buttons). The small map shows where you are; a click on it takes you there. The eye height can be set (1.60 m, or 1 m to see like a child). On a tablet, walk with the circle at the bottom left."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the sunlight in my house?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"3D view → “☀️ Light”. Choose the time and the day (Winter, Summer…), or “▶ See the day”. The sun is calculated for the location of the house (otherwise the centre of France). Set “North of the plan” if the top of your plan does not face north. At night, the lamps, chandeliers and candles placed light up. Also in the House tab → “☀️ Orientation and light”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I trace my house over the Napoleonic cadastre?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⤒ Import → “📐 An old plan, a cadastre” (or Floor tab → Old plan): choose the image in the media or import it. Click both ends of a known length and give its real length (metres, toises, French feet…). “↻ Straighten” makes a wall straight in two clicks. Set the transparency, lock, then “✏️ Trace the walls” and draw over it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I link a memory to a piece of furniture or a room?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click the piece of furniture or the room, then “🔗 Link a memory”: a photo or a document, a story, a person, or a family memory. A ✦ appears on the plan and in 3D; a click on it (even during the visit) shows the memory. House tab → “🏷 The memories of the house” to see them all."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the wallpaper, the stone or the colour of the walls?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click a wall. In “Colour and texture”, each side has its covering (render, cut stone, brick, half-timbering, panelling, wallpaper, toile de Jouy, tiles, limewash…) and its colour. “Pattern size” enlarges the bricks or the paper. “Same for: the room / the whole floor / the whole house” copies the choice. In a room, “🧱 Room walls” changes all its walls at once. Floor tab: plain or beamed ceiling."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep or share my house settings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🧲 Snap → “💾 Saved settings”: “＋ Save the current settings” (snapping, grid, wall thickness, eye height, light), then ⋯ → “⤓ Export” for a .json file and “⤒ Import” on another computer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export my house?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⤓ Export”: the floor plan as PNG, JPG, PDF or SVG, printing, an image of the 3D view, or the house file (.json) to import again with ⤒ Import. “Also keep a copy in the media” keeps the image in your media."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does colour blindness correction also apply in the editors?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: Settings → Accessibility → “Colours easy to tell apart” corrects the whole screen, including the 3D house, drawing, layout, retouching, tables, the map and full-screen windows. Your own colours are not replaced: the correction makes them distinguishable."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make the app easier to read or use (colour blindness, large text, voice)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Accessibility. Colours: choose your type of colour blindness (protanopia, deuteranopia…) — the app corrects its colours, with colours that are easy to tell apart and shapes (✓ ⚠ ⛔, hatching) in addition; “See as…” shows the screen as a colour-blind person sees it, to check an announcement or a chart. Text: size, line spacing, spacing, very legible font or one for dyslexia, underlined links. Also: high contrast, visible keyboard outline, big buttons, big pointer, fewer animations, messages shown for longer, and reading aloud (select a text then 🔊, or “Read the record”; Esc stops). “↺ Reset to default” puts everything back as at the start (“↶ Undo” in the message to go back)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose a person, a place or something else in a tool?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everywhere, the same box: type 2 letters (surname, first name, town…). For each person the list shows their initials or photo (blue male, pink female, orange 🐾 animal), their dates and places, and their Sosa number; for a place, first those in your genealogy (blue marker, number of events), then the communes of France and the cities of the world (white marker) — the list scrolls without limit, and you can narrow it down by typing the department, its number or the country (“saint-martin 32”, “springfield usa”). ↑ ↓ to choose, Enter to confirm, Esc to close; “＋ Create the record” if it does not exist. Once chosen, the person becomes a badge with “↗ Record” and × to change. You can also type “sosa 12” or “#45” (record number)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose an image from my media in a tool?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everywhere (announcements, layout, retouching, slideshow, presentation, montage, boards, museum, seals, dashboard…), the same window opens: your media as thumbnails. Type a title, a reference, a name, a place or a year; the buttons at the top filter (Photos, Documents, Sounds, Videos, the tool’s person, 🕘 Recent, ⭐ Portraits) and show how many there are. Click an image to see it large on the right (with its size and the format it prints well in), double-click or press Enter to take it. When you can take several, tick them (click or Space) then “Use these images”. An image from the computer? Drag it into the window, paste it (⌘V) or “📁 Import…”: it goes into your media and is ticked. The thumbnail size (large, small, list) is remembered."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Vector drawing: how do I cut out a group or a layer with a shape (mask)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Place the cutting shape (circle, oval, heart, outline of a commune, escutcheon…) at the very top of the group or layer, then, in the Layers panel, click ✂️ on the line of the group or layer: you only see what is inside the shape (portrait in a medallion, map cut to the outline of a commune, motif in a coat of arms). Nothing is erased: a 2nd click on ✂️ removes the mask and the shape goes back to the very top. The coloured ✂️ shows what is masked. For a few objects without a group: select them with the shape on top, then Object → “Clipping mask” (⌘7)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Announcements: more formats, and adding, removing or hiding fields?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the announcement window, “Format” offers cards (10 × 15 to 16 × 22 cm), squares (12 to 21 cm), long formats (DL, menu, bookmark, banner), posters (A4 to A1, 40 × 60, 50 × 70, 60 × 80 cm), screen formats (social networks, story, email, 16:9) and “Custom” where you type the width and height in cm. Each field has a checkbox (unticked: the field stays in the window but is not printed), ↑ ↓ to arrange it and 🗑 to remove it. “＋ Add a field” creates a new one, with its size (small, normal, large, calligraphic title), italic and bold. “Put back a removed field” and “↺ Reset to default” go back to the template. The chosen fields and format are remembered for next time."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I organise a family reunion?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Research and organisation → “🎉 Family reunions” → “＋ New family reunion” (or ⋯ on the ancestor’s record → “🎉 Organise a family reunion…”, or Lists → Editing tools). Choose the ancestor, or the couple: the app finds all their living descendants (and their spouses, if you tick it), arranged by branch, with their relationship to the ancestor (“grandson of…”) and to a reference person, usually you (“second cousin of…”). A person with no death recorded, born less than 100 years ago, is marked “Living?”: check. Confidential records are never shown."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I put the addresses and replies of the family reunion guests?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“👥 Guests” tab: the ✓ box says who is invited; the address, email and phone come from the directory of the living or from the last address on the record. You change them here without touching the record (unless you tick “also in the directory of the living”). The reply (yes, no, maybe), the number of people and the diet are noted on each line. “⤒ Import a list…” reads a table (CSV, Excel .xlsx, LibreOffice .ods) with a line of headings (First name, Surname, Email, Reply…); “↶ Undo” undoes it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I send the invitations for the family reunion?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“✉️ Invitations” tab: write the text once ({foyer}, {date}, {lieu}… are replaced by themselves); one invitation per household (the descendant, their spouse and their minor children). “🖨 Print the invitations…” (one page per household, the address for a window envelope, “⤓ PDF”), “📝 In the word processor”, “📰 Announcement (drawing)”, “📁 Copy in the media”. “✉️ Email ready” opens your email program with the text: nothing is sent by the app."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make the badges, the big tree and the seating plan for the family reunion?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🏷 Badges”: an A4 sheet (6, 8 or 10 badges), the first name in large print, the branch in colour, the relationship, the small photo and a QR code if you like. “🌳 Big tree”: all the descendants in large format (A2, A1…), one colour per branch, boxes to stick the photos of the day; it opens in the drawing tool to export it as PDF for the printer (bleed, crop marks). “📅 D-day”: the attendance list, the seating plan by branch (a household is never separated, ↶ Undo) and the photo album. “📊 Figures”: guests and replies by branch, and the colour of each branch."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do the family reunion addresses leave the app?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No: they stay in the family reunion project. No export (GEDCOM, website, simple mode, view-only mode) reads them. The only file that takes them out is “⤓ Export ▾ → Guest table (CSV)”, and the addresses, emails and phone numbers are only in it if you tick the box."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make an announcement, an invitation or a poster for a family reunion?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → “📰 Announcements and posters” (or Charts). Choose the kind (birth, baptism, marriage, death, family reunion, golden wedding, old newspaper article, invitation), a style (Belle Époque, Art Nouveau, Art Deco, 1950s, classic, modern, mourning) and a format; choose a person from the tree: names, dates, places, ages and family fill in by themselves. The result opens in the vector tool: each text, ornament and the photo can be edited, then you print (or PDF) and export (PNG, JPG, SVG)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a slideshow or the story in pictures of an ancestor?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → “🎞️ Presentation and slideshow”. “✨ A person’s story” builds the slideshow of a life by itself: a title, their photos, a map where the route of the places of their life is drawn, their biography. You then add photos (with a slow camera movement), titles, route maps (a migration: places to add, or those of a person’s life), your voice on each slide (🎙), music that lowers when the voice speaks; you play it full screen and export it as a video (Media or download)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a presentation as with Keynote or PowerPoint?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → “🎞️ Presentation and slideshow” → “＋ New” → “🎤 Presentation (Keynote style)”, then choose a theme and a format (16:9, 4:3, square, vertical, A4, custom). On the left the slides (drag them to arrange them; ⧉ duplicate, 👁 skip during playback, § start a section that folds up); “＋ Slide ▾” offers the layouts: title, title and text, title and photo, full-page photo, photo and text, two columns, quote, section, key figure, a person. At the top: insert a text, a shape (rectangle, ellipse, star, arrow, bubble, line…), an image, a table, a chart, the record, the portrait, the small tree or the timeline of the life of a person from the database, a video or a sound. An object can be moved, enlarged (⇧: proportions, ⌥: from the centre), rotated (top handle) and aligned with the others thanks to the guides; ⌥ while dragging copies it. On the right, the inspector: Format (font, size, bold, lists, colours, border, shadow, cropping and masking of photos…), Animate, Slide (colour, gradient or image background, notes), Objects (front, back, hide, lock) and Document (theme, fonts, format, music). ⌘Z undoes, ⌘C ⌘V copy (also from one presentation to another), ⌘G groups, ⌘L locks."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I animate a presentation (transitions, builds, magic move)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the presentation, “✨ Animate” tab on the right. The transition plays when arriving on the slide: fade, dissolve, fade through black, push, slide, reveal, wipe, zoom, swap, flip, cube, or “magic move”: duplicate the slide (⧉), move, enlarge or rotate the objects on the copy, and they glide from one place to the other during playback (objects with the same name work too). Choose an object then “＋ Build in” or “＋ Build out”: appear, fade, arrive from one side, grow, bounce, drop, wipe, blur, swirl, typewriter for a text; each animation starts on click, with the previous one or after it, with its duration and its delay; “Animation order” arranges them (↑ ↓) and “▶ Preview” shows them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I present with my notes, the next slide and a timer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the presentation: “▶ Play” (from the beginning, or “⏵ From here”) goes full screen; → space or click: next, ←: previous, a number then Enter: go to that slide, B: black screen, W: white screen, L: laser pointer, Esc: quit. “🎤 Presenter” (or P during playback) opens a second window to put on your screen: the current slide, the next one (or the remaining animations), your notes (A− A+), the timer and the time. Notes are written under the slide (📝 Notes) or in the Slide tab. A slide can also move on to the next by itself, and the presentation can loop (kiosk, exhibition)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import a PowerPoint, a LibreOffice file, Keynote or a PDF?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → “🎞️ Presentation and slideshow” → “⤒ Import a presentation”, or “⤒ Import” in an open presentation (added after the slide shown, or in its place). PowerPoint (.pptx) and OpenDocument / LibreOffice Impress (.odp): each slide with its texts in place (sizes, bold, colours, lists), its images (stored in Media), its shapes, its tables, its charts, its notes and its transitions. Keynote (.key) has a closed format: in Keynote, File → Export to → PowerPoint, then import the .pptx. A PDF gives its images (scans, photos), one per slide: its text cannot be read here. Also: photos (one slide per photo), a text or Markdown (“# Title” starts a slide, “-” makes a bullet, “---” separates, “Notes:” the notes) and the .json file of a presentation from the app."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export a presentation as PDF, images or video?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the presentation, “⤓ Export”: PDF or printing (one slide per page, or with the speaker notes under each slide; in the print window, choose “Save as PDF”), the slide shown as PNG, all the slides as PNG (a .zip file), a WebM video (each slide for its duration, with the music; to keep in Media or download) and the presentation file (.json, to reopen elsewhere with “⤒ Import”). A simple slideshow can become a presentation: “🎤 As a presentation” in the slideshow."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange or hide the presentation buttons and choose its starting settings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the presentation, “⚙️ Customise”: each group of buttons at the top (insert, arrange, playback, file), the bar above the slides, the bottom bar, the inspector tabs and the sections of each tab (Position and size, Text, Image, Animations…) can be shown, hidden and arranged; a layout is kept under a name with “💾 Saved settings”. It is also in Settings → Montage, retouching, layout and sound (“🎤 Presentation” editor), with the theme, the format, the starting transition and layout, snapping, the colour of the laser pointer, full screen on opening and your own keyboard shortcuts."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I restore an old damaged photo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → Photo retouching → “🧭 Restore an old photo”, or in Retouch: the “🧭 Restore” tab (Image menu). Eight steps, each optional: straighten and crop; remove yellowing and revive the colours; find and remove dust and small spots automatically; fill scratches, creases and tears (healing brush, stamp); reduce grain; make a face sharper (surrounded with the ellipse); colourise (automatically, or with a brush and a palette of period tints); compare before / after and save as a new version (the original stays)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I transcribe a record (baptism, marriage, death, notarial deed)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Editing tools → “📜 Transcription workshop”, or in an image viewer: “📜 Open in the Workshop”. The image of the record is on the left (zoom, contrast, negative, rotation), the text on the right (with old characters: ſ, œ, [illegible]…). Choose a passage then draw its area (▭ Area): a click on one highlights the other. “🖨 Read printed text” reads a typed text and places the areas line by line; “✍️ Read handwriting (fast)” reads handwriting (see the question “Read the record”). “🧭 Spot” finds the names and their role (godfather, witness, son of…), dates even written out in words (“l’an mil sept cent quatre-vingt-dix”) or in the Republican calendar (converted), places and occupations, and suggests the records that resemble them. “🧩 Template” gives the outline of a record to complete. These readings are in “✨ Read ▾”: each one says in one sentence what sets it apart (speed, what needs installing) and whether it is installed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the size of an area in a record transcription?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click the area: it shows 8 handles (the corners and the middle of the sides). Pull a handle to change its size, drag the inside to move it; it does not leave the image and keeps a minimum size. With the keyboard: the arrows move it, Shift + arrows change its size, Alt to go point by point, Delete removes it (“↶ Undo” puts it back). ⌘Z undoes the last area change, ⇧⌘Z redoes it. The area stays linked to its passage in the text. It also works with the image rotated (⟳), straightened or mirrored, and with a finger (pinch to zoom)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the ⤢ button for in record transcription?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It changes how the image fits at each click: “Whole image” (you see the whole record), “Width” (the image takes the full width: best for reading a record from top to bottom), then “100 %” (actual size). The mode is written on the button; after zooming by hand (＋, －, the wheel or two fingers, always centred on the mouse or fingers), the button shows the percentage and a click returns to “Whole image”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I read a pale, yellowed or stained record better?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In record transcription, the “🎚️ See better” settings: Sharpness (and its radius), Local contrast (brings out the writing everywhere, even in dark corners), Colour channel (All, Red, Green or Blue: brown ink often stands out better on blue), Remove the background (yellowing and stains disappear, the ink remains), Black and white threshold, Levels (black point, white point, gamma), Straighten to a tenth of a degree, Mirror (to read a text seen through the paper), Negative, Grey, and the 🔍 Magnifier that follows the mouse (the wheel changes the magnification; L key). “💾 Remember for this record” keeps these settings with the record; “↺ Reset to default” resets everything (with “↶ Undo”). Everything is calculated on your computer, without freezing the screen, even on a very large image. In “✨ Read ▾”, “Read and export the improved image” reads (printed or handwritten) and exports the image with these settings. “🎚️ See better ▾” also offers three one-click recipes: 🖋 Faint ink, 🍂 Yellowed or stained paper, 🪞 Back of the page showing through."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the transcription made in the Workshop show everywhere?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A document has only one text: the one in the 📜 Transcription workshop. The viewer’s “Document transcription” box shows and changes that same text, and it also shows on the document page (with its kind, its status and “📜 Open in the Workshop”), on the record of the linked people (block “📜 Records and transcriptions”), in search (the top search too), the lists, the GEDCOM export (box “Document transcriptions”) and the book (“With the transcription” when placing an image). Several records on one image: one block per record, each edited in the Workshop. Nothing is overwritten: a text already written for the document is kept below the new one. On a record, a name found but not linked yet shows “to be linked”, with “Link” (and ↶ Undo)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange the record transcription bar?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"With “⚙️” at the end of the bar, or in Settings → Montage, retouching, layout and sound → “📜 Transcription workshop: toolbar”. Two layouts: A (everything in the bar, which wraps onto a new line if needed) or B (a short bar and the “🎚️ See better ▾” panel, arranged into Bring out the ink, Black and white, Position, with a badge that counts the active settings). Tick what is shown, arrange with ↑ ↓ or by dragging ⠿, and in B choose what goes in the bar or in the panel. “↺ Reset to default” puts everything back as at the start; “💾 Saved settings” keeps a bar under a name. On a small screen, the settings go into “See better” by themselves. The short bar (B) is the starting one, and the recommended one; the same setting shows or hides the line numbers in the margin of the text (recommended: shown)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the “Read → Fix → Spot → Add” strip of the workshop mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top of the right-hand panel of the 📜 Transcription workshop, four steps: Read (“✨ Read ▾” or type the text), Fix (suggested corrections, doubtful words [?], “📖 Reading help”), Spot (“🧭 Spot” the names, dates and places), Add (“✓ Add to the family tree…”). The current step is blue, a finished step ticks itself in green, and under each one you read what is left: “7 lines”, “2 words to check”, “3 to link”. Clicking a step brings its buttons forward; “Next step ›” moves on. “✓ Add to the family tree…” always stays at the bottom of the panel."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I check a transcription line by line, with the image above?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“👁 Check line by line” (Fix step, Display menu, or the R key) opens the workshop in full screen: the piece of image for the line (its area) right above the line of text. You fix it on the spot: the text of the record follows. Enter: the line is checked, on to the next one; ⇧ Enter: previous line; ↑ ↓: change line without marking it; “↶ Undo” puts the line back as it was; Esc closes. Checked lines are kept with the record. It needs one area per line (placed by the reading, or drawn with ▭). This mode can be turned off in Settings → “📜 Transcription workshop” (recommended: on)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I go back to yesterday’s text of a record, or fix a name everywhere?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"File → “🕘 Versions…”: a version is kept at each save where the text changed, and just before big changes (“Replace all”, sure suggestions accepted, a reading put into the text). You see each version, compare it word by word with now, and “↺ Go back” restores it (the current text is kept first; “↶ Undo” right after). To fix a misread name everywhere: ⌘F searches the text (results highlighted, Enter / ⇧ Enter), ⌥⌘F adds “Replace” and “Replace all” (the areas follow; “↶ Undo”). ⌘/ shows all the keys of the workshop."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know how far I am in a transcription?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Under the strip of the 📜 Transcription workshop, a progress line: the status of the record (To do, In progress, Checked, Finished: one click to change it), the doubtful words, the checked lines (“5 / 7 lines checked”), the lines linked to their area and the people to link. Clicking a counter goes to the first place concerned: the next [?], the line without an area, the first line not checked. When everything is checked with no doubtful word, the workshop offers “✓ Mark as checked”, and when everything is at zero “✓ Mark as finished”, never on its own. The “Record transcriptions” list shows and filters these statuses."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What do the words underlined with dots in the workshop mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"They are abbreviations known to “📖 Reading help”: labr, Vve, led., dud., 7bre, inh.… (in French, and in the language of the text chosen in “✨ Read ▾”). Move the mouse over one (or tap the word): a bubble gives the full form (“7bre = September”) and “Open in 📖 Reading help” to learn more. The text does not change: you transcribe as written. Display → “Stop underlining abbreviations” hides them; the setting is also in Settings (recommended: underlined)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I transcribe a record on a phone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a small screen, the 📜 Transcription workshop shows three tabs at the top: 🖼️ Image (the image in large, its areas, “🎚️ See better” and the zoom), 📝 Text (the strip, the characters, the text and “✓ Add to the family tree…” at the bottom) and 👥 People (with their number: the people of the record, what the app found, and the record sheet: kind, date, place, source, status). One tab at a time, in large; “show in the text” switches to the Text tab by itself. Line-by-line checking also works on a phone."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I link the pages of one document and transcribe them together?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the document page (Documents tab) or in the viewer: “📑 Document pages” → “＋ Link another page…”. As many pages as needed, each with its name (Front, Back, Page 3, Appendix or a free name), put in order by dragging ⠿; “Remove from the set” and “↶ Undo”; a page belongs to only one document. The 📜 Transcription workshop then opens all the pages, as tabs above the image: one text split by “— page 2 —” (recommended) or one text per page (⋯ menu of the tabs). “✨ Read ▾” reads the page shown or all of them, and “What the app found” gives the page and line of each piece of information (“page 3, line 7”), also written in the source."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a record’s template (🧩 Template)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The boxes you note down for each kind of record (baptism: date, child, born on, father, mother, godfather, godmother, priest, signatures; and also birth, marriage, death, burial, notarial deed, act of notoriety, census, military register). In the 📜 Transcription workshop, “Spot” step: the “🧩 Template” tab next to “What the app found”. “🧭 Spot” fills in the boxes (in green, to be checked); type in a box to correct it, Enter or ✓ marks it as checked: Spot never touches a box you checked or wrote yourself again. A click on “l. 3” (or “page 2, l. 3”) shows the passage. With “To be specified”, the tab suggests the guessed kind. The template’s ⋯ menu: “Fill-in-the-blanks text (as before)” if you prefer the old template, “Paste the fill-in-the-blanks text”, “Fill in again”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add my own boxes to a template, and get a spreadsheet of records?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“✓ Add to the family tree” takes the boxes of the 🧩 Template that you checked (✓) or wrote: they come before what the app had guessed, and the preview says so before you accept. In a record: “＋ Add a box” (for example “Godfather’s occupation”); × removes it. For a whole kind of record: the template’s ⋯ menu → “🧩 My templates…”: hide a ready-made box or add your own (also for “Letter” and “Other”, which have no boxes to start with). ⋯ menu → “⤓ Spreadsheet of all … records”: one row per record of this kind, one column per box (a parish index). When printing and in PDF, “🧩 Template” adds the table of boxes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I cite a line of a record (line numbers)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The line numbers of the 📜 Transcription workshop show everywhere: in the text margin of the viewer and of the document page, in “What the app found” and the 🧩 Template (“l. 3”, or “page 2, l. 3”), and when exporting: “⤓ Export” → 📝 Text with “Line numbers” and “People and roles” (ticked to start with), and when printing or in PDF. To remove them everywhere: in the workshop, “⚙️” at the end of the bar → turn off “Line numbers in the text margin”. A name not yet linked shows 🔗: a click opens the workshop on the record to link it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export a transcription as TEI (for researchers or archives)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the 📜 Transcription workshop: File → “⤓ Export” → “🗂 TEI”. The .xml file (TEI P5 format) contains the title, kind, date, place and source, each page with its image and zones, the text line by line (each line linked to its zone), the people with their role (and their record if it is linked) and the boxes of the 🧩 Template."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which AI model should I choose for the in-depth reading?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the “🔬 In-depth reading” window, the app recommends the model that suits your computer’s memory (badge “recommended for this computer”). Each model shows its size, the recommended memory, its licence, its 👍 strengths and 👎 weaknesses. “🧪 Other models to try”: models not yet tried by the app (including one for 64 GB computers); compare them on your records with “The two readings side by side” before relying on them. “⚙️ Advanced: other model (Ollama)”: any model already installed in Ollama, if it can read an image. Everything happens on your computer: nothing goes to the Internet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I link two different documents (same regiment, same family…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a document’s page (📄 Documents tab) or in the viewer: “🔗 Linked documents” → “＋ Link a document…”, choose the document, then the reason (same regiment, same family, same matter, same place, same notary, or another reason you write). The link works both ways; “⋯” changes the reason or removes the link (↶ Undo). Under each link: “In common” (names, places, years, regiments spotted in both transcriptions) and “💡 What this document tells you” (what the other one adds, with its line). In an investigation, “🔗 Links between its documents” shows the pairs, offers “＋ Add to the case file” for a linked document that is missing, and “🔎 Make it a lead”. This is neither the pages of a single document (📑) nor a collection (📚)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a collection from documents, and search by collection?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"📄 Documents tab (with no document open): “☑ Choose several”, click the documents in page order (a number appears), then “📚 Make a collection with…”: name, reference, place, years, and you arrange the order with ⠿ or ↑ ↓. A multi-page document goes in with all its pages; files that are not images are left out. “📚 Add to a collection…” (also in a document’s “⋯”) adds to the end of an existing collection, without adding again what is already there. The documents stay where they are: the collection groups them. On a document’s page: “📚 In the collection … page 12 of 40”, with the previous and the next page. In the column, “📚 Collections” sorts the documents by collection (each group folds up, “In no collection” too). To search: the collection’s name in “Search…”, or “Filter ▾” → Collection, Record type (only births…), Town or place and “Within a radius of (km)”, years. The radius needs the place’s coordinates (Places)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see where a collection stands (transcribed, checked, absent pages)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 📚 Collections → a collection → “🗂 Status” tab: one box per page, one colour per status (⚪ To do, 🟡 In progress, 🔵 Checked, 🟢 Finished — the same as in the Workshop), the figures, the reliability (deduced from checked lines and “[?]”), and “📋 What’s left, in the recommended order”. The status is calculated automatically (the Workshop record on the photo, or a written text); on the page, you can choose it by hand (↺ back to the calculation), note the legibility and “📷 Photo to retake”. An absent page has a reason (missing, lost, not photographed, not digitised, not online, not open to the public, being restored, elsewhere, blank page) and can join the Archive gaps; “📮 Add to requests” puts it in Research → Register tracking → Requests. Enter the numbering (paginated, foliated, no order for a bundle) and the number of expected pages: the app flags gaps (“after f° 15 v° comes f° 18 r°”) and offers to add the absent pages. “☑ Choose pages” changes several pages at once; “⤓ Export the status” gives a spreadsheet. Everything can be undone (↶ Undo)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make the contents of a collection (chapters, passages)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 📚 Collections → a collection → “📑 Contents” tab. “＋ Chapter…”: a title, from one page to another (e.g. “Marriages 1870”, pages 15 to 19), a record type and years if you want; in a chapter’s “⋯”: “Sub-chapter…” (as many levels as you want) and “Passage in this chapter…”. A passage describes a precise spot without creating a person record: “from the middle of page 5 to the middle of page 7: marriage of Louis LACOMBE and Anne MAZAUD”, with a description. “✨ Suggest a split” looks at the records already transcribed in the Workshop (their type and year) and suggests “Year 1789 › Baptisms 1789…”: you untick, you fix the titles, nothing is added until you accept. Each line expands and collapses (▸ ▾, “Expand all”, “Collapse all”, levels), shows its progress, and a click on its title opens its first page. “📖 Reading mode” shows the contents in large print with the description of the passages. The app flags what’s out of place: two chapters that overlap, a record of another type or from another year. On a page: “📑 Year 1789 › Baptisms 1789”, also in Documents. A bundle (in no order) can be sorted by date, type or shelfmark: Pages tab → “🗃 Sort by…”. Everything can be undone (↶ Undo)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note where a collection is (archive service, fonds, reference) and who can see it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a collection: “Edit” → “🏛 Where it is, who can see it”. Choose the archive service from the list in Settings → Archive services (or create it), write the fonds, the series, the reference, the collection’s online link, and the link to an image with {n} in place of the number (“…/vue/{n}”) plus the offset “Image = page + …”: each page then has “↗ Image 23 online” and “⧉ Cite this page” (“AD Cher, 3 E 1234, f° 11 r° (image 23)”). “Other copy” links the municipal copy and the court registry copy. Public access is calculated from the years and record types (75 years for births, marriages and notaries, immediately for deaths) or chosen (open, restricted until…, by permission): the app suggests, the archive service has the final say. In view-only mode, a collection not open to the public is hidden (switch in the “🔐 Who can see it” block). Also note the reuse (licence), the medium, the physical condition, the language, the handwriting and the provenance. On an archive service’s page: “📚 Collections held there”, their progress and their gaps."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see all my collections at a glance, by archive service and by fonds?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 📚 Collections → “🏛 By fonds” (at the top, next to “📚 Collections”): the collections sorted by archive service then by fonds, and, within a fonds, by reference. At the top, the overall figures: collections, expected pages, photographed, transcribed, checked, absent pages and gaps. One row per collection: reference, dates, pages, photographed, transcribed · checked, online, open to the public, and what’s left (“2 to transcribe · 1 absent”); clicking “Remaining” opens the collection status. Each service and each fonds collapses (▸ ▾). “Show” keeps only what’s still to transcribe or proofread, and collections with absent pages, not online or not open to the public. “☰ Columns…” adds the series, medium, physical condition, language, reuse and numbering. “⤓ Spreadsheet” and “🖨 Holdings report” to send or print it. On the dashboard, the “🗂 Registers: still to do” widget shows the collections with the most left."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does a collection’s “💡 What’s in it” show, and what is the “Archive centre” profile for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a collection’s summary, the “💡 What’s in it” block is calculated from the records already transcribed in the Workshop: records per year (years without records are hatched), record types, people mentioned (and how many are linked to a person record), the most frequent surnames, occupations (from the templates), places, the share of people who sign, the years covered. It adds remarks to check, never stated as fact: a year without records in the middle (“Mark a gap” adds it to Archive gaps) or a year with at least twice as many deaths as usual. The same block, for all the collections of a service, is on the archive service’s page. Lists → 📚 Collections → ⚙️ “Collection settings”: the “My family tree” profile (recommended) or “Archive centre, society” (the fonds view first, the medium, physical condition, language and reuse columns, and the identity card at the top of new collections’ summary), and the options: flag numbering gaps, show reliability, show remarks, hide collections not open to the public in view-only mode."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you split a register, track progress and share the status of a collection?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🗂 Status” tab of a collection. Splitting the work: “☑ Choose pages” → “👥 Assign to…” (a first name; empty = nobody), then “Pages for: …” shows each person’s pages (or those not assigned yet); on a page, “👥 Assigned to” and “✎ Assign…”. Progress: the app records one point per day the collection is opened, and the “📈 Progress” block draws the curve (transcribed, checked or finished) and says “at the current pace, finished around …”; you set a goal in “✎ Numbering, expected pages” (“🎯 all checked by …”): the app tells you whether you are ahead or behind. “🌍 Shareable page” saves a single HTML file (the identity card, the page-by-page status, the contents you can open and close, what’s in it) that opens in any browser, with no program needed. In the fonds view, “⤓ EAD” saves the finding aid in the archives’ format (EAD): services, fonds, collections with reference, dates, public access, licence, and their contents."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note that a page is in Latin, and use a register’s table (or index)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Language: each page takes the collection’s language (“Edit” → “🏛 Where it is, who can see it” → Language), but it can have its own: on the page, “🔤 Language” → “✎ Change…”, or several at once in the “🗂 Status” tab (“☑ Choose pages” → “⋯” → “Language of these pages…”). When a page’s text looks like Latin, the app suggests “Note “Latin””. In the Status tab, “🔤 Language: Latin” shows only those pages, and “What’s left” says “including Latin: p. …”. Tables: mark the page “📇 Table or index” (on the page: “📄 Page type”, or “⋯” in the Status tab), then make its “📊 Page table”: one row per entry (name, year…) and one column for the reference (“f° 12”, “p. 3”). In the “📑 Table of contents” tab, “📇 Tables and indexes” gathers all the entries: a click takes you to the page referred to, and the app flags what the table refers to but is missing (absent page, or no page with that name). You can search it for a name. Covers, flyleaves and appendices are marked the same way."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I can’t read an old record (Latin, abbreviations, long s): where can I find help?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In record transcription, “📜 Reading help” (or Tools menu): first choose a word in the text, it is looked up automatically. Also: quick search (⌘K, type “filius”, “vve”, “dudit”…), ⋯ of the record, Settings → Glossary. Three tabs: 🔍 Dictionary (Latin of the registers, formulas for baptism, marriage and burial, abbreviations — including those of the censuses: cult., jlier, Vve, id., d° —, old words), filtered by kind and by record; ✍️ The letters (long s, round r, e and c, u and n, ligatures, barred p… drawn, with the letter not to be confused with); 🎓 Exercises: read a text written as in the register, type your reading, “✓ Correct” colours each word (right, to review with the explanation, forgotten, extra)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I read a record in German, Italian, Spanish, Polish, Dutch, Latin or Greek?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“📜 Help reading old records” (record transcription, quick search ⌘K, Settings → Glossary): at the top, the “Language” badges (with the number of words) keep a single language in the three tabs. 🔍 Dictionary: the words of baptism, marriage and death, the months (with the old names and 7bris, Xbre, Jänner, Hornung…), numbers and ages in words, occupations, kinship and whole formulas translated (“Im Jahre…”, “L’anno…”, “En la villa de…”, “Działo się…”, “Anno Domini…”). Type without accents: “malzonkowie” finds małżonkowie, “grossvater” Großvater; in Greek, write in Greek letters without accents or in Latin letters (“huios” finds υἱός). ✍️ The letters: German script (Kurrent, Sütterlin), the Greek alphabet and its handwritten forms, with a converter for Greek numbers (͵αωνβʹ = 1852). 🎓 Exercises: a model text per language, corrected word by word. In the transcription, choose the “Language of the text”: the help opens in that language, and “🧭 Spot” or “✍️ Read handwriting (fast)” show the known words with their translation. The lists come from the FamilySearch “Word Lists” and from GenWiki."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"And for a record in Swedish, Danish, Norwegian, Czech, Slovak, Hungarian, Romanian, Croatian, Slovene, Lithuanian, or in Occitan, Breton, Basque?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The same “📜 Help reading old records”: choose the language badge. For each: the words of baptism, marriage and death, the months (with the old names: Torsmånad, Boldogasszony hava, Gerar, svečan…), numbers and ages, occupations, kinship and translated formulas, plus a model exercise (example people) corrected word by word. Type without accents or signs: “szuletett” finds született, “brezna” března, “soen” Søn, “rodjen” rođen. Good to know: in Swedish, Danish and Norwegian, the surname changes with each generation (-son, -sen, -datter) and “aa” = å; in Hungarian, the surname comes before the first name (“Kovács Jánosné” = Mrs János Kovács); Danish counts in twenties (halvtreds = 50, a small converter is in the Dictionary); Romanian before 1860 is written in Cyrillic (alphabet in ✍️ The letters). Occitan, Breton and Basque are rare in registers: you will find them mostly in nicknames, place and house names, graves and a few notes by the priest."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"And for a record in Portuguese, Catalan, English, Russian, Ukrainian, Hebrew, Yiddish, Armenian or Arabic (graves included)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The same “📜 Help reading old records”: choose the language badge (“＋ other languages” to see them all). For each: the words of birth, marriage and death, the months, numbers and ages, occupations, kinship, translated formulas and a model exercise (example people) corrected word by word. English also has the census abbreviations (Ag. Lab., Unm., Do) and those of the Ellis Island lists (S.I., L.P.C.). For Russian, Ukrainian, Hebrew, Yiddish, Armenian and Arabic, you can search and answer in Latin letters: “krestianin”, “tichri”, “geshtorbn”, “krikor”, “marhoum”. Russian before 1918 (ѣ, ъ, і, ѳ) is explained in ✍️ The letters, with the pitfalls of handwriting. Hebrew and Arabic are displayed right to left; the formulas on graves are translated (פ״נ, תנצב״ה, المرحوم…) and a small converter reads the letter-numbers (תרנ״ה = 655, i.e. 1894-1895; Armenian ՌՋԺԵ = 1915; abjad and the digits ١٣٢٠)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are 300 livres of 1780 or 1,200 francs of 1880 worth today (Old money converter)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🪙 Old money converter” (Settings → Calendar / measures, quick search ⌘K, ⋯ of the record, record transcription): 💰 Money tab, write the sum as in the record (“12 livres 10 sous 6 deniers”, “1 200 francs”, “45 NF”, “3 écus”) and the year (“1780”, “an X”). You get the equivalent in today’s euros (≈), in bread and in days of work, and “The calculation, step by step”. From 1901, it is the INSEE price index (the same calculation as its franc-euro converter); before 1901, an order of magnitude. Exact parities: 1 livre = 20 sous = 240 deniers, 81 livres = 80 germinal francs, 1 new franc = 100 old francs, 1 € = 6.55957 F. “📋 Copy” puts the result on the clipboard."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much is an arpent, a toise, a boisseau? And the Republican calendar?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🪙 Old money converter” → 📏 Old measures: choose the region (Paris, Lyon, Brittany, Normandy, Burgundy, Provence, Lorraine, Alsace, Flanders), write “3 arpents”, “12 toises 3 pieds”, “2 setiers” (or “1.5 ha” for the reverse): the value in metres, ares or litres, the other measures of the region and the same measure elsewhere. → 📅 Republican calendar: “12 thermidor an II” or “14 July 1794”, both ways, with the day of the week and the 13 months of the year. In a record, a date in Year II is already understood (“✓ Understood: … (30 July 1794)”); “🪙 Convert” next to it opens the converter."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find all my record transcriptions?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Archives and sources → “📜 Record transcriptions”: each record with its thumbnail, its kind, its date, its place, its reference, the number of lines and areas, the people spotted. Search in the title, the text, the people, the place or the reference (without worrying about accents); filter by kind of record, person, place, period, state (to do, in progress, finished), with areas or linked to a record; List or Cards (with the first lines of the text); sort by date of the record. A click reopens the record; “＋ New transcription” starts by choosing the image; 🗑 deletes with “↶ Undo”. The state is chosen at the top of the tool."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I print a beautiful large-format tree (fan, wheel, drawn tree)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → “🌳 Decorative tree to print”, or Lists → Editing tools → “🌳 Decorative tree”: choose the person, the shape (fan, open fan, wheel, drawn tree with trunk, branches and foliage), the number of generations, the format (A4 to A0, posters), the style (parchment, classic, botanical, heraldic, modern, black and white) and what is written (years, dates, places, occupations, Sosa numbers, empty boxes, portraits). The tree opens in vector drawing: everything can be retouched (colours, fonts, ornaments, coat of arms), then File → Print / PDF or Export as PNG."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I draw a town or village plan or a map of a region?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In Vector drawing: Plan menu → “Plan mode”, or the 🗺️ Plan tab on the right. Choose what you are drawing in the palette (road, street, path, river, forest, field, building, church, mill, place name…), then one click per point: roads join up, rivers widen, names follow the streets. Period styles (État-Major, Napoleonic cadastre, Cassini, parchment), scale, compass rose, cartouche and automatic legend. “Base map of a place” or “Reference image” lays a map or an old plan underneath, transparent, to trace over; “Places from the database” places your places; each object can be linked to a person or a place and have its years (time slider)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a hemicycle, a horseshoe or the timeline of a group’s members?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → “My diagrams” → “＋ Create a diagram”: 25 drawings (parliament in a hemicycle, arc, horseshoe, Westminster, circle, hall; members timeline in the Wikipedia style, timeline of lives, schedule; columns, pie chart, pyramid, flow, organisation chart, Venn, word cloud, gauges…). Type your figures, paste them from a spreadsheet, import a CSV or take them from the app (“From the app”, with “Keep up to date”). It can then go in a widget, a note, a person’s record, an investigation, the Workshop (“Link” tab) or the layout (Object → Insert a diagram)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own chart (formerly “my own statistic”)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Chart editor (formerly “Statistics editor”) → “＋ New chart” (or “＋ Create a chart” at the bottom of Charts). Start from scratch (count people, events, one of your lists… by occupation, by place, by decade, by century), from one of the app’s statistics (Surnames, First names, Places, Birth years…) redrawn your way, from one of your widgets or from an example. Eight drawings: bars, columns, pie, ring, line, area, mosaic, table. It appears in Charts → “My charts”, and can also go on the dashboard or in one of your tabs."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put many conditions in a chart (e.g. woodcutters within 4 km of Pernay, married, with two children)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the Chart editor, start from one of the app’s statistics (Surnames, First names, Places…) or from a chart from scratch, then “＋ Criterion” as many times as you like: occupation, first name, surname, place, within … km of a town, between two precise dates, had an event (marriage, divorce, journey…), number of children (“2 and 2” = exactly two), number of unions, number of events of a type, age at an event, lifespan, sex, label, my ancestors. “✨ Suggest a caption” writes the sentence that sums up the criteria."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I share a chart on a forum or with an association?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → My charts → your chart: “🖼️ PNG image” (to post), “📄 CSV” (the figures for a spreadsheet) or “🖨 Print / PDF”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I display my chart: in Charts, on the dashboard, in a tab?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the editor, “Where to show it”: tick Charts and/or the dashboard. For one of your tabs: add it from the left column of the tab, or in Settings → Tab editor."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Name and saint of the day: how do I see the people concerned?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → Statistics → Name and saint of the day, at the very bottom: the list of people who bear the first name of the saint of their birth or baptism day (or all of them), sortable by date, by day of the year, by name or by saint. Click on a period or a family in the tables to keep only that one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"First names: the family and France, how do I see everyone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the “In the family” column, click “＋ 12 people” (the number varies) to unfold the full list, and “Fold” to close it again."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where does an ancestor’s first name come from (godfather, grandfather, brother who died before…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → Statistics → “Passing on of first names”: for each child, the app looks for the most likely lead — their godfather or godmother (the “godfather” and “godmother” witnesses of the birth or baptism), a brother or sister who died before their birth (the first name “taken up again”), a paternal or maternal grandparent, the father or mother, an uncle or aunt, or the saint of their birth day. Each lead states its rule (“same first name as their godfather”) and a confidence level (strong, medium, weak); Jean and Jeanne, Louis and Louise count as the same first name. At the top: the transmission arrows (one line per first name, the years across; click on a circle = the record), filterable by first name, by branch and by period; then the share of children who bear their godparent’s first name, period by period. On the record, the small line “👶 First name received from…” (Identity block) has a ⋯: “That’s right”, “That’s not it”, another lead, or “Choose the person…” with the search menu; “↶ Undo” right after. Nothing is written without your click; living people and confidential records are never counted or named."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare my family with French averages (age at marriage, children, mortality, signatures, occupations)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → Statistics → “Your family and the history of France” (just after “Family averages”): for each period, your family’s average (orange dot) next to that of France (blue dot, “≈”): age at first marriage of men and women, children per couple, infant mortality before age 1, life expectancy, share of spouses who signed their marriage record (“signed” box of marriages), share of farming occupations. Each French figure has its source at the bottom of the page (Louis Henry and Jacques Houdaille, Yves Blayo, INED, INSEE, Maggiolo survey…); when a figure was not certain, it was not included. Filters: branch and period; a click on the number of people shows who is counted; “⤓ Export” like the other statistics. A family has few records (“⚠️ few” under 10) and babies who died are often missing from trees: the comparison should be read broadly."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep a chart’s settings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the bottom of the Charts column, “💾 Save the view”: the diagram, the person, the depth and the colours are kept in the “Saved” tab."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Was my family part of a great population movement (rural exodus, emigration…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → History → “🌍 Your family in the great movements”. The app compares each person’s moves (birth, baptism, marriage, residence, death…) with 32 great migrations in history, each with its source: rural exodus to Paris, people from Auvergne and Aveyron in Paris, Bretons, masons from the Creuse, Barcelonnettes in Mexico, Basques in the Americas, Italians, Poles, Spaniards, Portuguese, pieds-noirs, the 1940 exodus… Same departure, same arrival, same period: “Sure” or “Possible”, never forced on you. “Yes, that’s it” confirms it, “No” removes it (with “↶ Undo”). “🗺 On the migration map” opens a map of these journeys, ready to keep. On the profile, the “🌍 The world around” block says it too: “Pierre was part of: …”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a population movement I know about?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → History → “🌍 Your family in the great movements” → “＋ Add a movement”: its name (“The glassworkers of Carmaux to Albi”), its years, where people left from and where they went (department numbers, towns or countries, separated by commas), what you should know and its source. The app immediately looks for who in your family was part of it. ✎ edits it, 🗑 deletes it (with “↶ Undo”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find out if an ancestor died during an epidemic (cholera, smallpox…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → History → “Epidemics, wars, terrible winters”. The crises of all of France and, on top of that, local crises (1832 cholera in Paris, 1813-1814 typhus in the East, 1875 Garonne flood, Courrières disaster…), each with its source, next to your family’s deaths in the same place at the same time. If the cause is written down (on the death, in its note, or in a transcribed record linked to the person: “died of cholera”, “died of the smallpox”), the app shows it and the death becomes “sure”; “Note as cause of death” keeps it on the death (with “↶ Undo”). For each crisis: the deaths month by month (the cholera summer, the flu winter) and children under 5 shown separately."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a local crisis (fire, flood, epidemic in the village)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → History → “🌍 The world around” → “＋ New event”, with the type “Epidemic”, “Famine”, “Terrible winter”, “War” or “Crisis”: it also goes into “Epidemics, wars, terrible winters” and into the “Births and deaths” chart. A single list of crises is used everywhere in the app."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I follow the law of the time, the taxes and the heirs of an estate?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In an estate file, “⚖️ Law and shares” tab: the law in force at the time of death (before or after 2002, 1972…), parentage, successive estates (an heir who died after the deceased), the matrimonial property regime, the will and gifts (reserved portion, disposable portion), an estimate of the inheritance tax and deadlines, and a deceased person with ties abroad. Amounts are never kept. The “🗂 Heir tracking” tab sorts each heir by stage, with their sources and next follow-up."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I open an estate file step by step?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Estates → “🧭 Step by step”: 1. the deceased person (the file opens, “↶ Undo” deletes it), 2. the family found, 3. the doubts (alive at the death or not, one click), 4. the heirs and their shares."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare my calculation with the notary’s deed of notoriety?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⚖️ Law and shares” tab → “📄 Compare with the deed of notoriety”: paste the list of heirs from the deed (one per line, with their share: “1/4”, “25 %”, “half”). The app says for each one “same”, “different” or “not in the deed”, and shows those in the deed it didn’t find."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does the search find old-style names, first names, places and dates?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everywhere (People, ⌘K, Lists, the picker, Where to search?, Duplicates), the search ignores accents, capitals, hyphens and particles (d’Albert = Dalbert = De Albert, Lacombe = La Combe). It understands first names as written in records: abbreviations (J.-Bte, Fçois, Jh, Cath.), Latin forms (Petrus, Joannes, Mariae), diminutives (Toinette) and compounds written as one word (Marianne). For places: St = Saint, “Le Mans” found by “Mans”, and merged communes if the commune history is installed. Dates are typed in French: “vers 1850”, “entre 1850 et 1860”, “avant 1900”, “années 1880”, “XVIIIe siècle”, “3 mars 1850”, “15 brumaire an III”. Exact names come first, then variants; if nothing is found, names that sound the same (“No ‘Dupond’; 3 people close…”). Under each result, “Found by” says why. Settings → 🔎 Search: automatic phonetics, Latin forms, abbreviations, margin for “about”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find the heirs of a deceased person for a notary (⚖️ probate genealogist)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Turn on the “⚖️ Probate genealogist” use (Settings → Appearance → 🧭 How you use the app): the “⚖️ Estates” tab appears. Open one file per deceased there (also: Lists → Research and organisation → “⚖️ Estate files”, or ⋯ on the deceased’s record → “⚖️ Search for the heirs…”). The file keeps the instructing notary, their reference, deadlines, the heirs and their shares, the devolution tree, the documents for each link (in hand, requested, missing), letters to the heirs and a log of every action. At the top of the tab, three buttons: “Files”, “Heirs to contact” (the living heirs of all open files, with address, contact status, ✉️ Write and CSV export) and “Letters” (only the letters of estate files, with answer and signature)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How does the app calculate the heirs’ shares (devolution)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"From the records, under today’s French Civil Code (art. 731 and following, to read on legifrance.gouv.fr; in the file, each article cited is a link): the surviving spouse first, then the first of the 4 orders that has someone (children; parents, brothers and sisters; other ascendants; uncles and cousins up to the 6th degree), with representation and the split between paternal and maternal branches. Shares are exact fractions. If it is not known whether an heir was alive, the app also shows the other hypothesis. The calculation is indicative: the notary checks it. For a death before July 2002, a warning says so: the law at the time was different."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do estate files leave the app?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No: living heirs, their addresses and their shares stay in the file. Only the papers you make yourself leave it (table for the notary, letters, CSV export of the heirs to contact). No export (GEDCOM, Geneanet, website, sharing with the family, book) reads them, and viewing mode and simple mode do not show them. A closed file can be archived: addresses are removed, the log remains."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where should estate files go: in my family tree or separately?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the first “＋ Open a file”, the app asks: in this family tree, in a separate family tree for all your files (“⚖️ Estate files”, in 🌳 My family trees), or one family tree per file. You can change your mind in Settings → Appearance → 🧭 How you use the app → “⚖️ Where to keep the files”, where “⇄ Move…” puts a file elsewhere (both family trees are backed up first). For the deceased, “⤓ Take the record from My family tree” copies the record up to the grandparents and all their known descendants, without photos."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I show only one estate file across the whole app (⚖️ button)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The ⚖️ button at the top right (probate use only): “Show all”, one file or several. A yellow banner reminds you; ✕ shows everything again. People, search, lists and the map show only the file’s records; in the tree, the others are greyed out without a name (or hidden: setting). A record created during the filter is linked to the file automatically, with “↶ Undo”. The Dashboard and Settings are never filtered."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I link a person to an estate file, with a role?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The deceased is linked when the file is opened, heirs and intermediaries by the calculation (a record removed by hand is never put back automatically). On a record, ⋯ → “⚖️ Files…”: one role per file (deceased, heir, intermediary, witness, to check). In the file, the “🧑‍🤝‍🧑 People” tab shows all its records. The record’s “⚖️ 2 files” badge lists its files; two records from different files you want to merge, or a namesake in another file, are flagged first."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the progress of all my estate files?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⚖️ Estates → “Files”: a dashboard of all files, even those kept in another family tree (state, notary, next deadline, heirs, documents, time, progress). Tick files for “⚖️ Show only these” or a CSV export. The Dashboard widget “⚖️ My estate files” sums it up. Progress counts 7 weighted steps (deceased and record, tree, heirs found, addresses, signed contracts, documents, handed to the notary): the weights are set in Settings → Appearance → 🧭 How you use the app."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I archive a closed estate file to a file, then re-import it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Closed file → ⋯ → “🗄 Archive to a file and remove…”: a .lcnda-succ.zip file keeps the file, the log, the documents, the letters, the tree and the records (addresses of the living kept or removed, your choice). Then the records used only by this file are removed from the database (backup first). Later: ⚖️ Estates → “⤒ Re-import an archived file…”; a record that looks like one already there is offered for comparison, never merged automatically."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see people grouped by estate file?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"👤 People → left column → “⚖️ By file” (when the family tree has files): one collapsible folder per estate, its records from A to Z, and “No file” collapsed at the end. Files are ordered A → Z or by hand (drag ⠿, or Alt + ↑ / ↓ on the file, with “↶ Undo”). “Current view” goes back to the usual list."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do confidential records count as heirs in an estate file?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, by default: for the calculation, a confidential record (🔒) is a person like any other. Settings → Appearance → 🧭 How you use the app → “⚙️ File settings” → “Do confidential records count as heirs?” → No: they are removed from the calculation, as if they did not exist (never the deceased). The 👥 Heirs tab then says so (“2 confidential records not counted: the shares may be wrong”), with a link to this setting."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange the tabs of an estate case?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At first, a single row of tabs: what does not fit in the width goes into “More ▾” (with its counters), and the open tab always stays visible; on a phone the row also scrolls. In the case, ⋯ → “🗂 Tab layout…” (or Settings → Case tools → “🗂 Tabs of a case”) also offers “By families” (📁 File, 🔎 Research, 📑 Documents, ✉️ Contacts, 🕓 Follow-up, then their tabs) or “A column on the left” (a single-choice menu on a small screen). The counters stay visible (Documents 1 / 2, Contacted 2 / 6). At the bottom of 📁 File, a clickable summary shows where the case stands: heirs, documents, contacted, next deadline."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"An heir is untraceable or not responding: how do I note it in an estate file?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the file, 👥 Heirs tab (or ⚖️ Estates → “Heirs to contact”), the “Contact” menu on their row: “🔎 Untraceable” or “🔇 Not responding”, dated. The ⚖️ badge on their record shows it (“⚖️ 2026-014 · 🔇 not responding”), and ⋯ on the record → “⚖️ Files…” changes it too. Each change goes to the file’s log, with “↶ Undo”. They do not count as contacted in the progress, and the shares do not change: this is not the “Presumed absent” status. Untraceable for over a year: the app suggests “❔ Absent or missing”, which explains the presumption of absence (French Civil Code, art. 112 et seq., on legifrance.gouv.fr)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I have a doubt about the law: how do I find the right article?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⚖️ Estates → “⚖️ Unsure about the law?” (or, in an open file, “⚖️ Unsure?”): write your problem in your own words, for example “deadline to renounce an estate”, then “🏛 Search on Service-public” (explains in plain French and gives the link to the right article) or “📜 Search on Légifrance” (the articles of the codes in force). The app chooses no article and writes no legal text: your question is sent as is to the official website, which opens in your browser. Don’t write any names: the question goes out on the internet. In a file, “✍️ Note in the file” keeps the question in the log, with the article’s link if you paste it (only Légifrance, Service-public or EUR-Lex)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I count my time on an estate file?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the open file, the ⏱ button at the top: it counts (a single stopwatch at a time, even if you change screen or window). “⏸️ Pause”, then “⏹️ Stop and record”: the time, rounded to the minute, goes into 💶 Time and costs (one line per day, ⏱ in front, editable with ✎) and into the “Time” column of the files dashboard. After 10 minutes without touching anything it pauses by itself (“Count these 10 min too” if you want). App closed along the way: when it reopens, the app offers to record or discard that time."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the red badge on 🔔 at the top right mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The number of deadlines overdue or due today, in all your estate files (all genealogies). Click 🔔: the list, with ✓ done, ⏭ tomorrow (or Monday) and ↗ to open the file, “↶ Undo” each time. The genealogy’s ⏰ Reminders and a computer notification when the app opens are set at the bottom of the list (off at first). Settings → Appearance → Top-right buttons to hide it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the list check before handing a file to the notary?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🖨 For the notary → “📋 Before handing over” (also when you switch the state to “Handed to the notary”): the death record, the complete tree, heirs whose existence is not established, a record for each link (a record requested but not received is “to check”), the addresses, the signed contracts, untraceable heirs, the total of the shares, overdue deadlines. Each point has a button to fix it. It warns, it never blocks: handing over anyway is noted in the log."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I print the chain of descent for the notary?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🖨 For the notary → “What the PDF contains”: the box “Chain for each heir” is ticked at first. For each heir, the records linking them to the deceased, from the deceased to them, with their reference (title of the source and page); a shared record is written once, then “shared, see 1”; a missing record is marked “record to be provided”. No legal text is copied: only the articles cited by the calculation are links to Légifrance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do the shares in euros include tax?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. 👥 Heirs → “💶 In euros”: you type the net assets given by the notary, the app applies the exact shares and each contract’s % (with no VAT added), rounded to the cent with an exact total. Inheritance tax and costs are not calculated: it is a rough idea, to be checked by the notary. The amount is never kept (neither in the database nor in the log): it is erased when you leave the file."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I write to all the heirs at once?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"✉️ Contacted heirs → “✉️ Letter to several heirs…”: tick the heirs (those without an address, 🔎 untraceable or 🔇 not answering are unticked and flagged), choose a template or one of yours, click a field ({civilité}, {nom}, {adresse}…) to insert it, look at each letter in “Preview” (one can be edited on its own). Then 🖨 print, 📝 word processor, or “📮 File in the Letter register”: everything is filed at once, with the follow-up, and “↶ Undo” removes them all at once. The deceased’s name is never a field. “＋ Save as a template” keeps your letter for all the files of this genealogy."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does a family tree’s password protect?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → 🔐 Password for this family tree (or 🌳 My family trees → 🔐): you must type it to open this family tree in the app, and it locks again after 15 minutes without any activity (5 min, 1 h or never), when the app closes and when you change family tree. Its backups are always encrypted with this password (AES-256). But the file itself is not encrypted: someone who copies it can read it with other software. For that, encrypt your computer’s disk (FileVault on Mac, BitLocker on Windows). Forgotten password: there is no back door, the family tree no longer opens in the app."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who saw my files?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → 📜 Who saw what (or, in an estate file, 🕓 Log → “📜 Who saw this file”): every file opening (once per session), print or PDF, export, mail merge letter and every password attempt, with the date and the computer’s name. Nothing in it can be changed or erased; lines older than 12 months go away by themselves (adjustable: 3 months to keep everything, and that is recorded). Never an address, an amount or a password. ⤓ CSV to export it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I open my family tree on another computer in my home?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the computer that keeps the family tree: Settings → 🖧 Several computers, then turn on the switch (it is off at first, and the family tree must have a password). The app shows an address (for example http://192.168.1.20:47321) and an invitation code. On the other computer, type this address in a browser, then the code and a name: a 6-digit code appears on both sides. If it is the same, click “✓ Allow” on the main computer; the other computer then gives the family tree’s password. Only over your home Wi-Fi, never over the internet; everything cuts off when the main computer goes to sleep or closes the app. In this version, the other devices are read-only, and what travels over the Wi-Fi is not encrypted: never open it on a public Wi-Fi."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I allow or remove a device, and what can it do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → 🖧 Several computers → Allowed devices: each accepted device, its address, its last visit. “Remove” cuts it off at once (“↶ Undo” for 10 seconds). A guest device sees everything, read-only: it changes nothing, exports nothing, changes neither family tree nor settings, and touches neither the password nor the backups. Each device gives the family tree’s password itself. 📜 Who saw what records the connections with the device’s name. A request without an answer disappears after 2 minutes; after 5 refusals or 5 wrong codes, no more requests for 15 minutes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I send the photos from my phone to the computer over wifi?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the computer: Settings → 🖧 Several computers (door open, password set) → “＋ Add a field phone”: scan the QR code with the phone, compare the 6-digit code on both screens and allow it as a “📱 Field phone”. The phone’s page opens in its browser, with nothing to install: give the family tree’s password (asked again after 15 minutes without activity), then 📷 Take or 🖼 Choose, a line of text, “Who is it for?” (optional), ⤒ Send. The photos arrive in Lists → Media and documents → 📥 To sort, outside the tree: “🔗 Link” each one to a person, an event, a source or the visit, or 🗑 Delete it (kept 30 days), always with “↶ Undo”. The GPS position is removed on arrival (a setting). The phone never sees the tree: for “Who is it for?” it only sees archive visits and deceased people. As with the other computers, what goes over the wifi isn’t encrypted: only use your home wifi, protected by its key."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"And without wifi, at the cemetery or the town hall?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the phone’s page before you leave and don’t close the tab: photos taken without the home wifi wait in the page (in the phone’s browser) and go off by themselves when it finds the wifi again, with a new try every 30 seconds. The page can’t open away from the wifi: that would need a “service worker”, which browsers only allow with HTTPS, and the wifi door has none. The phone can also erase waiting photos if it runs out of space (Safari does so after a few days): rather take the photos with the phone’s camera, then “🖼 Choose” when you’re back. For a whole day at the archives, the visit by file (a .html page taken along, then a .zip brought back) is still there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find out whether an heir has since died (INSEE death file)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"File → 👥 Heirs → “⚰️ Check deaths”: the “living” heirs are compared with the INSEE file already imported, under their birth name (never a married name), with a score out of 100. Nothing changes without your click: “✓ This is the person” records the death (setting), “✕ Not the same person” is remembered. “Nothing found” does not mean “alive”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I send a questionnaire to an heir?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"✉️ Heirs contacted → “📝 Questionnaire…”: tick the heirs and what you are asking. The .html file opens with a double-click, without internet; the heir sends you back their answers. “⤒ Import answers…” shows them field by field: nothing is written without your review, and a modified file is refused."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I note my calls and exchanges with the heirs?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the file, “📞 Contacts” tab (or in 👥 Heirs, depending on the setting): when, with whom, how, the summary and the next step. With a date, a deadline goes into the 🔔 bell (never the summary)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I track a registered letter and its acknowledgement of receipt?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a letter: “📮” → the number, the sending date and the date of the receipt. With no receipt after 15 days (adjustable), the 🔔 bell reminds you. The app does not query La Poste: the link opens the tracking page in your browser."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a quote or an invoice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"💶 Time and costs → “🧾 Make an invoice”: the lines are suggested (commission, costs, time). You type the VAT rate and the legal notes yourself (🏢 My office, with your accountant). Numbers follow each other with no gap; an issued invoice can no longer be changed: a credit note corrects it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see my office’s activity?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⚖️ Estates → “📈 Activity”: open files, average time until handover, contracts signed or refused, invoiced and collected, time per file. No heir names; nothing is written."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I send a copy of the file to the notary?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🖨 For the notary → “🤝 Copy to send to the notary…”: an .html file the notary opens with a double-click, without being able to change it, encrypted with a password if you wish (give it by another channel). The log, contacts and invoices never leave."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see on a map where the heirs live?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"👥 Heirs → “🗺 See on the map” (or ⚖️ Estates → ⋯ for all open files): one dot per municipality, from the Register of the living, and the notaries the app knows. Nothing is sent online."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I use my own revelation contract?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"✉️ Heirs contacted → “📝 My contract templates”: write or import your text (.docx or .txt), with fields like {nom}, {pourcentage}, {siret}. Each version is kept and each filed contract remembers its own. The app provides no contract text."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I track money received from the notary and paid out to the heirs?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"💶 Time and costs → “💶 Funds per heir”: note what the notary’s office paid you, the commission, the costs and what you paid out; the app works out what is still due. No estate amount is kept, only your real movements."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the “double proof” of a link?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"📑 Documents → 🔗 banner: for each parentage link, the number of documents that prove it (two copies of the same record count as one). A link with a single document is “to review” before handover; “✓ One document is enough here” notes it in the log."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get reminded of a date given by the notary?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"📁 File → “＋ An important date”: the date, what has to be done and where it comes from (required). The 🔔 bell warns you at D−30, D−7 and D−1 (adjustable). The app computes no legal deadline."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I bring two files together (a couple who died a few months apart)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the file, ⋯ → “👥 Merge with another file…”: the preview says what happens. Two deceased, two calculations side by side (or files only linked, setting). ↶ Undo separates them again for 30 days."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see the searches made for an heir?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"👥 Heirs → 🕵️ on the row: the notes from the Research log, the INSEE checks and the contacts marked “search”. “Nothing found” counts too; they can be attached to the notary’s copy."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I archive a closed file with a password?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Closed file → ⋯ → “🗄 Archive to a file and remove…”: the file also takes the contacts, the funds and a copy of the invoices, encrypted with a password (setting: offered, required or not offered). Forgotten = archive lost; re-importing asks for it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"An heir died after the deceased: how do I open their estate?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"👥 Heirs → “🔗 Open a linked file” on their row: a new file for them, with their descendants attached (setting); the two files point to each other."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I ask the same office for several records in a single letter?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"📑 Documents → “📮 One letter per office”: the missing records are grouped by town hall; reread each letter, then file them in the Letter register. The documents switch to “Requested”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does a town hall take to answer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⚖️ Estates → ⋯ → “⏳ Response time by office”: from sending to the answer received, based on your record requests (average or median, setting)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get warned that a copy of a record is getting too old?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"📑 Documents → 📅 on the row: the issue date and the validity period your notary asks for (none by default). The 🔔 bell warns you beforehand."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide names and amounts during a video call?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the file, ⋯ → “🙈 Shared screen”: names become initials (or numbers), addresses and amounts are hidden, without leaving the file. A second click restores everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I change the settings of these file tools?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → 🧭 How you use the app → “⚖️ File tools: settings” (or “⚙️ Settings for this tool” in each window). Each choice has a help sentence; the recommended option is the starting one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I note notary offices and how long they take to reply?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Archives and sources → 🏛 Notary offices (or ⚖️ Estates → ⋯): each office, its notaries and clerks, its files and its letters. Mark “✓ Reply received” on a letter: after 3 dated replies, the record shows the observed delay, what you have seen, never a legal time limit."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I typed the notary’s name by hand in my files: how do I link them to the office?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🏛 Notary offices → “🧩 Group them and create their records…”: the app suggests bringing together names that look alike (same surname, same town). Nothing changes before “Apply”, the typed name stays in each file and “↶ Undo” reverses everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the “⚠️ Namesakes” alert appear when I write to an heir?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Another record in your database has the same name, born within a few years in the same département: check that you are contacting the right person. “✓ This is the one I am contacting” notes it in the 🕓 Log; “It is not the same person” silences the alert for these two records."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see all the doubtful points of my family tree at once?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Research and organisation → ❓ All doubts (or “See all →” in My week): checks, open hypotheses, fragile links, proof disagreements, events without a source. Each line is read from the original tool, nothing is copied; “↗” takes you there and “👁 Mark as seen” sets it aside."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I take an archive visit on my phone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Research → What is missing? → “🗄 Prepare a visit”, then in Lists → Archive visits “📱 Take on the phone”: a single .html file that works without a network. On site, tick what you find, take photos and “⤓ Save my results”; back home, “⤒ Bring back the results” checks the .zip and lets you review it shelfmark by shelfmark."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I quickly find a person or a place?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⌘K: type a name, a place, a search sweep or the name of a screen."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I ask a question in plain words?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: Research → Type of search → “Ask a question in plain words”, for example “Which ancestors were bakers in Lille before 1850?”. The search can then be saved. It also understands marriage and children (write in French): “Quelles sont les femmes nées avant 1800 en Indre-et-Loire qui se sont mariées après l’âge de 20 ans ayant eu au moins deux enfants ?”, “jamais mariées” (never married), “remariées” (remarried), “sans enfant” (no children), “avec plus de 5 enfants” (more than 5 children). Age at marriage is the year of the first marriage minus the year of birth: a record missing either one is not counted, and the app says so."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search for a word in the page?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⌘F: the words found are highlighted and counted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I go back to the previous screen?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⌘← and ⌘→ on a Mac, Alt+← and Alt+→ on Windows and Linux. All the shortcuts: F1 key."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make my own list (e.g. all divorces between 1850 and 1920)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → List editor → “＋ New list”: choose events, people, or rows you write yourself (stands at a fair…); combine criteria, choose the columns, add your own columns to fill in (text, menu, checkbox, link, person from the database) and labels. You can also start from a list of the app (Births, Marriages, SOSA…) or from one of your lists, show it in the Lists tab, and share it with a relative (“Share” button, then “Import a list…” on their side). Can be exported as CSV and printed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know what to do today and this week in my research?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Add the “🗓 My research week” widget to the dashboard (or to the Follow-up page; it replaces the old “Daily work list”): everything waiting for you on a single list, the most urgent first — today’s and overdue reminders, letters without a reply (for how many days), upcoming archive visits, open leads and hypotheses, searches suggested by “What’s missing?” (including “search on SocFace”), what needs checking (SocFace readings, 🔴 / 🟠 alerts from the Database check), help requests, and what you did this week. On each line: ↗ to open it, ✓ when it is done, ⏭ to postpone to tomorrow or next week (“↶ Undo” at the bottom left). ✎ to choose “Today” or “This week”, sort by urgency or by kind, hide or reorder the kinds and the number of lines; ⛶ to see everything in large."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which records should I look for first?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Add the “💡 Research suggestions” widget to the dashboard: the gaps in your Sosa line (birth, marriage, death, unknown parents), with the place and the years to search, the closest generations first."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see the history of a village?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Village record: the families, the occupations, the church, the population over the censuses and the Cassini map. ⚙️ “Organise the record” arranges, hides or widens each part; the census plan and a notepad are there too."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide, arrange or add columns in a list?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In all the table lists of the Lists tab, the “⚙️ Columns” button."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the anniversaries to celebrate (Dates to celebrate)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Dashboard → “🎂 Dates to celebrate” widget (the former “Today’s anniversaries”): today, this week or this month. Living people with their age (“Marie is 40”), ancestors “150 years ago”; round anniversaries are highlighted (🎉, golden wedding…). Filters All, Living, Ancestors, ⭐ Sosa; with the pencil ✎: a single branch, only births, marriages or deaths, only round anniversaries. 🔔 on a line sets a reminder (the day before, a week before or on the day) in Lists → Reminders. Confidential records are never there; in view-only mode 🔒, a living person appears only by their first name."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I file an old postcard and find it on the map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “🏞 Postcards” → “＋ New postcard”: the front (the view) and the back (the written side), the publisher, the number, the date sent, the postmark, who wrote it and to whom, the address, the text on the back (“📜 Transcribe in the workshop” for the image of the back), the place shown and, if you like, the direction of the view. The cards in your media sent as “front/back” are already there, marked “To complete”. On the Map, the “🏞 Postcards” button shows them at their location, with a cone for the direction; the place’s page (Municipality monograph) and a village record have a “🏞 Postcards” block."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I say where an old photo or a postcard was taken, when I am not sure?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the image (viewer) → “📍 Where was it taken?”, or Lists → “📍 Images of places” → “＋ Add an image”. Add one or more hypotheses: a place in your database (picker), a precise point on the map, or an area (◯ circle or ⬠ polygon) if you only know the neighbourhood; the street and the address; the direction of the view (🧭, a cone on the map); the certainty (certain, probable, possible, to check) and why (shop sign, bell tower, caption, publisher…). The ★ hypothesis chosen becomes the image’s place everywhere in the app. Also note the date the picture was taken (“about 1905”) and the publisher or photographer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare an old postcard with the same place today?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “📍 Where was it taken?” → “🪟 Compare with today”: choose or import a current photo taken from the same spot. The comparer opens as a curtain (drag the line in the middle of the image) or as a cross-fade. “📌 Place 2 markers per photo” (the top of the bell tower, the corner of a roof) aligns today’s photo with the old one; “Adjust by hand” rotates and enlarges it, and “Drag moves photo B”. “💾 Save” keeps the comparison."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I ask for help to locate an old photo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “📍 Where was it taken?” → “🙋 Ask for help”: the app prepares the text (what can be seen, your leads with their certainty, your questions), to copy onto a forum, Geneanet’s mutual help, a Facebook group (the request is stored in Lists → 🤝 Help requests), or a small ad (stored in ✉️ Small ads). “⤓ Download the image” to attach it. When someone answers, “📨 Note an answer”: their lead becomes a new hypothesis, with their name (↶ Undo possible). Nothing is sent by the app."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see the old images of a street or a village?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A village record and a municipality monograph have an “🖼 Images of the place” block: the images arranged by street, then by period, with the map (areas and directions of view). Lists → “📍 Images of places” also sorts them “By street” or “By period”, with the filters Place, Street, Period, Certainty, Kind, No place. On the Map, the “📍 Images of places” button shows them at their location."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note my appointments and family birthdays?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → My diary: fairs, archive visits, reminders, letters, tours, by day, by week, by month… Lists → Family calendar: the events of the records by day, by month or by year, with an “anniversaries” mode and the date in other calendars (Julian, Republican…)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search the app’s datasets (Lists → Data)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “🧰 Data”: each dataset (castles, schools, fairs, wrecks…) has its list, with the same way of searching everywhere: combined criteria, “📅 Period” (in the year, entirely between, overlapping), “📍 Around” a municipality within N km, free search, sorting, Table or 🗺 Map, ⤓ Export (CSV), 🖨 Print and saved searches (★). A pack not yet installed offers “📦 Install”. “🔍 Search all the data” lists, for a person, a place or a year, every dataset that can say something about it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see what time it is in other cities (world clocks)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Research and organisation → “🕰 World times”: “＋ Add a city” (more than 200 cities, or any time zone, or a city of your own). Each clock shows the time, day or night, and the difference from the time here. “⇄ Compare” puts the cities side by side: click a time here, and its column shows the time in each city (green from 8:00 to 20:00, grey at night). “🎨 Style” sets everything: hands or digits, dial shape, colours, seconds, date."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the time zone map, and who has which time?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"World times → “🗺 Time zone map”: each country at its legal time today (summer time included), large countries split into regions (United States, Canada, Russia, Brazil, Australia…), night shown as a shadow; hover over a country to see its time zone. “Theoretical time zones” shows the 15° bands. “🎨 Time zone colours”: ready-made sets (cold to warm, rainbow, pastel, two-tone school atlas…) or a colour of your own for each time zone. At the bottom, “📋 Which countries and regions for each time zone” shows or hides the list, with a search."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put clocks on the dashboard?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Dashboard → “✎ Customise” → add the “🕰 World clocks” widget. In its settings: the cities to show (empty: those of the World times list) and a style of its own (hands or digits, dial, colours); otherwise it uses the list’s style."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add my own base map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Maps → Map backgrounds → “🗺 ＋ Add a base map”: a name and the tile address (https://…/{z}/{x}/{y}.png; {s} for subdomains, {-y} for the TMS format), from a site that allows it without a key. “👁 Try” shows a tile; the base map appears in “My base maps” in every menu. An address with a key is refused (it would be readable in plain text)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a map with my photos and videos?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “Photo maps” → “＋ New map” (or Maps → “📸 Photo maps” mode: at the top, one badge per map to go from one to another, “🗺 All my maps” and “＋ New map”; the ⚙️ button next to them chooses which ones get a badge: the most recent, the oldest (with their number) or those you pick by hand; the others are in “🔍 N other maps…”, which opens the search). Add photos from the app (“＋ Photos from the app”) or drag files into the “📁 Drag…” area: they go into the media, and those with GPS coordinates (JPEG, HEIC, PNG, WebP photos, MP4 and MOV phone videos) place themselves. The others go into “To place”: drag their thumbnail onto the map, or 📍 to choose a place from your database."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"A photo is in the wrong place on my photo map, how do I fix it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Drag its thumbnail to the right place: “↶ Undo” appears at the bottom left. The original GPS position is kept: ✎ (or right-click the thumbnail) → “↺ Back to GPS”. Several photos in the same place form a cluster with their number: click it (or zoom) to separate them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see my photos over time on the map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Under the map, the timeline shows how many photos there are per period (wheel to zoom: from centuries down to minutes; double-click to see everything). Drag on the timeline to choose a period. “▶ Play” fills the map over time; ⏮ ⏭ step forward. The step is typed freely: “3 d”, “1 month 12 d 3 h”, “2 years 6 months”, “90 min” (or the quick choices 1 h, 1 d, 1 wk, 1 month, 1 year, 10 years); the window is cumulative (everything before stays) or sliding. The exact time of a photo (read from the file, or written “7 May 1925 at 14:30”) is the local time it was taken; a date without a time is placed at noon."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I combine all my photo maps?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: “🗺 All my maps” (in Lists → Photo maps, or in Maps). Each map has its colour; the legend on the right lets you tick and untick the maps, with the same filters (📷 Photos / 🎬 Videos, period, timeline). The “📸 Photo map” widget can also go on the dashboard, in your tabs and in a note."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put the record of a specific village in a widget or a tab?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Widget editor (kind “App screen”) or Tab editor: “Village record…” or “Municipality monograph…” screen, then the commune. Tick the parts to show and arrange them (⠿ or ↑ ↓): this organisation only applies there, the village record in Lists keeps its own."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"A place is in the wrong spot on the map, what should I do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → ✅ Database check, “📍 Places” badge: the app spots unlikely coordinates (0, 0, outside the country, latitude and longitude swapped, namesake in another department), shows the map above the list and offers “✎ Fix” in one click (with “↶ Undo”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose an icon or a base map when there are lots of choices?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click “✎ Change” next to the icon (or the background): a grid opens. Type a word in the 🔍 box (“church”, “boat”, “heart”, “Cassini”…), or click a section at the top; your recent icons come first. The keyboard arrows move around the grid, Enter chooses, Esc closes. For base maps, each thumbnail shows a piece of the background. The “×” of the badge removes the icon when you can do without it. It is the same grid everywhere: event types, base maps, icons of sections, tabs, widgets, the big buttons of simple mode, notes…"}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find a setting in a long Settings page?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The long Settings pages (Keyboard shortcuts, Event types, Appearance, Glossary…) have a 🔍 box just under the title, which stays at the top when you scroll. Type a word (“sosa”, “print”) or a key (“ctrl p”): only the lines that contain it stay, the letters found are highlighted, the frames without a result fold up into a grey line. “/” puts the cursor in the box, Esc or × empties it, “Show all” brings everything back. Nothing found? “🔍 Search all Settings” takes the word to the box in the left column."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the base map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → map settings: Cassini, État-Major, IGN, satellite, old cadastre… A village record also has its own choice."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long did it take to go from one village to another in the old days?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → “🧭 Tools ▾” → “Distances and travel times”: choose the start and the destination (or a person, to follow their movements) and the year. You see the distance and the duration on foot, on horseback, by stagecoach, by boat and by train, with a sentence like “It took about 4 days to go from Tulle to Paris in 1820”. The speeds come from historians (Braudel, Arbellot, Studeny, Caron…): the sources are in “⚙️ Speeds and sources”, where you can also change them. On the record, the “🧭 Their journeys” block appears as soon as the person lived in two places placed on the map."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I print a large family map (A3, A2, A1, A0)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → “🧭 Tools ▾” → “Large map to print”: format A3 to A0, portrait or landscape, 5 styles (old, État-Major, modern, black and white, midnight blue and gold), cartouche (title, family, dates), legend, compass rose, scale, places of birth, marriage and death, migrations, a single branch, portraits in medallions. “⤓ Export…” gives a PDF, a PNG, a JPG or an SVG; “🖨 Print” opens the print window. Living people and confidential records are never on it. The background is drawn by the app (Natural Earth, IGN), without Internet tiles: their terms of use forbid downloading them in bulk for printing, and a drawing stays sharp at every size."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the relief around the family villages in 3D?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → “🧭 Tools ▾” → “3D relief”: choose the centre place and the radius, set the exaggeration of the relief; the family’s places are pins. Drag to rotate, wheel to zoom; with the keyboard ← → ↑ ↓, + and −, 0 to go back. The altitudes come from the relief map supplied with Weather of the past (14 European countries): elsewhere, the app tells you there is no altitude for this area."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I draw my own areas, paths and notes on the map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → “✏️ My layers”: create a layer, then 📍 Point, 📝 Note, 〰 Route or ⬠ Area and click on the map (Enter or double-click to finish, Esc to stop). Each layer has its colour, can be shown or hidden, and its ⋯ offers Rename, Frame, ⤓ Export as GeoJSON or KML, Delete (with “↶ Undo”). “⤒ Import…” takes a GeoJSON or KML file (Google Earth, uMap…). Click a shape to edit or delete it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a custom map (certain data, between two dates, for certain people)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → “🗺 My maps” → “＋ New map”: give it a name, tick the first layers. In “⚙️ Layers”, add what you want to see (births, marriages, deaths, burials, residences, censuses, migrations and journeys, a person’s places of life, surnames, occupations, houses, images of places, war memorials, sources, placed media, tracked animals, historical events, your drawn or imported layers) and open each layer to filter it: between two dates, one or more people with their ancestors or descendants over N generations, Sosa, father’s or mother’s side, branch, labels, around a place or within a drawn area, certainty, kinds of events. Each layer has its style (colour, shape, size, clustering, heat map, place names). Everything is kept straight away, with “↶ Undo”. A “🖼 Images of places” map is ready from the start."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I run time on one of my maps, and share it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top of one of my maps: ▶ runs through the years (everything up to the year, or a window of N years: ⚙️ Layers → “Time”), the slider chooses a year, “All” brings back all the years. “⤓ Export ▾”: image or PDF, GeoJSON, KML; 🖨 prints it; “⋯” puts it on the dashboard, in a layout book or tells you where to find it in My website (“🗺 One of my maps” block). What leaves the app never shows a living person or a confidential record. “💾 Share the definition” gives a file with the settings only (layers, filters, styles), never the data."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find my ancestors in the 1836 to 1936 censuses (SocFace)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The SocFace project used artificial intelligence to read the nominal lists of the 1836 to 1936 censuses; they are in FranceArchives’ “name database – censuses”, department by department. On a person’s record (Censuses block), in Research → “🧾 SocFace censuses” or in “Search the Internet” → “🔍 Online archives…”: the common window opens on the FranceArchives line, with “Also: 🧾 Censuses 1836-1936 (SocFace)” and the census years. The surname, first name, commune and years (between birth and death) are filled in; the search opens on FranceArchives in your browser. The app never connects to that site: only the address you see is opened. “What’s missing?” also suggests one line per census (“1906 census in Tulle”) with the link ready and “✓ Found / Not found”, noted in the Research log."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import a census found on FranceArchives (SocFace)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On FranceArchives, “Download the entry as CSV” (one person) or “Export the results” (a search); you can also copy the whole text of an entry (with “See the other members of the household”) and use “📋 Paste the text of an entry”. Then “⤒ Import SocFace censuses”: “Here is what will come in” shows each household, and for each person “Is this the same person?” with the close records from your database. Nothing is linked without your choice, nothing is replaced in your records, a household already imported does not come in twice. Each census gets its source (link to the entry and to the image, with the note “Automatic SocFace transcription — may contain errors: check on the image”) and the “TO CHECK” badge until you tick “Checked on the image”. “↶ Undo” removes the whole import. The FranceArchives CSV does not say who lives with whom: people with the same surname, commune and year are put together (untick those who do not belong); the text copied from an entry gives the real household."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the app tell me “SocFace reads born about 1922, your record says 1921”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"After the import, what SocFace reads (year and place of birth, sex, relationship to the head of household) is compared with your linked records. The differences appear on the record, in the import preview and in Settings → Data quality → “✅ Database check” (kind “🧾 SocFace census”, 🟡 curious or 🟠 to check, never 🔴: the machine can be wrong, and ages declared at censuses were often approximate). The tolerated difference on the year is set in “⚙️ Check thresholds”. Look at the image: if your record is right, “This is not a mistake”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What mistakes can automatic reading make (SocFace, ✨ Read the record)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A machine reading handwriting always makes the same kinds of mistakes. Letters that look alike: u and n (“Lavand” for “Lavaud”), m and rn or nn, a and o, e and c (“Jcan” for “Jean”), i, l and t, the long s ſ and f, v and r, cl and d, h and b, and old capitals S/L, I/J, T/F. Digits: 1 and 7, 3 and 8, 5 and 6, 0, 6 and 9, 4 and 9 — hence a son older than his father, a mother aged 8, a birth after the census. Shifted columns: an occupation in the first-name box, a commune in the surname box, an empty or absurd relationship (“son” for a woman, two “heads” in one household), a household cut in two at a page break. Abbreviations not expanded: “cult.” (farmer), “jlier” (day labourer), “dom.” (servant), “Vve” (widow), “id.” or “d°” (ditto: the same as on the line above). “🔍 Check this SocFace line” shows each box read, why it is suspicious (🟡 curious, 🟠 to check), one or more suggestions (“Lavaud?”) drawn from your records, the household, first names and communes, and the link to the image: nothing is corrected without your click, and “↶ Undo” goes back. The proofreading of “✍️ Read handwriting (fast)” spots abbreviations and names close to those in your database in the same way."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I search for an ancestor in online archives? (one window for all the sites)","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, “Search the Internet” block → “🔍 Online archives…” (or a button in a block: Poilu, Military, Departures, Child in care, In the media, Awards, the record of a place, an employer, an object…). A single window brings together all the sites (Mémoire des hommes, Gallica, Archives nationales, FamilySearch, European archives, libraries…), one line per site, arranged by country then by theme: the badges say how many there are (“🇫🇷 France (69)”, “🎖 Soldiers and wars (15)”), the 🔍 box finds a site, “Free only” hides the paying ones, “For:” changes the person. A block button opens the window directly on its section (🎯 badge); “All” shows the rest. Each address is written as it will open in your browser; the app never connects to these sites. Then “⤒ Import what you found ▾” (or the small ⤒ on the line) opens the import that goes with that site, and “🔗 Paste a link” recognises the site of a pasted link and opens the right import."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find a soldier “Mort pour la France” on Mémoire des hommes?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On his record, “Poilu 14-18” block (or “Search the Internet”) → “🔍 Mémoire des hommes”: the “Search the online archives” window prepares the search with his surname and first name for each database (Morts pour la France 1914-1918 and 1939-1945, war graves, deceased soldiers, sailors, his unit’s war diary) and opens it in your browser; the app never connects to the site. Then save the record found (image or PDF) or copy its link, and “⤒ Import what you found”: the document is stored in the media and linked to the record, a source is created with the link, and the app suggests the death “Mort pour la France”, the “Mort pour la France” box and his unit in the Poilu block — only if the record does not already have them (nothing is replaced, “↶ Undo” removes everything). What you read on these sites needs checking (namesakes)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find a Legion of Honour file (Léonore database)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, “🏆 Competitions and awards” block → “🔍 Léonore”: the search opens in your browser with their surname and first name (the Archives nationales’ Léonore database has the files of members who died before 1977). Download the pages of the file (image or PDF), then “⤒ Import…”: copy the rank, the date of appointment, the decree and the file number (reference) alongside; a “🎖 Decoration” award is added to the block, with the document and a source that keeps the link. To check on the file."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find an ancestor’s military register, and what is his class?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On his record, “Military” or “Poilu 14-18” block → “🪖 Military register”: the app calculates his class (the year he turned 20: born in 1892 → class of 1912) and the department where he lived at 20, then opens the website of that department’s Archives (list of departmental Archives in the app) or FranceArchives’ name database. The registers are arranged by class then by recruitment office. Then import the page (image): you copy alongside the number, the class, the office, the description (hair, eyes, height, education), the campaigns, the wounds and the successive addresses; only the empty boxes of the Military block are filled in."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find a naturalisation decree, a notarial deed, a sailor, a deportee or the description of a church?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, “Search the Internet” block → “🔍 Online archives…” (also in the Military, Departures, Probate inventory, Resistance blocks, on a place’s monograph, a house or a family object): Archives nationales (naturalisations 1883-1930, Minutier central), overseas archives (colonies, convicts), Service historique de la Défense (registered seamen, officers), Mémorial de la Shoah, Livre-Mémorial of deportees, Maitron of those shot, Journal officiel, old directories, Mérimée and Palissy, Belgium, Switzerland, Ellis Island (free account). The search opens in your browser with the fields filled in: the app never connects to these sites. Save the document, then “⤒ Import what you found”: it goes into the media with its source, and the app suggests what it says (naturalisation, marriage contract, inventory, departure, deportation…) after “is this the same person?”. Nothing is replaced, everything stays “To check”, and “↶ Undo” removes everything. A person who was deported or shot no longer appears in the games."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search for an ancestor in old newspapers (Gallica, RetroNews, Europeana)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, “📰 In the media” block → “🔍 Old press”: the full-text search opens in your browser with their name, the place and the period. Gallica (BnF) is free; RetroNews is partly paying (some newspapers and full reading require a subscription); Europeana brings together newspapers from all over Europe. To keep an article: image, PDF, screenshot or saved page, or “🔗 Paste a link” (the newspaper, the date and the page are filled in when the address gives them) → an “📰 In the media” entry with the newspaper, the date, the page, the link and the copied text. If a site changes its address: “⚙️ Site addresses”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find an ancestor on a war memorial (MémorialGenWeb, 1870, civilian victims)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, “Poilu 14-18” block: “🗿 War memorial (MémorialGenWeb)”; or “Search the Internet” → “🔍 Online archives…”, “Wars and memorials” section. You will also find the dead of the 1870-1871 war and the civilian victims of 1939-1945 there. The search opens in your browser (the app never connects to these sites). Then “⤒ Import the record…”: the photo of the memorial or the saved page goes into the media, a source is created with the link, the death and the unit are suggested (only if they are missing), everything stays “To check” and “↶ Undo” removes everything. In a municipality monograph, “🗿 War memorial” opens its memorial. The addresses of these sites could not be checked: if one no longer works, change it in “⚙️ Site addresses”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find an ancestor’s grave and keep its photo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, “Cemetery” block: “🔍 Search for the grave” opens Cimetières de France, BillionGraves (with the GPS position) or Geneanet’s grave surveys in your browser, with the name and years filled in. Save the photo, then “⤒ Import a grave photo…”: it goes into the media and into the burial’s “Dated photos”; if the record does not have a burial yet, the app offers to create it (cemetery, commune, plot, GPS) and the interment if it is missing. Nothing is replaced; “↶ Undo” removes everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find a village’s population since 1793 and its old names (Cassini, EHESS)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → Municipality monograph → “🏘 Population since 1793 and old names”. “↗ Open the commune on the EHESS site” (“Des villages de Cassini aux communes d’aujourd’hui”) opens your browser; save the page or copy the table, then “⤒ Import the table…”, or “✎ Copy by hand” (“1793: 512”, one year per line; “✓ Understood: …” below). A year already noted is never replaced; “↶ Undo” removes the import. The old names are matched with INSEE’s changes since 1943 (old borders supplied with the app). The monograph then shows the population curve."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where did my surname live between 1891 and 1990 (Géopatronyme)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “Search the Internet” (record): “Géopatronyme”; or in Charts → Surname map: choose a name, then “🔤 This name on Géopatronyme (1891-1990) ↗”. The map of births by department opens in your browser; the app does not connect to it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search the registers of Europe (Matricula, Norway, Sweden, Denmark, Ireland, Bohemia, Hungary, JewishGen) and among Huguenots or seafarers?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record, “Search the Internet” → “🔍 Online archives…”, “Europe · Registers and archives” section (Matricula is searched by place: the parish) or “Protestants and seafarers” (Huguenot refugees, merchant navy crew lists). Everything is free; JewishGen requires creating a free account. Then “⤒ Import what you found”: the image of the record or the saved page goes into the media, a source is created with the link and the page, and the record is suggested (baptism, burial, census, emigration…; never a second baptism or death). Pasted links from Matricula, Digitalarkivet, Riksarkivet or irishgenealogy.ie are recognised (register, page)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import an article from a foreign newspaper (Chronicling America, Trove, Papers Past, Delpher)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, “📰 In the media” block: “🌍 Foreign press” opens the search in your browser (name and years filled in). Then “⤒ Import (foreign)…”: as for Gallica, the article becomes an “In the media” entry with its source and its link; the date and the page are read from the link when it gives them (Chronicling America, Papers Past). “↶ Undo” removes everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find a prisoner of the Great War (Red Cross, ICRC)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, “Military” or “Poilu 14-18” block: “🌍 Prisoners 14-18, Arolsen…” (or “Search the Internet” → “🔍 Online archives…”). The ICRC site (grandeguerre.icrc.org) does not keep the name in its address: “📋 Copy the name and open” puts “SURNAME First name” on the clipboard and opens its search page; paste it into the box. Then copy the text of their entry or save the image, then “⤒ Import…”: the ICRC entry goes into the media, a source is created with the link, the “Prisoner of war” event (date and place of capture, camp) and their unit in the Poilu block are suggested. Nothing is replaced; “↶ Undo” removes everything. The app never connects to the site."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find out whether an ancestor was a deputy, a senator, an inventor, a doctor or a Polytechnique graduate?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“Search the Internet” → “🔍 Online archives…”, or the buttons in the “🔬 Science, inventions”, “🎓 Diplomas and education” and “🧩 This person was…” blocks: Sycomore (former deputies), former senators, invention patents 1791-1902 (INPI), medical theses (Sudoc, BIU Santé), registers of the École polytechnique, directory of learned societies (CTHS). When importing a copied entry: a patent becomes a 💡 invention with its patent (the “Inventor” profile is ticked), a thesis or Polytechnique a 🎓 diploma, a term of office a 🏛 mandate (political profile). Everything stays “To check” and can be undone with “↶ Undo”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I search in Paris, in museums and in local history books?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Village record: “🌍 Books and archives on this place…” opens Internet Archive and Persée on the name of the place (local history books and journals), and, for Paris, the reconstructed civil registers (before 1860) and the electoral lists (1860-1932) of the Archives de Paris. The Joconde database (museums of France) searches for an artist by name. For a book or article found, “⤒ Import…” stores it as a source with its link."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search for an ancestor in Italy, Spain, Portugal, the Netherlands, Flanders or Poland?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…” (record, “Departures / emigration” block or village record): Antenati (search by name, in addition to the search sweeps’ link), PARES (Spain), Torre do Tombo (Portugal), Open Archives (Netherlands, Flanders), Geneteka (Poland). Open Archives publishes its data: “⤓ the results as JSON” opens in your browser; save the file then “⤒ Import…”: each line (baptism, death…) becomes an event if you tick it; a marriage stays written in the source, for you to place in the family yourself."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search in England (FreeBMD, FreeCEN), Canada or the United States?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…”: FreeBMD (births, marriages, deaths since 1837) and FreeCEN (censuses 1841-1911) are searched with a form: the name is copied, paste it into the box. Then copy the FreeBMD results page and “🔗 Paste a link”: each line read (quarter, district, volume, page) can be ticked to become an event. In Canada: BAnQ and Library and Archives Canada; in the United States: NARA and Find a Grave (the text copied from a grave suggests the burial, the cemetery and the memorial number)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search the archives of Nazi persecution (Arolsen) and among the Righteous Among the Nations?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…”, “Memory of the Shoah and of persecution” section: Arolsen Archives (documents from the camps and displacements) and Yad Vashem (Righteous Among the Nations). An imported Arolsen document stays sober on the record, and that record is never used in a game (like those of deportees). A Righteous Among the Nations becomes an award in “🏆 Competitions and awards”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where are the addresses of the online archive sites? (an address no longer works, hide a site, add my own)","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A single screen for all the sites: in the “🔍 Search the online archives” window, at the bottom left, “⚙️ Site addresses”. The sites are arranged by country then by theme, in sections that fold up; the 🔍 box finds a site. Write the new address with {nom}, {prénom}, {lieu}, {de}, {à}, {département}… in place of what is being searched; the switch shows or hides the site; “📒 My genealogy society” gives the address of your society for each department; “⭐ My sites” adds your own (it is also in “Search the Internet”). Everything is kept straight away (“↶ Undo”, “↺ Reset to default”), and the settings made before in each window are carried over. “Free only” (ticked at first) hides the paying sites. The badge says whether the app’s address has been checked, partly checked or not yet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search FamilySearch and WikiTree from a record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“Search the Internet” block of the record → “🔍 Online archives…”: FamilySearch’s indexed records (surname, first name, place and years of birth filled in), its catalogue (which registers of a commune have been filmed) and WikiTree. FamilySearch requires a free account to see the results and the images. If the record has a FamilySearch identifier, its entry in the tree opens directly. The app never queries these sites: only the address is opened in your browser. Then “⤒ Import what you found” (or “🔗 Paste a link”): the source with the link, the missing events, and the FamilySearch identifier if there was none. A whole FamilySearch or WikiTree tree is imported as GEDCOM (Home → Import)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find an ancestor who left for Quebec or Acadia?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“Departures / emigration” block of the record → “🌍 Quebec, Castle Garden…”: the Fichier Origine (the French who settled in Quebec from 1600 to 1865: baptism in France, parents, marriage in Canada) and the Centre d’études acadiennes in Moncton. Copy the entry found and use “⤒ Import…”: the baptism is added if it is missing, and a departure for New France goes into “Departures / emigration”, with the entry number. Nothing is replaced, everything stays “To check”, “↶ Undo” removes everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find an ancestor who arrived in New York (Castle Garden)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“Departures / emigration” block → “🌍 Quebec, Castle Garden…” → Castle Garden (arrivals from 1820 to 1892, before Ellis Island). Copy the passenger’s line (ship, port of departure, arrival date, age, occupation) and “⤒ Import…”: a journey is added to the “Departures / emigration” block, and a birth “about …” based on their age if the record has none."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Did my ancestor receive the Saint Helena Medal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The soldiers of the Revolution and the Empire (1792-1815) still alive in 1857 received it: about 400,000 names. “Military” or “🏆 Competitions and awards” block → “🌍 Saint Helena medallists…” (offered if the person was born before 1810). “⤒ Import…” adds the medal to “🏆 Competitions and awards”, their birth if it is missing and their 1857 address."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find an ancestor in a factory’s staff registers (Archives du monde du travail)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Employers → the employer’s record → “🏭 Staff registers (ANMT)…”: the Archives nationales du monde du travail in Roubaix keep the archives of many companies, searched by name. “⤒ Import…” (kind “Staff register”) adds to the record an occupation “Employer: …” (the person then appears in “The family at this employer”) and a line in the employer’s timeline."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I search for a railway worker, a gendarme or a police officer who died on duty?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“Search the Internet” → “🔍 Online archives…”, “France — soldiers, railway workers, gendarmes, police officers” section. The exact addresses of these memorials could not be checked: the app launches a Google search of the name with the occupation; if you know the address of the memorial, write it in “⚙️ Site addresses”. On import, the death “Died on duty” is only suggested if the record does not already have a death."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find a record of a French person born, married or who died abroad (consular civil registration)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The registers of consulates and embassies are at the Archives diplomatiques in Nantes; they cannot be searched by name online. “🔍 Online archives…” → “Archives diplomatiques de Nantes” opens a FranceArchives search and copies the name for a request. The record received is imported as a “Record”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find the history of a village or a department (Geneawiki)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a commune’s monograph (Charts) and on a village record: the “📖 Geneawiki: …” buttons open the page of the commune and that of the department on Geneawiki (online registers, archives, history, transcriptions)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search for a Soviet soldier or a victim of repression in the USSR?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…”, “Russia and USSR” section: Pamyat Naroda (soldiers of 1941-1945; write the name in Cyrillic for better results) and Memorial (victims of political repression). What comes from Memorial stays sober on the record (“Political repression (USSR)”) and a record that carries it never goes into a game, as for deportees."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I date a photo using the photographer’s name?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Treasures → “📷 Date an old photo” → “📷 The photographer…”: write the name and town printed on the back of the card, open the Daguerre database, Portrait Sépia or Gallica’s old directories, then paste the line that gives their years of activity (“active from 1862 to 1895”): “Put these years in the photo” adds them to the clues."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I search for the biography of an activist, a trade unionist or an elected official (Maitron)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…” → “Maitron”: the whole biographical dictionary of the labour and social movement (not just those who were shot). Copy the entry and “⤒ Import…”: it becomes a note on the record, with the birth and death if they are missing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find manuscripts or private archives about an ancestor (CCFr, Calames)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…”, “France — manuscripts and private archives” section: the CCFr (manuscripts and collections of libraries) and Calames (universities). “⤒ Import…” (kind “Archive collection”) creates the source with the reference and the library, and a note if you tick it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search the archives of 30 European countries (Archives Portal Europe)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“Search the Internet” block of the record → “🔍 Online archives…” → “🇪🇺 Europe” section: the search opens in your browser, with the name and the place already written. The portal searches the inventories of the archives of 30 countries: it tells you in which collection and under which reference a register is. The app never connects to the site. Then “⤒ Import what you found” stores the document, creates the source and suggests the record (nothing is replaced, “↶ Undo” removes everything)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find out whether an ancestor was recognised as “Righteous Among the Nations”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…” → “🇫🇷 France — Righteous and war memorials” section → “Comité français pour Yad Vashem” (the name is copied for the site’s box). If you find their entry, “⤒ Import” adds the distinction to “🏆 Competitions and awards”, soberly, with the file and the note “to check”. A record recognised as “Righteous” no longer appears in the games or in the playable Treasures."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find the file of a foundling or a child in public care (Assistance publique)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On their record, “👶 Child in care” block → “👶 Search for their file”: Archives de Paris (foundlings and children in care) and, for the other departments, FranceArchives’ inventories. “⤒ Import the file…” stores the image, creates the source and adds a placement (register number, admission, wet nurse) next to those already noted; a placement with the same register number is kept as it is."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep the photo of a commune’s war memorial (MonumentsMorts, University of Lille)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → the commune’s monograph → “🌍 European archives, memorial, old cadastre, society…” → “MonumentsMorts” (the commune is copied). Save the photo, then “⤒ Import for this place…”: it is linked to the place (and to a record if you choose one), with its source. From a record’s “Military” block: “🗿 War memorial”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find the free transcriptions of a department’s genealogy society?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…” → “📒 Genealogy society transcriptions” section. Write the address of your society for each department in “⚙️ Site addresses” → “My genealogy society, by department”: it will open for anyone born in that department. Without an address, the app opens a search “genealogy society + department”, or the directory of the Fédération française de généalogie."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find out which registers exist for a village abroad (FamilySearch Wiki, GenWiki, GOV)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → the place’s monograph → “🧭 How to search here (FamilySearch Wiki)”: the wiki says, for each country and often each parish, which registers exist, where they are and which are online. For Germany and Central Europe, the “🧭 Help searching in a place” section adds GenWiki (one page per village) and GOV (the old names and parishes of a place)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search for a German ancestor (CompGen, Archion)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…” → “🇩🇪 Germany” section: the village family books (CompGen, free) and Archion, the registers of Protestant parishes, marked PAYING (subscription). Untick “Free only” to see the paying sites: they always stay at the end."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see the old cadastre of a village in Bohemia, Moravia or Austria-Hungary?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Place monograph → “🌍 European archives, memorial, old cadastre…” → “🗺 Central Europe”: Archivní mapy (cadastre of 1824-1843, free) and Mapire (maps of the Habsburg empire, partly paying). The name of the place is copied for the site’s box. Save the plan, then “⤒ Import for this place…” → “Cadastre plan”: it is linked to the place with its source."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search Hungarian newspapers (Arcanum ADT)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…” → “🗺 Central Europe” section → “Arcanum ADT” (PARTLY PAYING: the search is free, reading often requires a subscription). An article found is imported like those from Gallica: it goes into the record’s “📰 In the media”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I use Ancestry for free?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, but only in a library: many libraries and departmental Archives offer “Ancestry Library Edition” on their computers. “🔍 Online archives…” → “🏛 In the library” prepares the search (“Free in the library” badge); open it on site. At home, the “Ancestry” button opens the paying version."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add my own site to “Search the Internet”, try it and share it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → “Search the Internet” → “My sites”: the site’s name, its search address with {nom}, {prenom}, {lieu}, {annee_naissance}, {annee_deces}, {de}, {a}, {conjoint} or {departement} in place of what is searched for, its section and an icon. “🧪 Try” shows the address filled in with the open record (or an example) and opens it. “💾 Saved settings” keeps your list under a name; ⋯ → Export makes it a file to give away, which is opened with “⤒ Import”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find old books and newspapers about my region (Normannia, Yroise, Rosalis, Numelyo…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a village record (or a commune’s monograph), under the figures: the app suggests the digital library of its region — Numelyo and Lectura Plus (Auvergne-Rhône-Alpes), Rosalis (Toulouse, Occitanie), Mémoire vive (Besançon, Bourgogne-Franche-Comté), Normannia (Normandy), Yroise (Brest, Brittany). “↗” opens the search in your browser with the name of the place; “📚 Other libraries…” shows all the others, arranged by region. From a record: “Search the Internet” → “🔍 Online archives…”. If the site’s page finds nothing, “search this site with Google”. A page found is imported with “⤒ Import…”: it is linked to the place (or the person), with its source “to check”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"My ancestor wrote a book: how do I find their author record (IdRef, data.bnf.fr)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record of a person who published (a book or a work in their record) or who has an artist, writer, scholar, inventor… profile, the “✍️ IdRef · data.bnf (author)” button appears in “Search the Internet” and in the blocks of their profiles. The author record opens in your browser. Copy its text, or save it (IdRef: “Download” as XML or JSON; data.bnf: “Save as…”), then “⤒ Import…”: the app reads their dates and places of birth and death, shows you “Is this the same person?”, suggests the birth and death only if they are missing (nothing is replaced), and adds the link to their “Online records”. “↶ Undo” removes everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search for a name in transcribed old texts (Wikisource)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“Search the Internet” → “🔍 Online archives…” → “Wikisource — search in the texts”: the full-text search opens with the name in quotation marks (and the place if you have written it). Wikisource transcribes books, directories, newspapers: a notable ancestor may be mentioned there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"My ancestor was a sailor: where are his maritime registration records?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…” → “Seafarers” section: write his home port (Saint-Malo, Fécamp, Sète…). The app says which maritime district the quarter belonged to, which centre of the Service historique de la Défense (Cherbourg, Brest, Lorient, Rochefort, Toulon) keeps its registers, and gives the link to the departmental Archives of the port, where many registers have been transferred. This is an indicative breakdown: check on the site. What you find is imported as “Registered seaman” (Military block)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I read a reference from the Minutier central of Paris notaries (MC/ET/XLVIII/312)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"MC = Minutier central, ET = étude (office), then the number of the office in Roman numerals (XLVIII = 48), then the article (the bundle). “🔍 Online archives…” → “Paris notaries” opens the search at the Archives nationales and the Salle des inventaires virtuelle; a record found is imported as “Notarial deed (Minutier central)”: marriage contract, probate inventory, will."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search for a Finnish ancestor in HisKi?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…” → “Finland” → “📋 Copy the name and open”: HisKi opens; choose the parish and paste the name. First names are in Swedish or Finnish there (Johan / Juho, Maria / Maija). Then copy the table of results, then “🔗 Paste a link”: each line becomes a record to tick (baptism → birth and baptism, burial → death and interment). A marriage is never added by itself: it stays in the source."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find a trial in London (Old Bailey, 1674-1913)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🔍 Online archives…” → “United Kingdom” → “Old Bailey Online”: the search opens with their name. Copy the text of the trial (or save the page), then “⤒ Import”: the app reads the reference (t17800628-33 = 28 June 1780), the crime, the verdict and the sentence, translated, and suggests an event “Defendant (or Victim, Witness) at the trial” with the source."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why don’t Ancestry, MyHeritage, Filae or ScotlandsPeople appear any more?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because “Free only” is ticked at first: the paying sites are hidden. Untick it (in “Search the Internet”, in the “🔍 Search the online archives” window or in Settings → Appearance → Search the Internet) to see them: they always come at the end, with the “PAYING” badge and a sentence saying what is paid for. Geneanet stays shown: it is free, only its Premium part is paying (“PARTLY PAYING” badge)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the addresses of a census on a plan?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Surveys (“Plan” tab) or a village record: the households are placed from their address, for the year of your choice. Old street names and vanished streets are drawn by hand; favourite base maps are chosen in Settings → Appearance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I prepare a field trip?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Field tours: the stops (town halls, cemeteries, archives), the logistics and the practicalities of each. Lists → Archive visit planner for the references to consult."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note a cadastral parcel (a plot of land, a meadow, the ancestors’ house)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Places and houses → “🗺 Cadastre” → “＋ Add a parcel”. Choose the commune, write the section (“AB”, “B”) and the number as they appear on the deed, the register or the plan, then “🔍 Search”: the outline, the area (in m² and ares) and the number come by themselves. You can also “🗺 Click on the map”. Then link the owners and occupants (with a role and dates: “from 1834 to 1872”), the house from the House tool, deeds of sale or division, an extract from the register, the scanned plan, a source. The parcel appears on the person’s record (“🗺 Owner of…”), the house’s and the village’s."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What can be retrieved from the cadastre, and under what licence?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everything the app fetches is free of rights and free of charge, with no account or key: today’s cadastral plan (DGFiP, published by IGN: “Parcellaire Express”, Licence Ouverte 2.0 — you may reuse it by citing “IGN / DGFiP”) for the outline, the section, the number and the area (IGN’s API Carto, with the Géoplateforme as a backup); the nearest present-day address (Base Adresse Nationale, Licence Ouverte 2.0); sales since 2020 (“Demandes de valeurs foncières”, Etalab, Licence Ouverte 2.0: date, price, kind of land, never a name). It is your computer that asks, with only the commune, the section and the number (or a point on the map): no person’s name is sent. The answers are kept for 30 days. NOT free: the names of today’s owners (land files, restricted) and, often, the images of old plans held by the archives (conditions specific to each department)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I note who inherited what (will, division, probate inventory)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Media and documents → “🏠 Inheritances and estates” → “＋ New deed”. Choose the kind (will, division, probate inventory, gift, sale between heirs, marriage contract with the dowry, estate declaration), the date as on the deed, the notary, the office, the place, the reference and the source; a probate inventory already noted can be linked too. In “The people”: ⭐ the deceased (or the donor), ★ the heirs and beneficiaries, ☆ the others (witnesses, guardian…), each with their role (heir, legatee, usufructuary…). In “The property”: a house (House tool), a parcel (Cadastre), a family object, or land, a sum of money, furniture written by hand, with the value, the share (“one third”, “in usufruct”) and the “To whom” boxes (nothing ticked: to all the heirs). The images of the deed go through the image window. What you note follows the deeds: the app gives no legal advice."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the chain of owners of a house or a family object?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the house (House tool, “🏠 House” tab → “Chain of owners”) or the object (Lists → Family objects → Edit): a timeline arranges, year after year, each deed that passes it on (from whom → to whom, the share, the notary); for an object, the “Transmission” steps noted on the object are mixed in. “＋ New deed for this house” creates the deed with the property already chosen. On a person’s record, the “🏠 What they received / passed on” block (Settings → Appearance → record blocks) sums up their inheritances."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the division diagram of an inheritance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a deed’s page (Lists → “🏠 Inheritances and estates”), “🔀 Division diagram” draws as a flow who gives what property to whom: by value when each item has a sum in the same currency, otherwise one item = one share (divided equally). “📊 Edit in My diagrams” takes it into the diagram editor to dress it up or export it. Next to a sum in livres, sous or francs, “≈ … € today” gives an order of magnitude (the calculation of the 🪙 Old money converter, which opens with a click on the 🪙 next to the sum), not an exact value."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the parcels on the map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → “▦ Parcels” switch: today’s cadastral plan is shown over the background (zoom in close enough), with your parcels in blue (a click opens one). “📍 Parcel here” then a click on the map: the parcel at that spot, which you can add to your list. The “Cadastre parcels (IGN)” background also exists in the list of backgrounds (Overlay mode), and the “▦ Parcels” switch in a village’s address plan."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get the Napoleonic plan (old cadastre) of a commune?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The first cadastre (called “Napoleonic”, around 1810-1850) and its registers are in the departmental archives: the parcel’s page gives the link to the department archives’ website and a search on FranceArchives (links without guarantee: sites change). Many departments show them online; the conditions for reuse vary from one department to another (often: free personal use, publication on request). Download or photograph the section, then on the parcel’s page “＋ Align an old plan”: the image goes into the media, and you click 2 points (or more, which is better: the church, a crossroads, the corner of a house) on the image and then at the same spot on today’s map. The plan is laid on the Map with its transparency (“My old map”), and it is linked to the parcel. The old number is noted in “Old number”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"The parcel in my deed no longer exists in today’s cadastre?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Parcels have often been divided, merged or renumbered (land consolidation). Note the old number (“B 245 (1834)”) and the locality name, without an outline, or look on the map for today’s parcel at the same spot. “↻ Read online again” updates the outline and area of a parcel already noted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"A foreign place is not offered?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The cities of the world are prepared once at first launch (a few minutes). A place that cannot be found can always be typed by hand."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I locate at once the places that have no position on the map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → “📍 Places to locate” (or the dashboard → “Municipalities to complete” → 📍 Locate…, or Lists → Places): the places without a position, grouped by likely municipality thanks to the municipality reference shipped with the app. “✓ Locate here” for one place, “☑ Review and locate in a batch…” for several (only the “sure” ones are ticked); “↶ Undo” puts everything back. Nothing is located without your click, and nothing is searched online unless you allow it in ⚙️ Settings, place by place."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make candlesticks with the births, deaths or marriages in my database?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"New diagram → “🕯 Candlesticks” → the “From the app” tab → “Chart”: “Births per year”, “Deaths per year” or “Marriages per year”. Each candle is a decade: the number for the first year (open), the one for the last year (close), the lowest year and the highest. A very tall wick in the deaths shows a crisis year. Known wars and epidemics are added as bands. Everything stays editable in the “Data” tab."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create a territory from municipalities?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Map → “🧩 My territories” box → “manage” (or, in the vector drawing, Plan menu → “🧩 Territory from towns…”). Choose the municipalities by searching a name or a code, by clicking on the map, “all the municipalities of” a département, an arrondissement or a canton, or from the places in your database: their shared borders disappear. Give it a name, a colour, hatching, a label; it is kept and can be changed later. It can be placed on the Map, in the diagram maps and in the drawing, and serves as an “in the territory” filter in the Lists."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put open data on a map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a diagram map (Charts → Maps): “＋ Data”; in the vector drawing: Plan menu → “＋ Open data on the plan (earthquakes, castles, places…)…”. Choose a source (a dataset from the Lists, your territories, the country borders of a given year, the places in your database, earthquakes, weather stations…), filters (text, years, category, around a place, in a territory) and a style. The layer has its legend line and the credit of its source, is kept with the diagram and goes with the export."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange or hide the parts of the municipality monograph?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → Municipality monograph → “⚙︎ Customize”: drag ⠿ (or ↑ ↓) for the order, untick to hide, “↶ Reset” to go back to the start; for all municipalities or “only for this municipality”. Printing and export follow the chosen order; a part that is empty for this municipality is skipped automatically."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the sums and measures written in a record worth today?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the Transcription workshop, sums (“three hundred livres tournois”, “twelve livres ten sols”) and measures (“one and a half arpents”, “five pieds two pouces”) are underlined in green. Hover over them: the sum in today’s euros (based on the year of the record), in kilos of bread and in days of a day labourer’s work; the measure in metres, ares or litres, using the tables of the region where the record was made, with the source. “See the calculation” opens the 🪙 Old money converter, with the sourced prices of that year. Post-mortem inventories also give “≈ € today” for each object and for the total. The Workshop bar setting “Old sums and measures underlined” turns them off."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep several readings of a record and take the best of each?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the Transcription workshop, each reading (handwriting, printed text, in-depth AI reading) is kept on its own in 🕘 Versions, without overwriting the others. File → “⚖️ Compare readings…”: the passages that differ, word by word or line by line; one click picks a variant, “the majority” takes what most readings say, then “Put into the text” (↶ Undo)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I tell automatic reading to skip part of the image (a “do not read” zone)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the Workshop, draw a zone (▭), then in its bar choose its kind: “⛔ Do not read” (a stamp, a seal), or marginal note, title, signatures, added note. Lines in a ⛔ zone are skipped by automatic reading and exports; the other kinds set the reading order."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export a transcription as ALTO or PAGE (Transkribus, eScriptorium)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Workshop → Export: “🧾 ALTO” (lines and words with their position on the image, the format used by libraries and archives) or “📄 PAGE” (regions, lines, reading order; one file per page, as a .zip if there are several). TEI is still there too."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I transcribe a page of a collection in the Workshop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the collection page: “📜 Open in the Transcription Workshop”. The Workshop opens on its photo (the transcription already started, otherwise a new one); when you come back, if the page was empty, its text is put in (↶ Undo)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create a collection from an online register (IIIF)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Collections → “⋯” → “🌐 From an online register…”: paste the address of the IIIF manifest (often …/manifest.json, sometimes behind an IIIF logo) from the archive, the library or Gallica. The app gives its title and number of images; choose all the images or from… to…, then “Download”: each image becomes a page of a new collection (“Stop” keeps what is done; ↶ Undo deletes the collection)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I spot blank pages and photos taken twice in a collection?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Collection → 🗂 Status tab → “🔍 Blank pages and duplicates”: the app looks at each photo and suggests the pages that seem blank and the photos that look alike. Nothing changes without “Do what is ticked”; the removed photo stays in Media, and “↶ Undo” puts everything back."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see my documents on a map or on a timeline?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Documents, on the right: “🗺 Map” (one circle per place, a click shows its documents) or “📅 Timeline” (documents sorted by decade), next to “▦ Thumbnails”. The choice is remembered; it follows the filters in the left column."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a maze, and look for ancestors in it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the Game, choose “🌀 Maze” in “Game”. Set the shape (square, hexagons, round, heart, tree, France, star), the size, the corridors (long corridors, many dead ends, balanced, all random), the loops, the start and the finish, then the look (line, hedges, stones, parchment, colours of the walls, the floor and the path). The preview follows each setting; “🎲 Another one” changes the seed (the same seed gives the same maze again). In 3, “Things to go and find”: up to 12 ancestors, places, events, houses or photos from your genealogy, in the order you like, from the oldest to the most recent, or “Climb back up your line” (Sosa 16 → 8 → 4 → 2); the exit can stay closed until everything has been collected. Living people and confidential records are never in it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I play the maze with several people?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “4 · The players”, add up to 4 players. “Each in turn”: everyone goes through the same maze and the times are compared. “All at once”: a race, with the arrows, W A S D, I J K L and the numeric keypad. You can also drag your piece with the mouse or a finger. Space pauses, H gives a hint (a few yellow dots), “👁 Show the solution” draws the path. At the end: the time, the steps, the stops, and ↗ the record of each ancestor met."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I print a maze for children?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the game: “🖨 Print” gives the maze on one page, the list of stops, and the solution on a second page. “⤓ Export ▾” saves it as a PNG image or an SVG drawing, with or without the solution. Your favourite settings are kept in “💾 Saved settings” (with four “Ready-made sets”: Easy for children, Big challenge, Looking for the ancestors, Tour of France’s villages)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I play “Guess Who?” with my ancestors?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the Game, choose “🕵️ Ancestors’ Guess Who?” in “Game”. Choose the people (your Sosa ancestors, one generation, a person’s ancestors or the whole genealogy) and the number of portraits. An ancestor is hidden: ask questions (“Is it a woman?”, “Born before 1800?”, “Was she a ploughman?”, “Born in Auch?”, “On your father’s side?”); the portraits that no longer fit are greyed out by themselves. Click a portrait to set it aside yourself. “🎯 I’ll make a guess” when you think you know (a wrong guess counts as 2 questions). With two players, each has their hidden ancestor, drawn at random or chosen in secret by the other. Living people and confidential records are never in the games."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I play Portrait Memory?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the Game, choose “🃏 Portrait Memory”. Choose the kind of pairs (portrait ↔ name, person ↔ village of birth, person ↔ occupation, person ↔ year of birth) and the number of pairs (6 to 24). You turn over two cards; a pair found stays visible and the same player plays again. From 1 to 4 players, with the timer and the number of moves. With the keyboard: arrows then Enter or Space."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I play “The right date”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the Game, choose “📅 The right date”. You get a family event (birth, marriage, death… and, if you like, major dates in French history) without its date: drag it onto “⬇ Here” at its place on the timeline, or choose the place with the keyboard (Tab then Enter). Right: the card stays and you score a point; wrong: it moves to its real place and you lose a life. Set the number of cards, the minimum gap between two dates (the bigger it is, the easier) and the lives. “About” dates are marked “≈”; two dates that cannot be told apart go either way."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I play noughts and crosses with my own rules?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the Game, choose “❌ Free-rules noughts and crosses”. Choose the grid (from 3×3 to 30×30), how many in a row to win (3, 4, 5…), whether diagonals count, gravity (the pieces drop, as in “Connect 4”) or a grid that grows when you play at the edge. From 2 to 6 players, each with their symbol (❌ ⭕ ⭐ 🌳 ♥…, a letter or an ancestor’s portrait) and their colour; a player can be the computer (simple or medium). “↻ Play again” starts a new round keeping the evening’s scores; “↶ Undo the last move” to make up for a mistake. Ready-made sets: Classic noughts and crosses, Connect 4, Five in a row, Endless grid. “🧮 Play until the grid is full” option: you don’t stop at the first line, each line scores a point (a line twice as long is worth two), and the most points wins."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a jigsaw puzzle with a family photo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the Game (games table in the archive room), choose “🧩 Jigsaw” in “Game”. Take the image from your database (“🖼 Choose from the database”), a person’s record photo (“👤 A person’s photo”), or drag an image from the computer: it goes into the media. Choose the number of pieces (12 to 1000, or “Other…”), the shape (real pieces or squares), rotated pieces if you like (right-click or R to turn them), then “Start”. The pieces snap into the right place and against their neighbour; wheel or pinch to zoom, ⛶ for full screen. The timer runs while you play: Space or ❚❚ to pause (the image blurs); it also stops when you switch tabs. The game is stored in your genealogy database: it saves itself (a few seconds after your last move, on pause and when you leave the screen), “✓ Saved” shows it in the bar, and “💾 Save” (⌘S) does it straight away. Click the name at the top to change it. You find it again in the left column, even on another computer or after restoring a backup, exactly where you were (pieces, groups, time). If the app stops responding, the game is kept in the browser and goes into the database as soon as possible. At the end: your time, your record for this image and this number of pieces, and the history of finished puzzles."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I play patience (solitaire card games)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the Game, choose “🂠 Patience (card games)” in “Game”, then the game among 233 patiences arranged by family (Klondike, FreeCell, Spider, Yukon, Forty Thieves, Castles and gardens, pairing games). Drag a card (or a sequence) onto another pile, or click it then click where to put it; double-click: onto a foundation; click on the stock: draw. In pairing games (Pyramid, Elevens, Thirteens…), click the cards that go together."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which patiences have been added?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Twenty-three more, with their rules in the game’s “📖 The rules”: Agnes Sorel, White Horse, Four Seasons, Chameleon, Double Canfield, ForeCell, Fan with cells, Mrs Mop, Black Widow, Three Blind Mice, Gypsy, Irmgard, Queenie, Brisbane, Blockade, Good Fortune, Cadet, Martha, Morehead, Montana (the gaps: you arrange each row from 2 to King by sliding cards into the gaps, “🔀 Redeal” twice), Nestor (pairs of the same value), Pairs and All in a row."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which other patience games came next?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"One hundred and two more classic patience games, each with its rules in “📖 The rules” of the game. Klondike and Canfield family: Agnes Bernauer, Thumb and Pouch, Pas Seul, Blind Alleys, Eight Times Eight, Spike, Alternations, Harp, Big Harp, Acme, American Toad, Variegated Canfield, Duchess, Gate. With free cells: Penguin, Baker’s Game (King only), Relaxed Seahaven Towers, Repair, Ocean Towers, Seven by Four, Seven by Five, Challenge FreeCell, Super Challenge FreeCell. Spiders and gypsies: Scarab, Scorpion Tail, Double Scorpion, Grounds for a Divorce, Tarantula, Open Spider, Milligan Harp, Lexington Harp, Brunswick, Carlton, Miss Milligan, Millie, Elba, Mount Olympus. Yukon: Australian Patience, Canberra, Raw Prawn, Rushdike, Chinese Discipline, Double Russian Solitaire. Forty Thieves and its cousins: Ali Baba, Cassim, Rank and File, Busy Aces, Courtyard, Napoleon’s Square, Napoleon’s Exile, Deuces, Waning Moon, Famous Fifty, Forty Nine, Unlimited, Little Forty, Zebra, Congress, Parliament, Odd and Even, Royal Cotillion. Castles, fans and gardens: Lucky Thirteen, Portuguese Solitaire, Good Measure, Cruel, Perseverance, Wave Fan, Box Fan, Fascination Fan, Midnight Oil, Shamrocks, House in the Wood, House on the Hill, Bisley, Grandfather’s Clock, Stonewall, Wildflower, Chessboard, Stronghold, Fortress, Siegecraft, Penelope’s Web, Citadel, Exiled Kings, Bastion, Tea Caddy (and its American and English versions). Pairs, sequences and sums: Strict Tri Peaks, Double Golf, Aces Up (5 columns), Baroness, Sir Tommy, Puss in the Corner, Strategy, Giza, Tutankhamun, Blue Moon, Red Moon, Neighbour, Isabel and Fourteen Out."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I play cards against the computer (Belote, Tarot, Hearts…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the Game, choose “🂡 Multiplayer card games” in “Game”, then the game: War, Crazy Eights, Hearts, President, Belote (with 4, you and North against West and East, with declarations), 4-player Tarot (bid, guard, kitty, Excuse, Petit au bout), Rummy (gin, for two) or Crapette (for two). The number of players follows the game; you are the only person at the table, the others are the computer 🤖, each at their level (easy, medium or hard). The rules are in the “?” of the game bar. Click a card in your hand to play it (with the keyboard: ← → then Enter); ↶ undoes your last move (⌘Z), 💡 gives a tip, Esc pauses."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Card games: can the Kings, Queens and Jacks have my ancestors’ faces?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: in “New game”, leave “🖼 Kings, Queens and Jacks with my ancestors’ portraits” and choose the people (your Sosa ancestors, a generation, a branch or the whole genealogy). The Kings and (in Tarot) the Knights are men from the oldest generations, the Queens women, the Jacks younger men; their photo is taken from their record. Never a living person or a confidential record. “🤖 The computer bears ancestors’ names” also gives their names to the computer players."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Patience: how do I undo a move or get help?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“↶ Undo” (or Ctrl+Z / ⌘Z) undoes moves without limit during the game. “💡 A possible move” shows in yellow a card to play and where to put it. “⬆ Move all up” sends to the foundations everything that can go there; “⟲ Same deal” replays the same cards from the start. Each game keeps your record (time and moves)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: where can I see my achievements and my best games?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The bunker is hidden in the archive room (photo lab, three clicks on the red lamp). On its home screen, or in the game (⋯ at the top, and at the end of a game): “🏆 Achievements and records”. About thirty achievements (hold out 30 days, ten inhabitants, no deaths, build everything, a winter without a stove, a birth, fend off 3 raids, all the plans found, ten items made, one per scenario won…), some hidden (“?”) until you earn them. Each achievement earned is announced at the bottom left and keeps its date; a table shows the best games (days held, inhabitants, scenario, difficulty). Everything stays on this computer, never in the database."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: how do I play with my ancestors in 1709, 1720, 1870, 1910 or 1940?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “New game”, choose a scenario from “📜 In history, with your ancestors”: the great winter of 1709 (extreme cold), the plague of Marseille in 1720 (epidemic), the siege of Paris in 1870 (rationing, shells), the great Paris flood of 1910 (flooding), the exodus of 1940 (refugees, scarce petrol). The line below says how many ancestors of your family were alive at that date (born before, died after): they go down into the shelter, those from the region first; if some are missing, invented inhabitants of the period complete the group, and the game says so. Never a living person or a confidential record. The setting follows the period: no electricity in 1709, 1720 and 1870 (candle, fireplace, workshop by hand), the town crier and the gazette instead of the radio, period odds and ends and plans."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: can I go back after a mistake?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: every morning at 6 a.m., the game is saved (the last 7 mornings). “⋯ → ⏪ Resume on the morning of day…” (or the button at the end of a lost game) shows the list of mornings with the inhabitants and the supplies; “Resume that morning” asks nothing, and “↶ Undo” in the message at the bottom left brings you back to where you were. An achievement rewards those who hold out 30 days without ever going back."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: where do I find the plans for new items?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By searching the town. Each plan comes mostly from certain places (the beehive and the cider press at the farm, the accordion at the church, the fish traps at the harbour, the compass at the library…); the 🛠️ Workshop tab → “❔ To find” says so for each one; “rare” plans are found much less often. About twenty extra items, each with a real effect: well (water without rain), smokehouse, beehive, press, fish traps (food), eiderdowns (less cold), lanterns, clock, accordion, chess, wine cellar (morale), herb garden, grandmother’s medicine chest, stretcher (health), gutters, trap pit, arrow slits, compass, sledge, washtub, dovecote (news without electricity), solar oven and small turbine (not in the old-time scenarios). The places sometimes turn up a notebook of plans in the morning."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: how do I send an expedition into the region?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“🗺️ Region” tab: the map of the surroundings, drawn by the game (other towns — often those in your tree —, villages, farms, abbeys, depots, forests, mines, harbour), linked by roads that are more or less long and dangerous (green safe, yellow, orange, red very dangerous; dashed along the water). Click a known place, then “🧭 Form a convoy…”: who goes, to do what (search, trade, help, attack), which way (the shortest or the safest), the transport, the tools taken and the provisions (“＋ Plan just enough for the road”). The convoy leaves for several days: those who have left do not work at the bunker. You follow the convoy on the map and on the radio (or through the town crier): ambushes, accidents, finds, someone who joins you… It comes back with what it found, plans, wounded, news, new inhabitants, a pack animal — or not at all. “📻 Come back!” makes it turn around."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: who are the other groups of survivors?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the “🗺️ Region” tab, at the bottom: friendly neighbours, traders and rival gangs, each settled somewhere on the map, with a reputation from −100 to +100 (Enemies, Wary, Neutral, Friendly, Allies). It rises when you share, trade honestly, give (🎁 Give…) or come to help them, and falls when you refuse a request for help, rob a trader (“🦹 Steal” button at the hatch: everyone ends up knowing) or attack them. What it changes: better deals with those who like you, help from your allies when things go badly, and gangs who hold a grudge attack more often and harder (paying them what they ask calms them for two days). The groups also sometimes ask for help: “🤲 Share” or “Refuse”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: what are the ruined buildings in the town for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the town plan (“🏙️ Town” tab): a farm, a well, a mill, an infirmary, a forge… (with the period name). “🔨 Start the building work” takes the materials, then you have to send inhabitants to work there (“👷 Work 8 h”) until the work is finished. Then the building produces something every day (food, water, medicines, parts…), kept on site for up to three days: go and collect it (“🧺 Go and collect”). A building that is not visited can be looted or damaged (it then has to be repaired); a palisade protects it better."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: how does an inhabitant learn a trade?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the “👥 Inhabitants” tab, an expert inhabitant (their trade, or ×1.4 in a skill) or an elderly one (60 and over) has the “🎓 Train an apprentice…” button: choose the skill and the apprentice. Each evening when both are at the bunker, the apprentice progresses (never beyond their master); the log reports it week by week. When the master dies, their knowledge stays with the apprentice (and after two weeks, their notebooks help catch up to their level)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: can inhabitants become friends, fall out, form a couple?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Their bond grows by working together, going on a convoy, between master and apprentice; it drops with difficult characters and old quarrels. Friends keep their morale better and work faster when they do the same job; those who can no longer stand each other work more slowly. Two adults who get on very well can form a couple (never two close relatives according to your tree, nor a child); a baby can only be born within a couple. Everything can be seen on the inhabitant’s card (📔 Log → “💞 Their bonds”) and in the game log."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: what are “Memories of the ancestors”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"An option in the game settings (switch in the ⚙️ Settings tab, on at first). When an inhabitant taken from your database finds something linked to their real life — their occupation (a joiner in front of a saw, a midwife at the infirmary), their village (a convoy arriving in the town where they were born), a place on their record (the house where they were born) — they tell a short anecdote drawn from their record: their occupation, their birth, their marriage, their children. In a historical scenario, only what had already happened by that date. It appears in a speech bubble and in the log. Never anything about a living person, a confidential record or one hidden from sharing, and nothing is written in your database."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"☢ The bunker: how do I bring back more (bags, wheelbarrow, donkey, boat…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What you bring back is counted by weight: water, metal and petrol are heavy, cloth, plants and medicines light. On each person’s back, in addition: rucksacks, back basket, trunk with straps. Only one vehicle per outing, the best for the day’s terrain: wheelbarrow (bad in mud), cart, sledge (only on snow), cargo bike, bike trailer, shopping trolley and reinforced pushchair (present day only, on the road), horse-drawn cart (with a horse: the biggest load), boat (only on water: flooded streets in 1910, riverbanks), mine cart (on rails laid towards a nearby place). Pack animals (donkey or mule, horse), found on farms or traded with a group, carry a lot (+5 with pack saddles and straps) but they have to be fed and watered (on expeditions, in the provisions) and they can get hurt. Vehicles wear out: eventually they need repairing, and you need a workbench. 📦 Supplies tab → “🚚 Transport”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I play battleships or cables?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the Game, choose “⚓ Battleships” (against the computer or for two, with ships named after your ancestors) or “🔌 Cables” (turn the cable ends to connect all the stations to the server, with or without wrap-around edges). Games are kept in this browser."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I play chess, draughts, dominoes or Yahtzee?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the Game, “＋ New game”, then choose “♟️ Chess”, “⛀ Draughts”, “🁫 Dominoes” or “🎲 Yahtzee” in “Game”. For each player, “Who plays”: a person or the computer (easy, medium or hard). With two people, you play on the same screen (in dominoes, a hand-over screen hides the others’ hands). The pieces, counters or computers can bear your ancestors’ names (never a living person or a confidential record). Games are kept in this browser, nothing is written into your genealogy."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Board games: what are the buttons in the bar for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top of the game: who is playing and the scores, the timer, “↶” (⌘Z) which undoes the last move (against the computer, its move too), “💡” (H) which shows a good move (the number says how many are left), “⏸” (Esc) which pauses and hides the board, “?” which explains all the rules, and “⋯” (flip the board, offer a draw, resign). With the keyboard: the arrows to move from square to square, Enter to pick up a piece then put it down. Your record against each computer level is kept with its date."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Chess and draughts: which rules are followed?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Chess: all the official rules — castling (not allowed if the king is in check or passes through an attacked square), en passant, promotion of your choice (queen, rook, bishop or knight), checkmate, stalemate, draw by threefold repetition, by 50 moves without a capture or pawn move and by insufficient material; optional clock (3 to 30 minutes, with or without seconds added per move) and notation in French (R, D, T, F, C). Draughts: international rules on 10 × 10 (compulsory and majority capture, backward capture, flying kings) or English draughts on 8 × 8. A multi-path capture is played square by square."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Yahtzee: how do the score sheet and the bonus work?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Three rolls per turn: touch the dice to keep (they turn blue) before rolling again, then touch a free box in your column (the number of points shows in advance). At the top, the 1s to 6s; if they make 63 points or more, the bonus adds 35 points (“… missing” tells you where you stand). At the bottom: three of a kind, four of a kind, full house (25), small straight (30), large straight (40), Yams (50) and chance. The “American” sheet (Yahtzee) counts three and four of a kind with all 5 dice and gives 100 points for each extra Yahtzee. ↶ undoes the last box filled."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where can I see all my documents (records, letters, photos) as a list of people?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Show the “📄 Documents” tab: Settings → Appearance → menu tabs (it is hidden at first). The list is on the left, from A to Z with the index letters, or arranged in 📁 folders (like “My diagrams” in Charts: drag a document onto a folder, or “📁 File” from its page; each filing can be undone). The page on the right shows the image in large size, the reference, the date, the people, the transcription; “✎ Edit” opens the media viewer. These are the media of your database: nothing to type in again, and hiding the tab does not touch any document."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the people linked to each document in the Documents list?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the “📄 Documents” tab, the left column writes under each document’s title the names of the linked people (“Jean MAZAUD, Marie LACOMBE +3” when there are more), then its kind, date and place. “⋯” next to “A → Z” chooses what you read under the title: everything (recommended), the people, or the date and place. “👤 By person” files the documents under each person’s name (surname then first name, with the index letters to jump); a document linked to several people is under each of them, and those with no person go to “No person”. “Search…” also finds a document by a person’s name. Right-click a document to open it, edit it, download it or open a person’s record. In view-only mode, living people and confidential records are not named."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see an artist’s catalogue raisonné by albums, by songs or by period?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Show the “🎨 Catalogue raisonné” tab: Settings → Appearance → menu tabs (it is hidden at first). Choose the artist at the top of the column, then “Sort:” — 💽 by albums (the tracks in order, sides A and B), 🎵 by songs (“on 3” shows the albums it appears on), by label, by genre; for a painter 🕰 by period (“⚙️ Periods…”: one per line, e.g. “Blue period 1946-1952”, otherwise by decade), 🖌 by technique, 🏛 by place where it is kept, 🔢 by no.; everywhere by year or A → Z. The sort is remembered for each artist. The record appears on the right; “✎ Edit” opens its catalogue."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where are the Family treasures (letters to descendants, birth sky, gazette…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The ✨ Treasures tab is hidden at first: show it in Settings → Menus, column and lists → Menu. There you find letters to descendants sealed until a date, “At your age…”, dating a photo, the birth sky, the candle, the sound atmosphere, the gazette of the year, embroidery, the Happy Families game, the colouring tree, the passports and many more. Their lists are in Lists → ✨ Family treasures, and 13 widgets go on the dashboard."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Treasures: how do I save, export or print what I have created (colouring, sky, announcement, gazette…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"All the ✨ Treasures pages are laid out the same way: at the top, “← All treasures”, then the title; on the right the main button (blue), “⤓ Export ▾” and “🖨 Print”. “⤓ Export ▾” brings together the page’s formats (PNG image, SVG drawing, sound file, video…), “📐 Open in the layout” and, at the bottom, “📁 Store a copy in the media”. “🖨 Print” opens the app’s print window (preview, A4 or A5, ⤓ PDF). When the page has a preview, the settings are on the left and the preview on the right (one below the other on a phone)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make this month’s family letter (💌 Family letter)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"✨ Treasures → “💌 Family letter”: choose the month and it fills itself in (anniversaries, records and media added this month, a photo, an ancestor in the spotlight). Edit the text in the letter, then 🖨 Print, ⤓ PDF, ✉️ Ready email or 💾 Keep (Lists → “💌 Family letters”). Living people only get their first name and age."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I translate a record written in Latin?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the transcription workshop, “🌐 Translate” (next to “📖 Reading help”) shows the word-by-word right away: formulas in green, dictionary words in blue, numbers as figures. If the in-depth reading (Ollama) is installed, the full translation appears alongside and the two readings are compared. “📋 Keep in the transcription” stores the translation with the record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the look of a family record book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record book: “🎨 Appearance”. Templates (blue record book of 1877, green of 1975, burgundy, old paper, plain), colours, fonts for the titles and the text (“See all the fonts on my computer” adds yours), sizes, layout, frame, and the order of the parts. A box makes it the look of all your record books."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put a cover image, labels, or save the look of a family record book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record book: “🎨 Appearance” → “Cover image”: choose one of the record book’s photos (“none” to remove it), as a thumbnail on the cover or as a background; it is also used as the image in the list of record books. “💾 Save this look under a name” keeps it in “My looks”, for all record books (× to delete one). Size of the text and titles: slider or box in px (text 8 to 60, titles 8 to 120). “The parts”: the width of each (1, 1/2, 1/3, 2/3, 1/4, 3/4), like widgets. “🏷 Labels” at the top of the record book adds labels (also in “✎ Edit”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I delete a board, or say it is on hold?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the board, at the top: the list “In progress / On hold / Finished” and the bin 🗑. In the gallery, the bin appears when hovering over a board. The left column sorts by state; the Workshop has the same filters (and the state is chosen in the project settings)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a record for a pet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “Pets” (or People → Animals) → “＋ New animal”. Its record is the full record, like a person’s: name and kennel name, species, breed, sex, coat, markings, height, weight, birth and death, microchip, tattoo, passport, stud book (LOF, LOOF, SIRE…), registration number, DNA, breeder and kennel name, its owners (records from the family), titles and shows, vaccinations, health tests, vet, weight over time (with a curve), the moments of its life, its character, its story, memories, photos and documents (paper pedigree, health book). The animal appears on its owners’ records (“Pets” block, with “＋ An animal”) and in the dashboard’s “Pets” widget (with birthdays)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can an animal have a record like a person?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is now its only record: tree, events, photos, sources, tables, with its own blocks (🐾 Characteristics, 🌳 Pedigree, 🏆 Titles and shows, 🔖 Rings and markings, 👁 Sightings, 🩺 Health, 👥 Group, 🗺 Map / journeys) arranged in sections. Lists → Pets, People → Animals and “＋ New record” → 🐾 An animal all open this same record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does an animal still have two records?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Its record is now a single one, like a person’s, with everything the old animal record showed: characteristics, pedigree, titles, rings, sightings, health, group and map, arranged in sections (or tabs). Nothing is lost: an animal that only had the simple record got its full record by itself at the update (a message says so once in the list of animals), and the old links open the record, on the right section."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange the blocks of a record in sections (Identity, Family, Life, Documents…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → Person and animal records → “Record sections”: the style of the titles (Line and title, Framed, Large title, Banner, Centred, or Tabs at the top with the place of the tabs and “▦ See all”), foldable sections (remembered from one record to the next), numbers (1, 2, 3 · I, II, III · A, B, C), block numbers running on or by section, number in brackets in the tabs, empty sections in grey or hidden, sections specific to animals. The drawing (lines, corners, shadows, tabs) follows Designs to choose from, the colour of the banner that of Appearance. Everything is kept straight away."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the order of the sections and blocks of the record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → Person and animal records → “Order and visibility of blocks” (or, on a record, ⋯ → “⚙️ Arrange the sections…”, or the small ⚙️ at the end of the tabs). Drag the ⠿ handle of a section (its blocks follow it) or of a block (within its section, or onto another one: the blue line shows where it lands); with the keyboard: Tab to ⠿ then Alt + ↑ / ↓. A block’s ⋯: Move up, Move down, “Move to ▸”, Width, Hide. A section’s ⋯: Rename (or double-click its name), Change the icon, Duplicate, Folded at first, Move up, Move down, Delete (its blocks go to “No section”, “↶ Undo” puts it back). “＋ New section” at the bottom. “Editor layout: As before · New”, at the top of the page, only changes how this screen is drawn."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I send a link that opens a record on the right tab?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Copy the page’s address: it gives the record and the open section, for example “#personnes/12/famille”. When it is opened (link, reload, new window), the record opens on that tab; with a style other than “Tabs at the top”, the page scrolls down to the section and unfolds it. The browser’s ← → arrows go from one record to another. A section that no longer exists simply opens the usual tab."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the preview in the record settings show a real record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Settings → Appearance → Person and animal records → “Preview”: the real record, in small size, with its real blocks (read-only). Choose which one in “Preview with:” (by default the last one opened; with “🐾 Animals”, an animal). It changes straight away when you change a style, the order, a width or a section. “↗ Open this record” opens it for real. Nothing is changed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I delete an animal’s record: what happens to its tracking?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It goes with it: its record, its animal record, its markings, sightings, health, group and journeys. To restore everything: Settings → Change log → “Restore” on the line of the deletion. In Lists → Pets, ⋯ → “🗑 Delete” does the same. An animal whose record had been deleted before this update asks you when it opens: “↺ Restore it” or “🗑 Delete the animal”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange a single record differently from the others?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the record: ⋯ → “⚙️ Arrange the sections…” → “For this record only”. The record shows it with a blue dot “Arrangement specific to this record”; “↺ Use the one for all records again” removes the exception (with “↶ Undo”). In Settings, the “Exception for …” frame does the same thing for the open record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I put the “＋ New sighting” button of an animal’s record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → Person and animal records → “Place of “＋ New sighting””: a 👁＋ square first in the grid of buttons (at first, also arranged in “Order and visibility of buttons”), a blue button above the grid, a badge under the name, a quick-actions strip (👁＋ Sighting · 🩺＋ Health · 📍 GPS track · 🔖 Marking), a floating round button, or hidden. The O key opens the window from the record (not in a text box); it can be switched off there, or changed in Keyboard shortcuts."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does “👁 Last seen” at the top of an animal’s record mean?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The last sighting of the tracked animal: “yesterday, 17:40 · 📍 place · by …” (the full date on hover, a click opens the sighting). The badge is green up to the green threshold (7 days at first), yellow after that, red “Not seen for N days” after the “not seen again” threshold (30 days), grey “Never seen since ringing” with no sighting. It is set in Settings → Appearance → Person and animal records → “Order and visibility of the header items”: place (under the name, with the badges, at the bottom of the header), thresholds, “Show the place and the person” (off: only the badge). The “not seen again” threshold is also that of the “Animals to find” block in Tracking."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where has “A person’s record” from Designs to choose from gone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is now set in Settings → Appearance → Person and animal records → “Header shape”, just before “Order and visibility of buttons”: the same four cards (current header, photo banner, old record, identity card), the same “Extra settings” and the preview. Your previous choice is kept as it was. Designs to choose from has kept a small line that leads there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where has the “🎨 Designs to choose from” page gone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It has been removed: its settings are now where they are used. The General style has become the “Record style” (Settings → Person and animal records, with the header shape) and now only affects the records; the left column has its own lines, corners and shadows (Menus, column and lists → Column); the page background is in Theme and colours (🧩 Page background); Movements in Accessibility → 🎞 Movement. The rest of the app keeps its original style. Your choices are kept."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How are words like “born”, “died”… agreed for a person who is neither male nor female?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In French, these words agree with the sex. On records of men and women (and of male or female animals), they agree by themselves. For records with the sex “Other / non-binary” or “Not given”, you choose the spelling in Settings → Theme and colours → “✍️ Word agreement”: as before (at first, nothing changes), feminine (as one says “la personne”), masculine, with a middle dot (“né·e”), in brackets (“né(e)”) or without agreement when a neutral wording exists (“Naissance le 12 mars 1888”, “Décès”). The two kinds of records are set separately, the preview shows the result straight away, and each choice is kept straight away (“↶ Undo” in the message). The setting travels with the 💾 Saved settings and the site default."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the page background (squares, dots, lined paper, my photo…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Theme and colours → “🧩 Page background”: 9 backgrounds (small squares at first, plain, dots, lined paper, graph paper, paper grain, canvas, gradient, your photo), then the size of the pattern, its intensity, its colour (pale blue, grey, main colour, sepia, your theme’s lines, a colour of your own) and the pages concerned (unticked: plain background). The preview follows straight away. Your photo stays on this computer (not in the database nor in the site default: there, plain background). These come first: Accessibility → High contrast and “One thing at a time” (plain background), and on a record the grain of the “Old paper” style. The background is never printed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make text bigger (or smaller) throughout the app?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Theme and colours → “Text size”: from “Very compact (13 px)” to “Very large print (32 px)”. It is the only text size setting: Accessibility → Text points to it (the old “Text size” slider in Accessibility was carried over by itself, to the nearest step). Line spacing, spacing between letters and words and the font stay in Accessibility. Simple mode, if it follows “Same as the app”, also takes this size."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a genealogy of animals (a breeding)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Pets → “Base type”: 👪 Family, 🐾 Breeding or 👪🐾 Both. In Breeding, the left list, the counter at the top and the statistics start from the animals, and new records are animals; the root person (the starting record) can be an animal, and the tree then starts from it. The statistics never mix people and animals."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get a separate “Animals” tab?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → Order of the main menu: tick “Animals” (hidden at first). Then “People” only lists people and “🐾 Animals” only animals, each with its last record opened. Without this tab, in the left list, an “🐾 Animals” frame appears between “People” and “Bookmarks” as soon as there are animals (and “People” disappears if there are only animals)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see the animals in the family tree?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Charts → Ancestors, Descendants or Hourglass: tick “🐾 with the animals” (not ticked at first). Each person’s animals are placed under their owner, with a dotted line; a click opens their record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange an animal’s record (show, hide, move its parts)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As for a person: Settings → Appearance → Person and animal records → “Order and visibility of blocks”, with “🐾 Animals” chosen at the top of Sections (or, on the record, ⋯ → “⚙️ Arrange the sections…”). Its six starting sections: 🪪 Record, 🔖 Rings and markings, 👁 Sightings, 🩺 Health, 👥 Group, 🗺 Map. The order you had chosen on the old animal record (“⚙️ Organise”) was carried over as it was."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange the blocks of animals’ full records without touching people’s?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → Person and animal records → “Record sections”: choose “🐾 Animals”, then “Order and visibility of blocks”: the order, the width and the blocks shown for all animal records, separately from people’s records. Next to it, “Exception for…” only sets the open record. If the open record is an animal, the “PERSON RECORDS” exception is greyed out (and the other way round for a person)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find an animal created from People or Animals?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everywhere: it has only one record, whether it is opened from People → Animals, Lists → Pets, the picker, a board, a house or Animal tracking."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"On an animal’s full record, what does “Identity” show?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"What matters for an animal (species, breed, sex, coat, birth, microchip…), never a person’s fields. “Edit” opens the animal’s form. Its other parts (🐾 Characteristics with identification and origins, 🌳 Pedigree, offspring, siblings, owners, 🏆 Titles and shows, 🩺 Health with weight and height, its life, its story, notes, photos, and tracking) are blocks of the record, each with ✎ to go straight to that part of the form. The “Censuses” block (and the other blocks specific to people: maternity, military, voters…) is hidden by default; you can put it back in Settings → Appearance → Person and animal records (“🐾 Animals”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a species, change their order or hide some?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Pets → “⚙️ Settings” (or Settings → Animals): tick the species to show, arrange them with ↑ ↓, add your own (its name, and its icon: click it and choose one, animals first) or delete it with 🗑."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I set up the “New animal” form (order of the parts, fields shown)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Animals → “Form”: for all animals, or for one species (“own setting” box): the order of the parts and the order of the fields in each part (↑ ↓ or by dragging ⠿), the parts and all the fields shown (the name and the species always stay). By default, the UELN number only appears for horses."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add my own categories and fields to an animal’s record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Animals → Form: “＋ Category” (e.g. 🧬 Genetics, Training) creates a new part of the form; “＋ Field” under a part (the app’s or yours) adds a field: short text, long text, date, number, yes / no box or list of choices (separated by ;). They are arranged and hidden like the others. On the record, an added field is shown in its part; your categories form the “🗂️ My categories” block of the full record. A deleted field keeps what was written in it (it is no longer shown)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep several ways of setting up animals, or move them to another computer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Animals → “💾 Saved settings”: “💾 Save under a name…” gives them a name (e.g. Horse breeding); choose one and “Apply” to go back to it. “⤓ Export” makes a .json file (the settings and all the sets); “⤒ Import” takes it into another database."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do the photos and notes of an animal with a full record go?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In its record, as for a person: its photos and documents (paper pedigree, health book…) in the “Media” block, its notes and sources in the “Notes” block. Those from the old animal record were moved there: nothing is lost."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make the pedigree (the tree) of a pedigree animal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the animal’s record, choose its sire and dam if they have a record (otherwise write their name and registration). “🌳 Pedigree” shows 2 to 8 generations (sire’s side at the top, dam’s side at the bottom); an ancestor who appears several times has its own colour. The inbreeding coefficient (Wright’s coefficient, from the known ancestors) and the common ancestors are shown; the pedigree can be printed or downloaded as a spreadsheet. The record also shows the offspring by litter and the siblings (same parents or half). “＋ A young one” creates the record of a young animal with this parent already filled in."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I set the colours, shapes, arrowheads, fonts and menus of boards?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Boards”. Colours: the swatches offered for each use (fill, border, text, drawing, notes, board background), in the order you want, with several possible palettes. Arrowheads: those offered and their order, and the default arrowhead at the start and end. Shapes: the order of categories and shapes, those to hide, quick access. Fonts: font lists of your own (with the computer’s fonts). Sizes: from which to which thickness (0.5 to 500 px) and text size (up to 500 px), the default rounding. Spreadsheet: how it looks when placed. Brushes: your own, with their hardness. Menus and options: the order (or hiding) of the tools, the top buttons, the settings on the right and the “Add” menu. At the top of the page, “💾 Save under a name…” keeps all these settings together (like an Appearance design); “My saved settings” → “Apply” to go back to them, 🗑 to delete them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I set the caption of a line or an arrow?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the line: on the right, write the “Caption”, then “Caption settings”: size, font, bold, italic, text colour, frame (fill, border, border thickness, padding, rounding; “transparent” fill for no frame), position along the line (0 % at the start, 100 % at the end), side (on the line, above, below), distance from the line, and “follow the angle of the line” to tilt it like the line."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose and arrange the quick-access shapes?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Boards → Shapes: ⚡ on a shape adds it to quick access (the shapes offered directly in the bar of the Shapes tool); in “Quick access”, drag a shape or use ‹ › to change the order, × to remove it. Don’t forget “Save”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find the letters, numbers, coats of arms, frames and other shapes for boards?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a board: Shapes tool (⬠) → “All shapes”. The library has nearly 5,000 shapes: the whole alphabet (A to Z, a to z, accented letters É È À Ç…, digits and numbers, punctuation), each alone, in a circle, in a circle (outline), in a square or in a square (outline); genealogy signs (° birth, † death, ⚭ marriage, ∞, parent → children links, braces); shields and divisions of coats of arms (per pale, per fess, quarterly, chevron, cross…); frames and friezes; labels and ribbons; simplified flags to colour; occupations, tools, animals, buildings, transport, weather, music, sport, emotions… Type a word in the box (without worrying about accents: “eglise”, “flags”, “É”); the filters at the top give the number found per kind (Tab moves from one to the next); ↑ ↓ ← → then Enter to take it. The extra shapes load the first time it is opened (a short moment), not at start-up. A shape placed stays in the board as it is."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"The “All fonts” window doesn’t show all my fonts: what should I do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the app installed on the computer, the full list of fonts is shown directly. In a browser (Chrome, Edge), click “🔓 See all the computer’s fonts”: the browser asks for permission once. If you refused, open the site settings (the padlock to the left of the address) → “Fonts” → Allow, then reopen the window. Safari and Firefox do not give the list: the app shows the common fonts it finds, and you can type the exact name of another font. The fonts appear little by little as you scroll down, each written in its own font."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose the colour of what I place in a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As soon as a tool has colours, the top bar shows the swatches of your palette and, at the end, an “other colour” square to choose one freely: Text (text colour), Note (note fill), Shapes (fill), Line and Arrow (line colour), Drawing. For an arrow, “Arrowheads” gives the arrowheads a separate colour (“=”: like the line). Once the item is placed, everything can be changed on the right, including “Arrowhead colour”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the order of the Settings pages?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the bottom of the Settings column, next to “Archive room”: “Reorder”. Drag a line, or use its arrows ↑ ↓, then Save; “↺ Reset to default” puts back the original order. (The order can also be set in Appearance.)"}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where are the glossary and the FAQ?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In Settings, two pages: “Glossary” (genealogy vocabulary, old occupations, help reading records, your personal dictionary) and “FAQ / guide” (quick pointers and these frequently asked questions, with the “Ask your question” search). A button at the top of each page leads to the other."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I underline, strike through, or make a bulleted or numbered list in a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the text, note or shape: on the right, B (bold), I (italic), U (underline), S (strikethrough), then align left, centre, right, justify. “Bullets and numbers”: bullet, 1. 2. 3., I. II. III., a. b. c., A. B. C., dash, checkboxes — one line = one item. “Font” offers your font lists; 🖥 adds the computer’s fonts; you can also type a name."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get more shapes, or create my own?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Shapes tool (S) → “All shapes ▾”: nearly 800 shapes arranged in 23 categories (basic, geometry, arrows, bubbles, stars, symbols, genealogy, people, beliefs and memory, writings and documents, places, house and objects, clothes, occupations and tools, transport, animals, nature, weather, cooking, music games and sport, health…), with a search. “＋ Create a shape”: with the pen (as in Photoshop: click = corner point, click and drag = smooth point with its handles, click on the first point = closed shape, ⌥ / Alt for independent handles, a path inside the shape = a hole), from a drawing in the board or from an image. “🧩 From a shape”: take a shape, flip it (mirror, upside down), rotate it, stretch it, add other shapes to it or cut holes in it, and give it a name (also with ✏️ on the right on a placed shape, or on each shape in Settings → Boards → Shapes). Your shapes go into “My shapes” and into quick access."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export, print, import a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top of the board: “⤓ Export” → PNG or JPG image, PDF, Print…, the selection as PNG, or “Board file” (.lcnda-tableau.json). For the file, two choices: “Light, without the photos” (small, easy to send, perfect in the same database; elsewhere the missing photos become notes) or “Complete, with the photos” (heavier, but complete everywhere: on import, the missing photos, sounds and PDFs are added to the database). “＋ Add → Import a board” reopens this file as a new board; the links are kept when it is the same database. The zoom (the percentage) has a menu: fit to selection, fit content to the window, 50 / 100 / 200 %."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I merge spreadsheet cells?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click a cell, then ⇧ + click on another: the cells between the two are selected. On the right: “⊞ Merge” (the texts are joined end to end in the first), “⊟ Split” to undo it; the fill is applied to all the selected cells."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which brushes and erasers are there in a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"✍︎ tool (P), then the brush button: Pen, Monoline, Marker, Pencil (grain), Pastel, Fountain pen (the line gets thinner when you go fast), Reed pen (bevelled tip), Brush, Watercolour, Fill (the traced shape is filled), Highlighter, Chalk, Neon, Airbrush, Dotted. At the top of the menu, five ready-made thicknesses; next to them, the exact thickness in px and a colour of your choice. ⌫ tool (E): “Fine eraser” erases only what it touches (the line is cut), with its size in px; “Stroke eraser” removes the whole line. A drawing already made can change brush in the right panel."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put a spreadsheet in a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"▦ tool (G) then click, or “＋ Add” → A spreadsheet. Double-click a cell to write; Enter goes down, Tab moves to the next one (and adds a row at the end), Esc cancels. Formulas: =B2+B3, =SOMME(B2:B9), =MOYENNE, =MIN, =MAX, =NB, =PRODUIT, =ARRONDI(B2;1)… (English names such as SUM also work). Paste a piece of Excel or Numbers into a cell: it fits into the grid. On the right: add or remove rows and columns, column width, header row and column, every other row shaded, cell fill, download as CSV."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see what is grouped in a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Each group has a coloured dotted frame and a badge “Group n · number of items”: click the badge to select the whole group (and move it by dragging). The ⧉ button near the zoom shows or hides the frames."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I take a snapshot of a board, or make a new board from another one?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top of the board: “📸 Snapshots” freezes the board as it is (give it a name). Later, “Go back to this state” (the current state is kept as a snapshot first, nothing is lost), or “Make it a board” to create a separate one. “⧉ Copy” immediately creates a new board with the current content, with or without the same arrangement. In addition, the app automatically keeps one “⏱ automatic version” per working day (the last 30), stored separately and folded at the bottom of the list of snapshots."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I place an automatic tree in a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“＋ Add” → “🌳 An automatic tree”: choose the person, then their ancestors, their descendants or their close family (parents, siblings, spouses, children), the number of generations, with or without spouses, arrows or lines. The records are placed linked and grouped (next to what is already there); each card stays linked to its record: double-click to open it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put a timeline or a map in a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“＋ Add” → “🗓 A live timeline”: a person’s dated events (or those of their couple and children) place themselves on a time line, and follow the record. “🗺 A map”: the places of a person, their family or the person of an investigation; on the right, “Place a marker” adds your own points (a commune, a place), and “link the places in order” draws the route."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose what a record shows on a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the record placed: on the right, “The record shows”: photo, full dates, years, places, occupation, age, parents. Everything is read again from the record each time the board opens: if you correct a date, the card follows."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I mark a lead as to check, confirmed or false?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose a note: on the right, “Investigation lead” → ❓ To check, ✅ Confirmed or ❌ False. The note takes the colour of its state and a badge. At the top of the board, “❓ n to check” counts the remaining leads: a click takes you to the next one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put a comment on an item of a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the item → “💬 Comment” on the right. Choose a word for the bubble (To check, Question, Idea, Source?, Important…) or “＋ another word…” for your own (it is kept in your list, “My words” to remove some). A small bubble appears on the item; a click on it reopens the comments, which you tick when they are settled."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make areas (frames) on a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“＋ Add” → “▭ A frame” (if there is a selection, the frame surrounds it). Give it a name (“Paternal branch”, “Sources to see”): what is entirely inside moves with it. ▾ at the top left folds it (its content is hidden, the number of items stays shown); ▸ unfolds it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I align or distribute several items?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose several (⇧ + click or by dragging in empty space): on the right, “Align” (left, centre, right, top, middle, bottom) and “Distribute” (same gap horizontally or vertically, same width, same height). The 🧲 button at the top turns on the snapping grid: while dragging, items snap to the grid and to the edges and middles of the others (pink lines show them); ⌥ / Alt to do without it for a moment."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I use the layers of a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top: 🗂 Layers → “＋ New layer”. The circle chooses the layer where new items go; 👁 shows or hides it (even in exported images), 🔒 locks it (it can no longer be selected), ↑ brings it to the front, ⬚ selects what it contains. “Put the selection on the chosen layer” moves items; on the right, “Layer” too."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I search in a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top: 🔍 (or ⌘F): type a word or a name. It searches the notes, texts, spreadsheets, record names, comments and markers. Click a result (or Enter, ⇧ + Enter to go back): the board goes there and shows it; a folded frame opens, a hidden layer is shown."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get a board’s mini-map?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top: 🗺 shows or hides it (also the × on it). The whole board in small, the current view in red: click or drag inside to move around. Drag its title bar to put it where you like: it stays there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I present a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the top: “▶ Present” → “＋ The current view as a step” (zoom and frame first), “＋ The selection as a step” or “＋ One step per frame”. “☰ The steps” to rename them, put them back in order, review them. “▶ Start”: full screen, the board glides from one step to the next (→ or space to go forward, ← to go back, Esc to finish). Without steps, the frames are used as steps."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make an arrow in several segments, one that goes around, or a ready-made “brother of”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the line: the small “＋” in the middle of each segment can be dragged to bend it (a blue elbow appears; double-click it to remove it). “Line shape” → “⤳ goes around items”: the arrow passes around whatever is in its way. “Ready-made link” (on the right, or in the bar of the Arrow tool before drawing): brother of, sister of, husband of, wife of, father of, daughter of, cousin of, godfather of, witness of, wrote to, same person?… with its caption, colour and style."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens when I paste an Excel table or a list onto a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Paste (⌘V) onto the board: for several lines, the app asks what it should become: a spreadsheet (for a piece of Excel or Numbers), one note per line, one note per spreadsheet row, or a single text. The notes are arranged in a grid, next to what is already there, and grouped if you like."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make my own board templates?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In a board: “⤓ Export” → “⭐ Make it a template” (the whole board or the selection, with or without the texts). It appears in “New board”, under “My templates”; the × on a template deletes it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I duplicate an item on a board by dragging it (as in Photoshop)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Hold down ⌥ Option (Mac) or Alt (PC) and drag the item (or the selection): a copy goes with the mouse, the original stays in place. A frame takes along a copy of what it contains. ⌥ + click without dragging copies nothing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose a font’s weight (Light, Medium, Bold…) on a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the text: on the right, under “Font”, “Weight”. By default, the nine usual weights (Thin 100 to Black 900). Click 🖥 (the computer’s fonts): the list then shows the weights the font really has (e.g. Futura Std: Light, Book, Medium, Heavy…). The B button switches back to normal / bold."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own arrowheads?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Boards → ➤ Arrowheads → “＋ Create an arrowhead”: from a shape (look for it among all the shapes, yours included) or drawn with the pen; rotate it (0°, 90°, 180°, 270°), filled or outlined, attached by the tip, the middle or the back; give it a name. It is added to the list (with a preview of each arrowhead), where you arrange, hide or delete it (🗑). The app also offers 26 arrowheads: triangle (filled or empty), thin or long arrow, double chevron, circle, square, diamond, cross, plus, star, heart, crow’s foot…"}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I set the colours and settings of line captions?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Boards → 🎨 Colours: three categories “Line captions” (text, frame fill, frame border). → ☰ Menus and options: “Settings of a line caption”: the order of these settings and those to hide (size, font, bold, colours, frame, position, side, distance, angle)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record a voice memo on a board?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“＋ Add” → “🎙 A voice memo” → ⏺ Record, then ⏹ Stop: you can listen to it again, give it a title, then “Place on the board”. The sound is stored with the board and in the app’s media. The first time, the computer asks for access to the microphone."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I link a board to a person, an investigation, a part of an investigation…?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Under the title of the board: “🔗 Link…” → a person, an investigation, a search sweep, a Workshop project, a collection, a place (its commune), a media item, a label, a family (a couple), an event, an animal or another board, with, if you like, the name of a part (lead 1, the witnesses…). You can add several; × to remove one. The board then also appears on the person’s record (“Boards” block) and in the linked investigation, search sweep or project. “change” next to where it is filed moves the board itself."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a free-form board (like Freeform)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Investigations → “Boards” tab → “＋ New board” (empty, evidence wall, mind map, timeline, hand-drawn tree). Tools at the top: text, note, shapes, line, arrow (it follows what it connects), freehand drawing, eraser; “＋ Add” places a file (image, GIF, sound, video, PDF), a media item from the app, an image or a website from the web, a record, an app screen (with a live preview), one of your widgets, a collection, a label, a folder (a board within the board). Drag or paste images directly. ⌘G group, ⌘L lock, ⌘D duplicate, ⌘Z undo. Everything saves itself."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put a board in an investigation, a search sweep or the Workshop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the investigation (or the search sweep, or the project), “🧩 Boards” section → “＋ New board here”. Give it a part (“Lead 1”) to have several. For a board already made: at the top of the board, “change” next to where it is filed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I transcribe a family record book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “Family record books” → “＋ New record book”: issue, marriage, husband and wife with their parents, children, annotations, full transcription and photos of the pages. Link each person to their record (or “create the record”); the record book then appears on the record. “Carry over to the records” adds what is missing (births, deaths, marriage, the couple’s children) without overwriting anything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I retouch a photo precisely?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Viewer → Retouch: zoom (⌘ + wheel, + −, 1:1), move around with the space bar. The healing brush and the stamp have a size ([ and ] keys) and a hardness; “🎯 Choose the spot to copy from” for the stamp. Brush (6 kinds) and text go on layers, above the photo. The “History” tab lets you go back to any step."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I fill in an employer’s record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Employers → “🏢 Create the record” (or click on the employer in a census, or “🏢 This employer’s record” under the Employer field). Identity, head office, managers, establishments, timeline, notes and sources; “Fill from the Annuaire des entreprises” fills in, in one click, a company that exists (or has existed since the 1970s). The record also shows the family who worked there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"In what formats can I save an edited sound?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sound workshop (✂️ Edit) → Save: MP3 (plays everywhere, quality from 96 to 320 kbit/s), WAV or FLAC (lossless), Ogg Opus (the lightest), AIFF (Mac). You can switch to mono, change the sample rate, and “Only download the file” without changing anything in the database."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add photos or scans?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the Media block of the record (“＋” button). For a whole folder that fills up by itself (scanner, phone): Settings → Watched “Scans” folder."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What size of photo, scan, sound or video can I add?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “📁 Media files”: at most 100 MB (recommended) for an image or a document (25, 50, 100, 200 or 500 MB), and 250 MB (recommended) for a sound or a video (up to 2 GB). A bigger file is refused, with a message saying so (for example “img003.jpg is larger than 100 MB…”). What it means: each file is copied into your family tree, so it takes its room on the disk; full backups (and the copy at a cousin’s) get heavier and slower; opening it needs more memory. The phone over Wi-Fi keeps its own limits. Each change can be undone with “↶ Undo”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why don’t “See better”, reading or retouching process a very large image?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"To work on an image, the computer unfolds it entirely in its memory: 120 million pixels (12,000 × 10,000) already need about half a gigabyte. Beyond the limit (Settings → “📁 Media files”, 120 million pixels recommended, from 50 to 300), the image is kept and shown, but the server thumbnails, “🎚️ See better”, “✨ Read ▾”, the transcription workshop and retouching don’t process it, with a clear message. To process it anyway: raise the limit if the computer has plenty of memory, or add a reduced version of the image."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I brighten or retouch an old photo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the photo, then “🎚️ Retouch”: brightness, contrast, shadows, colours, black and white, sepia, negative and a curve as in Photoshop. “Replace the photo” always keeps the original (“Back to the original”); “Save a copy” adds the retouched version alongside."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I request a recent record from the town hall?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Letter register → “Records to request from the town hall”: the letter is prepared, and a reminder can be set for the day the record becomes public."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange the pages of a photographed register or notebook?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Collections: the pages in order, with their transcription, a cover image, a table per page (to paste from a spreadsheet) and lines linked to the records."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I go through a census or a register page by page?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Surveys: the views of the scan alongside, the people arranged by household with the household’s address, and a padlock 🔒 so that nothing is changed by mistake once the page has been checked."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I listen to a recording or cut a video?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Audio or Videos: player with speed from 0.10× to 6×. “✂️ Edit” opens the sound and video workshop (cut, enhance, assemble); the original is always kept."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I combine several photos into a single image?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Media (or the viewer) → “🧩 Assemble photos”: resize, rotate, set the transparency."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I prepare a book, a thesis or an exhibition?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Investigations → Research workshop: projects with nested folders, references imported from Zotero, CSL JSON or EndNote, exports to Word, PDF and CSL JSON."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find out what language a record is written in, or write in Cyrillic or Greek?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Calendar / measures → “Alphabets and languages of records”. “🔤 A language” shows the alphabet of 40 languages (accented letters, old letters such as the long s ſ, Latin abbreviations, transliteration of Cyrillic, Greek, Hebrew and Arabic) and how to recognise it. “🔎 Which language?”: type a letter or a word from the record (ł, ő, ж, natus…), the possible languages are shown, the most likely first. “⌨️ Cyrillic and Greek”: a small keyboard to write a name, copy it and read it in Latin letters."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I convert an old date (Republican calendar, Julian…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Calendar / measures → “Convert a date”: 26 calendars, including the Republican, the Julian, the Roman (calends, nones, ides) and the English Old Style."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Handwriting recognition doesn’t work?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is installed only once: Settings → Handwriting recognition (HTR). A 3-step assistant says what is missing and why: ① the engine (Kraken, with a ready-made Python that the app downloads itself: nothing for you to install), ② the handwriting model, ③ the check (“✓ Ready: handwriting reading”). If there is a problem, the message says what to do depending on your computer (Mac, Windows, Linux), then “↻ Try again”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Read the record: how do I automatically read a handwritten record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the transcription workshop (“📜 Open in the Workshop” on an image, or Lists → Editing tools → “📜 Transcription workshop”): “✨ Read ▾” → “✍️ Read handwriting (fast)”. The app finds the lines and reads the whole page, or only the chosen area (draw it with ▭, then “✨ Read this area”). Reading happens in the background, on your computer, without the Internet: you can keep working (“■ Stop” to interrupt it). Then the text is offered line by line next to its piece of image; uncertain words are highlighted in yellow: click them to correct them, Enter goes to the next line. “✓ Put the lines in the text” adds them (with one area per line), then names, dates and places are spotted by themselves. “↻ Read again with this model” tries another model."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Suggested corrections: how does the app help fix words misread by “✨ Read the record”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"After reading, words that look like a known word are underlined in purple (uncertain words stay highlighted in yellow): “décembe” → “décembre”, “mil huit aut” → “cent”. Click a word: 1 to 3 suggestions, “Keep as is” or “Add to lexicon”. “✓ Accept the N safe suggestions” applies them at once and “↶ Undo” goes back: nothing changes without you. On the workshop text: “🪄 Corrections”. The lexicon: the words of records, those of “📖 Reading help”, and the surnames, first names, places and occupations in your database (without living people or confidential records); smaller for Latin and German. Settings → Handwriting recognition (HTR): “Suggest corrections” and the threshold. A correct French word (one in the general French dictionary, over 330,000 words) is never replaced. In the window with both readings, hover over a suggestion or reach it with the keyboard: its word is highlighted in the text and the tooltip says where (“… mil huit cent …”, line 1)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Read the record: what about notes written vertically in the margin?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A note written at 90° in the margin (bottom to top or top to bottom) is detected: its piece of image is turned both ways, read again, and the surest reading is kept. Its text is no longer mixed with the lines of the record (no more “2”, “1”, “6” in the middle): it comes apart, after the main text, under a “———” line and the title “Margin note:”. If the turned reading isn’t surer, the lines stay as they were read. Lines written normally don’t change."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"In-depth reading: how do I link the AI’s text to the lines of the image?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"When you keep the text of “🔬 In-depth reading (AI)” (“Put into the text” or “Keep this one”), the app splits the page into lines and links each line of the text to its line on the image, like “✍️ Read handwriting (fast)”: coloured areas; clicking a line lights up its area, clicking an area selects its line. The splitting comes from “✍️ Read handwriting (fast)” if it is installed (it also gives a text per line, which helps pair the lines), otherwise from a simple splitting of the image (the enhanced image if “Read and export the enhanced image” is ticked). A line cut in two by the AI, or two lines stuck together, are found; notes written vertically in the margin stay apart. A line linked without certainty is grey with ❔ in its note: check it. A line that is not on the image gets no area: nothing is made up. “↶ Undo” removes the areas placed. “▤ Link the lines to the image” does the same afterwards, on text already entered or typed by hand; the box of the same name, in the window with the two readings, lets you skip it. Suggested corrections (🪄) also work on this text, in the window with the two readings and in the workshop, with the same settings (careful at first) and “↶ Undo”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"In-depth reading and “What the app found”: from the record read to the person records?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the transcription workshop, “✍️ Read handwriting (fast)” first spots a screenshot of an archives website (toolbars, grey edges) and offers “✂ Crop to the text”: only the page is read (or only the areas you drew ▭). If the text read is uncertain, “✂ Crop and read again” or “🔬 Try in-depth reading”: an open vision model (Qwen3-VL, Apache 2.0) running on your computer with Ollama, installed once (“✨ Read ▾” → “🔬 In-depth reading (AI)”); both readings are shown side by side and you keep the better one. Nothing goes on the Internet except the model download. Then “🧭 Spot”, then “✓ Add to the family tree…”: the app shows the event, the people with their role (spouses, parents, witnesses, godfather…), age, occupation and residence; for each one “🔗 Link”, “⇆ Compare” (the record and the profile side by side), “＋ Create”, “Ignore” or “Witness only”. The source (archives, register, reference, image n / N) is prefilled. Everything goes in at once, family links included, and “↶ Undo” reverses it all."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"“🧭 Find”: what does “Found in the text” show, and how do I add it to a record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"After “🧭 Find”, the workshop shows one card per person with what the text says about them: age (and estimated birth “about 1787” if the date of the record is known), occupation, origin, birthplace, residence, relationship (brother, son of, widow of…), role and status; each field can be ticked or unticked. Variants of the same name (“Jemielet” / “Jemichet”) are grouped, false names (“Cafins de”, “BTIS”) are set aside, and the records in your database that look alike are suggested. Then come the sections 📍 Places, 📅 Dates (even written in words or in the republican calendar), 🎂 Ages, ⚔️ Events and context (a battle, prisoners… linked to “🌍 The world around” if it knows it), 📜 Kind of record (only suggested) and 🔢 Numbers. On every item, “＋ Add to a record…” (also through “⋯” or a right-click): you choose the record, then “as what” (event, note, occupation, residence or info), with a preview; the source (this document, the line) and the sentence as a quote are always attached, and “↶ Undo” reverses the addition. If in-depth reading is installed, “🔍 Find with the AI” sends only the text to the model on your computer; its suggestions are marked 🔍 AI. ⚙️ sets the sections shown and the AI (suggest, automatic or never)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which handwriting model should I choose to read my records?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Handwriting recognition (HTR), step ②: “McCATMuS” (recommended) for parish registers and French civil registration from the 17th to the 19th century, letters and today’s handwriting; “CATMuS Medieval” for Latin and old scripts (before 1500). Each model downloads in one click from its official source (zenodo.org); its licence (CC BY 4.0: free, crediting the authors) is read at the source and the file is checked by its fingerprint before being kept. For other languages, add a .mlmodel file by hand (“📁 Choose the file…”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I uninstall handwriting reading?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Handwriting recognition (HTR) → “🗑 Uninstall…”: the engine (≈ 1.5 GB) is deleted from the data folder; you can keep the models to reinstall faster. Transcriptions already made stay. A single model is removed with “🗑 Remove” (and “↶ Undo” puts it back)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a backup?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “🗓 Automatic backup”: “💾 Back up now” (or ⌘⇧B) makes one straight away, and the page sets up the copies made automatically. The full backup with all the media in a single file, password-protected if you like, is in Settings → Clean export."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I set up automatic backup (every day, on a USB stick, in iCloud or Dropbox)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “🗓 Automatic backup”. When: every N hours, every day or every week, and “Also when closing the app” (only if something has changed). Where: write the folder (a USB stick, an external disk, or an iCloud Drive, Dropbox, OneDrive… folder: it is just a folder, the app uses no online service); empty = in the app’s folder, on the same disk. What: how many copies to keep (10 at first: the oldest automatic copy is deleted), with or without the media (copied once, then only the new ones), and all your genealogies if you have several (each in its subfolder). Each copy is a clean snapshot of the database, checked (integrity check) before it gets its name. Everything is kept by itself; “↶ Undo” in the message at the bottom left. If the stick is unplugged, a message says so, without blocking anything, and the copy is made as soon as it comes back. “Last backup: …” is shown at the top of the page and in the dashboard’s “🗓 Automatic backup” widget."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I go back to a backup?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “🗓 Automatic backup” → “The copies”: each copy shows its date, its size and “CHECKED” (or “DAMAGED”). “↺ Go back to this copy” replaces your genealogy with this one, after a question (it cannot be undone with one click). Your previous database is first kept in the backups, under a name ending in “avant-restauration”, never deleted by itself: you can go back to it the same way. A damaged copy is refused."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I have my genealogy on two computers (the laptop and the office one)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Working together → “🔄 Two computers”. Choose a shared folder: a USB stick, or an iCloud Drive, Dropbox, OneDrive… folder (no server, no online service from the app), then “🔗 Link this folder”: the app puts a starting copy there, “depart.db”. On the other computer, open “depart.db” (start screen → Import a file, or a new empty genealogy), then choose the same folder at the same place. After that, “🔄 Synchronise” (or automatically on opening and every 15 minutes): each computer drops its changes in the folder and takes those of the other — records, families, events, places, notes, sources, citations, media (with the photo file). Just after, “↶ Undo” puts everything back as before; the undone changes are “set aside” and “Review” lets you choose them one by one. Not yet exchanged: the other tools (Workshop, boards, houses, animal tracking…) and the app settings."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Two computers: the same record was changed on both sides, what should I do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Everything else is merged by itself; only these records wait for you in Settings → Working together → “🔄 Two computers”, side by side: your version on the left, the other computer’s on the right, only what differs. “Keep mine” (your version goes back to the other one), “Take the other” (it replaces yours), and for a note, “Keep both” (the other’s note is added alongside, with the name of its computer in the title). “↶ Undo” in the message at the bottom left if you made a mistake."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"The app says “Genealogy open elsewhere”: what should I do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Your database is stored in a folder that two computers can see (iCloud, Dropbox, a network drive…) and the other computer has opened it: if both wrote to this file at the same time, it could be damaged. Close the app on the other computer, then “↻ Try again”. If the other computer is off or the app stopped abruptly there, “Open anyway” (the app also does it by itself after 5 minutes without any sign of life). To really work in two places, use “🔄 Two computers”: each keeps its own database, and changes go through a shared folder."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When starting, the app says “The database is being used by another program”: what should I do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Another program is holding your family tree’s file: another copy of the app, software that has opened it, or a sync (OneDrive, Dropbox, iCloud…) copying it right now. The app says so after a few seconds and changes nothing. Close that other program or wait for the sync to finish, then restart the app. If it happens at every start, restart the computer: the program that was holding the file will be closed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I send the tree to the family without risk of it being changed (Share with the family)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Export → “📤 Share with the family…”: choose the branches (nothing = the whole database), what happens to living people (hidden, or first name only) and what is shown (photos, records, notes). The summary “Here is what goes out” updates before the .zip file is made. The family unzips it and opens index.html with a double-click: the tree, the records, the photos and the search, without the app or the Internet, and nothing can be changed. Confidential records never go out, nor a photo showing a living person; the full name of a living person written in a note is replaced by “[name removed]”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I have a cousin review a branch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Export → “📤 Share with the family…” with the box “🖊 Ask for a review” (or record → ⋯ → “🖊 Have this branch reviewed…”). Your cousin ticks “agree” or “wrong” on each fact and sends you back a .json file. Import it in Questions for relatives → “⤒ Import the answers”, then ✓ Accept, ✕ Refuse or Later for each fact; “↶ Undo” undoes everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep a copy at a cousin’s?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Copy at a cousin’s: the copy is encrypted, they keep it without being able to read it and give it back to you if your computer breaks down."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I ask a cousin or an aunt for their memories?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 💬 Questions for relatives → “＋ New questionnaire” (or “💬 Ask questions about her” on a record, or “💬 Questionnaire” in the Directory of the living). Choose the recipient, a title, a welcome message and ready-made questions (childhood, house and village, occupations, war, celebrations, recipes, photos “Who is in this photo?”, a specific person) or your own; arrange them with ⠿. On the right, the preview as your relative will see it. “⤓ Download the questionnaire” makes a single .html file to attach to an e-mail (“📋 Copy the message” gives the text): it opens in their browser, without the Internet, keeps their answers as they go, and “⤓ Save my answers” gives them a small file to send back to you. “🖨 Printable version”: lines to answer by hand."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I bring a relative’s answers into my genealogy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 💬 Questions for relatives → “⤒ Import the answers”: drop the .json file they sent back, then choose for each answer where it goes: “💬 Testimony” on the record of the person concerned (“Testimonies / memories” block), “📖 Family memory” (recipe, tradition, story…) or “Do not import”. Their name and the date go automatically into “Where it comes from”. An answer received by plain e-mail: “✎ Paste an answer” on the questionnaire’s page. “↶ Undo” removes everything that has just come in. Only files from a questionnaire you sent are accepted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do two people work on the same genealogy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Working together: you start from the same database, then exchange packages of changes, accepted or refused one by one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a short video montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 🎬 Videos → “🎬 Montage” (far right), or “✂️ Edit” on a video. Five tracks: 🎬 Video 1 (the clips one after another, each with its transition: cross-fade, through black, through white), 🖼️ Video 2 (a video inset in a corner), 🔤 Texts (still or moving titles: fade, slide, rise, grow, scrolling credits, typewriter; font, size, colour, banner, position), 🎵 Sound 1 and Sound 2 (music, voice). “🔊 Detach the sound” puts a clip’s sound on a separate track, to move it, lower it or remove it. 🎨 Colours tab: brightness, contrast, saturation, temperature, tint and ready-made styles (Old film, Black and white…), for the whole film or a single clip. Click the timeline to position yourself, drag the insets, texts and sounds. “⤓ Export the video…” replays the montage and makes a file of it (MP4 or WebM); “💾 Save the project” keeps the montage to continue it later."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I save a montage to continue it later?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the montage: “💾 Save the project” (or ⌘S; ⇧⌘S for a copy under another name). Files imported from the computer are then sent to Media. To reopen it: “📂 Projects…” at the bottom of the montage, or Lists → Videos → “🎬 Montage”, which offers your projects (open, rename, delete) or a new montage. “⤓ Export the video…” makes the video file, without closing the montage."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I speed up or slow down a video or a sound in the montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the clip on the timeline, 🎯 Item tab → “Speed”: from 0.25 × to 4 × (or the 0.5 × 1 × 1.5 × 2 × buttons). The clip gets longer or shorter on the timeline; “keep the pitch of the voice” avoids the duck voice."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a photo last as long as I want in the montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the photo on the timeline, 🎯 Item tab → “Time on screen”: type the seconds (for example 5, or 12.5), or click 2 s, 3 s, 5 s, 8 s, 10 s, 15 s."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I adjust the sound of a single clip, or of the whole montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"One clip: choose it, 🎯 Item tab → “🔊 Sound of this clip”: clearer voice, less hiss, bass, treble, mono (and volume, fades for a sound). “Apply to all the sounds of the project” copies these settings everywhere. The whole film at once: 🔊 Sound tab (same settings, plus the overall volume and “without sound”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I undo in the montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⌘Z undoes the last change (cut with B, move, adjust…), ⇧⌘Z redoes it; also the ↶ ↷ buttons at the start of the toolbar."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I zoom in an editor (retouching, drawing, layout, house, montage…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"All editors have the same bar at the bottom: on the right “− 100 % ▾ +”. Click the percentage for “Fit to window”, 50 %, 100 % or 200 %. With the keyboard: ⌘ + and ⌘ − (Ctrl on a PC), ⌘ 0 to fit, ⌘ 1 for 100 %; or ⌘ + the mouse wheel. On the left of this bar: where you are (page, layer, slide…); in the middle: help for the chosen tool."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is 🧲 at the bottom of an editor for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🧲 Snap: while dragging, objects stick to others, to the edges, to the guides or to the grid. Click 🧲 to turn it on or off; the tooltip tells you which key to hold down to do without it for one gesture. 📏 next to it shows or hides the rulers and guides (when the editor has them)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where have the tools and the “Save…” buttons at the bottom of the editors gone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The tools are in the narrow column on the left (same place in all editors; hover over them for their name and key). The ways of saving (show this version, without showing it, separate copy, keep in media…) are at the top: click ▾ next to “💾 Save”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange a list with the keyboard?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Each line that can be arranged has a grey ⠿ handle on the left: drag it, or go to it with Tab and press Alt + ↑ or Alt + ↓ (⌥ on a Mac). In Appearance, the order is saved straight away (“✓ Saved”, and “↶ Undo” at the bottom left)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do record transcription and quick retouching have “Undo”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: ↶ and ↷ at the top (or ⌘Z and ⇧⌘Z) undo and redo everything: text, title, areas, image settings, retouches. In a text box, ⌘Z first undoes what you have just typed. The File, Edit, Tools and View menus are also at the top."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I have the calculator directly on the dashboard?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: in the left column of the Dashboard, add the “🧮 Calculator” widget with ＋. Type your calculation and press Enter; the last 5 calculations stay below (click to reuse them, ↑ for the last one). “⛶ Open the calculator” opens the big calculator (scientific, graphs)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where has the events’ 📅 “＋ Add to my calendar” button gone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It is hidden by default. To show it on the events of records: open My diary → ⚙️ Settings, then tick “Show the 📅 ＋ Add to my calendar button on record events”. The change is kept straight away."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"The settings of My diary or of books no longer have a “Save” button: is that normal?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: each change is kept straight away (“✓ Saved” at the top of the window), and “↶ Undo” at the bottom left undoes it. “↺ Reset to default” puts back the original settings, without a question. “Close” closes the window."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I set my own shortcuts in the montage, sound, retouching or layout?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Montage, retouching, layout and sound” → ⌨️ Your own shortcuts (or Settings → Keyboard shortcuts, at the bottom). Choose the tool at the top, then “Choose” next to the command and press the key. A key already taken by the system (macOS, Windows, Linux) or by the application menu is refused; if it is already used in the tool, you are warned and your shortcut takes priority. Without ⌘ / Ctrl, it only works outside text boxes. ✕ removes a shortcut."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Layout: how do I apply, remove or replace a master on a page?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Pages panel → Masters: the “Page N” list changes the master of the current page (or “No master” to remove it). “Several pages…” (also in the Page menu) does it on all pages, a range, the left or right pages, or replaces one master by another wherever it is. When you delete a master, you choose the one its pages take. ⌘Z undoes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a note built the same way again (same order of blocks)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the note: “⧉ Duplicate”, then “▦ The same order of blocks” (a new note with the same blocks, titles, widths and colours, but empty) or “⭐ Make it a template” (you can also keep the texts, dates, links and people; images do not go into a template). Your templates appear in “New note”, under “Start from”, in “⭐ My templates” (✎ to edit it, 🗑 to delete it), and at the bottom of “My notes”. Deleting a template does not change the notes already created with it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I come back later to a sound I am working on?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “Edit the sound”: “💾 Save the project” (at the bottom left, or ⌘S; ⇧⌘S for a copy under another name) keeps the sound in progress, without loss, separately from Media. To come back to it: “📂 Projects…” in “Edit the sound”, Lists → 🎧 Audio → “📂 Sound projects”, or Lists → 🛠️ Editing tools → 🎧 Sound. To start one: Lists → 🎧 Audio → “＋ New sound project” (a sound from the database or from the computer). When the sound is finished, “💾 Save…” makes a version, a copy or a new sound of it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is “⚡ Quick sound” and how do I get it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A window with big buttons to clean up a sound in two minutes: 🧹 hiss, ✨ clicks and crackles, 〰️ mains hum (found by itself, 50 or 60 Hz), 🗣️ clearer voice, 📏 even volume, ✂️ start and end trimmed (black handles on the waveform), ⏩ silences shortened; strength Gentle / Medium / Strong; “✨ Clean everything at once”. By default, “✂️ Edit” on a sound keeps the previous editor (“🎧 Edit the sound”); for the new one: Settings → Montage, retouching, layout and sound → 🎧 Sound → “⚡ Quick sound: the design” → “New ⚡ Quick sound” (kept by itself). Three choices: “As before” (the original editor, each tool with its name), “As before, tidied” (the same, tools in a column on the left and bottom bar shared with the other editors) and “New ⚡ Quick sound”. Nothing is damaged until you have saved: the original stays in the versions."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I compare the sound before and after cleaning?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In ⚡ Quick sound: 👂 Before / ✨ After, at the same place in playback (the sound continues, you only switch), or hold down the A key to hear the before. In 🎚️ Sound studio, holding A plays the sound without the rack’s effects."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the “🎚️ Sound studio”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The full version, in the style of Adobe Audition: waveform with selection, overview of the whole sound, zoom, 🚩 markers and named coloured regions, VU meters, clickable history ↶ ↷; an effects rack you can adjust at any time (graphic and parametric equalisers, compressor, limiter, noise reduction with a noise print, De-clicker, De-hum, De-esser, reverb, speed and pitch, normalise, even volume, fades with 4 curves): ⠿ to arrange, 👁 to switch off, × to remove, “Only on the selection”; the spectrum and its repair brush; Multitrack; transcription. You get there with “🎚️ Sound studio” in Quick sound (or the previous editor), or Lists → 🛠️ Editing tools → 🎧 Sound → “＋ New sound project”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I switch from Quick sound to Sound studio: do I lose my settings?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, the work follows: each button switched on becomes an effect in the rack, already set; the trimmed start and end become cuts of the waveform (in the history). When you go back (“⚡ Quick sound”), what Quick sound can show comes back as buttons; the rest stays applied and a badge says so (“2 Studio settings remain applied”). The last choice is remembered: “✂️ Edit” reopens the same one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I remove a cough or a ringtone in the middle of a sentence?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In 🎚️ Sound studio, “Spectrum” switch on: the cough shows as a bright patch. Take 🖌 (brush, B key) or ➰ (lasso), paint over the patch, then “🩹 Repair”: it is replaced by what surrounds it, without touching the words around it. Each repair is in the rack and the history (it can be removed)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I link a passage of a sound to a person? Where can it be heard afterwards?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In 🎚️ Sound studio: select the passage, “＋ New region” (or M), give it a name; then in “🔗 Link this passage” choose the person (the usual picker, with a role: speaks, sings, tells…) and, if you like, an event. Right-click a region: “🔗 Link to a person…”. On the person’s record, the “🎧 Heard here” block lists their passages: ▶ plays just that passage."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I transcribe what is said in a sound?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In 🎚️ Sound studio, Window → 📝 Transcription: listen, “⏱ Insert the time” (⌘T) and type what is said, with who is speaking. “🐢 Play at 75 %” slows it down, “Pause while I type” stops playback while you type. Clicking a time plays that passage. The text is searchable, and can be exported as .txt, .srt or .vtt (⤓ Export → 📝 Text and markers). Automatic transcription (an engine on the computer) will come later."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a “family podcast” with music under the voice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🎚️ Sound studio → “🎛️ Multitrack” tab: Voice, Music, Sound effects tracks (and others), with volume, pan, M (mute), S (solo), ● (arm to record from the microphone). “＋ Add a sound…” places a sound on a track (or drag it from “Files”). Turn on “🔉 Lower the music under the voice”: the music goes down by itself when the voice speaks. The yellow line is the volume curve: drag its ◆ points, double-click to add one, right-click to remove one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I rescue an old cassette step by step?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 🛠️ Editing tools → 🎧 Sound → “🧭 Rescue an old cassette”: choose the sound, then follow the steps (listen, hiss, hum and clicks, voice, start and end, save). Each step switches on the right button; compare with 👂 Before / ✨ After."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I record a sound from the microphone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 🛠️ Editing tools → 🎧 Sound → “🎙️ Record from the microphone”: ● Start, then ⏹; the sound goes into the media and opens in 🎚️ Sound studio. In the Studio, ● (under the waveform) records at the orange marker; in Multitrack, on the armed track ●."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which formats can I export a sound in?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⤓ Export: MP3 (plays everywhere), M4A (iPhone, iTunes — only if your browser can do it), WAV, FLAC (lossless), Ogg Opus (the lightest), AIFF (GarageBand, Logic), 📝 Text and markers (.txt, .srt, .vtt, .cue). You choose the quality, “What” (everything, the selection, each region in a file, each track in a file), the channels and the sample rate; “Write the regions and people into the file” puts the title and chapters into the MP3 or FLAC."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep my sound settings for the next cassette?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“💾 Saved settings” (in Quick sound and in the Studio rack): three ready-made sets (📼 1970s cassette, 🎙 Dictaphone, 💒 1987 wedding video) and your own (“＋ Save current settings”), shared between both windows; ⋯ to rename, duplicate, export, delete; ⤒ Import."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I clean the sound of a piece of the video montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the 🎬 montage, choose the sound on the timeline, 🎯 Item tab → “🔊 Sound of this piece” → “🎚️ Open in Sound studio”. Clean it, then 💾 ▾ → “Save and show this version”: it comes back clean in the montage. The other way round, Sound studio has File → “🎬 Open in the montage” to put pictures on a sound."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I separate the sound and the picture of a video in the montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the video on the timeline, 🎯 Item tab → “🔊 Separate sound from picture” (or “🔊 Detach sound” in the bar): the sound goes onto a sound track of its own, and can be moved, cut or adjusted on its own; the picture becomes silent. To take only the sound of a video: 🎞️ Rushes tab → “＋ Sound only”, or drag the video onto a sound track."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where does a rush dragged onto the montage timeline land?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the track at the height of the mouse. On Video 1, it slots in between the pieces, where you drop it; on an overlay or sound track, at the moment where you drop it. A video keeps its sound, even as an overlay."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I rename a rush in the montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🎞️ Rushes tab: ✎ under the rush. The new name shows on the timeline and in the saved project."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the 🎞️ Rushes tab of the montage for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"These are the videos, photos and sounds of the project. “⤒ Import” (several files at once) or “🗂 From the database”: they wait there, with their length, without going onto the timeline straight away. Drag a rush onto a track (video, overlay, sound) where you want it, or use “＋ Add after”, “＋ Overlay”, “＋ Sound”. The blue number says how many times it is on the timeline; ✕ removes an unused rush."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I set up the video montage (layout, buttons, export)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Montage, retouching, layout and sound”: the settings panel on the right or the left, the height of the picture and tracks, the help, the colour of the blocks on the timeline (main video, overlays, texts, sounds); the default export (MP4 or WebM, size: same as the video, 4K, 1080p, 720p, 480p, 360p; quality; frames per second; sound); the buttons and options of each editor (montage, retouching, sound), ticked or not and ordered; full screen on opening. When you save a video, you can still change the size, quality and frames per second."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the “Notes” tab for and how do I show it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A note is a page on a subject: “Poland before the Napoleonic wars”, a place, a trade, a custom… The tab is hidden at first: Settings → Appearance → “Main menu order”, tick “Notes”. “＋ New note”: a title, an icon, and a starting point (History topic, A place, A trade or an empty note)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What can I put in a note?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“＋ Block”: 📝 a text (# title, ## subtitle, - list, **bold**, *italic*, __underline__, [link](https://…), > quote), 🖼️ images and documents (from the computer or already in the app; as a mosaic, large or as a banner), 👥 people from the database, 📅 a timeline of dates, ❝ a quote and its source, 🔗 links, ➖ a subheading, 📺 a screen of the app (map, tree, list, My lists, statistic, a person’s record, village…) and 📊 a widget or chart of your own. “✏️ Arrange”: order with ↑ ↓, choose the width (¼ to full width), duplicate, delete; ✎ on a block to edit it; a colour per block or for the note. “🖨 Print” turns it into a clean page."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many tracks can a montage have? How do I add or delete them?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As many as you like, of video, text and sound. Under the timeline: “＋ Video track”, “＋ Text track”, “＋ Sound track”. Adding an overlay, a text or a sound at the same moment as another creates an extra track on its own. To the left of each track: 👁 hide it (neither seen nor heard), ↑ ↓ move it (a higher video track goes in front), 🗑 delete it (straight away, with its items: “↶ Undo” in the message at the bottom brings it back, like ⌘Z), double-click its name to rename it. Drag an item from one track to another of the same kind, or choose its track in the “🎯 Item” tab."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I shrink and move an overlaid video or a text in the montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On the picture: click the overlay (Video 2 track) or the text, then drag it; pull the small square in the bottom right corner to make it bigger or smaller. The exact settings (width, position, size) are also in the “🎯 Item” tab."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I zoom with the keyboard?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⌘ + and ⌘ −, ⌘ 0 to go back: in the montage this zooms the timeline; in “Edit the sound”, the waveform; in Retouch, the photo. Elsewhere, the whole page of the app gets bigger or smaller."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put retouching, sound or the montage in full screen?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The ⛶ button at the top right of the window (as for the boards); it is kept for next time. Settings → “Montage, retouching, layout and sound” also lets you choose full screen on opening."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I order or hide the buttons of Retouch, sound or the montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the window, “⚙️ Customise”: tick what is shown and order with ↑ ↓ (or by dragging ⠿) the tools, options, settings, playback buttons… Kept for next time; “↺ Reset to default” goes back to the original buttons."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put a photo in a video montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“＋ Video / photo after” adds a video or photo (from the database or the computer) to the Video 1 track; “＋ Overlay” puts it in a corner. A photo stays 5 s on screen at first: “Duration on screen” in the Item tab."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I move a montage item to another track, or remove it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Drag it onto a track of the same kind: a video or photo goes from the Video 1 track to an overlay track (and back), a text onto another text track, a sound onto another sound track. ⌫ (Backspace) or Delete removes the chosen item, B cuts it at the playhead."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I animate a video or photo (keyframes) in the montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the item, 🎯 Item tab → “🎛️ Picture settings” (as in Final Cut): Compositing (opacity, blend mode), Transform (position, rotation, scale, X and Y scale, anchor), Crop, Distort, Spatial conform. Place the playhead, click ◇: a keyframe; go further, change the value: a second one. Between the two, the value changes smoothly (or evenly). ◀ ◆ and ◆ ▶ jump from one keyframe to the next."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep several montage layouts or several sets of buttons?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Montage, retouching, layout and sound”: “💾 Saved settings” for the montage layout and for the buttons of each editor. Give a name, apply, delete, export or import a .json file."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What tools does Retouch have (Photoshop style)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A menu at the top (File, Edit, Image, Layer, Select, Filter, View) and a column of tools to the right of the photo: ➤ move, ⬚ ◯ selections, ➰ lasso, ✒︎ pen, 🪄 automatic selection, ⌗ crop and straighten, 🩹 healing, 🧬 clone stamp, 🖌 brush, ⬜ shapes, T text, ✋ hand, 🔍 zoom. The photo itself is a layer (visibility, opacity, mask). The original is never changed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I select part of a photo in Retouch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tools ⬚ (rectangle), ◯ (oval), ➰ (freehand lasso), ✒︎ (pen: point by point, Enter or double-click to close) or 🪄 (click a colour: neighbouring points of a similar shade; set the tolerance). ⇧ adds, ⌥ removes. Select menu: all, none, invert, feather, expand, contract. The selection can then go onto a layer (⌘J), be filled, erased (⌫: transparent), used as a mask or for cropping."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I save a retouch to continue it later?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In Retouch: “💾 Save the project” (bottom left, ⌘S, or File → Save the project; ⇧⌘S for a copy under another name). Everything is kept: corrections, settings, filters, cropping, layers, groups, adjustment layers, masks and selection. To pick it up again: “📂 Projects…” at the bottom of Retouch, or Lists → Media → “Retouch” → “📂 Projects”. The buttons “Save and show this version”, “Save without showing it” and “Separate copy” make the final image."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get more room for the photo in Retouch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The ⇥ button at the top right of the panel (Settings, Filters, Layers, History) folds it; ⇤ opens it again (also the Tab key, or View → Fold / open the panel). This is kept for next time. You can also widen the panel by dragging its edge."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the montage have a menu like Retouch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, next to the title: File (save the project, projects, export the video), Edit (undo, redo, cut here, starts and ends here, forward, back, separate the sound, remove), Add (import rushes, a rush from the database, video or photo after, overlay, title, sound), Tracks, Playback (play, to the start, keyframes) and View (tabs, timeline zoom, full screen)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make the small track buttons in the montage (👁 🔓 ↑ ↓ 🗑) bigger?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the montage: View → “Track buttons: bigger” (or smaller, or starting size); it is kept straight away, with “↶ Undo” in the message. Or Settings → “Montage, retouching, layout and sound” → 🎬 Montage layout → “Size of track buttons” (from 16 to 44 px). The column of track names widens with them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the “B = cut” key (or Space, ⌫) in the montage?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the montage: Edit → “⌨️ Montage shortcuts…” (or Settings → Keyboard shortcuts, 🎬 Video montage tab): click the command, press the key you want. If you give another key to “✂️ Cut here”, “▶ Play / pause” or “🗑 Remove the chosen item”, the original key (B, Space, ⌫) no longer does that action: it is free. The menus, tooltips and the help at the bottom show your key."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"In the montage, what is the difference between “💾 Save” and “⤓ Export”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“💾 Save” keeps the montage project (tracks, cuts, titles) so you can reopen it and continue later; its ▾ menu also offers “Save the project under another name…” and “Keep in media (make the video)…”. “⤓ Export ▾” makes a file (MP4, WebM, sound only MP3 or WAV, subtitles) in the common export window, with a copy in the media if you want."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I use my computer’s fonts for the montage titles?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: choose the title, 🎯 Item tab → “Font”: the common font list opens (search, recent, fonts of the app and of the computer; fonts already used in this film come first). If the browser asks, “🔓 See all the computer’s fonts”. The font is also used in the exported video; on another computer that does not have it, a similar font replaces it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the track buttons of the video editor be small, medium or large?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: in the video editor, Display → “Track buttons: small” or “large” (“default size” = medium, as before); or Settings → “Editing, retouching, layout and sound” → 🎬 Video editor layout → the “Small”, “Medium (default)” and “Large” buttons under “Track button size”. It is kept straight away, with “↶ Undo” in the message."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a clip’s sound rise or fall over time in the video editor?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the clip (video, overlay or sound), 🎯 Item tab → “📈 Volume over time”: set the fade-in and fade-out, then “✎ Place points on the clip”. On the clip, in the timeline: a click places a point (at the top: louder, up to twice as loud; in the middle: the set volume; at the bottom: silence), drag a point to move it, a double-click removes it. “＋ Point at the playhead” places one where you are; “✕ Clear the curve” removes them all (with “↶ Undo”). The curve is used for the preview, the exported video and the standard volume (LUFS)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What effects can I put on the sound of a clip in the video editor?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🎯 Item tab → “🔊 Sound of this clip” (or the 🔊 Sound tab for the whole film): clearer voice, less hiss, bass, treble, mono, “🗜 More even volume” (quiet passages come up, loud ones go down), “⛪ Reverb” (a room, a large hall, a church, a cellar) and “↔ Left ↔ right”. These are the same effects as in 🎧 Sound and the 🎚️ Sound studio (a single engine). “↺ Reset to default” removes them; there is also one for the colours of a clip, the colours of the whole film and the sound of the whole film, with “↶ Undo” in the message."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I arrange the settings of the video editor’s right panel?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Each tab (🎯 Item, 🎨 Colours, 🔊 Sound, 🎞️ Film) is made of sections that you fold or open with a click on their title. To change their order: drag the ⠿ handle in front of the title, or go onto it (Tab) and press Alt + ↑ / ↓. The order (for a video, an overlay, a text or a sound) and the open or closed sections are kept for next time. Display → “Right panel sections: ↺ Reset to default” puts everything back as before."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I put an outline, a shadow or a band on the titles of the video editor?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: choose the title, 🎯 Item tab. “🔤 Font and colour”: font (from the app or the computer), colour, bold, italic, shadow, band, fade. “✒️ Outline and shadow”: a coloured outline around the letters and its thickness, the colour and strength of the shadow. “▭ Band behind the text”: its opacity and rounded corners. “✨ Movement”: the entrance (fade, slide, typewriter…), the movement during and the exit; “📐 Duration, size and position”: the alignment and position. Everything looks the same in the exported video."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find all my montage, sound, retouching and layout projects?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 🛠️ Editing tools: one tab per tool (🎬 Video montage, 🎧 Sound, 🎚️ Photo retouching, 📐 Layout). Search by name, sort (newest, oldest, A → Z, Z → A), open, rename (✎) or delete (🗑) a project, or start a new one."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I rename, resize or change the format of several images (or documents) at once?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 🛠️ Editing tools → “⚙️ Batch processing” → “＋ New process”: a kind of Automator. 1. The files: “🖼 Choose from media…” (several at once, with search and filters) or drag them from the computer: images, documents (Word, LibreOffice, RTF, Markdown, text, web page; PDF read-only), spreadsheets (Excel, LibreOffice, CSV) and sounds. 2. The actions, as stacked cards that you order with ⠿ (or Alt + ↑ / ↓), switch off with the toggle, duplicate (⧉) or remove (×): Rename (a text, a sequence 001, 002…, the date, the title, the person or the place, find / replace, capitals, without accents), Resize (longest side, width, height, percentage, without enlarging), Crop, rotate, mirror, Black and white, Watermark, Change format (JPG, PNG, WebP, AVIF; .docx, .odt, .rtf, .md, .html, .txt; .xlsx, .ods, .csv; MP3, WAV, FLAC), Quality and size, Erase metadata (GPS position…), Tags and person, Combine into one PDF. 3. “Here is what will happen” shows each name, size and format before → after; choose the result (new version of the media item, the original staying in its versions; copy in the media; or ⤓ a ZIP) then “▶ Run”. In the database, “↶ Undo” puts everything back as before. Keep your chains in “💾 Saved recipes” (ready-made sets: Photos for the web, Rename in sequence, Scans to PDF…)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does Retouch not show “Separate copy” and “Save without showing it”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"These buttons are for photos in the database: the retouch becomes a version of the photo, the original being kept. For an empty canvas or an imported photo, there is no original to keep: “🖼 Save the image in Media” and “⤓ Download” are enough, and “💾 Save the project” keeps the layers so you can continue."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I put the Retouch tool column?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → “Montage, retouching, layout and sound” → 🎚️ Photo retouching: the tool column (➤ ⬚ ◯ 🪄 ⌗ 🖌 T…) next to the image, far left or far right, and the panel (Settings, Layers…) on the right or left. Then “Save”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I order or hide the buttons of the layout workshop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the workshop, “⚙️ Customise” (top right), or Settings → “Montage, retouching, layout and sound” → Buttons and options → Editor “📐 Layout”: top menus, ↶ ↷, tools of the left column, tabs of the right panel and bottom bar, to tick and order with ↑ ↓. Open the workshop once so that the list fills up."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make layer groups in Retouch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Layers → “＋ Group” (or Layer → New group), or “Group” (⌘G) to put the chosen layer into a new group. A group can contain layers and other groups, as many levels as you like. The simplest: grab the ⠿ handle of a row and drag it — above or below another item, onto the middle of a group’s row (or onto “empty group”) to put it inside, onto the Photo row to put it right at the bottom. Also: the 📁 menu of each row, ↑ ↓ for the order, ▾ to fold a group. A group has its own visibility, opacity, lock and mask. ✕ on a group deletes it but keeps everything it contains."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put settings or filters in a layer (adjustment layer)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Layers → “＋ Adjustment” (or, at the top of the Settings and Filters tabs, “＋ in an adjustment layer”): the sliders, the curve and the filters then adjust that layer (the banner at the top says what you are adjusting; “adjust the photo” to go back). It acts on everything below it; tick “only on …” so that it only affects the layer or group just below (like a Photoshop clipping mask). Put in a group, it only affects that group. It can be masked, dosed (opacity) and hidden like a layer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I start from a blank image in Retouch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → Media → “Retouch” → “⬜ Blank canvas”: choose the size (screen, square, A4, postcard or your own) and the background (white, transparent or a colour). The canvas is added to Media and opens in Retouch, ready for layers, text and shapes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I crop or straighten a photo in Retouch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tool ⌗ (C): drag the handles of the frame or draw a new one, choose proportions (square, 4:3, A4…), rotate with “Straighten”, then “✓ Confirm” (or Enter). Escape cancels. Select menu → “Crop to selection” crops as tightly as possible."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide part of a layer without erasing it (mask)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Layers → 🎭 on the layer (or the photo): a mask is added and the brush paints into it, ⬛ to hide, ⬜ to show. Click the layer thumbnail to go back to the layer. “invert the mask”, “remove the mask”, or Select → “Mask” to make one from the selection."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I copy a whole area with the clone stamp?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Select the area (⬚ ◯ ➰ ✒︎ 🪄), take the clone stamp 🧬 and drag: a copy of the area lands where you let go. Without a selection, the stamp copies with a brush from the chosen spot (🎯 or ⌥ + click)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I write or draw shapes on a photo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tool T: click, type, Enter (bold, italic, white outline, computer fonts). Tool ⬜: rectangle, rounded rectangle, ellipse, line or arrow, empty or filled (⇧: square, circle, right angle). With ➤ you move them, ⌫ deletes them, double-click a text to rewrite it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What filters does Retouch have?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"✨ Filters tab (or Filter menu): blur, noise reduction, sharpness, clarity, grain and vignette, applied after the settings, also on the full-size saved image."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I set up the layout of boards (toolbar, inspector)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Boards → “Menus and options” tab → “🧭 Board layout”: toolbar at the top, bottom or side, inspector on the right or left, button size, title shown or not. The ⚙️ button in a board leads straight there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I edit a block of a note on a subject?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The block’s ✎ flips it over, like a dashboard widget: title, width (1, 1/2, 1/3, 1/4, 2/3, 3/4: you see it straight away), colour and content, then Save or Cancel. “＋ Block” adds a card at the bottom of the note, already flipped over to its settings."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide the empty lines of a record’s Identity block?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"At the bottom of the Identity block: “Hide on this record” or “Hide on all” (and “Show…” to go back). The setting for all records is also in Settings → Appearance → record blocks."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I hide lists in the Lists menu?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → “Reorder” (bottom left): it takes you to Settings → “Menus, column and lists” → “Lists” tab. The eye on each row hides or shows the list; drag or use ↑ ↓ for the order."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I assemble sounds from scratch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 🎧 Audio → “🎬 Montage”: choose a first sound, then “＋ Add a sound…”, cut, fades, volume… “Save” makes it a new sound in the list."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add criteria to a distribution (Charts → Statistics)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Under “From … to … · Limit · Sex”, “＋ Criterion” adds as many criteria as you like, the same as in My lists and the Chart editor: name, occupation, tag, place, within … km of, had a marriage, number of children, my ancestors (Sosa), without source… The “not” box reverses a criterion, 🗑 removes it, “↺ Reset to default” removes them all. The statistic then only counts the records kept (and their events)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I see who is behind a bar of a distribution?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Click the bar (or column): a window shows the people counted, with a search, sorting by name or birth and a CSV export — as for a chart of your own. This works for surnames, first names, places and countries of birth, marriage and death, sexes, years of birth and death and age at death."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I choose the starting filters of all distributions?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a distribution, “⚙️ Default settings of distributions”: set the years, limit, sex and criteria, then “⭐ Keep these filters as default for all”. They are used at the start of each distribution; “↺ Back to default filters” puts them back, “No more default setting” starts again from all the data."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep all my app settings, or move them to another computer?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance, at the very bottom → “💾 Saved settings: the whole app”: “＋ Save current settings” and a name keep all the settings (appearance, lists and columns, your own charts and tabs, widgets, boards, animals, books, shortcuts…). “Apply” goes back to them (the screen reloads), ⋯ → “Export” makes a file of them, “⤒ Import” takes it up on another computer. Your data is never touched; what targets a specific record (root person, a record’s exception) and what is specific to this computer are not kept."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do saved settings keep Designs to choose from, portraits, Accessibility and Simple mode?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. “💾 Saved settings: Appearance” keeps everything about appearance: the design, Designs to choose from, portraits, the left column, menus, colours… “💾 Saved settings: the whole app” also keeps Accessibility, Simple mode settings and, for each list, its sort and the List or Cards view (not what you typed in the search). Never included, even in an exported file: the code to leave Simple mode, the person watching, hidden photos, the backup folder. A setting saved before these new features puts them back “As before”, without touching the Simple mode code; an old setting that contained the font, the dashboard or sets from Designs to choose from applies without error, those parts are simply ignored. An imported file is checked: anything unknown or damaged is left out."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep or share my layout?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance → “💾 Saved settings: Appearance”: give a name to the current settings (design, menus shown or hidden and their order, blocks, colours…), go back to them in one click, or send the file to another user who imports it in the same place."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I show the tree without the risk of it being changed?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"View-only mode (padlock at the top right) blocks any change; you leave it with your password."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know if there is a new version of LCNDA?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the desktop app, LCNDA checks on its own at start-up (at most once a day) the latest version published on GitHub. If there is a newer one, a message at the bottom left says so: “⤓ Download”, “See what’s new”, “Later” or “Don’t tell me about this version again”. To check right away: Settings → Help → “🔄 Updates” → “🔍 Check for an update” (or the application menu: LCNDA on Mac, Help on Windows and Linux). Only the question “what is the latest version?” goes to GitHub: nothing from your genealogy, no identifier. To never check: “Check for updates: Never” on the same page. Nothing is shown in Simple mode or in View-only mode."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I install a new version?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⤓ Download” takes the right installer for your computer (Apple or Intel Mac as .dmg, Windows as .exe, Linux as AppImage or .deb depending on what you use), puts it in your “Downloads” folder with a progress bar, then checks its fingerprint (the SHA256SUMS.txt file published with the version): a damaged file is deleted. Then “↗ Open” and you install it yourself (nothing is replaced on its own, the installers are not signed): on Mac, drag LCNDA into “Applications”; on Windows, follow the installation. Your genealogy, media and settings do not move."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Change the app language","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Appearance (“Theme and colours”), at the very top: “🌐 App language”. Thirty-two languages: français, English, Español, Deutsch, Italiano, Português, Polski, Nederlands, Čeština, Svenska, Dansk, Slovenčina, Norsk (bokmål), Română, Suomi, Magyar, Hrvatski, Slovenščina, Lietuvių, Latviešu, Eesti, Íslenska, Türkçe, Lëtzebuergesch, Shqip, Malti, Tiếng Việt, Bahasa Indonesia, Русский, Українська, Ελληνικά, Български; the choice is kept straight away on this computer (“↶ Undo” to go back), and can also be made on the first-launch screen. French remains the reference language: the other translations are made by an AI, not by a person. A banner reminds you of this at the bottom right, with “Report a mistake” (it opens “🐞 Report a problem”, already filled in with “translation”); it closes with a click on × and comes back with “Show the banner again”. The whole app is translated: menus, records, tools, Settings and this FAQ. Your data (names, notes, places) is never translated."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which language does the app open in the very first time?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In your computer’s language, if the app knows it (for example in German on a computer set to German); otherwise in English, and in French on a computer set to French. On this first screen, the “🌐” menu lists every language, and two buttons “🇫🇷 Français” and “🇬🇧 English” switch with one click. Your choice is kept afterwards. A genealogy you have already started never changes language on its own."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I report a problem (a bug)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → FAQ / tutorial (or About, or “🔄 Updates”) → “🐞 Report a problem”, or the Help menu of the desktop app. Write what happened and what you expected. A technical report is attached, visible and editable: version, system, size of the database in numbers only, useful display settings and the last 50 errors caught. Names, dates, places and file paths are removed from the errors: nothing from your genealogy is in it. Then “📋 Copy”, “Open an issue on GitHub” (the message is ready) or “✉️ Write an e-mail” if an address is set in “🔄 Updates”. Nothing is sent without you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"The 🐞 button at the top right and the “Something didn’t work” banner","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The 🐞 “Report a bug” button, at the top right, opens the same “🐞 Report a problem” window (you arrange or hide it in Settings → Appearance → “Top-right buttons”). When the app runs into a real error, a small banner also appears at the bottom right: “🐞 Something didn’t work. Do you want to report it?”. “Report” opens the window, already filled in with the screen and the error (with nothing from your family tree): all you have left to do is say what you were doing just before; × closes it. It comes back at most once per screen and per minute, never in simple mode, and sits above the languages banner if it is there. To stop seeing it: Settings → “🔄 Updates” → “Offer to report when something doesn’t work”. Nothing is sent without you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"My database is very big (100,000 people): does the app stay fast?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. It has been tried on a database of 100,000 people (13 generations, 340,000 events, 20,000 places): the list of people, the search (even phonetic), a record, the trees, the map and the lists open in less than a second. Big calculations (statistics, ✅ Database check, GEDCOM export) take a few seconds, with the spinning circle, and the Check is then kept as long as nothing changes. In Lists › Occupations, each occupation shows its first 24 people, then “See the next 24 ↓”. A very large GEDCOM import (100,000 people) takes about a minute."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get back a record deleted by mistake?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Change log: a deletion is restored with everything that was attached. ⌘Z undoes the last change to a record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the export say some records are “to review before publishing”?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Before the GEDCOM “For sharing” and “🌐 For Geneanet”, the app counts the records whose 🛡 Reliability score is below the threshold (without living people or confidential records). “See them” shows them in Lists → People, “Leave them out” removes them from the file, “Export anyway” keeps everything."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I use the spreadsheet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 🛠️ Editing tools → “🧾 Spreadsheet”, then “＋ New workbook” (or “🧩 Start from a template”). Click a cell and type: a number, a text, a record date (“about 1784”, “an II”), or a formula starting with “=” (=SUM(D2:D9)). While typing a formula, click or drag over cells to put them in; the list of functions opens from the first letters (Tab to complete). Sheets are at the bottom (＋, double-click to rename, right-click to duplicate or delete). In the bottom bar: on the left the sheet and the cell (with the sum, average and count of the selection — click to choose what is shown), in the middle some help, on the right the zoom. ↶ ↷ undo and redo everything; “💾 Save” puts the workbook in the list."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I enlarge the grid, change the font or set up the spreadsheet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Zoom: bottom right [− 100 % ▾ +] (⌘+ and ⌘−, ⌘0 to fit the filled columns to the window, ⌘1 for 100 %, or ⌘ + mouse wheel); it is remembered from one time to the next. Font: the “Font” button in the toolbar (or Format → “Aa Font…”) opens the list of fonts of the app and of the computer; the box next to it gives the size (13 px at first); both also go into the Excel file. The column of icons to the left of the grid brings together 🔍 Find, ⚲ Filter, ↕ Sort, ƒ Function, Σ, 👤 Person, 📍 Place, 💬 Note, 📊 Chart, 🎨 Conditional formatting, = Show formulas and ◫ the right panel. “💾 Save ▾” offers a copy, a template or the export. File → “⚙️ Spreadsheet settings…”: each change is kept straight away (“↶ Undo” at the bottom left, “↺ Reset to default”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What formulas does the spreadsheet understand?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Formulas are written as in Excel in French, with “;” between the parts: SOMME, MOYENNE, NB, NB.SI, SOMME.SI, SI, SI.ERREUR, RECHERCHEV, RECHERCHEX, INDEX, EQUIV, CONCAT, GAUCHE, TEXTE, DATE, ANNEE, DATEDIF, ARRONDI, MEDIANE, UNIQUE, TRIER, FILTRE… English names work too (SUM becomes SOMME). For genealogy: AGE(\"vers 1784\";\"an II\") → ≈ 10, ANNEE.ACTE(\"baptisé le 3 frimaire an XII\") → 1803, SOSA.GENERATION(12) → 4. “≈” means that a date is approximate. An error (#DIV/0!, #NOM?, #BOUCLE!…) is explained in plain words under the formula bar, with the cell concerned. Insert → “ƒ Function…” shows the whole list."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do my workbooks keep everything when I exchange them with Excel or LibreOffice?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, for the most part. ⤓ Export → XLSX (Excel) or ODS (LibreOffice) keeps values, formulas (translated into English for Excel), sheets, bold, colours, widths, merges, frozen panes, range names, 💬 notes, validation (choice lists, numbers between two limits), the filter and, in XLSX, conditional formatting. ⤒ Import reads all this back in both directions. Specific to the app: the “👤 person” or “📍 place” cells from the database, record dates in validation, and genealogy functions (AGE, SOSA…), which go out as values. In a formula, Shift + arrows extends the range pointed at (A2 → A2:A4) and brackets forgotten at the end are closed on Enter."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I open an Excel, LibreOffice or CSV file in the spreadsheet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the spreadsheet: File → “⤒ Import a table…” (or “⤒ Import…” in the list of workbooks). .xlsx (Excel, Numbers), .ods (LibreOffice), .csv, .tsv and .json files are read: several sheets, formulas, bold, colours, widths and merges. For a CSV, the separator and the accents of an old Excel file are guessed. You can also paste a table copied from Excel or Google Sheets (⌘V in the grid). Damaged or booby-trapped files are refused."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export a workbook to Excel, LibreOffice, CSV or PDF?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⤓ Export ▾” at the top of the spreadsheet: XLSX (Excel, Numbers), ODS (LibreOffice), CSV and TSV (the open sheet), JSON, PDF, 🖨 Print, or the tool’s own file. Formulas go out in English for Excel; those that do not exist elsewhere (AGE, SOSA…) go out with their result. In a CSV, a cell starting with “=” is protected so that no formula can slip in."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I sort, filter or clean up a transcription in the spreadsheet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Data menu: “↕ Sort A → Z” (record dates are sorted by their date), “⚲ Filter” (the search and filter bar of the lists appears above the grid, with “N rows of M”), “Remove duplicates…”, “Split text into columns…” (“MARTIN Jean, 34 ans, laboureur” → 4 columns), “Data validation…” (a choice list, or a person or place from the database chosen with the usual picker, ⌘K). Everything can be undone with “↶ Undo”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a chart from the spreadsheet figures?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the range (for example Decade, Men, Women) then “📊 Chart ▾”: the spreadsheet suggests the types that suit the range (decades: the line first; age bands and two series: the pyramid; a single series: the pie chart…) and opens the diagram editor. The diagram stays linked to the range: at the top of its data, “🔗 Data: “Workbook” ! A1:C4 · kept up to date”. Change a figure in the workbook (or add a row just below) and save: the diagram redraws itself wherever it is placed. “Freeze them” cuts the link. The workbook is stored in the Spreadsheet along the way."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I edit the figures of a diagram, chart or board in the spreadsheet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Wherever there are figures, a “🧾 Edit the data” button opens the spreadsheet full size, already set up with the right columns (the blue banner says what is expected): Diagrams (Data tab), Chart editor (“✍️ My own figures”), Presentation (Table and Chart objects), Boards (▦ Spreadsheet object: “🧾 Open in the spreadsheet”) and Collections (one-page spreadsheet). You have formulas, sorting, pasting from Excel… then “✓ Save and go back” sends everything back. A cell like “approx. 12” is counted as 12. The small quick grids remain for 5 rows."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is “🎯 Prepare for…” in the spreadsheet for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Data menu → “🎯 Prepare for…”: choose where the data of the range is going (a diagram, a chart, a presentation, a board, a collection). The spreadsheet shows what that place expects and which column it found (✓), flags the cells to check (⚠️ “C7 contains “env. 12”: counted as 12”, with “See the cell”), makes a preview, then creates the thing. For a board or a collection, it copies the range: paste it (⌘V) where you want."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I take a Spreadsheet workbook for a diagram?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the diagram editor, Data tab: “🧾 Take a workbook from the Spreadsheet” (also at the top of “📋 Paste a table”). Choose the workbook with the picker, then the sheet and the range (by default the whole sheet), look at the preview, and choose “Copy once” or “Keep the link” (the diagram then follows the workbook)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I only see 400 rows in the data of a diagram: how do I see the others?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Under the table: “See the next N ↓” adds the following rows (editable like the others, colour included), “See all” adds them all, and “🧾 See all in the spreadsheet” opens everything in the spreadsheet. All rows count in the drawing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where is the calculator, and how do I keep it open?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The 🧮 button at the top right (or ⇧⌘Y — Ctrl+Shift+Y on a PC —, the “Shortcuts” widget, or Lists → 🛠️ Editing tools → “🧮 Calculator”) opens a small window that floats above the app: it stays open while you change screens, and even over an input window (a record, an event), so you can calculate without closing anything. Drag its title to move it, the bottom right corner to enlarge it (double-click the title: back to its place). Escape clears the box, then closes the calculator. Three tabs: 🔢 Simple (big keys, tape, memory), 🧪 Scientific, 📈 Graphs. It is not the 🧮 Spreadsheet (tables of cells to keep)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I use the calculator with the keyboard?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Type the calculation (12 × 3,5, 1/3 + 1/6, 2^10, √2…): it is redrawn neatly above (stacked fractions, powers, roots) and the result is shown in grey; Enter confirms it. A sign typed just after a result continues the calculation (“+ 8” becomes “ans + 8”). ↑ ↓ bring back earlier calculations; a click on a line of the tape takes back its result, ✎ takes back the calculation to edit it, 📋 copies it (⌘C with nothing selected copies the result). MC MR M+ M−: the memory. An error is explained in plain words under the box (“A closing bracket is missing”) and the cursor goes to the faulty spot. Fractions stay exact (1/3 + 1/6 = 1/2, the decimal value below); 200 + 10 % = 220."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What can the scientific calculator do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"🧪 Scientific tab, sections above the keys (each section has examples to click): trigonometry in degrees, radians or grads (DEG RAD GRAD), hyperbolic functions, ln, log, exp, π, e, φ; complex numbers (3 + 4i, modulus, argument, conjugate, √(−4) = 2i); gcd, lcm, C(n ; k), A(n ; k), prime numbers and factors(360) = 2³ × 3² × 5, rounding; binary, octal, hexadecimal (255 in binary, 0xFF); statistics on a list [12 ; 15 ; 9] (mean, median, standard deviation, quartiles); matrices [[1 ; 2] ; [3 ; 4]] (det, inverse, product, or “▦” to write them in a grid); equations up to the 4th degree typed with “=” (x^2 − 3x + 2 = 0, with the exact value ± √…) and systems resoudre(2x + y = 3 ; x − y = 0); derivative, integral, limit, sum of a sequence. In French notation, the comma is for numbers (2,5) and “;” separates the numbers of a function: pgcd(12 ; 18). ⚙️ sets the theme, the angle, the number of digits, comma or point, fractions, the region for measures, with “💾 Saved settings”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I convert toises, arpents or livres-sols-deniers?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the calculator, write “3 toises en m”, “5 arpents en ha”, “12 pieds en m”, “2 toises 3 pieds en m”, “1 livre poids en g”, “785 deniers en livres” (→ 3 livres 5 sols 5 deniers), “81 livres en francs”, “100 °F en °C”, or use the “Units” section (Value, From, To). Old measures follow the region chosen in ⚙️ (Paris by default, Lyon, Brittany, Normandy, Burgundy, Provence, Lorraine, Alsace, Flanders): these are the same tables as Settings → Calendar / measures, given to 6 digits (“≈” when the value changed from one village to the next)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can the calculator count with record dates?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, like the date fields (C4): “3 frimaire an XII” gives 25 November 1803 and the day of the week; “14 juillet 1794 en républicain” gives 26 messidor an II; “7 mai 1925 → 8 décembre 1960” (or “du … au …”) gives the age or the gap: 35 years, 7 months and 1 day, i.e. 12,999 days. With an incomplete date (“1925 → 1960”), it gives a range. “Dates” section of scientific mode: two boxes ready to use. The French Republican calendar was only used from 22 September 1792 to 31 December 1805."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I plot a graph?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Calculator → 📈 Graphs tab: write y = sin(x), x^2/4 − 1… (up to 6 curves, each in its own colour; “＋ Add a curve”, × to remove one). Drag to move around, mouse wheel or [− 100 % +] to zoom, hover over a curve to read x and y; “⤓” downloads the image (PNG). Curves are calculated in radians. A wrong formula is explained under its line."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I open a list or a statistic in the spreadsheet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Above a list (Lists): “⤓ Export ▾” → “🧾 Open in the spreadsheet” (the columns and filters shown) or “As CSV”. For one of My charts, a list of artists or a diagram: “⤓ Export” → “🧾 Spreadsheet”. The workbook opens without being stored: “💾 Save” keeps it in the Spreadsheet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I fill the spreadsheet with data from my genealogy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the spreadsheet: Data → “⤒ Fill from the app…”. 1. What to take: 👥 People or 📅 Events (with the usual bar: 🔍 Search, Filter ▾ by sex, Sosa, place of birth, tag, years), 🏠 Censuses (a municipality, a year), 📜 Index transcriptions, 📈 Statistics (first names, occupations, ages at death…) or 📑 My lists. 2. The columns: order them with ⠿, remove them with ✕, “＋ A column taken from the data” adds one (father, mother, spouse, nationality…). 3. The preview, then “Where” (new sheet or from the chosen cell) and “⤒ Fill N rows”. People and places arrive as chips linked to their record; an empty cell in these columns is filled with the picker. “🔒 Without the living or confidential records” leaves them out (always in View-only mode)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does “Keep up to date” mean in the spreadsheet?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"If the “🔗 Keep up to date” box is ticked when filling, the sheet stays linked to your database: a green banner “🔗 Kept up to date” shows it, with “↻ Reload”. It is read again on its own each time the workbook is opened (and when you click ↻): new records added, deleted records removed, names and dates corrected. Columns you add on the right (a formula, a note) stay on their person’s row, even if the order changes. ⋯ → “Stop keeping up to date” keeps the values as they are. In all workbooks, a person or place chosen in a cell follows its record (new name); a deleted record becomes a grey “?” chip again."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I turn a transcription (census, register) into records?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the spreadsheet: Data → “📤 Send to the database…” (or the button in the transcription’s banner). 1. The columns: what it creates (👤 records, 🏠 a census with its households, 📅 events) and, for each column, where it goes (Surname, First name, Age, Occupation, Relation to head…, guessed from the headings). 2. “Here is what will come in”, row by row: ALREADY IN THE DATABASE (the record is linked), NEW RECORD, TO CHECK (several possible records: choose ▾ the right one, a new record, or leave the row), PROBLEM (impossible age, missing name…: the row is left out). 3. “📤 Create N records and link N”: everything comes in at once; for a census, each household becomes a census of the municipality, the wife and children of the head are linked as a family. The message at the bottom left offers “↶ Undo”: everything just created is removed and the database is as before."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I prepare a census transcription with the right columns?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Spreadsheet → “🧩 Start from a template” → “🏠 Census transcription”. The banner at the top lets you choose the year (1836 → 1936): the columns change with it (age and calculated “Born about” until 1901; year and place of birth, nationality, employer after that); rows you have already typed stay under the same heading. Choose the municipality with the place picker, type the transcription, then “📤 Send to the database…”. From Lists → 📜 Record indexes, “🧾 In the spreadsheet” opens the people and households of a page (street, no., household, surname, first name, age, occupation, relation), kept up to date; sending links each transcribed person to their record. In the Research workshop, “Add an item from the software” → “🧾 A workbook” puts a workbook (budget, schedule, table of sources) in a folder of the project, and Export → “🧾 Table of all items, in the spreadsheet” opens it right away."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make my family website (without knowing how to code)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 🛠️ Editing tools → “🌐 My website” → “＋ New site”: choose a template (My family, My ancestor, My village, Artist portfolio, Blank page). On the left, “＋ Blocks”: drag a header, a banner, a text, an image or a gallery (image window), and the blocks from your genealogy (tree, record, timeline, map, figures, photos of a person, people of a place) into the preview. “T Write”: click in a text to change it. “🎨 Styles”: colours, fonts, width. 🖥 💻 📱: the preview on a computer, tablet or phone. 💾 Save (⌘S) stores it in the list, with its versions."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I link my code to the visual site (“this is my header”)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “🌐 My website”, “◫ Both” shows the preview and the code side by side: click an element of the preview, it says what it is (“This is: header.site-entete”) and its lines light up in the code; click in the code, the element is selected in the preview. On the right, give it a role with one click (Header, Menu, Main content, Section, Footer, Card), a name, a tag, a class, an id. Typing in the code updates the preview straight away; writing in the preview updates the code. Tab expands an abbreviation: ul>li*3, div.carte>h2+p, m10 → margin: 10px;."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does my website show living people?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The genealogy blocks (tree, record, timeline, map, figures, photos) follow the rule of “📤 Share with the family”: no living person and no confidential record, and a photo only goes out if nobody hidden is linked to it. If you have written by hand the full name of a living person, it becomes “[name removed]” in the exported site (the export window says so beforehand)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put my website online?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “🌐 My website”: “⤓ Export ▾” → “📦 The whole site as .zip” gives a .zip (index.html, the pages, css/, js/, images/ reduced and without GPS data, and a README). Unzip it and open index.html to see it; to put it online, send the whole folder to your web host (OVH, o2switch, Netlify, GitHub Pages…). “🌐 This page only” gives a single .html file, everything inside. “↗ Preview” opens it in a browser tab, like a visitor."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I put my JavaScript in My website, and is it safe?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the “Site JS” tab of the code. It only runs in “▶ Test” (an isolated preview that only sees your site, never the app or your database) and in the exported site (js/script.js). A script tag written in the HTML of a page is removed when saving, like onclick attributes and javascript: links: the message says so."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I move a block or an element of my website?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “🌐 My website”, tool ↖ Select: take the element in the preview and drag it; a blue line shows where it will go (before, after, or inside with dotted lines when you aim at the middle of a section). With the keyboard: select it, then Alt + ↑ or Alt + ↓. The code follows on its own, and ↶ Undo puts it back."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put one of my diagrams on my website?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “🌐 My website”: ＋ Blocks → “🏛️ Diagram” → “One of my diagrams” (the picker) or “Create a diagram”. It is drawn by Charts → My diagrams with today’s figures, redrawn each time the site is opened, and frozen on export (the names of living people are removed). On the right: “✎ Edit the diagram”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I open my website on another computer, with MAMP, WAMP or XAMPP?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “🌐 My website”: “⤓ Export ▾” → “📦 The whole site as .zip” (site-name-YYYY-MM-DD.zip). Unzip it wherever you like: a double-click on index.html is enough (pages, photos, scripts and the interactive map work without a server; the map background and Google fonts come from the Internet). With MAMP (Mac): copy the folder into htdocs, then http://localhost:8888/mon-site/. With WAMP: into www; with XAMPP: into htdocs, then http://localhost/mon-site/. Addresses are relative: it also works in a subfolder and with any web host. No need for PHP or a database. Everything is explained in LISEZ-MOI.html, in the .zip. As when publishing, no living person and no confidential record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change the style of all elements of the same kind on my website?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose an element in the preview: at the top of the right panel, “Apply to” offers “All .class elements” (the default when the element has a class) or “This element only”. With the class, your settings (colour, margins, size, alignment…) write a real rule in the site’s CSS, updated without duplicates: you see it change in the CSS tab, and what you type in the CSS comes back into the panel. “On hover” writes a :hover rule; “💻 Tablet” or “📱 Phone” writes the rule in an @media (and the preview switches to that size). ↶ ↷ undo and redo."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the interactive map of my site move in the editor?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: in “🌐 My website”, a 🗺 map with “Interactive map” ticked can be moved (drag) and zoomed (＋ −, mouse wheel) directly in the preview, with the same small script and the same backgrounds as the exported site. A single click still selects it so you can set it up on the right."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get a real map background on my website?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose the 🗺 Map block, then on the right “Map background”: the same list as Settings → Maps and base maps (Cassini, État-major, OpenStreetMap, satellite…). The map is made as an image with the credit of the background. “Interactive map”: in the published site, people can move it and zoom; it uses your background if allowed (OpenStreetMap, CARTO, OpenTopoMap, OpenHistoricalMap), otherwise the OpenStreetMap background; the image remains for anyone without JavaScript."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put my website online directly (Netlify, GitHub Pages, FTP)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In “🌐 My website”: “📤 Publish”. Netlify: create a free account, then a token (User settings → Applications → Personal access tokens) and paste it. GitHub Pages: a “fine-grained” token with Contents and Pages permissions; the repository is created if it does not exist. Secure FTP: the server, username and password given by your web host; the connection is always encrypted. The token or password stays on this computer: it is never shown or backed up with the database; “× Forget” erases it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I put the calculator on the desktop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: 🪟 in the calculator’s bar, or Dashboard → “🪟 Desktop widgets…” → 🧮 Calculator. In the desktop app, it is a small separate window (always on top if you tick it); in a browser, a standalone .html file."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put a widget on my computer’s desktop (PC, Mac, Linux)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the LCNDA desktop app: Dashboard → 🪟 on a widget (Today’s anniversaries, This week, Random person, Random image), or “🪟 Desktop widgets…” at the bottom of the column: On this day, An ancestor, Photo of the day, Countdown. A small frameless window, read-only; drag it by its title (it remembers its place); “Always on top” and “Start with the computer” are ticked in the same window; right-click on the widget: open LCNDA, remove it. In a browser, “⤓ Download the widget (.html)” gives a standalone file to keep in a tab (without living people)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I write a letter, a story or a report in the app (the word processor)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 🛠️ Editing tools → “📝 Word processor”, then “＋ New document” or “🧩 Start from a template” (letter to the archives, request for a record, an ancestor’s story, visit report, research report, letter to the family, record transcription…). The text is written on real pages, with their margins: a paragraph that no longer fits moves to the next page. 💾 Save (⌘S) puts it in the list; “⚙️ Settings…” lets you save it on its own every few minutes."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do my documents work well in Word, LibreOffice or pandoc?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes: ⤓ Export → Word (.docx), LibreOffice (.odt), RTF, Markdown, text or web page. Headings, bold, italic, colours, multi-level lists (the numbered list properly restarts at 1), tables with merged cells, images, notes, header and footer, landscape sections, comments and tracked changes are kept; RTF now writes real lists and vertical merges, and its accents are readable everywhere. ⤒ Import also reads files from Word, LibreOffice, Google Docs and pandoc (styles “Titre 1” or “Heading 1”, “List Bullet 2”, header of a previous section, YAML header of a Markdown file). ❓ Help (View menu) shows the answer over the document."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I format text (styles, bold, lists, line spacing…)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose a style on the left of the bar (Normal, Heading 1 to 6, Quote, Caption…): headings form the outline, the table of contents and the 🧭 panel. Bold ⌘B, italic ⌘I, underline ⌘U, strikethrough, superscript, subscript, small caps, font (the single font list), size, colour and highlight. Bulleted and numbered lists: Tab goes down a level, Shift+Tab goes back up, Enter on an empty line leaves the list. The right panel (🎨) sets alignment, line spacing, space before and after, indents, drop cap, border and shading."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create my own styles, change style set or take those of a book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Right panel → Styles: “＋ New style” starts from the paragraph at the cursor; “✎ Edit the style” changes all paragraphs of that style at once. “Style set” (Classic, Modern, Elegant, Archives, Very readable, Old book) changes the fonts and colours of headings and text. Format → “📐 Take the styles of a book…” copies the styles of a book from the layout."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add an image, a table, a note or a link?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Insert menu (or the bar): 🖼 Image (the single image window; a pasted or dropped image also goes into the media), ▦ Table (rows, columns, repeated header row) or 🧾 Table from the Spreadsheet, 🔗 Link (⌘K), ¹ Footnote (⌥⌘F, it shows at the bottom of its page) or endnote. Click an image to resize it by a corner; right-click: inline, square (text around it), top and bottom, caption. Table menu: rows and columns, merge (drag from one cell to another), sort, borders, shading."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I cite a person, a place or a source from my database in a text?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Insert → 👤 A person from the database (⇧⌘P): the usual picker, then what to write — the name (link to the record), the name and years, the birth or death with date and place. These texts follow the record: they are updated when the document is opened. 📍 A place, 🎞 A media item, 📖 a person’s biography (written by the app), 🌳 a small tree of 3 generations, 📊 a diagram. ❝ Cited source: the ready-made citation (4 styles), as a note; 📚 Bibliography picks it up on its own."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a table of contents, an index or a bibliography?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Insert → 📑 Table of contents (based on Heading 1, 2, 3, with page numbers), 🔤 Index (the people, places and words marked with “Mark for the index”) or 📚 Bibliography (the cited sources). They redo themselves when the text changes; a click on a line of the table of contents takes you there."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I set up the page: size, margins, orientation, header and footer, watermark?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Right panel → Page (or File → “📄 Size, margins and orientation…”): the app’s common sizes or “Custom size”, portrait or landscape, margins, page colour, watermark (DRAFT, COPY…). Double-click at the top or bottom of a page: the header and footer (page number, number of pages, title, date; different first page). Insert → “Section break”: what follows on a landscape page, for example for a large table; “▥ Columns” splits paragraphs into columns."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do several people review a text: tracked changes and comments?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tools → “✍ Track changes” (⌥⌘T): what is added is written in green underlined, what is deleted stays struck through in red, with the person’s name (Settings → “My name”). The ✍ panel lists the changes: ✓ Accept, ✗ Reject, or all at once. “Show changes”: all, simple or none. To comment: choose a passage then 💬 (⌥⌘M); you can reply, mark “Resolved”, delete (↶ Undo right after). Word and LibreOffice pick up the changes and the comments."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I find and replace a word in a document?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"⌘F (or 🔍): the search bar, with “Aa” (match case), “Whole word” and “Simple patterns” (* = some letters, ? = one letter, # = one digit). Each match is highlighted and listed in the 🧭 “Results” panel. ⇧⌘H goes straight to replacing: “Replace” or “Replace all” (↶ Undo right after)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I open or save a Word (.docx) or LibreOffice (.odt) file?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open: “⤒ Import…” in the list (or File → Import, ⌘O): Word (.docx), LibreOffice (.odt), RTF, Markdown, text or web page; the window says what will come in before importing. Save: “⤓ Export ▾” at the top: Word, LibreOffice, PDF, RTF, Markdown, text, web page, or the tool’s own file; “Also store a copy in the media” keeps the .docx with the other documents. Everything is done in the app, with no extra software; a booby-trapped file (too big, zip bomb, XML with declarations, scripts) is refused or cleaned."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I print or make a PDF of a document?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"“⤓ Export ▾” → 📄 PDF or 🖨 Print (⌘P): each page comes out exactly as on screen, with the header, footer, footnotes and watermark. For the PDF, choose “Save as PDF” as the printer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I keep versions of a document and compare them?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"File → “🕘 Save as new version” keeps the text as it is. File → “🕘 Versions…” opens the versions window: click a version to see it on the right, tick two versions (or “Now”) to compare them (removed words struck through in red, added ones in green), “↺ Go back to this version” (the current text is kept first, ↶ Undo possible)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find older versions (text, site, photo, sound, montage, board)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The same “🕘 Versions” window everywhere: File → “🕘 Versions…” in the word processor, My website, Retouch, Sound studio and the montage; “🕘 Versions…” under the photo or sound in quick retouch and Quick sound; “📸 Snapshots” in a board. At the top, “All / ✎ Named / 🕘 Automatic”; the list is grouped by Today, This week, Older, with “Now” at the very top. Click a version to see it; tick two to compare them (text struck through / added, two sounds to listen to as A / B, two images with a line to drag). “↺ Go back to this version” asks nothing: what was there is kept first, and “↶ Undo” puts everything back. The ⋯ button of a version (or right-click): ✎ Rename, ⧉ Make a copy, ⤓ Export, 🗑 Delete (with “↶ Undo”). The original of a photo or sound is never deleted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I check a story against my records, count the words or listen to it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Tools → “🔎 Check against the records”: the dates and places written near a name from the database are compared with that person’s record (birth, marriage, death, ages, Sosa numbers); nothing is changed on its own. “🔢 Word count” (also at the bottom left); the document’s language is used for spelling (words underlined, corrections on right-click) and for “🔊 Read aloud”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I send a document into a book in the layout?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"File → “📐 Send to the layout…”: a new book, or the end of an existing book. Heading 1 paragraphs become chapters, bold and italic stay, images get their own page with their caption, notes go into a “Notes” part. The layout remains the tool for composing the book; the word processor the one for writing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I ask the family who is in a photo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open the photo (viewer) and click “📤 Ask who it is” (also: Lists → Media → 👤 Faces → “📤 Ask the family” on unknown faces, or Lists → 💬 Questions for relatives → “🔍 Who is in this photo?” → “＋ New request…” for several photos). Click the frames to choose the faces (or draw one with the mouse; “🔍 Find faces” looks for them on its own), choose the names to suggest, then “⤓ Download the file”. Your cousin opens it at home, without the app or the Internet: they click a face, write a name or choose it from the list, add a note, then “Save my answers”. The file only contains the photos, frames and names you chose: never a confidential record, never a living person unless you tick the box."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import my cousins’ answers about a photo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 💬 Questions for relatives → “⤒ Import the answers” (the file “reponses-visages.json” sent back to you). For each face: the suggested name, who said it, the record to link (“Is it the same person?”, through the picker), and ✓ Accept / ✕ Refuse / Later. If several cousins answered, you see whether they agree (green “Agreement” chip) or not (red “Disagreement”). An accepted face becomes an identification on the photo; “↶ Undo” puts everything back as before."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I write a letter to request a record?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a record: “⋯” → “📮 Request a record…”; also in “What’s missing?” (“📮 Request the record” button on a missing birth, marriage or death) and in Lists → Letter register (“📮 Request a record”, “Prepare the letter” for recent records). Choose the record and what you are asking for (full copy, extract with or without parentage, search in the archives, transcription of a record abroad, dispensation file): the letter writes itself, with the person, the dates, the parents, your relationship and the recipient’s address (town hall, departmental archives, Central civil registry in Nantes, diocesan archives), which you can correct. The time-limit rule is explained: in France a birth or marriage record is freely accessible after 75 years; before that, the full copy is reserved for direct family. Then: 📝 word processor, 🖨 Print or ⤓ PDF, ✉️ e-mail ready (nothing is sent by the app), 📁 copy in the media, and “📮 Store in tracked letters” (with the reminder date, also in “My research week”)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I set my address and my letter templates?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the “📮 Request the record” window, ⚙️ button at the top: your name, your address, your town, your e-mail, the reminder delay and the templates for each kind of letter (with placeholders like {personne}, {evenement}, {demande}). Everything is kept straight away; “💾 Saved settings” keeps several sets under a name."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find “Write about this person” and research reports?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On a person’s record, the 📝 button at the top opens a story already filled with their biography (also: 📖 Biography block → “Put in ▾” → word processor). In the Research log, “⋯” → “📝 Report” makes a document of the research shown; in archive visits, “📝 Report” on each mission sheet."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I organise the family book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Clean export → Family book: tick the parts (cover, preface, figures, tree, records, chronology, places, index, closing word) and order them; the same for what each record shows. Choose the style, font, page size, then the format: PDF (through printing), HTML, Word, OpenDocument, ePub (e-reader), Markdown, text or CSV."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the layout workshop (InDesign style)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Clean export → Family book → “Open the layout workshop”. It is a small InDesign for making the book of your genealogy: single pages or facing pages, masters (folio, running head), layers, text frames (computer font, size, leading, tracking, capitals, alignments, indents, drop cap, columns), shapes (rectangle, ellipse, polygon, star, line, pen), image frames, swatches and gradients, strokes, effects (opacity, blend, drop shadow, feather, rounded corners), text wrap, Pathfinder, align and distribute, links. You save, pick it up later, export."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Where do I find the books made in the layout workshop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Lists → 📐 Family books: all the books and your designs, with their cover. Open, duplicate, rename, export, delete; at the top, “New book from the family”, “From a design”, “Blank page”, “Default settings” and the workshop."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I start a book in the layout workshop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the workshop: “＋ New book from the family” (a branch, the parts you want, a design: the cover, one page per person with their photo, the chronology, the index… texts that are too long continue on the next page), “🎨 New from a design”, or “📄 Blank page”. You choose the size, the margins (top, bottom, inside, outside), the columns and gutter, the bleed and the baseline grid. “⚙️ Default settings” sets what new documents get."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a design in the layout workshop, and how do I make one?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A design is a template: cover, masters, paragraph and character styles, colours. The app offers six (Classic, Modern, Elegant, Sepia, Photo album, Plain): “Use” or “Edit (as my own)”. Your own design: open a book, then File → “🎨 Save as design”. The list shows the cover of each book and each design."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add pages or go to a page in the layout workshop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Page menu (or 📄 Pages panel): “＋ Page at the end”, “＋ Insert…” (as many pages as you want, before or after a given page, at the start or end, with the chosen master), duplicate, delete, move a page forward or back. To go to a page: the “Page” box at the bottom, ⌘J, or Page Up / Page Down on the keyboard."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the shortcuts of the layout workshop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"V selection, T text, F image frame, M rectangle, L ellipse, Y polygon, E star, \\ line, P pen (double-click or Enter to finish, click on the first point to close), H hand, Z zoom. ⌘Z / ⇧⌘Z undo and redo, ⌘C ⌘X ⌘V ⌘D, ⌘A, ⌫ delete, arrows to nudge (Shift: 5 mm), ⌘] ⌘[ bring forward and send backward, ⌘0 whole page, ⌘S save, W preview. Alt while dragging: a copy. Shift: proportions kept."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export the book from the layout workshop as a PDF?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"File → “⤓ Export as PDF (print)” opens the book ready to print: “Print”, then “Save as PDF”. For a printer: “PDF with bleed and crop marks”. Also: HTML page (photos included) and document file (.json) to keep or pass on to someone (“Import a document” in the workshop)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I undo a change in the layout workshop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"↶ ↷ buttons at the top (or ⌘Z / ⇧⌘Z). The 🕘 History panel lists each change: a click on a step goes back to it, the following ones stay greyed out as long as you do nothing else."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I widen the right panel of the layout workshop or of Retouch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Drag its left edge (the cursor becomes ↔) to the left to widen it, to the right to narrow it; the width is kept. Double-click the edge: starting size."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I create or change a master in the layout workshop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"📄 Pages panel → Masters: “＋ New master” (you give it a name), “Edit” to place what comes back on its pages (folio, rules…), “→ p. N” to apply it to the current page, “Page without master”, or “Detach items” to change on this page only what comes from the master."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I put the colour I want on a text or an object in the workshop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Text: choose the words, then 🅰 Text panel → Character → “Free colour” (the square opens all colours, or type a code like #7a2e2e; the circles are the swatch colours). Object: click the fill or stroke square at the bottom of the tools, the palette opens directly. ⇄ (or ⇧X) swaps fill and stroke, ⧄ (or /) empties them, X switches from one to the other."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make text flow around a photo (text wrap)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"In the layout workshop: choose the photo, 🔧 Object panel → “Text wrap”: around the frame, around the shape (ellipse) or jump object, and the distance. Neighbouring text frames flow past it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I import my tree from another program?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Import → “⤒ Import a tree…”: GEDCOM file (also from Ancestry, MyHeritage, Geneanet, FamilySearch), Gramps (.gramps or .gpkg), Hérédis (.hmw), GedZip or MacFamilyTree."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I add a cousin’s tree to my database?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Merge a tree: their GEDCOM is compared with yours, then you decide record by record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I make a family book as a PDF?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Clean export → Family book: the whole database, or the ancestors and/or descendants of a person over the number of generations you want, with the tree generation by generation, the biographies, the photos and the chronology. “Open for printing” then “Print / save as PDF”."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I export only one branch?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Settings → Clean export: choose the starting person and the ancestors or descendants to export."}}]}</script>
    <!-- FAQ-JSONLD:FIN -->
    <style>
        :root { --presentation-ink:#080808; --presentation-blue:#0066cc; --presentation-paper:#fbfbfb; --presentation-muted:#515151; }
        * { box-sizing:border-box; }
        html { scroll-behavior:smooth; }
        body { margin:0; font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; color:var(--presentation-ink); }
        a { color:inherit; text-decoration:none; }
        .eyebrow { display:inline-block; color:var(--presentation-blue); font-size:12px; font-weight:900; letter-spacing:.14em; }
        .button { display:inline-flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; gap:9px; min-height:42px; padding:11px 15px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:var(--presentation-blue); color:#fff; box-shadow:4px 4px 0 rgba(5,93,255,.25); font-weight:700; line-height:1.2; text-align:center; transition:.15s ease; }
        .button:hover { transform:translate(3px,3px); box-shadow:1px 1px 0 var(--presentation-ink); }
        .button.secondary { background:#fff; color:var(--presentation-ink); }
        .button > span[aria-hidden] { font-size:16px; }
        .presentation-body { min-width:320px; overflow-x:hidden; background-color:var(--presentation-paper); background-image:linear-gradient(rgba(0,102,204,.035) 1px,transparent 1px),linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(0,102,204,.035) 1px,transparent 1px); background-size:28px 28px; }

        /* ─── En-tête : texte à gauche, tableau des notes à droite (centré verticalement) ─── */
        .presentation-hero { display:grid; grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(360px,540px); gap:clamp(32px,6vw,96px); max-width:1450px; min-height:640px; margin:0 auto; padding:clamp(40px,6vw,80px) clamp(24px,7vw,108px); align-items:center; }
        .presentation-hero-copy { position:relative; min-width:0; }
        .presentation-acronym { margin:17px 0 -14px; color:var(--presentation-blue); font-weight:900; font-size:clamp(64px,11vw,160px); line-height:.8; letter-spacing:-.09em; opacity:.14; user-select:none; }
        .presentation-hero h1 { position:relative; max-width:750px; margin:0; font-size:clamp(45px,6.5vw,92px); line-height:.9; letter-spacing:-.075em; }
        .presentation-lead { position:relative; max-width:600px; margin:30px 0 0; font-size:clamp(17px,1.7vw,24px); line-height:1.35; font-weight:600; }
        .presentation-actions { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:14px; margin-top:34px; }
        .presentation-hero-board { position:relative; width:100%; min-height:410px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#fff; box-shadow:10px 10px 0 rgba(8,8,8,.13); overflow:hidden; }
        .presentation-hero-board::before { content:""; position:absolute; inset:0; background:repeating-linear-gradient(90deg,transparent 0 50px,rgba(0,102,204,.07) 50px 51px),repeating-linear-gradient(0deg,transparent 0 50px,rgba(0,102,204,.07) 50px 51px); }
        .presentation-note { position:absolute; z-index:2; display:grid; gap:8px; width:58%; padding:18px 19px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); box-shadow:6px 6px 0 rgba(8,8,8,.14); transform:rotate(-3deg); }
        .presentation-note span { color:var(--presentation-blue); font-size:11px; font-weight:900; letter-spacing:.1em; }
        .presentation-note strong { font-size:19px; }
        .presentation-note small { font-size:12px; line-height:1.3; font-weight:700; }
        .note-blue { top:37px; left:25px; background:#dbeaff; }
        .note-white { top:152px; right:20px; background:#fff; transform:rotate(4deg); }
        .note-black { bottom:34px; left:64px; background:#111; color:#fff; transform:rotate(-1.5deg); }
        .note-black span { color:#fff; }
        .presentation-thread { position:absolute; z-index:1; height:2px; background:var(--presentation-blue); transform-origin:left center; }
        .thread-one { width:67%; top:118px; left:44%; transform:rotate(37deg); }
        .thread-two { width:57%; top:294px; left:5%; transform:rotate(-18deg); }

        /* ─── Sections ─── */
        .presentation-section { position:relative; max-width:1450px; margin:0 auto; padding:clamp(70px,9vw,120px) clamp(24px,7vw,108px); }
        .presentation-section::before { content:""; position:absolute; top:0; left:50%; width:100vw; border-top:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); transform:translateX(-50%); }
        .presentation-section-head { max-width:760px; }
        .presentation-section h2 { margin:13px 0 0; font-size:clamp(34px,4.7vw,64px); line-height:.96; letter-spacing:-.065em; }
        .presentation-section-head > p { margin:22px 0 0; color:var(--presentation-muted); font-size:18px; line-height:1.5; font-weight:600; }

        /* ─── Téléchargements ─── */
        .presentation-downloads { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr)); gap:18px; margin-top:44px; align-items:start; }
        .presentation-os { display:grid; gap:14px; padding:24px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#fff; box-shadow:6px 6px 0 rgba(5,93,255,.21); }
        .presentation-os h3 { margin:0; font-size:28px; letter-spacing:-.04em; }
        .presentation-os-req { margin:-6px 0 0; color:var(--presentation-muted); font-size:14px; font-weight:700; line-height:1.4; }
        .presentation-os-group { display:grid; gap:10px; padding-top:14px; border-top:1px solid #d7d7d7; }
        .presentation-os-group > strong { font-size:12px; letter-spacing:.1em; text-transform:uppercase; }
        .presentation-os .button { width:100%; justify-content:space-between; text-align:left; }
        .presentation-os .button small { display:block; font-size:12px; font-weight:600; opacity:.85; }
        .presentation-os-help { margin:0; color:var(--presentation-muted); font-size:13px; line-height:1.45; font-weight:600; }
        .presentation-os-help code, .presentation-callout code { padding:1px 4px; border:1px solid #b8d5f5; background:#eaf2ff; color:#111; font-size:.92em; word-break:break-all; }
        .presentation-os-source { grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(260px,420px); align-items:center; gap:24px; margin-top:18px; }
        .presentation-os-source .presentation-os-group { padding-top:0; border-top:0; }
        .presentation-os-source .presentation-os-help { margin-top:10px; }
        @media (max-width:760px) { .presentation-os-source { grid-template-columns:1fr; } }
        /* ─── Nouveautés de la version (bloc qui s'ouvre et se ferme) ─── */
        .presentation-changes { margin-top:18px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#fff; box-shadow:6px 6px 0 rgba(5,93,255,.21); }
        .presentation-changes summary { display:flex; justify-content:space-between; align-items:center; gap:16px; padding:18px 24px; cursor:pointer; list-style:none; font-size:20px; font-weight:800; letter-spacing:-.02em; }
        .presentation-changes summary::-webkit-details-marker { display:none; }
        .presentation-changes summary::after { content:"+"; flex:none; display:grid; place-items:center; width:32px; height:32px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); font-size:20px; line-height:1; }
        .presentation-changes[open] summary::after { content:"−"; }
        .presentation-changes summary small { display:block; margin-top:3px; color:var(--presentation-muted); font-size:13px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0; }
        .presentation-changes-body { padding:4px 24px 22px; border-top:1px solid #d7d7d7; font-size:15px; line-height:1.55; }
        .presentation-changes-body h4 { margin:18px 0 6px; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:.1em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--presentation-blue); }
        .presentation-changes-body ul { margin:0; padding-left:20px; }
        .presentation-changes-body li { margin:4px 0; }
        .presentation-changes-body p { margin:0; }
        .presentation-changes-old { margin-top:12px; }
        .presentation-version { margin-top:14px; border:1px solid #d7d7d7; }
        .presentation-version summary { padding:12px 16px; font-size:16px; cursor:pointer; list-style:none; font-weight:800; }
        .presentation-version summary::-webkit-details-marker { display:none; }
        .presentation-version summary::after { content:"+"; float:right; font-weight:800; }
        .presentation-version[open] summary::after { content:"−"; }
        .presentation-version summary small { display:block; color:var(--presentation-muted); font-size:13px; font-weight:700; }
        .presentation-version-body { padding:0 16px 14px; }
        .presentation-callout { display:grid; grid-template-columns:200px 1fr; gap:18px; margin-top:22px; padding:19px 22px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#fff5d6; box-shadow:5px 5px 0 rgba(8,8,8,.14); font-size:14px; line-height:1.5; }
        .presentation-callout p { margin:0; }
        .presentation-callout p + p { margin-top:8px; }
        .presentation-callout a { color:var(--presentation-blue); font-weight:800; text-decoration:underline; text-underline-offset:3px; }

        /* ─── Découvrir (texte de présentation) ─── */
        .presentation-intro { max-width:980px; margin:30px 0 0; font-size:clamp(19px,2vw,26px); line-height:1.38; font-weight:600; }
        .presentation-intro strong { color:var(--presentation-blue); }
        .presentation-about-grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr)); gap:18px; margin-top:48px; }
        .presentation-about { padding:24px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#fff; box-shadow:6px 6px 0 rgba(5,93,255,.21); }
        .presentation-about.about-intro { grid-column:1 / -1; }
        .presentation-about h3 { margin:0 0 12px; font-size:24px; line-height:1.05; letter-spacing:-.04em; }
        .presentation-about p { margin:0; color:#3f3f3f; font-weight:600; line-height:1.5; }
        .presentation-about p + p, .presentation-about p + ul, .presentation-about ul + p { margin-top:10px; }
        .presentation-about ul { margin:0; padding:0; list-style:none; font-weight:600; line-height:1.45; color:#3f3f3f; }
        .presentation-about li { padding:7px 0; border-top:1px solid #d7d7d7; }
        .presentation-about li::before { content:"→"; color:var(--presentation-blue); margin-right:7px; font-weight:900; }
        .presentation-strengths { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr)); gap:0; margin-top:28px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#111; color:#fff; box-shadow:10px 10px 0 rgba(8,8,8,.13); }
        .presentation-strengths div { min-width:0; padding:20px 22px; border-right:1px solid #333; border-bottom:1px solid #333; }
        .presentation-strengths strong { display:block; color:#7fb6ff; font-size:15px; letter-spacing:-.01em; }
        .presentation-strengths span { display:block; margin-top:6px; color:#d9d9d9; font-size:14px; font-weight:600; line-height:1.4; }
        @media (max-width:760px) { .presentation-strengths { grid-template-columns:1fr; } }
        .presentation-beta-note { margin:22px 0 0; color:var(--presentation-muted); font-size:14px; font-weight:700; line-height:1.5; }
        @media (max-width:760px) { .presentation-about-grid { grid-template-columns:1fr; } }

        /* ─── Fonctionnalités ─── */
        .presentation-feature-grid { display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr); gap:18px; margin-top:48px; }
        .presentation-feature { min-height:265px; padding:24px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#fff; box-shadow:6px 6px 0 rgba(5,93,255,.21); }
        .presentation-feature.feature-blue { background:#dbeaff; }
        .presentation-feature.feature-dark { background:#111; color:#fff; }
        .presentation-feature h3 { margin:28px 0 10px; font-size:25px; line-height:1; letter-spacing:-.04em; }
        .presentation-feature p { margin:0; color:#4f4f4f; font-weight:600; line-height:1.45; }
        .presentation-feature.feature-dark p { color:#d9d9d9; }
        .feature-index { display:block; color:var(--presentation-blue); font-weight:900; font-size:13px; letter-spacing:.12em; }
        .presentation-feature.feature-dark .feature-index { color:#7fb6ff; }
        .presentation-feature ul { margin:22px 0 0; padding:0; list-style:none; font-size:13px; font-weight:800; }
        .presentation-feature li { padding:8px 0; border-top:1px solid #d7d7d7; }
        .presentation-feature li::before { content:"→"; color:var(--presentation-blue); margin-right:7px; }

        /* ─── Aperçu (carrousel sans défilement automatique) ─── */
        .presentation-carousel { position:relative; margin-top:44px; }
        .presentation-carousel-track { display:flex; overflow-x:auto; scroll-snap-type:x mandatory; scroll-behavior:smooth; scrollbar-width:none; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#fff; box-shadow:10px 10px 0 rgba(8,8,8,.13); }
        .presentation-carousel-track::-webkit-scrollbar { display:none; }
        .presentation-carousel-track:focus-visible { outline:3px solid var(--presentation-blue); outline-offset:4px; }
        .presentation-slide { flex:0 0 100%; margin:0; scroll-snap-align:start; }
        .presentation-slide img { display:block; width:100%; height:auto; aspect-ratio:3456 / 2234; object-fit:cover; background:#f1f1f1; }
        .presentation-slide figcaption { display:flex; gap:12px; align-items:baseline; padding:14px 18px; border-top:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); font-weight:800; }
        .presentation-slide figcaption span { color:var(--presentation-blue); font-size:12px; font-weight:900; letter-spacing:.12em; }
        .presentation-carousel-controls { display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between; gap:16px; margin-top:22px; }
        .presentation-carousel-controls .button { min-width:52px; font-size:20px; }
        .presentation-carousel-controls .button:disabled { opacity:.35; cursor:default; transform:none; box-shadow:4px 4px 0 rgba(5,93,255,.25); }
        .presentation-carousel-dots { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center; gap:8px; }
        .presentation-carousel-dots button { width:13px; height:13px; padding:0; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#fff; cursor:pointer; }
        .presentation-carousel-dots button[aria-current="true"] { background:var(--presentation-blue); }

        /* ─── FAQ ─── */
        /* Cadre de la FAQ, dans la page */
        .presentation-faq-box { margin-top:44px; padding:clamp(22px,3.5vw,44px); border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#fff; box-shadow:10px 10px 0 rgba(5,93,255,.21); }
        .presentation-faq-box > h3 { margin:0 0 18px; font-size:clamp(22px,2.6vw,30px); letter-spacing:-.03em; }
        .presentation-faq-hint { margin:12px 0 0; color:var(--presentation-muted); font-size:16px; font-weight:600; }
        .presentation-faq-search { display:block; width:100%; max-width:760px; margin-top:0; padding:14px 16px; border:2px solid var(--presentation-ink); background:#fff; box-shadow:4px 4px 0 rgba(5,93,255,.25); font:600 16px Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:var(--presentation-ink); }
        .presentation-faq-search:focus-visible { outline:3px solid var(--presentation-blue); outline-offset:3px; }
        .presentation-faq-cat { margin-top:28px; }
        .presentation-faq-cat h3 { margin:0 0 8px; padding-bottom:8px; border-bottom:1px solid #d7d7d7; font-size:20px; letter-spacing:-.02em; }
        .presentation-faq-cat h3 small { margin-left:8px; color:var(--presentation-blue); font-size:12px; font-weight:900; letter-spacing:.1em; }
        .presentation-faq-item { border-bottom:1px solid #e3e3e3; background:#fff; }
        .presentation-faq-item summary { display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; padding:12px 0; cursor:pointer; list-style:none; font-size:17px; font-weight:800; line-height:1.3; }
        .presentation-faq-item summary:hover { color:var(--presentation-blue); }
        .presentation-faq-item summary::-webkit-details-marker { display:none; }
        .presentation-faq-item summary::before { content:"▶"; display:inline-block; flex:none; font-size:12px; transition:transform .15s ease; }
        .presentation-faq-item[open] summary::before { transform:rotate(90deg); }
        .presentation-faq-item p { max-width:900px; margin:0; padding:0 0 16px 22px; color:#3f3f3f; font-weight:600; line-height:1.55; }
        .presentation-faq-empty { margin-top:30px; color:var(--presentation-muted); font-weight:700; }

